return A.a(A.Nd(a2,360,f,e,'You read a person\'s dictated note and work out what it IS, before anyone\ndecides where to file it. Read ALL of it.\n\nReply with JSON only:\n{"about": "<one clause: what this note is about>",\n "purpose": "<decision|debrief|reminder|idea|question|log|vent>",\n "topics": ["<the few things it actually covers>"],\n "subjects": ["<each SEPARATE subject the note is about - usually just one>"],\n "asides": <true|false>,\n "clarity": "<clear|rambling|unclear>",\n "singleSubject": <true|false>,\n "recording": "<dictation|conversation|phone call|talking to someone|background|meeting|test or misfire>",\n "dictated": "<all|most|some|none>"}\n\nRULES:\n- THE FIRST LINE IS AN AUTO-GENERATED TITLE AND IT IS SOMETIMES WRONG. The\n  words spoken after it are the truth. If the title disagrees with the body,\n  ignore the title completely. ("End Of Year Dictation Plan" was the title on\n  a note whose actual words were about keeping two buildings by year end -\n  there was no dictation in it at all.)\n- "about" is the DOMINANT subject, not a list. If someone is mentioned in\n  passing while the note is really about something else, the something else is\n  the subject: "Dwan came up while I was working out cash flow" is a note\n  about CASH FLOW.\n- "clarity": "clear" = one subject, plainly stated. "rambling" = real content\n  but it wanders across several threads or thinks aloud. "unclear" = not\n  enough to act on - chatter, a fragment, a stuck repeat, a misfire.\n- A note that is only the speaker TESTING the recorder or the app - "this is\n  a test", "testing one two", "hello this is Roger", talking fast to see if it\n  keeps up - is "unclear": a test, not a note. A note ABOUT improving\n  dictation is a real note; a few words checking that dictation works is not.\n- "singleSubject": false when the note genuinely spans several unrelated\n  things, so that a reasonable person could file it in more than one place.\n- "subjects" is NOT the same as "topics". Topics are everything mentioned;\n  subjects are the separate things the note is ABOUT - the things you would\n  file it under. A plan to raise money that mentions the bank, the IRS and an\n  investor is ONE subject. A note that works through the Dwan property and\n  then, separately, a feature for the dictation app is TWO. Most notes have\n  one. List at most four, most important first.\n- "asides": true when the note has its subject(s) but ALSO drifts into\n  something unrelated to them - a personal remark, an errand, feeding the\n  dog, a different project mentioned in passing without being worked on.\n  Asides are NOT subjects: do not list them in "subjects".\n- "recording" is what KIND of recording this is. A "dictation" is the owner\n  speaking a note to the app - an idea, task, plan, reminder, question or\n  reflection. Otherwise: "conversation" (people talking with each other in\n  person), "phone call" (either side of a call), "talking to someone" (the\n  owner speaking to another person rather than to the app), "background" (TV,\n  radio, a video, music or people nearby, picked up by accident), "meeting" (a\n  group discussion, even one about a real subject), or "test or misfire" (a\n  mic check, a few stray words, a fragment, a stuck repeat). There are no\n  speaker labels: turn-taking, greetings, questions answered by someone else,\n  "incoming call", people addressed by name and broadcast-style speech are the\n  signs.\n- "dictated" is how much of the recording is the owner dictating a note: "all",\n  "most", "some" or "none". A real note that opens with a few words to someone\n  nearby is still a dictation, "most". A conversation or call with one sentence\n  of note in it is not: "some".\n- BE HONEST RATHER THAN HELPFUL. "unclear" and singleSubject=false are the\n  right answers more often than they are comfortable. A note nobody could\n  confidently file is better left alone than filed wrongly.\n- Say nothing about folders. You are not choosing where it goes.\n',d,a,"understand"),$async$aPI)
