B.aiK=s(["DARK MODE IS READABLE NOW TOO. If your phone is set to dark, a lot of Voicley was too faint - the grey hint inside the dictate box worst of all, at less than half the standard for readable text. That hint, the outline around the box and the quiet grey labels are all clearer. In the whole history of the app dark mode had never once been measured. This is the first time.","The two shades of grey text still read as two. Making the fainter one readable would have brought it level with the shade above it, so that one moved as well - otherwise two tiers would have quietly collapsed into one.","Daylight mode is exactly as it was. So are the blue, the teal and the record red."],t.s)
B.aZl=new A.v(1206,B.aiK)
B.agr=s(["LISTEN AND DICTATE ON ONE SCREEN. The menu now reads Inbox, Listen and Listen and Dictate. Listen is the reader. Listen and Dictate shows your words in the Inbox box at the bottom and the reading above it, in two equal halves, each with its own pause. Opening it from the menu starts both. The box stays put; only the words inside it scroll. New dictation posts the words in the box, mic on or off.","YOUR WATCH. Voicley on a Galaxy watch: Record, Stop and New on the watch face, with a one-tap Record shortcut. Each watch recording lands in the phone Inbox as its own dictation and is written out when it arrives.","TEXT SIZE. Nothing in the app is smaller than 13 any more - the fine print that was 11.5 is 13 now.","LONG NOTES. A very long note opens in sections, so typing in it is quick. The sweep that measures recordings runs off the screen.","SYNC LINE. The line counts notes, not saves, and stays quiet while you dictate. A note with no project can no longer be dropped on upload."],t.s)
B.aVI=new A.v(1243,B.agr)
B.aiB=s(['USAGE. A Usage screen shows the AI tokens you have, what you used today and what uses them. Every "cost first" line speaks tokens, and the Inbox says when you are running low.',"NEW LOOK. The website brass, paper and ink colours and its fonts replace the iOS blue.","LISTENING. Starred folders and notes come back more often in Play all, and favorites come first after a break.",'GARBAGE. A bin in the Inbox count row opens what looks like garbage, and "Find garbage..." is in the Inbox and every folder menu. A bin on a row trashes that note; X keeps it.','SORTING. "One at a time" shows a note and its five closest folders - one tap moves it, with Undo. Sort by voice reads each note out and asks where it goes. A note the AI was not sure about says "Not sure" instead of "No folder fits".','FULL NOTE. The tab you leave a note on - My words, Tidy up or AI Rewrite - is what its row shows and what Read aloud reads. Press and hold a tab to make it the default; the default has a check box. "AI title - Undo" is gone.','TITLES. "AI is making a title..." shows until the title arrives. A newly installed device can no longer switch AI titles or rewrites off or on for all your devices.',"LONG RECORDINGS. Recordings longer than about 3 and a half hours now reach the cloud and play on every device.","SCROLLING. A button appears at the right while a list moves - hold it to jump.","READER. A plain Dictate no longer opens the full-screen reader."],t.s)
B.b_N=new A.v(1242,B.aiB)
B.alq=s(['FOLDER SUGGESTIONS. The slide-up shows whole folder names and moves one note at a time with a tick. Every move shows Undo - no "Move this item?" question anywhere.','SORT OUT LOOSE NOTES. "Sort these out with AI" reads the notes with no place: which fit a folder you have, which could start a new folder (5 or more alike), and which are not worth keeping.','RE-RUN. "Re-run folder suggestions..." in every folder menu reads its notes again, cost first. The Inbox menu adds "Choose folders to re-run...".',"FULL NOTE. Type straight into the AI Rewrite or the Tidy up; titles show without ##. Only the version on screen stands out. Read aloud is in the ... menu, and the move and trash buttons are bigger, in the list too.",'AI. Titles, tidy-ups and sorting run on Claude Haiku with a backup model. Every AI title can be undone, and "Name new notes with AI" is a switch.',"REWRITES. A rewrite is checked for anything it left out, leaves out the extra words the clean-up marked, and is made again when your words change.","CLEAN UP. Long recordings are read in full. Each row's X is a Keep button, and what the clean-up moved to Trash is listed with Restore.",'FOLDERS. "3 to clean" and folder suggestions in every folder. Moving a note no longer shoves the list. A new library gets folder ideas at the foot of the Inbox.',"SCROLLING. One standard scroll bar in the Inbox and every folder, with a handle you hold to jump.","DICTATION. Words left in the box keep their recording when you post them later.","SMALLER THINGS. Every Back button names the page it returns to. Search is the top row of the Inbox menu."],t.s)
B.aYC=new A.v(1241,B.alq)
B.adR=s(["THE FIRST SCREEN. A new Inbox now shows Your folders and Play all at the bottom, and a brand-new library starts with its Inbox on the reading list, so Play all reads back what you just said.",'AUTO SORT. Its suggestion is a "Suggested Folder" line under the date, in the Inbox list and in the full item, with your own move button beside the trash.','AUTO SORT. "Suggest folders now" works for one note, one folder or everything. The switch covers new notes only and is one setting for every device. After "Accept all" in a folder, Undo is where you tapped.',"RE-WRITTEN. One switch, in the full item's top bar and in the folder menu. Switches cover new notes; the commands do the notes you already have, and show the cost first.","CLEAN UP. Type straight into the note as it will read, with a trash can inside its box. Each marked part has its own Keep button, and a review can save and go back to the list.","PROPERTIES. The full item's ... menu has Properties: where a note lives, with a way back from a folder to the note.","PHONES. A phone now catches up on rewrites and clean-ups the same as a PC, and the folder and Inbox menu hangs from its button.","MOVE. One Move screen: tap a folder and the note moves there.","HIDDEN. Hidden and the folders inside it are never offered as a place to move a note.",'SMALLER THINGS. The two buttons under the dictation box are pills a finger can find. No word count beside the player\'s clock. On a PC, no black window flashes when Voicley starts. "Point out text that is not my note" is now "Mark TV, calls and other voices".'],t.s)
B.aTv=new A.v(1240,B.adR)
B.adx=s(["CLEAN UP. The box now shows your note itself, with the text it would remove marked. A long note opens on the marked parts; tap the header to see the whole note.","CLEAN UP. After you approve, you see the note as it will read. Save writes it and brings up the next note.","CLEAN UP. Trash is offered only when every word of a recording is marked, never for a note that is only partly not yours."],t.s)
B.b48=new A.v(1239,B.adx)
B.ah4=s(["CLEAN UP. Tap struck-through text in the dictation to keep it, and tap again to take it out. When you keep every part, the big button is OK, and answering moves straight on to the next note.",'CLEAN UP. The kinds of text it found are listed in the order they sit in the note, and "tap to see it" lights the part up.'],t.s)
B.aU6=new A.v(1238,B.ah4)
B.ahQ=s(['HANDS-FREE. "Start hands-free" dictates and plays in one press, and the screen follows the voice while it reads.',"TWO VERSIONS. A note shows your own words or the re-written version - one switch, and the tab you leave becomes that note's default.","BACK TO WHERE YOU WERE. On a full-size item the back button names the place it returns to: Back to Favorites, Back to a folder, Back to Search.",'AUTO SORT. "Suggested for here" can be closed and its notes open in place; every clean-up step can be cancelled, and each part shows where it sits in the note.'],t.s)
B.b2Z=new A.v(1237,B.ahQ)
B.ae8=s(["CLEAN UP. Auto Sort points out text in a note that is not yours - something pasted in or picked up by mistake - and takes it out when you tap.","YOUR LIBRARY. Moving your library out of OneDrive now happens exactly once, even when several parts of the app ask at the same moment."],t.s)
B.b2D=new A.v(1236,B.ae8)
B.aoa=s(["AUTO SORT. Accept all and Move now take every note waiting to be sorted, including ones that never had an Inbox spot.","READER VOICES. A voice button sits beside every Play and on the Now playing line, so you can change the voice wherever you start listening."],t.s)
B.b2X=new A.v(1235,B.aoa)
B.adL=s(["PLAY AT THE TOP. A folder, the Reading list and Favorites each have their Play button at the top right of their own page now, instead of on the side panel's rows - and the side panel's counts and headphones sit closer to the edge.","ONE BOX, THREE VERSIONS. A note can show your own words, a tidied version or a rewrite - switch on the note itself, or set a default for a folder. It opens on your own words.","READER VOICES. Change the voice from any reader, from a shorter list of the best voices.","AUTO SORT. Undo also takes an item back off the Reading list when the move had put it there.","READING LIST. A folder's headphones switch that folder and its own items only, never the folders inside it.","FOLDERS. Delete Folder always shows in a folder's menu, even in a short window.","RESTARTS. After a restart, only words that never became an item come back, so a whole session no longer returns as one duplicate note."],t.s)
B.aXd=new A.v(1234,B.adL)
B.amC=s(["HIDDEN. A Hidden row above Settings: Move to Hidden tucks a note or a folder out of the way. Hidden notes stay out of the Inbox, search, the Reading list, Favorites and Auto Sort, and a search box inside Hidden finds them - in one folder or all of Hidden.",'SAY IT. "Dark mode" and "light mode" change the look; "scratch that" and "undo" take back what you just said; "Call" and a name, while paused, rings them through Google Voice, and "Dial" calls in the middle of a dictation; "Navigate to" and a place starts Google Maps directions.',"READER AND READING LIST. The reading screen is now called Reader, and the list of items waiting to be read is the Reading list. A folder row shows one Play, and the Reading list row says Stop only while the list itself is reading.","FULL-SIZE ITEMS. The box fits the window, so its bottom line - the date, Move and the trash - always shows. Search results, Favorites and a folder's search open an item from its title too. The Dictate button left the full-size page, and a full-size dictation box goes back to normal once the dictation is saved.",'DICTATION. Your words are never taken for the reader\'s while it is silent, "new dictation" is heard two more ways, and a dictation that heard only silence no longer leaves an empty backup item behind.',"SYNC. A PC that falls behind now catches up instead of starting its download over every minute, and the extra Inboxes older versions left behind fold into your one Inbox on every device."],t.s)
B.b39=new A.v(1232,B.amC)
B.ahH=s(["THE DICTATE BUTTON DOES MORE. It shows how long you have been dictating, and the gear inside it opens the dictation settings - the listening timings and the voice commands - so Settings has one door to them. Clear Dictation shows only when there is something to clear, and a drop-down on New dictation shows what you can say.","DICTATING WHILE IT READS. Every dictation during a reading gets the pauses: the reader stops for your words and carries on after them, on the usual timers, so the Keep listening while I dictate switch is gone. The reader never turns the microphone on by itself, Pause only pauses, Play picks up at the word it stopped on, and the title lights up word by word as it is read.","AUTO SORT COMES TO YOU. The Inbox says how many new notes already have a place and files them with one tap, and every destination shows what is on its way. Any holding folder can sort what lands in it, and the full item shows Sort's suggestion and asks before it moves.","THE SIDE PANEL. Every place it opens shows the three-line menu instead of a back arrow. To read sits above Favorites, the Inbox row stays red for the whole dictation, and a reading that is paused and waiting for you shows Play, not Stop.","STATUS AT THE BOTTOM. Sync and background messages sit in a thin line at the foot of every screen, clear of the phone's own buttons, instead of under the dictation box - and inside the Now playing line while something reads.","ITEMS. The headphones and the star only toggle; the rest of the title bar opens the item. Delete in the full item works like the list, with Undo, and moving an item out of the list you came from shows the next one. The empty dictation box no longer lists commands under Speak or type.","THE OFFLINE VOICES DOWNLOAD AGAIN. Their old download site was emptied; they now come from copies checked to be identical, and a download that drops carries on from where it stopped. The long voice list has Show fewer at the top."],t.s)
B.b3a=new A.v(1231,B.ahH)
B.ahU=s(["DICTATE WHILE IT READS STOPS THE READING. Press Dictate while the reader is reading and the reading stops where it is - no countdown, it never starts again by itself - and your words land at once. Press Play to carry on from the same spot.","OFF MEANS OFF. Turning dictation off ends every pause and countdown that was waiting for your words: the reader reads straight on and every Dictate button goes back to rest. The reading notification on your phone now goes away when the reading stops.","THE SIDE PANEL. While the Reading list reads, its row says Reading with a Stop button. The Inbox row always has its Dictate button, and a click anywhere in the side panel no longer stops a dictation you started from the Inbox. Sort and Destinations moved below Trash.","FAVORITES AND FOLDERS. Favorites sits first under Folders with its count, a play button and headphones, and dropping an item on it stars it. A folder play button turns into Stop while that folder plays.","MENUS. The Inbox has its own menu with Auto sort in it, and every folder keeps Auto sort in its menu too. Move and Also file in became one line, Move or add to. Reorder Folders, Sort by Type and the rule card under a folder name are gone from the folder view.","THE READING LIST. Its labels read CAPTURE and LISTEN like the side panel, the dictation box stays put on the PC, a tap near the star or the headphones no longer opens the item, and Move lives in the full item only.","A network blip that comes straight back no longer switches a dictation to offline. Release notes read well in Dark mode, and Dark is set per device."],t.s)
B.aXB=new A.v(1230,B.ahU)
B.afP=s(["YOUR PHONE LOOKS FOR UPDATES BY ITSELF. A few seconds after Voicley opens, whenever you come back to it, and every half hour, it checks for a newer version and offers Update available with Install or Later - the way your PCs already do. It never interrupts a dictation or a reading."],t.s)
B.aWh=new A.v(1229,B.afP)
B.ac9=s(["STOP MEANS STOP. Stop on a dictation while you listen now holds for the rest of that listen: the microphone stays off and the reading plays straight through until you press Dictate again. Pressing Dictate pauses the reader at once, and it picks up 8 seconds after you stop talking.","ONE SOUND AT A TIME. Playing an item, Read aloud or the reader pauses whatever else was playing, and while something is being read a Now playing line with Pause and Stop sits at the bottom of every screen.",'The Reading list looks like the full item: your paragraphs and text size, the sentence being read lit where it sits, and the words being read kept two lines above the bottom. Its dictation box keeps one size, a finished note says "Sent to your Inbox", only your own swipe changes the item, and picking up after a pause between items starts the next item properly.','An item whose words were all removed shows "Words removed - Undo" to put them back. Recovered words show on the item with Add and Dismiss, and clearing a dictation also clears any words waiting to be recovered from it.',"Phone updates without the cable: Settings, Check for updates, Install works again after Voicley has been closed and reopened (from the update after this one).",'"Split into N items" sits on the same line as "New dictation", the dictation box grows to 6 lines at most, and the Inbox and Reading list rows in the side panel stand the same height.','A phone call during dictation no longer shows you as offline: no offline notice during the call and no "While you were offline" card after it.'],t.s)
B.b28=new A.v(1228,B.ac9)
B.aqm=s(["STOP WHERE YOU NEED IT. While something is reading, the Reading list screen has a Stop beside back, play-pause and forward; in the side panel the Reading list button is Play, or Stop while it plays; and a folder's play button turns into Stop while that folder plays, with a little more room beside its count.",'The "Speak now" and "Player resumes in" bubble shows again on the Reading list screen.','The reading screen is called Reading list, its top bar no longer has the pencil and settings buttons, and the dictation box sits as close under "Your words" as the item sits under "Listening".'],t.s)
B.aYv=new A.v(1227,B.aqm)
B.aeh=s(["A FOLDER PLAYING FROM THE SIDE PANEL CAN BE STOPPED. Its row shows a pause button where the pulsing dot was: tap it to pause, tap it again to carry on.","The counts beside Inbox, Reading list and Favorites in the side panel now all look the same."],t.s)
B.aYN=new A.v(1226,B.aeh)
B.al0=s(["SORTED ITEMS JOIN YOUR READING LIST. Anything moved into a folder with its headphones on - by Auto Sort, drag and drop or the folder chips - now goes on your reading list, as the Move menu always did. Items sorted before this stay off until you tap that folder's headphones off and on.","The reading list's number counts only what will play - items with no words or audio yet are not counted.","The headphones look lighter when they are off, so on and off are easy to tell apart.","The button on the Inbox and on a folder now says Sort.","FAVORITES. A star sits left of the headphones on every item, in the full item and on Now Playing: one tap stars it, a second tap takes the star off (with Undo). The side panel has a Favorites row with a count and Play, the Favorites list shows every starred item newest first, and Search has a Favorites chip.",'By voice while the reader is going: "favorite this" or "star this" stars the item being read, "unfavorite this" takes the star off, and "play my favorites" plays your starred items.'],t.s)
B.b2z=new A.v(1225,B.al0)
B.ajH=s(["NOTHING OPENS UNDER A SIDE-PANEL ROW ANY MORE. Pressing Play used to grow a block under Reading list with the title, a stop button and a clock. Now the same button turns into Pause where it stands, and the row itself turns the listening colour while it reads. The whole player is still on Now Playing, which that row opens.","THE DICTATE BUTTON IS A BUTTON. On the Inbox row it sits at the right end with its own edge, and it shows what dictation is doing: red while your words are landing, a pause mark in commands-only, amber while recording with nowhere to send it. The Inbox row itself turns red while words land, so you can see it across the room.","THE FULL ITEM SHOWS ITS ROUNDED BOTTOM CORNERS - the box now stops above the phone's gesture bar instead of running under it."],t.s)
B.aYx=new A.v(1224,B.ajH)
B.atm=s(["TAP THE TITLE BAR TO OPEN THE ITEM. On Now Playing, a tap on the item being read opens it full screen and pauses the reading; the back button brings you straight back and the reading carries on, with your changes.","Every item is read with its newest words - edits made since your reading list started are read too.",'NO MORE FALSE "OFFLINE" WHILE YOU LISTEN. One sound at the end of a pause no longer makes the app think it has stopped hearing you.',"When the app re-checks audio for words it missed, it leaves out the reader's own voice, and the main dictation box can get missed words back too (it always asks first).","Now Playing is calmer: the dictation box always holds 5 lines, the page is grey with the item as a card, back / play / forward sit beside Move, and when nothing is playing it says how many items are on your reading list.","In the left panel, the Inbox and Reading list counts sit right after their names, with Dictate and Play at the right end of those rows, and the Playlists row is gone."],t.s)
B.b_z=new A.v(1223,B.atm)
B.ahb=s(['ONE DICTATE BUTTON AND ONE COUNTDOWN ON NOW PLAYING. The side panel no longer repeats its own Dictate button or "Speak now" countdown while you are on Now Playing - the box at the top has both.',"A DICTATE ICON BESIDE INBOX. In the left panel, the line under Inbox is gone: a small Dictate icon sits to the right of the word and shows what dictation is doing.","The Inbox row turns red while your words are landing.","A full-size item shows all four rounded corners - it stops above the phone's gesture bar."],t.s)
B.aV0=new A.v(1222,B.ahb)
B.afp=s(["READING OPENS ON NOW PLAYING. The Reading list button takes you straight to Now Playing, with a link through to Folders & list. The item there is built like a full-size item, with the player inside it.","Each word lights up as the voice reads it.",'While the reader is talking, the Dictate button says "Listening in pauses" in teal - so you can see it is waiting for you, not recording.',"AUTO SORT HAS DESTINATIONS. A destination is a folder with a rule, and a blue dot marks each one. Make a folder a destination, or stop sorting into it, from the folder itself.","A Destinations page, with its own door from Auto Sort.","Summary comes first on the Auto Sort screen.","A suggestion you accept in the review list now stays where it is.","No trail above a folder title."],t.s)
B.aS9=new A.v(1221,B.afp)
B.aeu=s(["NOW PLAYING IN TWO HALVES. Your Inbox box sits on top - type, fix a word or press its Dictate button - and the reader plays underneath. Both halves stay put: nothing appears or disappears. Dictating there keeps the reader pausing for you between sentences, and a tap on the reader never stops your dictation."],t.s)
B.aY9=new A.v(1220,B.aeu)
B.aj2=s(["DICTATION WORKS IN A BROWSER. voicley.com/app now dictates with the same engine your phone uses, and keeps the recording in the browser until it is safe. The page updates itself when a newer one is put up, and Settings names the copy you are running.","CLEARING MEANS CLEARING. Clear Dictation used to hand your words back a second later, when the part you were still speaking finished. It no longer does, on any engine. An emptied box also drops back to its normal size instead of standing full height with nothing in it.","THE FULL ITEM IS A LIST ROW AT FULL SIZE. Same fill, same grey title bar, same spacing - just a screen tall. The words scroll inside it, and the date, Move, the trash and the player stay in view at the box's foot. Tabs read straight onto the page, with no grey box inside a box.",'LISTENING FIXES. While something is read aloud, the app no longer says it has gone offline when it hears the reading voice itself. Saying "new line" now breaks the line where your cursor is, exactly as dictated words land there.',"DARK MODE READS PROPERLY. The dark greys had never once been measured; the faint ones were see-through, so their real contrast changed with whatever sat behind them. Every one is now a solid colour, chosen against the lightest dark surface, so it holds on a card, a sheet and the black page alike."],t.s)
B.aYi=new A.v(1219,B.aj2)
B.abo=s(["DICTATE FROM NOW PLAYING. The Dictate button sits on top of the reader, so you can talk while something plays - and each thought you dictate remembers what was playing.","THE FULL ITEM'S BOTTOM LINE READS LIKE A LIST ROW: the date, the number and Move, with Dictate and Read aloud at the right. The icons on the right stand in one column, and the full-size button is just its icon - tap to expand, tap again to shrink.","THIS PC'S LIST MATCHES THE FOLD'S. A Move button on each row, and only Search and Auto sort on top.","DICTATION FIXES. The first Dictate tap no longer crashes a phone that has not allowed the microphone yet. Talking is heard as a voice, not a noise level, so road noise no longer counts as speech. A list row under the full item never takes the item's words.","SMALLER THINGS. A leftover microphone service is switched off when Voicley starts or comes back to the screen. The natural reading voices no longer take over the phone's audio, and a player is never closed while it is still loading. A kept OneNote page row fixes its own frozen title."],t.s)
B.aY0=new A.v(1218,B.abo)
B.amP=s(["EACH ITEM HAS A HEADER. The title now sits in a grey bar across the top of every item, with the headphones at its right - in the list and on the full item alike. Tap the bar to open the item; rename it on the full item. There is more room between items, too.","A TIDIER LIST. The expand arrow is gone from every row - the bar opens the item. On a phone the bottom line is just the date, Move and the trash. When an item is too long to show, an arrow after its last word opens it.","THE FULL ITEM READS TOP TO BOTTOM. Title, then My words, Tidy up and Rewrite, then the words, then the date, then Move, Dictate and Read aloud. Back and the menu stay at the top; the trash sits at the end of the tabs. An item with no recording has no player line.","TIDY UP AND REWRITE WORK THE SAME WAY. Each opens on the finished text, plain. Show edits marks every change - for Tidy up with a tick and a cross on each one. Opening Rewrite writes the rewrite for you the first time; the tab then says Tidied up or Re-Written.",'SMALLER THINGS. The pencil, the Read aloud words and the folder trail are gone from the top bar; "Create dictation" is now "New dictation"; the item icons are one size; and the Now Playing card no longer runs off the edge of the sidebar.'],t.s)
B.aVJ=new A.v(1213,B.amP)
B.ata=s(["A SIMPLER PHONE LIST. On a phone the Inbox bar keeps only Search and Auto sort, and a folder bar keeps Auto sort and the menu. The plus and play buttons stay on a wide window.",'EVERY ITEM SAYS WHERE IT COULD GO. The top row of an item on a phone carries AI sort - with a folder already suggested it reads "AI sort to Cash Flow" and one tap files it, with Undo; with no suggestion yet it opens Auto sort for the list you are in.',"MOVE OR LINK, RIGHT ON THE ITEM. Above the date line a Move/Link button moves or links the item, and the edits count sits at its right. The date line itself holds the date and the trash."],t.s)
B.b22=new A.v(1212,B.ata)
B.aj1=s(["ITEM TITLES IN THE LIST ARE BOLDER, and the darker band from the last update is gone again - the title is what separates one item from the next.","DICTATE HAS MOVED OFF THE LIST ROWS. A row no longer offers a Dictate button; the full-size item has it. While an item IS the one you are dictating into, its red light and clock still show on the row, so you can always see where your words are landing."],t.s)
B.b2b=new A.v(1211,B.aj1)
B.ak4=s(["THE LIST IS EASIER TO READ. Every saved item wears a darker band across its top, so one item stops where the next begins. The band is the same neutral grey on all of them - it tells you where an item ends, not what kind it is.","FEWER BUTTONS ON A ROW. The play triangle is gone from the list - headphones still read an item aloud, Play all still plays, and the full item still has its player. The fold arrow is gone from an open item, and so is the one that folded them all at once.","THE FOOTER OF A BOX NO LONGER RUNS OFF THE EDGE. A long folder suggestion or a long edit count now shrinks to fit instead of overflowing the row."],t.s)
B.b2j=new A.v(1210,B.ak4)
B.aqd=s(['A FULL ITEM NOW HAS THREE TABS: MY WORDS, TIDY UP, REWRITE. My words is what you said, always kept and always editable. Tidy up keeps your words and only fixes them - fillers and repeats cut, punctuation fixed, titles and paragraphs added. Rewrite reads the whole note, works out what you meant, and writes it properly in your voice. Each tab shows its own word count, the one your note is actually using wears a green mark, and a version made before you edited your words says "old words" until you ask for it again.','NOTHING IS SAVED UNTIL YOU SAY SO, AND YOU CAN ALWAYS GO BACK. The button says exactly what it will do - "Save 6 edits", "Save the rewrite" - and until you press it the line reads "nothing saved yet". Your own words are kept on the item, so "Revert to my words" works days later, not just while you are looking at it. A saved item says what was done and when: "AI tidied this today at 7:52 PM".','A REWRITE TELLS YOU WHAT IT LEFT OUT. Because a rewrite says things in its own words, a second pass reads your words against it and lists anything a reader of the rewrite would never learn - or says "nothing of yours was left out". One that drops too much is never shown at all.',"LONG DICTATIONS READ AS PARAGRAPHS, AND HEADINGS STAY HEADINGS. A long note gets a paragraph roughly every hundred words, and a title the AI adds is a real title: larger and underlined in the app, a proper heading in OneNote. A recording of a thousand words or more - the microphone left running - is left alone unless you ask for it.",'THE FULL ITEM IS TIDIER. The recording bar sits at the foot of the page with the word count beside its time, one AI button in the header row changes with what the item needs, and the empty "Related - nothing linked" line at the bottom is gone.'],t.s)
B.aX5=new A.v(1209,B.aqd)
B.aex=s(["OFFLINE RECORDINGS WITH NO WORDS STOP WAITING ON YOUR OTHER DEVICES. When a recording made offline turned out to be silent, your other phones and PCs kept listing it as an offline dictation still waiting. They now know it had no words and say so, and the recording and its audio stay in your library.","AUTO SORT IS EASIER TO FIND AND DOES MORE. A button with words on it sits on the Inbox and every folder, the full-size view shows its suggestion too, and Not clear can move the recordings with nothing dictated in them to the Trash in one go, with one Undo.","AI EDITS IN THE FULL-SIZE VIEW. Turn on the AI edits switch above the words to see suggested edits right in the text. It asks about the cuts first, fillers and repeated phrases, then the fixes to the words that stay. Answering an edit never moves the page, and the switch stays on as you go from item to item.","LONG DICTATIONS HAVE A FULL SIZE VIEW, AND DICTATE IS BACK ON THE FULL ITEM PAGE. Once your words outgrow the box, a Full size button shows every one of them, and the page holds still while you read as new words land. On a phone the Dictate button floats in the corner, so it is in reach at the end of a long item."],t.s)
B.b43=new A.v(1208,B.aex)
B.amK=s(["DICTATION KEEPS HEARING YOU WHEN THE SCREEN GOES OFF. If you tapped Dictate and the screen went dark a moment later, Android could quietly switch the microphone off - Voicley kept recording silence and said you were offline. The part that keeps the microphone on now starts the instant you tap Dictate, so turning the screen off straight away is safe.",'IF ANDROID EVER DOES TAKE THE MICROPHONE, YOU HEAR ABOUT IT. Within a couple of seconds your phone sounds and vibrates with "Voicley can\'t hear you", and tapping it brings Voicley back, which gives the microphone back. It no longer tells you that you are offline when the problem is the microphone, and it stays quiet during phone calls.',"SWIPING VOICLEY AWAY NO LONGER CUTS A DICTATION SHORT. Closing it from Recent Apps while you dictate keeps it going, with its notification still showing so you can tap back in and stop it there."],t.s)
B.b_o=new A.v(1206,B.amK)
B.arx=s(["DICTATION STARTS HEARING YOU STRAIGHT AWAY AGAIN. The last update could take a long time to pick up your first words, and sometimes did not pick them up at all. That is fixed: it hears you within a few seconds of pressing Dictate, the way it did before."],t.s)
B.aZw=new A.v(1205,B.arx)
B.ar8=s(["THE DICTATE BUTTON IS EASIER TO FIND. It now wears the app blue - the outline, the microphone and the word itself - instead of the faint grey it had, which had quietly become the palest thing on the screen. While it is actually recording it still turns red, exactly as before.","AND THE FAINT GREY TEXT IS DARKER EVERYWHERE, not just on the first screen. Quiet labels and captions all over the app were too light to read comfortably in daylight mode; they are now a shade you can actually read, and the ordinary body text darkened a little too so the two still look different from each other.",'The green "done" and amber "careful" colours were the worst of all as words - pale enough to be nearly invisible on a white screen, including on the setup screen where the green is a sentence you are meant to read. Both are deeper now, same colours, readable.',"WORDS YOU SPOKE INTO A SILENT PATCH ARE NOT GONE ANY MORE. If the app stops hearing you part-way through a dictation, it now goes back to the recording afterwards, works out what you said in that stretch, and offers those words to the note they belong to.","IT ASKS YOU FIRST, EVERY TIME. The item shows a quiet line saying words are waiting; open it and you see them sitting exactly where they would go, and nothing is written into your note until you tap Keep.","If you have edited that part of the note since, it will not guess where they belong - it offers to put them at the end, or to copy the words so you can place them yourself.","DARK MODE IS NOW A SETTING. Voicley has always had a dark look, but it only ever wore whatever your phone was wearing - so a phone in light mode at ten at night gave you a white page. Settings, Screen, Appearance: follow the phone, or hold it in Light or Dark whatever the phone does.","VOICLEY NO LONGER RECORDS WHILE YOU ARE ON A PHONE CALL. It used to take the microphone back part way through a call and write the whole conversation into your note, both sides. It now waits until you hang up, tells you it is paused, and carries on in the same note afterwards. This does not reach calls it already recorded.","THE MICROPHONE HOLDS ON THROUGH MORE INTERRUPTIONS. An alarm or another app taking the sound can no longer quietly freeze the recording, and starting a dictation no longer stops your music - it ducks instead.","THE MIC NOW WAITS 15 MINUTES OF QUIET before switching itself off, up from 5 - long gaps between thoughts on a drive were switching it off mid-journey. It also counts speech it can hear, so a wobbly connection no longer looks like silence while you are still talking.","AND IT STOPS BLAMING YOUR SIGNAL for things that are not your signal. If something takes the microphone for a few seconds, the amber bar now says the microphone was interrupted instead of saying you have a bad connection, and it no longer counts against your connection history.","NEW: KEEP RUNNING IN THE BACKGROUND, in Settings under Mic auto-off. Android can put apps to sleep and stop a recording; this shows whether it is allowed to and gives you the one tap that fixes it.","VOICLEY NOW OFFERS TO FILE AN ITEM AND TO TIDY ITS WORDS, and it never does either on its own. A new dictation can arrive wearing two small buttons: one names the folder it belongs in, the other says how many small fixes were found in the words.","ONE TAP FILES IT. The folder button carries the folder Auto Sort would have chosen, so tapping it is the same as saying yes on the Sort screen: the item moves, and you get Undo. The x beside it keeps the item in the Inbox for good, and holding the button down tells you why that folder was picked.","THE OTHER BUTTON OPENS THE WHOLE ITEM WITH THE SUGGESTED FIXES MARKED INSIDE IT. Words that would go are struck through in red, words that would come in are green, and a paragraph break shows where it would fall. Each one is Skip or Accept on its own, nothing changes in your note until you press Done, and Undo puts it all back.","What it looks for: a phrase said twice, filler words, punctuation and capitals, a word it plainly misheard, and a missing paragraph break. It does not reword your sentences unless you switch that on in Settings, where you can also turn the whole thing off."],t.s)
B.aS4=new A.v(1204,B.ar8)
B.ake=s(["EVERYTHING IS EASIER TO READ IN DAYLIGHT MODE. A new phone starts in light mode, and in light mode a lot of Voicley was too faint to read properly - the grey hint in the dictate box, the outline around it, the steps on an empty Inbox. They are all darker now, measured against the published readability standard rather than judged by eye. Dark mode is unchanged.",'The first screen says what to do more clearly, and one of its instructions was wrong: it told you to say "new note" between thoughts, which quietly turned every new thought into a Note even when you were working in Tasks or Meetings. It now says "new dictation", which starts the next thought and leaves your type alone.','The empty Inbox no longer points the wrong way. It used to say "Tap Dictate below" when the Dictate button is above it.',"On a narrow phone the words in the dictate box were being cut in half. They now fit on one line, and shrink to fit rather than spill if they ever cannot."],t.s)
B.aVY=new A.v(1203,B.ake)
B.ahm=s(["EVERY ITEM NOW HAS A DOOR TO ITS FULL PAGE. Open an item and a small arrows button sits at its top right, beside the fold arrow: one tap opens that item full size. Before this only long items had a visible way in, and everything else needed a double-tap nobody could guess.",'WHILE IT READS TO YOU, THE PROMPT NO LONGER LIES. The tone that says "speak now" only sounds when the microphone is really listening. When it is not, you get silence instead of an invitation.',"IT NOTICES WHEN IT STOPS HEARING YOU. If you are talking in the pauses and no words come back for about twenty seconds, Voicley says so on screen and re-opens the connection by itself. It used to keep prompting into nothing - one stretch ran six minutes.","Pressing play, resuming or stopping the reading now all clear a dictation you stopped earlier, so the pauses start listening again without you having to think about it.",'The "Link an item" button is gone from every screen. Items you have already linked, and Unlink, are unchanged.'],t.s)
B.b0A=new A.v(1202,B.ahm)
B.asy=s(["THE DICTATE BUTTON NOW TELLS YOU WHEN THE MICROPHONE IS OFF. If you turned Voicley down for the microphone twice, your phone stops asking and Dictate quietly stopped working with nothing on screen to explain it. Now it says what is wrong and gives you a button straight to Settings to turn the microphone back on.","You can still type while the microphone is off - the box takes typed items either way."],t.s)
B.aUK=new A.v(1201,B.asy)
B.agN=s(["DELETED PROJECTS STAY DELETED. A project you delete no longer comes back the next time the app starts.","Until every one of your devices has this version, don't delete older projects: a device still on an older version can put them back."],t.s)
B.aVw=new A.v(1200,B.agN)
B.aon=s(["ONE INBOX PER ACCOUNT. A new phone or PC, a reinstall, or a library that had to start over used to make its own Inbox project before sync brought yours down, so extra Inboxes piled up and different devices could file into different ones. Now an Inbox a device made that early folds into your account's Inbox, with its items, once the device has synced."],t.s)
B.aWY=new A.v(1199,B.aon)
B.arW=s(["A TITLE YOU TYPED STAYS YOURS. Dictating more words into a note no longer swaps the title you typed for an AI title, on any of your devices. A title you type while an AI title is being made wins, and so does one you typed on another device.","EDITING WORDS IN AUTO SORT IS SAFE. Changing an item's words in the Sort window no longer resets its title to the first line, keeps the breaks between your paragraphs everywhere except the part you change, and saves the words even if the row closes while you are still typing. An item you empty there stays empty instead of being transcribed again, Home shows the new words right away, and the title refreshes afterwards unless you typed it.","A SOFT TICK TELLS YOU IT'S YOUR TURN. When Listen while dictating reads to you, a short rising tick now plays at the start of every pause, between sentences and between items, so you can hear when to talk without looking at the phone.","ITEMS NO LONGER VANISH INTO A DELETED PROJECT. When a project was deleted on another device, this device kept saving new items into it, and Auto Sort left them out. Now, once this device has its full project list, new items go to your Inbox instead, and Auto Sort shows every item, even one whose project is gone."],t.s)
B.aZI=new A.v(1198,B.arW)
B.agi=s(['OFFLINE WORDS CONTINUE THE DICTATION YOU WERE IN. When the signal comes back while you are still dictating, what you said offline now finishes the dictation you were in, instead of coming back as separate items. Say "new dictation" while offline and a new one starts there, just as it would online. New words wait a moment until the offline part is in, so everything reads in the order you said it. If anything goes wrong, the offline part comes back as separate items, as before.','OFFLINE ITEMS ARE NUMBERED. For a day after an outage, each item it made or finished says "Processed 2 of 3 from offline session" in a green strip that fades, and folder lists show "2 of 3 \xb7 offline". Number 1 is where you left off.','YOU CAN CLOSE THE OFFLINE MESSAGES. The banner that shows offline dictation transcribing has an \u2715, and it closes by itself after 10 minutes; the short line that says it is done still shows. The "While you were offline" card has an \u2715 whenever nothing on it needs your tap, and it also closes once you open the recap, or an hour after everything is caught up - on all your devices.'],t.s)
B.aXQ=new A.v(1197,B.agi)
B.al9=s(['OFFLINE SHOWS AS SOON AS THE CONNECTION IS GONE. In 1194 and 1195 the dictation box turned amber only after you had talked for two seconds with no words coming back, so an outage while you were quiet - airplane mode, no signal - could show nothing at all. Now, two seconds after the connection drops, the box turns amber and says "Recording/Dictating - Offline", whether you are talking or not. A connection that reconnects and dies straight away does not restart those two seconds.'],t.s)
B.aVi=new A.v(1196,B.al9)
B.agf=s(["OFFLINE DICTATIONS TRANSCRIBE AS SOON AS YOU ARE BACK ONLINE. In 1194 a dictation saved while you were offline could wait until you stopped recording before it transcribed. Now it starts shortly after the connection comes back, even while you keep dictating."],t.s)
B.b3R=new A.v(1195,B.agf)
B.ae9=s(['OFFLINE DICTATIONS COUNT THEMSELVES. A dictation saved while you were offline now reads "4 of 10 Offline dictation processing" while it transcribes and "4 of 10 Offline dictation waiting" until it can - numbered in the order you said them, out of everything that offline stretch saved. On Home and in folder lists.',"WORDS SAID INTO A CLOGGED CONNECTION ARE KEPT. When a weak signal clogged the live connection, the app noticed only after about 8 seconds - and the words you said in those seconds never became text. Everything the connection has not answered is now held and replayed first when it reconnects, so those words land in order; a longer outage makes them the start of your first offline dictation.",'OFFLINE SHOWS WITHIN TWO SECONDS. When you have talked for two seconds and no words have come back, the dictation box turns amber and says so - "Recording/Dictating - Offline", and underneath, "Offline \u2014 your words are safe. Keep dictating." It turns back once words have flowed again for two seconds. A connection that keeps reconnecting can no longer hold it off, a dropout under six seconds drops its words back into your note, and audio sent to a connection that died on the spot is kept too.','THE DICTATE BUTTON SAYS WHAT IT IS DOING. While your words are landing it reads "Recording - Dictating"; while they are being saved to transcribe later, "Recording/Dictating - Offline".','SAY THE PUNCTUATION. End a sentence with "question mark" and you get ?, or "exclamation mark" or "exclamation point" and you get ! - not the words. If the transcriber already added the mark, you get one, not two.',"THE FULL-SIZE ITEM IS TIDIER. The date is back under the title with the length, the word count and the item number on one line. The player and the reading-list headphones sit above the words, so a long item no longer hides play at the bottom. The old 'Dictate 2' line is gone, and the OneNote note waits behind a small \u24d8.","NO MORE TITLE THAT JUST REPEATS THE FIRST LINE. When an item never got a real title, the full view says Add a title, with a Suggest one button that writes one for you. Nothing in your lists changes until you pick one.","RELATED ITEMS ON EVERY ITEM. The Related section Claude items had is now on every item's full view: Link an item, tap to jump, minus to unlink.","TWO COLORS FOR TWO THINGS. Everything you listen with is now teal: play buttons, headphones, Play all, Read aloud and what is playing now. Dictate now, folders and the rest keep the blue, and red still means the mic is on."],t.s)
B.b0v=new A.v(1194,B.ae9)
B.abr=s(["OFFLINE COMMANDS NOW ACT WHEN THE CONNECTION COMES BACK. What you said offline is replayed the way it would have worked live: new note makes a note, new task a task, new meeting ... end meeting a meeting, sort later a To Sort item, and new note in a folder files it there - each dated when you said it, each with its own recording.","NOTHING SENDS OR PLAYS BY ITSELF. A message for Claude said offline waits for your tap under Needs a tap, and play the listening folder said offline is listed as skipped instead of starting late. Words you cleared or paused are kept, so Undo can bring them back.","WHILE YOU WERE OFFLINE. After an outage, a card at the top of Home says how long you were offline and what happened, and opens the full recap: Send now or Skip for waiting messages, Undo for replayed commands, Play or Try again for clips nobody could hear. Past recaps live in Settings - While you were offline, and your answers sync to your other devices.","THE DICTATION BOX STAYS YOURS. Back online, the transcribing progress shows above the list instead of inside the box, and a dropout long enough to turn the box amber comes back as its own item rather than pouring into the note you are dictating.",'SAYING "AND DICTATION" NO LONGER TURNS THE MIC OFF. When a sentence ended on the words "and dictation" or "in dictation" - talking about listening and dictation, say - Voicley could hear it as "end dictation", save your note and stop listening mid-thought. Those misheard forms now count only when said on their own, as a sentence by themselves. "End dictation" and "stop dictation" work exactly as before.','THE SAME CARE FOR "NEW DICTATION". A sentence that ends like "how do I do dictation" no longer starts a new note by accident. Said on its own, "do dictation" still does.',"CLEAN DOTS IN TITLES. Meeting and segment titles showed a stray accented A in front of the middle dot. New titles are clean, and the existing ones have been repaired."],t.s)
B.aSw=new A.v(1193,B.abr)
B.ali=s(["OFFLINE MODE: WHEN THE CONNECTION DROPS, VOICLEY SAYS SO AND KEEPS RECORDING. If the network goes while you are dictating - a drive, a dead zone - the Dictate button turns amber and reads Recording - offline, the box shows level bars and a timer, and a line tells you everything is being saved. Nothing turns red, because red means your words are landing, and offline they are not yet.","YOUR WORDS ARE SAVED IN PIECES, SO NOTHING IS LOST. While offline, what you say is cut at natural pauses into saved recordings, about one a minute, each kept on the device the moment it is cut. The mic also no longer switches itself off after five quiet minutes while you are talking offline.","BACK ONLINE, IT CATCHES UP BY ITSELF. When the connection returns, Voicley transcribes those recordings one at a time, in the order you said them, and shows Back online - transcribing 4 of 14 with a progress bar. While you keep dictating it gives the live connection priority, and once you stop it runs at full speed. Voice commands spoken while offline come back as plain words for now.","AUTO SORT READS BEFORE IT FILES. It reads each note's whole text, judges folders by what is actually in them, and files a note only when it plainly belongs. The rest wait for you in named groups - Not clear, Two topics, Has unrelated asides, and Not a dictation for calls, conversations and background noise. Meetings get their own group, and a guess is shown to you, never filed.","YOUR CORRECTIONS TEACH IT. When you move a note the sorter got wrong, that correction becomes a lesson: every sort is shown the corrections most like the note in front of it, so the same mistake stops coming back.","FOLDER RULES STAY YOURS. Each folder keeps your own rule and, separately, the app's reading of what is in it - the AI can no longer rewrite your rule or cut it short. You can also tell Auto Sort to ignore a whole folder and everything under it.","REVIEWING IS QUICKER. The review list shows each note's words and turns every held note into a one-tap decision, with a trash icon on every row. Each folder shows when it was last recalculated, the Move picker says what is inside each folder, and a dropped connection mid-sort is retried instead of written off.","UNDO ON EVERY MOVE, AND A DELETE ICON IN THE FULL-SIZE VIEW. Moving items or whole folders now offers Undo, and an item opened full size has a visible delete button.","A SYNC FIX FOR EMPTY SCREENS. Sync could treat rows that had only been rewritten as deleted, which left some screens empty for a while. Fixed.","DRAGGING PAST A NOTE NO LONGER THROWS AWAY WHAT YOU ARE TYPING. A drag that passed over an open note used to close its editor and lose the words you had not saved yet."],t.s)
B.aY7=new A.v(1192,B.ali)
B.amo=s(["SORT: THE APP PROPOSES WHERE THINGS GO, AND NOTHING MOVES UNTIL YOU SAY SO. Open Sort and it reads your folders, works out what each one is actually FOR from the items already in it, and then tells you where your unfiled items could go. Every proposal is a suggestion with a confidence on it - you approve, you edit, or you ignore. It never files anything behind your back.","THE RULE FOR EACH FOLDER IS YOURS TO EDIT. The AI writes a first draft of what belongs in a folder, and you can rewrite it anywhere it appears. A folder is judged by what it CONTAINS, not by a sentence describing it, so a rule that stops matching reality can be refreshed - either since the last time you looked, or from scratch with fresh eyes.","REVIEW BEFORE ANYTHING HAPPENS. The Review screen triages your Inbox by where the rules WOULD send each item, so you see the whole picture at once instead of one decision at a time. You can open an item, read its words, edit them, or delete it without leaving the sort window.","AND YOU CAN EDIT AN ITEM'S WORDS RIGHT THERE. Expand an item inside the sort window and the text is live - tap in, fix it, and it saves when you tap away. Changing the words also throws away whatever the app had decided about that item, so the next pass reads what you actually wrote instead of arguing from the old version.","THE ROW OF FOLDERS AT THE TOP CAN BE SCROLLED. It had more folders than fit and no scrollbar, and a mouse could not drag it - so the ones off the right edge were unreachable unless you knew to hold Shift and use the wheel. It now has a proper scrollbar you can grab, and you can drag the row with the mouse.","AND A RECALCULATE BUTTON, FOR WHEN THE SORTING ITSELF CHANGES. Open Sort and tap Recalculate: tick any folders you want done again and the app throws away what it had decided about them and works it out fresh from the words. It tells you how big the job is before you press anything. Nothing moves - the proposals just get made again. And from now on every decision the app stores remembers WHICH version of the sorting made it, so when the sorting is improved the old answers retire themselves instead of sitting there looking current.","\u201cPAUSE DICTATION\u201d AND \u201cRESUME DICTATION\u201d. Say \u201cpause dictation\u201d and the mic stops taking your words down while it keeps listening for commands - your note stays open exactly where it is. Say \u201cresume dictation\u201d and it picks that same note up again. Resume used to close your note and start a new one, which is not what the app told you it did; now it does what it says.","THE SETTINGS TOGGLES LINE UP. Every switch in Settings sat stranded in the middle of its row while every other control was over on the right. They now end where the arrows end, on Windows and on the phone, everywhere they appear.","YOUR EDITS STOP GETTING UNDONE. An edit could be overwritten by a stale echo of the app's own earlier save, and the reader could read you the version it started with instead of the words you had just changed. Both are fixed, and an editor may now only overwrite the version it actually last saw.","THE READING VOICE STOPS DISAGREEING WITH ITSELF. Your picked voice was being kept in two places that could drift apart, so the voice you chose was not always the voice you got. One home now, and a voice that fails to load says what the server actually said instead of just \u201cfailed\u201d.","WHEN THE APP FREEZES, IT NOW SAYS WHAT IT WAS WAITING ON. A frozen screen used to be recorded as \u201ca database read\u201d and nothing more - on one phone session that was 205 freezes, none of which could be chased. Each one now names the exact query that held it up. This does not stop a freeze; it is what lets the next one actually be fixed."],t.s)
B.aVh=new A.v(1191,B.amo)
B.agC=s(['PICK SEVERAL ONENOTE PAGES AT ONCE. Settings -> OneNote integration -> "Link a page to the Reading list" closed the moment you tapped a page, so linking eight pages meant walking the whole flow eight times. Now a tap TICKS the page and a button at the bottom says how many you have chosen. Search, flip between Recent and Browse, drill into a different section - your picks come with you, and the same page tapped in two places is still one page. It asks where they should live ONCE, not once per page.','AND IT SHOWS YOU WHAT IS ALREADY LINKED. Any page that already has a folder wears a "Linked" tag right there in the list, so you see it before you tap instead of finding out afterwards. Tapping it anyway is still fine and still does what it always did - refreshes the folder you already have rather than building you a second one.',"IF ONE PAGE FAILS, THE REST STILL GO. It links them one at a time and shows how far it has got, so a page it cannot reach no longer costs you the other seven. At the end it names exactly which pages landed, which were already there and got refreshed, and which failed - no silent half-success. Nothing is written to OneNote either way.","CHOOSING A PAGE TO DICTATE INTO IS UNCHANGED - still one page, on purpose. A recording streams into a single page, so that picker stays exactly as it was; only choosing pages to LISTEN to is a many-at-once question.","ALSO IN THIS BUILD: THE LIST KEEPS UP WHILE YOU TYPE. Editing an item was making the library re-count everything you own on every keystroke - on your desktop that count reached 15 seconds and was over 90% of the work in a single refresh. It now checks far less often. This one is not the OneNote work; it rides this build because both landed the same morning."],t.s)
B.b_9=new A.v(1190,B.agC)
B.amm=s(['READING ALOUD NO LONGER STOPS ITSELF. With "Keep listening while I dictate" on, playback opens the microphone for you so you can just talk - and opening it was silencing the reading. The phone handed the microphone the audio, the reader treated that exactly like a phone call interrupting it, and stopped. On your Fold this happened 81 milliseconds into the first sentence and the reading sat silent for 83 seconds. The reader now knows the difference between a microphone IT opened and a real interruption: a phone call still stops the reading, its own microphone does not.',"AND YOUR STOP MEANS STOP. When you ended a dictation that playback had started, the next pause between sentences opened it straight back up - you stopped it, it restarted, over and over. It now stays stopped for the rest of that reading. Press play again, or tap Dictate whenever you want it back.","SILENCE NO LONGER COUNTED AS YOU TALKING. The reading pauses when it hears you and picks up eight seconds after your last word. A recognition that came back with NO words in it was being counted as you speaking, so the wait kept getting pushed further out while you sat there waiting for it to continue. Empty is now empty.","AND LISTENING NO LONGER LEAVES EMPTY ITEMS BEHIND. Because the microphone re-opened at every pause, each pause where you said nothing left a blank entry in your library - three of them in one two-minute reading. A listening session that heard only silence now leaves nothing at all. It checks the actual recording first, so if you DID speak and it simply missed you, the entry stays put.",'PLUS: CLEARING A NOTE NOW KEEPS A COPY. Saying "clear dictation" wiped an open note of 44,000 characters, offering an Undo that lived for seconds on a screen you weren\'t looking at. Every clear now writes a copy that survives - so the answer to "where did that go" is somewhere to look, not a shrug.',"Under the hood: every pause the reader takes now records WHY it paused. That one missing line is what made this take an evening to find."],t.s)
B.aXF=new A.v(1189,B.amm)
B.apb=s(["YOUR EDITS TO ITEMS ARE BEING SAVED AGAIN - THIS IS THE BIG ONE. Since the transcript storage change a few builds back, editing an item's words did nothing: the item's title and timestamp updated, so it looked like something happened, but the words themselves were never written anywhere. The item simply kept its old text. The same applied to speaking INTO an existing item - those words were discarded too. New items were never affected, which is why this hid for so long: only edits to things you already had went missing. Every edit you made since then will need making again, but nothing was corrupted or overwritten - the original words stayed put the whole time.","AND A SAVE NOW PROVES ITSELF BEFORE SAYING IT SAVED. The diagnostics said 'saved' whether or not your words reached storage, because the message was written the instant a save was started rather than after it finished. It now reads the item back and reports what actually landed - so if this ever happens again, the log says so on the spot instead of looking healthy while words go missing.","PLUS: THE BOX YOU ARE TYPING IN NO LONGER GETS REBUILT UNDER YOU. Three pieces of chrome - the glow on the item being read aloud, the not-yet-uploaded badge, and the selection ring - each rebuilt the whole row when they appeared or disappeared, which threw away the cursor and anything typed but not yet saved. Playing a folder did this every time it moved to the next item. They now light up without disturbing what you are doing."],t.s)
B.aSQ=new A.v(1188,B.apb)
B.ao9=s(["THE FIRST ERROR OF A LAUNCH NO LONGER VANISHES FROM THE DIAGNOSTICS LOG. An error or warning recorded while the log file was still opening was silently dropped, and the log could show two 'Log file at' lines. It now waits for the file and lands - so when a launch goes wrong in its very first moment, the evidence survives.","AND THESE UPDATE NOTES WILL ACTUALLY APPEAR AGAIN. The release-testing harness on the build machine was quietly consuming the 'you've seen this' mark for each new build before your real app ever launched - which is why updates stopped showing you what changed. The harness now stands aside, so from the next update on, the popup returns. (If you're reading this, it worked.)"],t.s)
B.aVN=new A.v(1187,B.ao9)
B.agk=s(["DICTATING OVER A SELECTION FINALLY REPLACES IT. Your report from the bedroom machine: highlight text, speak over it, and the old text CAME BACK \u2014 and dictation sometimes repeated words already on screen. Four defects, all in the box's editor: selecting text never counted as touching the field, so the next recognizer snapshot rewrote the whole transcript; an edit you made survived exactly one snapshot before being put back; the duplicate words came from the editor diffing two different texts against each other; and a highlighted range was structurally barred from ever being replaced. All four are fixed: your highlight now wins \u2014 speak, and the selected words become your words, even if you'd parked the cursor somewhere else first. Your edits stay yours until you clearly hand the field back.","FOLDER-TO-SUBFOLDER DRAG WORKS AGAIN ON THE DESKTOP. Dragging one folder onto another to nest it inside had a drop zone only a few pixels tall in the middle of the row, so it almost always just reordered instead. That zone is wide enough to hit now \u2014 drop a folder onto the middle of another and it files inside, with the row outline showing where it'll land.","AND YOUR DIAGNOSTICS LOGS STOPPED COUNTING GHOSTS. Test runs on the development machine were writing startup banners into the same synced log files your real apps use, inflating launch counts 5-10x. Tests now write to their own home, so when a log says the app started, the app started."],t.s)
B.aRU=new A.v(1186,B.agk)
B.amv=s(['TELL EACH FOLDER WHAT BELONGS IN IT. Open any folder and, right under its name, there is a place to describe it in a plain sentence - tap it and say or type what goes there: "contact info, quotes, pricing - not meetings." AI Sort now reads those sentences when it decides where an item belongs, and the Sort sheet shows each folder\'s sentence under its name so you can see what every lane means. A folder without a sentence works exactly as before.','EVERY SORT PROPOSAL NOW SAYS WHY. Each suggested move on the review list carries a short reason - "pricing, customer follow-up" - so when a guess is wrong you can see which sentence to sharpen. The AI also sees each item\'s length, whether it has a recording, its type and its date, so a sentence like "recorded conversations longer than a few minutes" is one it can actually act on.',"The Folders screen shows each folder's sentence as a second line, and the folder's ... menu has a \"What belongs here...\" entry as a second way in."],t.s)
B.aT8=new A.v(1185,B.amv)
B.am4=s(['YOUR WINDOWS LOG NOW KEEPS ITS FIRST LINE. The moment the app\'s window is laid out it writes one line saying whether it opened in the wide desktop layout or the narrow phone one - the line that tells us, from your own log, which layout the app was in when the left pane came or went. That line was being thrown away on every launch: it arrived while the log file was still being opened, and the app treated "still opening" as "broken", started opening the file a second time, and forgot the line. A search of every Windows log this machine has ever written found it nowhere. Anything written while the file is opening now waits its turn and lands, and the file is only ever opened once.'],t.s)
B.aSY=new A.v(1184,B.am4)
B.ahB=s(["SETTINGS' RIGHT SIDE IS AT THE RIGHT EDGE NOW - ACTUALLY. You reported this once before and it was called fixed. It was not, and there were two things wrong, stacked. First, Settings was still being drawn in a narrow centred column on Windows: the app was checking which screen was open by reading a value that does not change when Settings is opened the normal way, so the fix never switched on. Second, inside every row the current value was sharing the empty space with the label instead of leaving it all to the label, so the value and its arrow drifted toward the middle - further the shorter the value. Both are fixed, and there is now a test that opens Settings the way you do and measures where the arrows land, so it cannot quietly come back.","AND SETTINGS CAN NO LONGER CRASH IF SYNC NEVER STARTED. Found by that same test: the Account row asked the sync service for your email in a way that would crash the whole screen if sync had failed to start. It now just shows you as signed out, which is the truth in that case."],t.s)
B.aTL=new A.v(1183,B.ahB)
B.abO=s(['AI SORT NOW FINDS YOUR NESTED FOLDERS. Your first live sort looked at 200 items and filed none of them, with no error anywhere - every answer was being thrown away. The model was naming the right folder, but writing the arrow between folder names its own way ("Sweetie > Marketing" instead of "Sweetie \u203a Marketing"), and the app treated the mismatch as no fit. Folders at the top level never hit it, which is why it looked random. The app now reads the folder the model meant, however it writes the arrow; an exact name still wins first.','AND THE CLEAR BUTTON NOW SHOWS ITS PHRASE IN QUOTES: "Clear Dictation". Your ask. The quotes are the point - a plain label looks like a thing you press and nothing else, while a quoted one tells you it is also something you can SAY. Tapping it still empties the box exactly as before; the quotes just add the half you could not see. The words come straight from the command list, so the label can never say something the app will not answer to.'],t.s)
B.aVo=new A.v(1182,B.abO)
B.ar7=s(["DICTATE NOW LIVES INSIDE THE INBOX BOX, AND PLAY ALL INSIDE THE READING LIST BOX. Your question - should those two buttons sit inside their containers? Yes. The button was already tucked under its row wearing the row's colour, so the outline now goes around the pair and each reads as one thing. Play all had also drifted under Playlists, which is not what it plays; it sits with Reading list again and Playlists moves below. Same buttons, same taps, nothing moved on the phone that did not move on the desktop.",'WHILE YOU DICTATE, THE INBOX BOX GOES RED AT THE EDGE. The pill inside it already says "Dictating - Inbox" with the clock; the box\'s outline now says it too, so the place the mic is pointed is named twice. When you play the reading list, the little player lives inside that box the same way.'],t.s)
B.aVT=new A.v(1181,B.ar7)
B.aro=s(["SILENT RECORDINGS ARE NO LONGER TRANSCRIBED OVER AND OVER. You were seeing repeat OpenAI charges. When the app finds a recording with audio but no words, it sends it off to be transcribed - and if that came back with nothing in it, the app forgot it had ever asked. So the next time it looked, the same clip qualified again, and it kept paying for the same silence. In your phone's log, six of those went out and every single one came back empty; not one produced a word. Now an empty answer is remembered as an answer: that clip is done being asked. Your audio is untouched, the item stays in Files, and the Retry button there still transcribes it on demand whenever you want to overrule it."],t.s)
B.b_O=new A.v(1180,B.aro)
B.alJ=s(['THE CLARITY METER STOPS CRYING WOLF. Your report from the Fold: red "unclear" while you were sitting right next to the phone. Your log said all eleven of those were wrong - every one had a strong, clean signal, three with your voice practically on top of the microphone. It was averaging how confidently each word was heard, and one throwaway word ("I", "um", the first word after a pause) scores low even in perfect audio, so a single word outvoted three good ones. It now asks whether MOST of the words came through poorly, which is what the meter always claimed to be telling you.','AND IT JUDGES YOUR ROOM, NOT THE GAPS INSIDE YOUR OWN SPEECH. That same log flipped between "noisy" and clear sixty times in one sitting - seventeen of them with the words heard exactly as well both times, so it was the levels alone, moving less than a decibel. It had been measuring "the room" from the quiet moments between your syllables, which your phone\'s microphone and your laptop\'s report completely differently. It now measures the room while nobody is talking. Replayed on your own recordings that withdraws fifty-five of the sixty complaints, and keeps the five from the one sitting that genuinely was loud.','A WARNING NOW HAS TO EARN ITSELF, AND IT LETS GO. Green appears instantly; red has to hold for a second and a half before it says anything, so a momentary dip cannot repaint the meter. And when you stop talking a red goes back to neutral instead of sitting there - one "unclear" stayed on your screen for twenty-one seconds and one "noisy" for over two minutes, and the only thing that could clear either was speaking again. That was your "took a while to get to green".','THE GREEN "CLEAR" LABEL IS GONE. Your question - what was that for? It looked like a button, it was not one, and it put the word "clear" a few inches from the CLEAR DICTATION button meaning the opposite thing. The green bars already say it. The word still appears when something is actually wrong, because "Noisy" and "Unclear" are both red and want different fixes.'],t.s)
B.aX0=new A.v(1179,B.alJ)
B.asz=s(['"CLEAR DICTATION" IS NOW INSTANT, AND CLEARS ALL OF IT. Both of your reports from the Fold. It was waiting for the finished transcript rather than the live one \u2014 a two-to-five second lag on a weak connection \u2014 so it now fires the moment you finish saying it, like the other spoken commands do. And it was leaving a tail behind: the wipe ran, then the rest of that same chunk of speech landed back in the box you had just emptied. The whole chunk is dropped now, so clearing means clearing.'],t.s)
B.aXI=new A.v(1178,B.asz)
B.acf=s(['SAY "CLEAR DICTATION" TO EMPTY THE BOX. The button was always there; now the phrase works too, and it does exactly what the button does \u2014 wipes the words in the open box, with Undo right beside it if you didn\'t mean it. It only answers to those two words: not "clear the dictation", not "clear note". Every other command in the app is deliberately generous about mishearing you, because missing one costs you a line break. Missing this one costs you a tap; firing it by mistake would cost you what you just said.','AND THE BUTTON NOW SAYS WHAT IT CLEARS. Your question \u2014 what does "clear" even mean here? Clear the box, the item, the screen? It clears the dictation, so that is what it says now. It doubles as the only place in the app where a spoken command teaches itself: the words on the button are the words that work.'],t.s)
B.aRI=new A.v(1177,B.acf)
B.arC=s(["MIC AUTO-OFF MOVED IN WITH THE DICTATION SETTINGS. It was filed under Advanced; it is a dictation dial you actually tune, so it now sits right below the microphone rows \u2014 same setting, same sheet, easier to reach.","THE CLARITY BAR NOW READS YOUR ROOM, NOT A FIXED SCALE. On the phone the bar sat half full in a quiet room while the same room showed almost nothing on the Windows app. Your phone's microphone genuinely hears a quiet room far louder than the laptop's does, and the bar was drawn on one fixed scale that could only suit one of them. It now starts empty at whatever your room's own background level is on that device and fills as you speak up, so a quiet room reads empty everywhere and the bar means the same thing on the phone as on the desktop."],t.s)
B.b32=new A.v(1176,B.arC)
B.abl=s(["A CLARITY METER IN THE DICTATION BOX. While you dictate, a row of segments right under the Dictate control shows how loudly your voice reaches the microphone, and its colour says how clearly the words are coming through: green Clear, amber Faint, red Noisy. If it stays faint or noisy for a few seconds, a line under it tells you the fix - bring the phone closer, or turn down the background noise - and clears on its own when things improve. It goes dark the moment the mic is off.","ANDROID: A NOISE-HANDLING CHOICE FOR THE MICROPHONE. Settings > Dictation > Noise handling. Raw is what every recording has always used. Speech-tuned opens the phone's dictation microphone instead - boosted for speech, with the phone's own noise handling - for the car or a loud room. Takes effect the next time you start dictating.","A ONENOTE PAGE-FOLDER NO LONGER DOUBLES ITS ITEMS EVERY TIME YOU PLAY IT. Your Goals page had 133 paragraphs and the folder was growing past that on every play; the refresh was re-adding what it had written the time before. It now recognises its own paragraphs, follows the page's order so the folder settles, and leaves alone any items of your own you filed into that folder - those used to be removed on the next play."],t.s)
B.aYl=new A.v(1175,B.abl)
B.adI=s(["YOUR ONENOTE PAGES ARE LISTED THE WAY YOU ARRANGED THEM. When you browse into a section, its pages now appear in the order they sit in OneNote, and subpages are indented under their parent the way you nested them. They used to be sorted by whichever you edited last, which reshuffled the list every time you touched a page. Notebooks and sections stay alphabetical - OneNote's API does not publish the order you arranged those in, so alphabetical is the one order that stays put."],t.s)
B.b_M=new A.v(1174,B.adI)
B.as1=s(['FIND A ONENOTE PAGE BY TYPING. The picker used to be a list you scrolled \u2014 now there\'s a search box, and it matches the page title AND the notebook and section it lives in, so "cash" finds the page and "Finance" finds everything in that notebook. It also looks through far more pages than before.','AND IT SAYS WHERE YOU ARE. While you\'re browsing, the header now reads "Notebook > Section" instead of just the section name \u2014 so two sections with the same name in different notebooks stop looking identical.',"SETTINGS' RIGHT SIDE REACHED THE RIGHT SIDE. Your report, an hour after the overhaul shipped: on Windows, every switch, value and chevron floated in the middle of the screen. The desktop shell was centering Settings in a reading-width column built for list screens; Settings now spans the window the way Canvas does, so each row's value sits at the true right edge \u2014 where a Windows settings page puts it."],t.s)
B.aZh=new A.v(1173,B.as1)
B.akZ=s(["SETTINGS SPEAKS WITH ONE VOICE. Your report \u2014 the options were different font sizes \u2014 and your pick from the three mockups, built as drawn. Every option is now one clean row: its name on the left at one size, its current setting on the right, so the whole screen states itself at a glance. The explanations didn't vanish \u2014 each moved into the panel its row opens, sitting next to the control it explains, read at the moment you're changing that setting. Same settings, same order, about a third of the scrolling \u2014 and the Diagnostics rows that were invisible in light mode got real cards.","FILING A BATCH NO LONGER FREEZES THE APP. The long stall you photographed \u2014 moving items into folders kicked off a background sweep that starved the app's own reads while you were mid-click. That sweep now yields to your hands: it defers while you're interacting and catches up in the quiet moments. Approvals are never deferred."],t.s)
B.aW_=new A.v(1172,B.akZ)
B.amN=s(["THE SYNC SAVINGS SWITCH ON. Yesterday's split gave your words their own synced row; this build stops writing the old copy. Flipping a listen toggle, filing an item or stamping a status now moves a few hundred bytes between your devices instead of re-sending the whole note \u2014 the shape that burned 73 GB in August is structurally gone. Nothing changes on screen; the meter is the feature."],t.s)
B.aZf=new A.v(1171,B.amN)
B.alf=s(["YOUR READING VOICE FOLLOWS YOU NOW. Pick Bella on the phone and your desktops adopt her; pick Asteria at the desk and the phone follows. One choice, every signed-in device \u2014 including \"System default,\" which travels too. A device that can't honor the pick (a voice the other platform doesn't have, Kokoro not downloaded there yet) reads in its own system voice and quietly waits for one that can. Reading speed stays per-device on purpose \u2014 say the word if it should travel as well.","SYNC STOPPED RE-SHIPPING YOUR WORDS. The engine used to re-send an item's entire text to every device any time anything on the row changed \u2014 flipping a listen toggle re-shipped the whole note, which is the shape behind August's 73 GB overage on a 2 GB plan. The words now live in their own synced row that only moves when the WORDS change; toggles and statuses travel alone. You gave the go this morning and the two production steps ran on the spot; the one-time re-pull your machines did right after was this change settling in.","QUIET VOICE SAMPLES NOW SAY WHY. Your report: changing voices while the player was going played no sample and explained nothing. The samples were held on purpose \u2014 one played mid-reading would be heard as YOUR voice and pause the program \u2014 but silence with no reason was wrong. The picker now shows the why before you even tap, and says plainly that your pick still lands: the next sentence uses it.",'THE FOLD SHOWS TEN VOICES, NOT HUNDREDS. Your report. The phone\'s engine lists dozens of near-identical variants \u2014 most of them online/offline twins of the same voice. The device shelf now curates to the best ten (US English first, the always-works half of each twin), and "Show all voices" still reveals every last one. Nothing hidden, just tidied.'],t.s)
B.aYB=new A.v(1170,B.alf)
B.apV=s(["NATURAL READING VOICES ARE HERE \u2014 the trial you took tonight, released. The Reading voice sheet now has three shelves. NATURAL \xb7 AURA (online): Asteria, Luna, Stella, Athena, Orion, Arcas, Angus and Zeus \u2014 tap one and it speaks instantly, streamed over the same account your dictation already uses. NATURAL \xb7 KOKORO (offline): one tap downloads the voices once (~360 MB, resumable), and then Bella, Nicole, Sarah, Sky, Adam, Michael, Emma, Isabella, George, Lewis and the Blend read with no signal at all \u2014 free forever, car-proof. Your device's own voices stay on the third shelf. Whichever you pick reads everything \u2014 items, the Listening list, reading while you dictate \u2014 and the Reading speed dial works on all of them.","A NATURAL VOICE CAN NEVER SILENCE YOUR READING. No network for Aura, Kokoro not downloaded yet, anything at all going wrong \u2014 the reading continues in your device voice the same second instead of stopping, and the diagnostics log notes why. Picking a voice is never a way to lose your words out loud.","SPOKEN COMMANDS FORGIVE THE MISHEARINGS YOU ACTUALLY GET. Your report \u2014 \"'do dictation' seems like a common misheard word\" \u2014 checked against a sweep of over a million transcribed chunks from the real logs, which is a labelled record of every command that fired and every one that missed. The mishear list now covers what the sweep actually found, and the seam gaps that let command words slip between chunks are closed with it.","THE PATH CHEVRONS ARE VISIBLE NOW. They were always there between the folder names at the top \u2014 drawn so faint on the dark theme they read as absent, which is why it looked like the labels were floating. Same glyph, ink turned up. The search empty-state and a count that shared that faintest ink got the same lift, and the move sheet now says WHAT you are moving ('Move \"Cash flow ideas\"'), not just where."],t.s)
B.b_B=new A.v(1169,B.apV)
B.arI=s(['THE TOP BAR CALLS IT THE INBOX. The trail across the top said "Home" \u2014 a name you retired a long time ago \u2014 while the pane row, the screen title and everything else said Inbox, and tapping it landed you on a screen headed Inbox. One name for one place now. Underneath, the link no longer depends on the word: it used to decide whether that crumb was clickable by comparing its text, so renaming it would have left a crumb that looked like a link and quietly wasn\'t.',"ONE INBOX DOOR. The capture destination stops presenting itself as an ordinary folder \u2014 it has one way in, and it no longer offers the menu options that only make sense for folders you made yourself."],t.s)
B.b2c=new A.v(1168,B.arI)
B.akx=s(["THE FOLDER \xb7\xb7\xb7 MENU OPENS AT YOUR THUMB NOW. Tap the circle at the top of a folder \u2014 or a row's \xb7\xb7\xb7, or long-press, or right-click on desktop \u2014 and the menu appears right there, grouped with icons, instead of a full-width sheet sliding up from the bottom. Create and rename first; then Move\u2026, Also file in\u2026 (the new name for \"File into Folder\u2026\" \u2014 it ADDS a second home, Move transfers), and Reorder; then sorting; and Delete alone in red at the bottom where a thumb-slip can't reach it. Your approved mockup, built as drawn.",'THE MENU LOST ITS DUPLICATES. "Add to Play List" is gone from it \u2014 the headphones toggle in the title bar and on every folder row already does exactly that in one tap (canvas cards keep the menu row; they have no headphones yet). Move Up / Move Down are gone too \u2014 Reorder Folders\u2026 drags anywhere in one visit.',"AI SORT MOVED INTO THE MENU. The sparkles button left the folder title bar \u2014 the bar is down to four icons \u2014 and AI Sort now also works from a folder ROW and from canvas for the first time, not just from inside the folder.",'A FILTER CHIP CAN NO LONGER EMPTY A FOLDER. Your Release Voicley scare: tapping a type pill hid all 37 items behind a filter that could never match them, and the "showing 0" line whispered in the faintest grey. A chip is now only offered when the folder actually holds something it matches, and at zero the message says in full-strength text that everything is still here and Clear brings it back. Nothing was ever lost \u2014 the server had every link the whole time.',"TRASH STOPPED TOWERING OVER ITS NEIGHBOURS ON DESKTOP. It was holding a 44-pixel phone-sized touch target where Search and Settings use 28. It keeps the big target on the phone, where you aim with a finger and drop drags onto it, and matches its neighbours where you aim with a mouse."],t.s)
B.aXS=new A.v(1167,B.akx)
B.abG=s(["THE LISTENING SETTINGS ARE ONE ROW EACH \u2014 the layout you picked. Every setting in that card now reads as its own line instead of a stack you had to parse, and the playback mode moved into its own sheet where the choices can actually explain themselves.","HOVERING NOW OUTLINES THE BUTTONS. Your report, and you were right that it wasn't working: hovering only ever changed a row's background shading, and on the dark theme that shading works out to about a 6% brightness lift on a black pane \u2014 technically there, practically invisible. Nothing ever drew an outline. Now hovering draws the boundary, which is what makes a row look like a button. Inbox and the Reading list change visibly too, instead of looking permanently hovered.","\"SET UP PHONE\" IS GONE FROM THE PANE. It transferred an OpenAI key that now ships inside the app, and installed over a USB cable to a phone that updates itself over the air \u2014 obsolete twice over, and only ever visible on the desktop. Nothing was deleted: it's one switch away if it's ever needed again.","\"PLAYED BEFORE\" IS GONE FROM THE LEFT PANE, on your call \u2014 you asked for it a day ago, used it, and didn't want it. The pane says what's playing now and nothing else. The Reading list still marks where you left off.",'REFRESHING A ONENOTE FOLDER WHILE SIGNED OUT NOW SAYS SO. It used to blame the network \u2014 "Couldn\'t reach OneNote" \u2014 when the truth was that nobody was signed in. Now it names the real cause and offers a Sign In button that opens the OneNote screen directly. Your ask, the same afternoon you hit it.',"SIGNING INTO MICROSOFT ON WINDOWS NO LONGER FREEZES THE APP. Hitting Continue in the browser locked the whole app into Not Responding \u2014 what your office PC hit today. The invisible helper that carries the sign-in back into the app opened a window of its own for a split second, and the app tried to grab the screen from that dying window and hung forever. The helper is now truly windowless \u2014 nothing to hang on \u2014 and both halves of the hand-off keep a small log, so if sign-in ever misbehaves again it can say exactly where."],t.s)
B.aRA=new A.v(1166,B.abG)
B.aqt=s(['YOU CAN GET OUT OF THE READING LIST AND PLAYLISTS AGAIN. Your report: "there is no way to get out of it once in there" \u2014 and you were right twice, because it was one fault wearing two names. Opening a screen from the left pane replaces what you were looking at instead of stacking on top of it, so there was nothing for a back arrow to return to, and four screens never grew one of their own: the Reading list (which is also every opened playlist), Playlists, the program tree, and the reader\'s Now Playing \u2014 whose empty version said "Nothing to read" and gave you nothing at all to press. All four have a back arrow now. Screens you reached by tapping through keep their normal one, so nothing gained a second button.','THE READER NO LONGER THROWS AWAY THE WORD "I". Your report: a pause before the timer updates when you start dictating over the reader. Your phone\'s log had it twice in one session \u2014 you opened with "I" and waited 2.1 seconds; you opened with "Hi." and waited under a second. A check that decides whether the microphone heard real words or just a noise was requiring at least two characters, meaning to reject things like "." and "-" \u2014 but "I" is one character and it is how most sentences start, so your first word was discarded and the reader waited for your next one. Worse, the countdown you were watching kept ticking toward the reader resuming the whole time, so it looked like the app had not heard you. It now rejects noises rather than short words.',"AI SORT IS IN THE INBOX. You asked why it was only in folders: the button was built into the folder screen, and the Inbox has its own. Same sparkles button, same review-before-anything-moves, now on the pile that most needed it \u2014 and since a sorted item leaves the folder it came from, the Inbox actually empties.",'DELETING A FOLDER COUNTS WHAT YOU CAN SEE. Your "Group" folder said 2 items but the delete warning said 4. Both were honest about different things: two of those links pointed at items you had already sent to Trash, which the folder row hides and the warning counted. The warning now matches the folder, and says separately when links to already-trashed items will end. Fixed alongside it: deleting a folder was re-deleting items that were already in Trash, quietly restarting their 30-day restore countdown \u2014 an item you trashed three weeks ago no longer gets its clock reset.',"THE \uff0b IS GONE FROM THE FOLDER HEADER, ON YOUR CALL. Everything it offered was already on the same screen: the dictate box at the top of every folder already files what you say into that folder, a new folder comes from the \xb7\xb7\xb7 menu or the \uff0b in the left pane, and inside the Claude folders the box is already a Claude box. The Inbox keeps its \uff0b.","THE WINDOWS INSTALLER IS HALF THE SIZE AGAIN. v1163's installer jumped from 20 MB to 38.6 MB because the test pass before a release left a 92 MB debug file in the build folder, and the installer packs that folder whole. It skips that file now, so it can never ship again no matter what a test run leaves behind \u2014 and the app itself stops carrying 92 MB it could never use."],t.s)
B.b1s=new A.v(1165,B.aqt)
B.aox=s(['LISTEN TO YOUR ONENOTE PAGES. Settings \u2192 OneNote integration \u2192 "Link a page to the Reading list". Pick a page and it becomes a FOLDER in Voicley, with each paragraph as its own item \u2014 so you can resume mid-page, skip a paragraph, and see where you are, instead of one enormous block. Every time an item plays, Voicley fetches that page again first: what you hear is what the page says now, not what it said when you linked it. The items are read-only here on purpose \u2014 they are a mirror of the page, and the next play would overwrite anything you typed. Edit in OneNote; Voicley never writes to your pages.','ONENOTE FOLDERS SAY SO, AND YOU CAN REFRESH THEM BY HAND. A linked folder carries a page icon and a line telling you its contents come from OneNote. Long-press it for "Refresh from OneNote" \u2014 it tells you whether it updated, was already current, or couldn\'t reach OneNote. Before this, the only way to pull an edit you had just made in OneNote was to start listening to the page.','SIGNING INTO ONENOTE ON WINDOWS WORKS \u2014 IT NEVER DID BEFORE. Your report: "it seemed to shut down after I logged in". It did. Windows was never told that Voicley owns its sign-in link, so after you logged in the browser handed the result back to Windows and Windows dropped it \u2014 and the sign-in window took the app down with it. Voicley now registers that link with Windows on every launch, so this fixes itself on each of your machines with no reinstall. This blocked setting up OneNote dictation sync too, not just listening.',"ITEMS WITHOUT A DESTINATION ARE NO LONGER INVISIBLE. Any item not attached to a project was being silently dropped from every list \u2014 not hidden, not greyed out, simply gone, with nothing anywhere saying so. Found because a linked OneNote page produced 131 real items and showed an empty folder. If you have older items that vanished this way, they are back.","LINKING THE SAME PAGE TWICE NO LONGER MAKES A SECOND FOLDER, AND DELETING A PAGE-FOLDER IS QUICK. Linking a page you have already linked takes you to the folder you already have instead of building a duplicate. Deleting one used to walk its items one at a time \u2014 131 of them, with the count ticking down while you waited; it now goes in a single step.","YOUR PAGES ARE READ IN THE ORDER YOU WROTE THEM. A page laid out in columns is read column by column the way it reads on screen, rather than jumping across the page line by line.","AI TITLES ARE SHORT NOW \u2014 SIX WORDS AT MOST. Every title the AI writes for you obeys the same limit, wherever it comes from: dictation rows, snips, meetings, section topics, the retitle sweep and the backfill all pass through one place. Long titles were being cut off mid-phrase by the row they had to fit in.","SETTINGS IS SHORTER, AND ITS SECTIONS STAY HOW YOU LEAVE THEM. Storage, AI titles and Show item numbers moved into Advanced \u2014 they are one-off tools and a set-once preference, not things you visit. Every collapsible section is a real row now, with an arrow that turns when it opens, and all five remember whether you left them open.",'ONENOTE DICTATIONS SHOWS WHAT YOU HAVE LINKED. A "Linked pages" row with a count, matching the Reading list\'s "Linked folders" \u2014 tap either to see every link and open the folder or project behind it. The Tasks page row is gone for now; nothing pointed at a page was changed.'],t.s)
B.b0Y=new A.v(1164,B.aox)
B.aqV=s(["PLAYLISTS. A playlist is a named, ordered list of folders the reader plays in your order \u2014 the same folder can sit in as many playlists as you like, and none of it moves or re-files anything in your library. Pane \u2192 Listen \u2192 Playlists: create one, name it, add folders, drag the play order, play it. Delete comes with Undo, and deleting a playlist deletes nothing else \u2014 same promise as unchecking headphones.","YOU CAN SEE WHAT'S PLAYING AND WHERE YOU LEFT OFF. The pane now shows a NOW PLAYING row while the reader has the floor and a PLAYED BEFORE row for where you last were \u2014 each with its folder line, each a tap to open. The Reading list gained a Folders | All items switch: the flat face lists every item in play order with its folder path \u2014 tap a row to play it, tap the path to open the folder.","AI SORT FOR A FOLDER. The sparkles Sort button in a folder view asks the AI to sort that folder's items into folders \u2014 it can propose brand-new folders too (Settings toggle, on by default). You approve the plan before anything moves; an accepted item leaves the sorted folder so the pile actually shrinks.","PICK THE READING VOICE \u2014 AND ITS SPEED. Settings \u2192 the reader section: choose the voice the reader uses on this device and how fast it talks. A voice that goes missing never blocks reading \u2014 the reader falls back and reads anyway. On Windows, returning to the system default takes effect at the next launch; immediate on phones.","SEARCH AND TRASH MOVED IN WITH SETTINGS. One block at the bottom of the pane in both layouts, full drag-and-drop kit included, and the transport buttons are finger-sized now.","EVERY PANE ROW ANSWERS YOUR POINTER. Hover washes, presses depress, and Inbox and the Reading list carry a resting hairline so the pane's fixed rows read as the buttons they are."],t.s)
B.b_H=new A.v(1163,B.aqV)
B.aj5=s(['DELETING ALL OF AN ITEM\'S TEXT NO LONGER LOCKS THE BOX. Your Windows report: select-all + delete on an existing item, and the box refused typing from then on \u2014 with "Waiting to transcribe \u2014 needs a connection" sitting where your words go. Emptying an item that has audio made it look exactly like an offline dictation still waiting on its transcription, so the app swapped in the read-only waiting card and stood ready to re-transcribe the old recording \u2014 which would have brought back the very words you deleted. An item you empty by hand is now just an empty note: typable immediately, on every machine, and never auto-refilled. The audio stays attached and playable, and Retry in Files can still bring the old words back \u2014 only when YOU ask.',"AND THE TITLE GOES WITH THE TEXT. Deleting everything in an item now clears its title too \u2014 your rule. Any other edit leaves titles exactly as they were, hand-typed ones included.","WHEN THE INTERNET COMES BACK, WAITING ITEMS ACTUALLY RECOVER. \"The internet is back and it hasn't recovered\" \u2014 right, and it never would have: recovery ran only when the app launched or when you pressed the Settings Recover button. The app now notices the connection returning and runs one recovery pass on its own \u2014 throttled so a flapping connection can't run up your OpenAI bill, and every existing cost guard (attempt caps, budget hold, silence gate, yield-to-recording) still applies. One reach limit stays true by design: each device can only transcribe audio it holds \u2014 a phone recording recovers on the phone."],t.s)
B.b2N=new A.v(1162,B.aj5)
B.amU=s(['THE 14-SECOND RECONNECT LOOP IS CLOSED. Your drive report \u2014 "dictation was super sketchy" \u2014 and the log showed why: one network blip at the start, then the connection was rebuilt every 14 seconds, 44 times in a row, on a connection that was working the whole time. Two recovery paths were each opening a socket per drop; the spare got abandoned, timed out, and its death was mistaken for a fresh outage \u2014 forever. One drop now costs one socket. Your words were never at risk (the recording always had them); what suffered was live text, the yellow banner flickering, and voice commands.',"IF THE CONNECTION DIES ANYWAY, DICTATION KEEPS GOING. When the live engine drops and the offline engine is downloaded (Settings \u2192 the engine row \u2192 tap it once to download, 189 MB), live captions now hand over to it at your next natural pause \u2014 rougher text, no punctuation, but words on screen instead of a dead stretch \u2014 and hand back the moment the connection returns. Phones and Windows; iPhone keeps its built-in fallback.","THE READER NO LONGER TALKS OVER YOU MID-WORD. Dictating between its sentences, you were cut off 23 times in one drive \u2014 the gap gave you 5.4 seconds flat, however clearly you were still talking. What holds the gap open is now the engine actually hearing your words (road noise can't fake that), so it waits up to 12 seconds while you're speaking; a noisy car with nobody talking still moves on quickly.","THE DICTATION BOX ONLY LIGHTS WHEN YOUR WORDS ARE LANDING. Your report: it glowed while your microphone was muted for the reader \u2014 so you kept talking into a dead mic. The now-playing highlight was painting over the deliberately-dark box; it no longer can. Dark box = the reader has the floor.","THE RESUME COUNTDOWN FOLLOWS YOUR VOICE. It froze at your first word and drained to zero while the reader was in fact still waiting for you. Every word you say now pushes the number out, so the timer and the reader finally agree.",'AND THE ENGINE-ERROR NOTICE SPEAKS ENGLISH. Last build made it name its reason \u2014 but an unrecognised cause pasted raw programmer text at you ("MissingPluginException(\u2026)"). Anything readable is still quoted verbatim; code scaffolding now becomes a plain sentence, a reminder your words are still being recorded, and a pointer to Settings \u2192 Diagnostics where the full text lives.'],t.s)
B.aSa=new A.v(1161,B.amU)
B.aoR=s(['THE ENGINE ERROR NOW TELLS YOU WHAT IS WRONG. On your office PC it said only that the dictation engine could not start \u2014 while the app already knew the exact reason and had written it to the log: no microphone was found. It now says that, and where to look: Windows Sound settings, and the "let desktop apps access your microphone" switch that hides a working mic from the app. Permission problems and a mic held by another app get named the same way, and anything unrecognised is quoted verbatim rather than hidden.'],t.s)
B.aUX=new A.v(1160,B.aoR)
B.akS=s(["WINDOWS NO LONGER DICTATES THE READER. Your report, and the cause was a mute that lasted milliseconds: the app muted the microphone, asked the system to speak \u2014 and that call returns the instant speech STARTS, not when it ends. So the microphone was live for the whole sentence, and the only thing standing between the reader's voice and your box was a word-match check that needs five words in a row and lets anything mangled through. The mute is now held until the sentence genuinely finishes, which is the same guarantee the phone has had since Tuesday \u2014 with no pauses added to Windows.","It releases on every path that can end a sentence \u2014 finishing, pausing, stopping \u2014 so the microphone can never be left muted on you."],t.s)
B.b1w=new A.v(1159,B.akS)
B.ajq=s(['LISTEN WHILE DICTATING IS NOW OFFERED ON iPHONE AND iPAD. Your report: you opened the TestFlight build, went to Settings for the toggle, and it was not there. It was hidden on iOS deliberately \u2014 but for a reason that stopped being true three days ago. Settings \u2192 the reader section \u2192 "Keep listening while I dictate".',"WHY IT WAS HIDDEN, AND WHY THAT NO LONGER APPLIES. On 22 August the feature worked by LISTENING FOR YOUR VOICE OVER THE READER \u2014 and on a phone speaker your voice sits only a hair above the reader's own, so it could not tell you apart. That is why it was desktop-only. Since then the whole design changed: the reader now STOPS DEAD in each gap, so nothing is playing when you speak, and what triggers it is Deepgram actually recognising your words rather than a loudness measurement. The app has treated iPhones as gap-mode phones since Tuesday; only this one row was still hidden, checking a rule that had already been retired.","IT IS UNTESTED ON iOS AND I AM SAYING SO RATHER THAN LETTING YOU FIND OUT. Nobody has run it on an iPhone or iPad. The design does not depend on the platform in principle \u2014 but eight things broke this week that were fine 'in principle', so your use is the only real test. If it misbehaves there, say so and it goes back off for iOS while we look."],t.s)
B.b2g=new A.v(1158,B.ajq)
B.afr=s(['THE COUNTDOWN SAYS WHAT RESUMES. It read "Dictating \u2014 resumes in 13", which described what YOU were doing and left the one thing the number is about unsaid. It now reads "Player resumes in 13" \u2014 your words. One shared label, so every screen says it the same way.'],t.s)
B.aU4=new A.v(1157,B.afr)
B.aq5=s(['THE "BAD CONNECTION" BANNER CAN NO LONGER STICK. Your report from the car: the yellow bad-connection notice stayed up more than five minutes after the connection was fine again. Found in the code: connecting is given ten seconds to succeed, but the recovery added three days ago was starting a FRESH attempt every eight \u2014 so attempts overlapped, and when an old one finally gave up it tore down whichever connection was current, including a healthy one that had just come back. Every recovery was being killed by the failure of the attempt before it, forever. A dead attempt can no longer speak for a live one, and recovery waits for an attempt to finish before starting another \u2014 while still rescuing one that is genuinely wedged.'],t.s)
B.aT9=new A.v(1156,B.aq5)
B.aqQ=s(["WINDOWS READS STRAIGHT THROUGH AGAIN. Your report, and the log agreed: your desktop had been chopping items into 3-second-paused sentences \u2014 13 gaps in one item \u2014 when it is supposed to read continuously. Your machine measures its room over and over and the readings swing wildly (-42, -15, -56, -11); the app took the LOUDEST as the truth and, since three days ago, remembered it forever. One loud moment put your desktop in the phone's mode permanently. Both changes that caused it were fixes made FOR the phone that ended up governing only the desktop. The room measurement no longer decides: a phone gets pauses, a desktop reads through, full stop.",'AND THE TIMER LEAVES A RECORD WHEN IT CHANGES, not only when it appears \u2014 so "it registered but didn\'t give me the 20 seconds" can be answered from the log instead of guessed at. The countdown ring also redraws correctly when the 3-second pause hands over to the 20.'],t.s)
B.aV8=new A.v(1155,B.aqQ)
B.adh=s(['THE HAND-OFF, INSTALLED. When it stops for you, YOUR words take the reader\'s line \u2014 red, live, with red bars moving and "You \u2014 dictating" above them \u2014 then silence hands the line back to the reading. One unbroken thread from its voice to yours, exactly like the mockup.',"THE PURPLE COMMANDS-ONLY IS GONE FOR GOOD WHILE THE READER OWNS THE MIC. You had this removed once already. It came back through a different door: tapping into an item deliberately parks dictation \u2014 a rule written in July for sessions YOU start, and browsing while listening is normal now. A reader-opened microphone now refuses that park exactly as it already refuses to be closed by navigation.","AND A GHOST TRIGGER IS SHUT OUT. Your report: the first attempt in a silent room started dictation on its own. Recognition can emit a stray fragment on near-silence, and ANY fragment was counting as you speaking. It now has to look like actual words \u2014 two characters with a letter in them \u2014 and every stop records what triggered it, so a ghost is named by the log instead of guessed at."],t.s)
B.aVd=new A.v(1154,B.adh)
B.ajW=s(["THE READER'S LINE, ON THE SCREEN YOU ACTUALLY WATCH. The voice header \u2014 the moving bars and the exact sentence being read, dimming in your pauses \u2014 was only on Now Playing; you press play and watch the reading list. It now lives there too, right above the timer pill, exactly like the mockup."],t.s)
B.b0X=new A.v(1153,B.ajW)
B.ab8=s(["THE TIMER NOW LOOKS LIKE THE MOCKUP YOU APPROVED. One ring-pill everywhere: a small circle that drains with the time, beside big colored seconds \u2014 amber Speak-now in every pause, red Dictating-resumes-in while it has stopped for you, refilling live as you talk. Composer, reading list, Now Playing and the side panel all wear the same face.","AND NOW PLAYING GOT THE VOICE HEADER: four little bars that move only while the reader is actually speaking and lie flat in your pauses, with the exact line being read \u2014 which dims whenever the voice goes quiet, so a glance tells you whose turn it is."],t.s)
B.aSI=new A.v(1152,B.ab8)
B.age=s(["IT STOPS THE MOMENT YOUR WORDS APPEAR, NOT TWO SECONDS LATER. Your 'Yo, dog' report caught the final seam: the grey draft shows your words within a third of a second, but the stop was riding the FINALIZED copy, which arrives up to two seconds later \u2014 so a pause could expire and the next sentence start under words you had visibly already said, with the 20 arriving late. First sight of your words in a pause now stops the reading on the spot \u2014 draft and pause land together. This is only safe because of the hard-stop fix: in a pause the reader is provably silent, so words appearing there can only be a real voice.","And when the end of a pause briefly waits for words still in flight, the Speak-now ring keeps breathing instead of going dark \u2014 a correct wait no longer looks like a timeout."],t.s)
B.aZT=new A.v(1151,B.age)
B.ahP=s(['YOUR FIRST HELLO HOLDS THE PAUSE. Your two reports cornered two stacked timing flaws. First: when your word and the pause it caused were stamped in the same millisecond \u2014 which is routine \u2014 a 4.5-second referee read that as "no words came back" and resumed reading over you. That is why a single quiet hello registered but never got its 20 seconds, and why only the FIRST use failed: later attempts carry follow-on words that stamp clearly later. The same millisecond now counts as you, and when recognized words are what triggered the pause the referee never sits at all \u2014 the question it asks is already answered.',"AND SPEAKING AT THE END OF A PAUSE NO LONGER GETS CUT OFF. Your catch: the beginning of the 3 seconds worked, the end did not \u2014 because the next sentence started mid-word and the mute chopped your unfinished sentence, which then never registered. The end of every pause now waits while your voice is still going \u2014 briefly and with a hard cap, at most 2.4 extra seconds \u2014 so your words finish and land before the reading carries on."],t.s)
B.aTQ=new A.v(1150,B.ahP)
B.amV=s(["RECOGNITION CAN NO LONGER DIE SILENTLY. Your receipt caught the night's real ghost: a full minute of perfect pauses with the microphone flowing and the transcription connection DEAD \u2014 its one recovery path was a single retry chain, and one hung attempt killed it forever with nothing anywhere whose job it was to notice. That is why words sometimes vanished and then 'worked the second time.' The demand for a live connection now rides the audio itself: any sound arriving at a dead connection forces a fresh one within seconds, every revival is written to the log, and a hung attempt gets superseded instead of trusted. The microphone is the one thing that always keeps running \u2014 recovery now lives exactly as long as it does."],t.s)
B.b2E=new A.v(1149,B.amV)
B.arN=s(["THE TIMER IS EVERYWHERE THE READER IS. One shared countdown now lives on the reading list, the Now Playing screen, and the left pane's player \u2014 red counting down your 20 while it has stopped for you, Speak now during each pause, nothing while it reads. And every time it draws, it writes a receipt naming the screen \u2014 so a first-moment miss like tonight's is answered from the record, not by another round of you testing."],t.s)
B.b2T=new A.v(1148,B.arN)
B.an4=s(["WHEN IT STOPS FOR YOU, THE VOICE ACTUALLY STOPS. You caught the real one: the sentences in your box were not a TV \u2014 they were the reader itself, because on your phone the platform's pause quietly leaves the in-flight sentence PLAYING while the app believes it is paused. And stopped-for-you is exactly when the microphone is open, so the reader dictated itself and stray words leaked all week. In pause-and-listen mode the pause is now a hard stop of the voice \u2014 silence means silence. Nothing is lost: resuming already re-reads the current sentence from its start.","AND THE TIMER IS ON THE SCREEN YOU ACTUALLY USE. Every 'no timer' tonight was a missing window, not a missing clock \u2014 the countdown only existed above the Home dictation box, and you press play from the reading list. The reading list's player card now shows it: red 'Dictating \u2014 resumes in Ns' while it has stopped for you, 'Speak now \u2014 Ns' during each listening pause. Your oval-label request, delivered."],t.s)
B.b0P=new A.v(1147,B.an4)
B.aip=s(["RULE 1 IS NOW STRUCTURAL. Press play on a phone with listen-while-dictating on and the app IS in pause-and-listen mode \u2014 no measurement, no mode-sorter, nothing a volume knob can influence. The last three releases broke your first rule three different ways, and all three were the same underlying mistake: the app chose between phone-mode and desktop-mode by measuring the speaker's echo, and an echo reading moves with the volume knob. In 1143 my replacement dividing line had the inequality BACKWARDS \u2014 your quiet phone still sorted as a desktop \u2014 and the memory I added then saved that wrong verdict, which is why 1144 dictated the reader from the very first sentence. On a phone the deciding fact is now the only stable one there is: the speaker sits centimetres from the microphone. Desktop keeps its measured path untouched.","And your first rule finally has what every other rule got this week: a test with teeth. The mode decision is a pure function pinned at every echo level that ever fooled it \u2014 including my backwards arithmetic, by name \u2014 so it cannot ship wrong silently a fourth time."],t.s)
B.aT3=new A.v(1145,B.aip)
B.amh=s(["THE OLD LOUDNESS PAUSE IS SWITCHED OFF FOR GOOD ON THE PHONE. Your reports: dictating with no timer showing, then playing for a second or two and stopping for over a minute. Both were one thing \u2014 the original energy-based pause detector, which sat harmlessly dead on your phone all week because the full-volume echo made its threshold unreachable. Turning the volume down brought it back to life. It paused mid-sentence through its own side door (no timer, because that door never starts one), and its own resume needs a stretch of silence the room never provides (the minute-long stalls). While pause-and-listen mode owns a session it can now only measure, never pause \u2014 your voice, recognised as words, is the one and only trigger, which is the spec you gave me."],t.s)
B.aVP=new A.v(1144,B.amh)
B.agu=s(["THE VOLUME KNOB CAN NO LONGER TURN THE FEATURE OFF. Tonight's circle \u2014 lights on while reading, the reader dictating itself, listening dead \u2014 was ONE fork: the app decides between pause-and-listen mode and read-straight-through by measuring the speaker's echo, and with your media volume turned down your phone measured quiet enough to be mistaken for a Windows machine, which reads straight through by design. The dividing line now sits where the measurements actually are (your phone at its quietest is still far louder than any Windows machine), and your phone REMEMBERS the loudest echo it has ever measured \u2014 so one normal-volume session pins it in pause-and-listen mode for good.","THE COUNTDOWN RESETS ON YOUR FIRST SYLLABLE. Your report: saying a word took about 3 seconds to reset the 20-second timer. The reset waited for a finalised transcript, which needs a stretch of silence the room does not always provide; it now resets the moment the live draft hears you \u2014 a few hundred milliseconds.","And the reading-aloud signal is set on every path that speaks, so even a wrongly-sorted device can no longer sit lit through a reading."],t.s)
B.b0s=new A.v(1143,B.agu)
B.ai2=s(['THE LEFT PANE STOPS SAYING "DICTATING" WHILE THE READER READS. Your report: the dictation lights were on during reading. Found in the code, not guessed: the pane\'s dictation pill was built three days BEFORE listen-while-dictating existed, and it only ever asked "is the microphone open?" The reader holds the microphone for the whole session, so the pill sat there lit, saying Dictating with a running clock, through every sentence \u2014 the sixth piece of the app found still living in the old design. While the reader is speaking the pill now wears its idle face, exactly as if no dictation were happening; in the pauses, and while it has stopped for you, it lights and counts just as before \u2014 which is your own spec: in between sentences it is listening.','And lights questions now leave a paper trail: every time the reader starts or stops speaking is written to the diagnostic log, so "the lights were on" can be checked against the record instead of argued about.'],t.s)
B.b46=new A.v(1142,B.ai2)
B.at8=s(["YOUR FIRST WORDS NOW STOP THE READER EVEN WHEN THEY ARRIVE A BEAT LATE. Your report: the first dictation landed in the box but never started the 20-second timer, and the second one worked. The transcription takes a moment to finalise, so words you start inside a pause routinely come back just AFTER the reader has begun its next sentence. Since this morning those words survive to your box \u2014 but nothing told the reader to STOP for them, so it read on over you with no timer. Late-arriving words now stop it exactly like on-time ones: it pauses mid-sentence, and your 20 seconds start.","And a miss like this can never be invisible again: every time the app hears you and decides NOT to pause, it now writes down which rule declined and why \u2014 so the next report like this is answered from the record instead of reconstructed."],t.s)
B.aX2=new A.v(1141,B.at8)
B.arJ=s(['ONE PAUSE, EVERYWHERE. Your call: "whether it is a title or the body the pause should be the same." Every pause the reader takes is now the same length \u2014 between sentences, between a title and its body, and between items. The one number is Pause between sentences in Settings, and the separate Pause between items dial from yesterday is gone. Measured before changing: the between-items wait was exactly 6 seconds every single time, so the shorter pauses you heard were sentence pauses inside an item \u2014 the machinery was honest, the two lengths were just one too many.'],t.s)
B.aUh=new A.v(1140,B.arJ)
B.alF=s(["IT STOPS HANGING UP ON ITSELF WHILE YOU LISTEN. Your report: it worked in the afternoon and got worse as the session went on, then stopped recognising you between sentences. The connection to the transcription service has a guard that rebuilds it when the server has said nothing for 8 seconds while audio is flowing. That is right for ordinary dictation \u2014 you are talking, so silence that long means something is wrong. It is exactly wrong while the reader is running, because then you are mostly LISTENING, and your own setting allows up to 30 seconds of deliberate silence. So it was tearing down a perfectly healthy connection for being quiet, and after one of those rebuilds the recognition did not come back.","While the reader owns the session it now waits far longer before deciding anything is wrong, which clears your longest legitimate silence with room to spare. The protection is not gone \u2014 a genuinely broken connection is still caught, just later \u2014 and the connection is held open the whole time regardless, which is what makes the longer wait safe rather than reckless.","FOR THE RECORD, THIS IS THE FOURTH TIME THE SAME THING HAS BITTEN. A guard written for the old design, still standing after the design changed underneath it: the loudness meter, the discarded voice-detection events, the wake-word gate, and now this 8-second timer. Each was correct when written and wrong afterwards, and each cost a release to find."],t.s)
B.b1v=new A.v(1139,B.alF)
B.ak3=s(["THE REPAIR FOR AN EMPTY LIBRARY ACTUALLY RUNS NOW. Your bedroom computer showed nothing at all. The library file on that machine is damaged, so the app could not open it, so nothing ever downloaded \u2014 signed in, sync fine, an empty screen. A repair for exactly this went in two days ago and it never ran once, on any machine. Your office PC hit the same damage on the 24th and had to be fixed by hand.",'WHY IT NEVER RAN. Before setting a damaged file aside the app asked "are these bytes really damaged?" \u2014 and it asked by opening the file a second time, read-only, just to check. Anything holding the file made that check come back "cannot tell", which the app read as "the file is fine", and it then took the path meant for a brand-new install instead of the repair. That is exactly what your bedroom machine\'s log shows: the check was blocked twice, while the real open had ALREADY come back saying the file was damaged. The proof was sitting right there and was being thrown away.',"SO THE FAILED OPEN NOW COUNTS AS PROOF. It is the better witness of the two \u2014 it failed while genuinely opening the library, which is the only place this kind of damage shows up at all. The read-only check still speaks first whenever it can; it simply can no longer veto the repair by being unable to answer.",'AND A FILE THAT IS MERELY BUSY IS STILL NEVER TREATED AS DAMAGED. That distinction is the one that matters most here \u2014 reading "another program has this open" as "this is broken" is what once nearly moved your live 818 MB library aside \u2014 so it is now enforced in two separate places and pinned by tests that use the exact error text from your own machine.',"NOTHING IS EVER DELETED. A damaged library is renamed, not removed: anything in it that had not yet reached the server stays on disk and can be recovered. What opens afterwards is a clean file the server fills back in. Setting it aside is also given a second of patience now, rather than giving up on the first try while the app's own connections are still letting go.","AND IT STOPS BUILDING AN AUDIO PLAYER FOR EVERY PHRASE YOU DICTATE. Since 1136, every chunk of dictation quietly constructed the whole reader \u2014 a real audio player and its speech plumbing \u2014 just to ask what the reader had recently said, even with no reader open anywhere. It now asks the cheap question first and only builds the reader when one is genuinely playing. Nothing changes while you are actually listening; it is simply work the phone was doing for nothing, every time you spoke."],t.s)
B.aWQ=new A.v(1138,B.ak3)
B.alU=s(['YOUR WORDS ARE NO LONGER THROWN AWAY FOR ARRIVING A MOMENT LATE. Your report: "now it seems to not be triggering dictation." This one was not a detection problem at all \u2014 the app heard you perfectly and then deliberately binned it. Your log: "Yep. He\'s right here." recognised, and discarded 70 milliseconds later.','WHY. The retired "say start dictation" experiment was still sitting in the live path, and while the reader was speaking it threw away everything that was not that exact phrase. You cannot avoid tripping it: you speak in a pause, the transcription takes a beat to come back, and by then the reader has begun its next sentence \u2014 so your real dictation looked like something to discard. It now only ever discards what it can PROVE is its own reading, word for word against its own script. Anything else is you, and survives.'],t.s)
B.aRn=new A.v(1137,B.alU)
B.aek=s(['IT NEEDS TO HEAR WORDS NOW, NOT JUST A SOUND. In 1135 the reader stopped four times in two minutes in a silent room. Deepgram\'s quick voice-detector does not mean "someone spoke" \u2014 it means "energy that might be speech" \u2014 and in a quiet room it says yes to almost anything, down to -47 on the meter. Each false stop also OPENED A DICTATION, and an open dictation faithfully captures whatever is audible. The trigger is now RECOGNISED WORDS: silence produces no words, so it cannot fire on a quiet room.',"AND THE READER SUBTRACTS ITS OWN SCRIPT AGAIN. The app knows exactly what it is reading, word for word, so any text coming back that matches what it is saying is dropped as its own voice. That protection was described in the code and had been quietly overwritten \u2014 it only still applied while a sentence was actually playing, never while the reader sat paused for you, which is precisely when the microphone is open. It now holds the whole time the reader is active. It stays cautious on purpose: five words in a row must match before anything is discarded, because losing your sentence is permanent and a stray line you can see and delete is not."],t.s)
B.b3P=new A.v(1136,B.aek)
B.as3=s(['IT USES DEEPGRAM TO HEAR YOU NOW, NOT A NOISE METER. Your question \u2014 "why would we use a noise analyzer when we already have Deepgram?" \u2014 was the right one, and the answer is that there was no good reason. In the pause after each sentence the app was deciding "is he talking?" by measuring how LOUD the room was. A loudness meter cannot tell your voice from a pothole, a door, or the radio. Deepgram can, it was already listening in those pauses, and it was already telling us the moment a voice started \u2014 we were throwing that away.',"WHY THE METER EXISTED AT ALL, AND WHY IT SHOULD HAVE GONE MONTHS AGO. The original design tried to hear you UNDERNEATH the reader while it was still speaking, and there Deepgram genuinely could not help: it would have transcribed the reader straight into your box. So a loudness meter was the only tool available. Then the design changed \u2014 the reader now stops on purpose between sentences, so inside that pause there is nothing playing and the microphone is already streaming to Deepgram. That killed the meter's whole reason to exist, and nobody removed it.","WHAT THIS ENDS. Every reader fault today came out of that meter \u2014 a number that was right in one room and wrong in another: it stopped on its own voice and re-read items, then went deaf, then stopped on ordinary noise, then cut out. There is no number left. Nothing to calibrate, nothing to learn, nothing that behaves differently in a car than in a quiet room. If a voice starts, it stops for you."],t.s)
B.aXU=new A.v(1135,B.as3)
B.aoe=s(['THE PAUSE BETWEEN ITEMS IS ITS OWN SETTING NOW. Your report: "settings are for 3 seconds and sometimes it waits/displays 6 seconds." It was doubling your number behind your back - the sentence pause was yours, and the pause between items was quietly twice it, stated nowhere you could see. A setting has to mean what it says, especially on an evening spent trying to tell a misbehaving reader from a misread number. There are two dials now, Pause between sentences and Pause between items, and each one means exactly the seconds it shows.',"NOTHING MOVES UNDER YOU. If you had never touched the sentence pause, items stay at 6 seconds. If you had set it to something else, the item pause starts at double whatever you had - the same behaviour you have been using - and from there it is yours to change on its own."],t.s)
B.b1y=new A.v(1134,B.aoe)
B.ahR=s(["THE READER IS BACK TO THE VERSION THAT WORKED. Your words: it was working really well in the afternoon and got worse through the evening. You were right, and the fault was mine - I changed how it decides you are talking three times today and each change fixed what you reported while breaking something else: first it stopped on its own voice and re-read items, then it went deaf and the timers vanished, then it stopped on ordinary noise, then it began cutting out entirely. The decision has been put back exactly as it was this afternoon, character for character.","WHAT IS KEPT FROM TODAY, because none of it is part of that decision: the connection to the transcription service no longer tears itself down between every pair of sentences (it was mistaking its own deliberate silence for a dead connection, and rebuilding it right on top of the pause you were meant to talk in), and the reader's own voice is never replayed into your box after a reconnect. Those were separate faults, separately evidenced, and they are not implicated in any of this evening.",'ARROWS THROUGH YOUR LISTENING LIST. Open an item from the listening list and you can now step to the next one without going back: a bar above the player with \u2039 \u203a arrows and a counter that names where you are \u2014 "3 of 12 \xb7 Cash Flow \xb7 Listening list". The arrows disappear at the ends rather than wrapping, and they do not appear at all for a list of one.',"AND THE REASON THEY COULD NOT WORK BEFORE. Opening an item from the listening list handed the viewer a list of exactly ONE item \u2014 itself \u2014 so swiping and the arrow keys had nowhere to go, from any of the three listening doors. They now hand over the whole list in your order, so swipe, the keyboard and the new arrows all work the same way. The arrows show from every door, so folders, Files and search get them too.","PAGING MOVES WHAT YOU ARE LOOKING AT, NOT WHAT IS PLAYING. The reader keeps reading whatever it was reading \u2014 the same rule as the pencil. Browsing while it reads never interrupts it.","AND A FULL ITEM NOW SHOWS ITS FOLDERS AT THE TOP. The folder chips moved from the bottom of a long item to just under the title, and they are accent-coloured instead of quiet grey, so you can see where an item lives without scrolling to the end of it. Every folder it belongs to lights up in the left pane as well, and re-lights as you page between items \u2014 from any door, not only when you came in through a folder.","AND ON WINDOWS YOU CAN DRAG A FULL ITEM STRAIGHT TO A FOLDER. A grip sits in the header beside the title; drag it onto any folder in the pane. It follows the same rule as everywhere else: an item that lives in one place MOVES, an item that lives in several gets ADDED to the new one, and holding Ctrl always adds rather than moves. Nothing is ever removed from a folder except by a drop you made deliberately."],t.s)
B.b0h=new A.v(1133,B.ahR)
B.aqK=s(["IT LISTENS AGAIN, AND THE TIMERS ARE BACK (phone). Your two reports on 1131 \u2014 \u201cisn't stopping when I dictate into the box\u201d and \u201cnot seeing the timers we setup today\u201d \u2014 were one fault. After each sentence the app waits for the room to go quiet before it starts listening, and it was waiting for a silence deeper than your room ever gets. So it never started listening, never heard you, and never showed the countdown, which only appears once it is listening.","THE CAUSE WAS A FIXED NUMBER WHERE A MEASUREMENT BELONGED \u2014 twice in one day, so it is now measured everywhere. The app was holding out for about -42 on its meter; the very same log line reported your actual room at -33. It now uses the room reading it already takes for every item, and if a room ever sits above the level it expected, it watches for a second and a half and then uses the quietest thing it actually heard. It can no longer wait for a silence that is not coming."],t.s)
B.aRO=new A.v(1132,B.aqK)
B.afv=s(["THE READER NO LONGER GETS STUCK ON ONE ITEM (phone) \u2014 and this one was mine, introduced in 1130. Your report: \u201cit is reading the same item over and over each time and never going to the next item.\u201d It was hearing ITSELF. Your Fold's speaker sits a centimetre from its microphone, so the reader's own voice arrives at full volume; the app decided that was you talking, stopped for twenty seconds, resumed, heard itself again, and never reached the end of an item.","WHY 1130 MADE IT WORSE RATHER THAN BETTER. In 1130 I stopped the app learning the room from any pause where it thought it heard a voice \u2014 meant to keep your voice from being mistaken for background noise. But when the voice it keeps hearing is its own, it never learns anything at all, so its threshold stayed at the lowest setting forever and its own reading cleared it every time. In 1129 that same learning is what let it settle down after a minute; I removed the recovery and kept the fault.","IT NOW WAITS FOR THE ROOM TO GO QUIET INSTEAD OF WATCHING A CLOCK. The phone tells the app when it has finished PREPARING a sentence, never when the speaker has finished PLAYING it, so any fixed waiting time is a guess that is wrong on some device. It now simply listens until the microphone confirms the sound has actually stopped \u2014 self-correcting on any phone and any speaker. And it learns the room from the quiet moments only, so it keeps getting smarter without ever mistaking your voice for the background."],t.s)
B.aT2=new A.v(1131,B.afv)
B.ak2=s(["IT NOW ACTUALLY LISTENS IN THE GAPS (phone). Your report: \u201cstopped listening to my dictation words in the time between the reader sentences.\u201d It was hanging up on itself. The app has a watchdog that reconnects when it is sending your voice and hearing nothing back \u2014 and while the reader speaks it deliberately sends nothing, so the watchdog read its own silence as a dead connection and rebuilt it. Between every pair of sentences. Each rebuild landed squarely on the gap you were meant to talk into, which is why the whole feature kept feeling half-dead no matter what else was fixed.","THE READING NO LONGER TRAILS ITS LAST WORDS INTO YOUR BOX. Your report: \u201cstill a few words leaking.\u201d Two causes, both closed. The phone tells the app a sentence is finished when it has finished PREPARING it, not when the speaker has finished PLAYING it, so the last word was still coming out of the phone while the app had started listening \u2014 it now waits properly. And each of those false reconnects above was replaying the previous couple of seconds into the recogniser, which while the reader is talking is the reader. That replay never includes the reading again.","AND IT NO LONGER MISTAKES THE CAR FOR YOU. Your report from the road: the radio and engine tripped it into dictation for about the first minute, and then it settled down by itself. You had it exactly right \u2014 it learns what the room sounds like, but it needed three pauses before it would trust what it had learned, and until then it used a number chosen for a quiet room, which road noise clears without anyone saying a word. It now measures the room on the very first pause, so a car is treated as a car from the start. The tests pin both halves: the radio must not get through, and you must.","AND THE FOLD STOPS ACTING LIKE A PHONE WHEN THE KEYBOARD IS UP. Three things quietly switched to the small-screen versions of themselves while you were typing on the unfolded Fold: the drag grip on a full item disappeared, folders went back to teaching phone gestures, and the project picker opened the mobile sheet instead of the dropdown anchored where you tapped. All three were asking \u201cis this a wide screen?\u201d from inside a page that hides the keyboard's height from them, so all three answered \u201cphone\u201d. That question now has one answer, computed once, in one place."],t.s)
B.b21=new A.v(1130,B.ak2)
B.afq=s(["TALK TO IT WHILE IT READS (phone). Press play and the microphone opens itself \u2014 no separate Dictate press. The reader speaks a sentence, takes a beat, and in that beat it is listening to you. Say anything and it stops, waits as long as you keep talking, and picks up again a few seconds after your last word. Nothing you say is a magic phrase; there is nothing to remember.","AND YOU CAN SEE WHAT IT IS DOING, which is what was really missing. Above the box: \u201cspeak now\u201d with a ring that empties over your gap, then \u201creading resumes in\u201d with a ring that empties over your silence \u2014 and every word you say fills it straight back up, so you can watch it notice you. While it is reading, the box shows nothing at all: no ring, no colour, no countdown. A lit box means your words are landing, and nothing else.","BOTH TIMINGS ARE YOURS, in Settings under Listen while dictating: how long it pauses between sentences, and how long your silence lasts before it carries on. The pause between items is twice the sentence pause, so finishing an item leaves room for a whole thought.","THE COMPUTER READS STRAIGHT THROUGH as it always did. The sentence pauses exist because a phone speaker sits a centimetre from its own microphone and drowns you out; your laptop cancels that in hardware, so it does not need them and does not get them. That is decided by measuring the room, not by which machine it is \u2014 a laptop in a bad room would get the pauses automatically.","AND A LIBRARY THAT COULD NOT REPAIR ITSELF NOW DOES. Your office computer sat empty all day on a damaged library file: the app knew how to set a broken file aside and download a fresh copy, but the damage was only visible at a moment where that repair could not be reached, so it retried forever instead. It now repairs it. Nothing is ever deleted \u2014 the damaged file is renamed and kept, in case anything in it never reached the server."],t.s)
B.b_J=new A.v(1129,B.afq)
B.abs=s(["ONE KIND OF ITEM NOW \u2014 exactly as you called it for version one. Every item wears the same look: no more green, amber or grey headers, no type distinctions anywhere. The one exception is Claude items, which keep their clay color and their whole round-trip, so the special thing stays visibly special. Underneath, nothing about your existing items changed or re-synced \u2014 the old types are still stored invisibly, so bringing types back later is a switch flip, not a migration.",'THE CREATE DOORS MATCH: every \uff0b menu now offers New note and New Claude message, nothing else \u2014 the Inbox, folders and the Canvas all agree. The left pane\'s type rows collapsed to just the Claude row, and search\'s type chips went with them. Spoken "new task" / "new meeting" still act for now (retiring them rides the listening work and lands next); what they create looks identical to every note.','AND A CURSOR BUG THAT WAS WAITING FOR YOU, caught by the night tests before it reached your machines: pressing Dictate could silently fail to put the caret in the main box \u2014 the press\'s own focus shuffle was being judged as "not your click on the box", so the box gave up instead of taking the cursor. Pressing Dictate now counts as your click on the main box, and the caret follows it through anything. This is the same family as the cursor bug that has come back on you four times; if the caret still ever fails to appear on a Dictate press, say so \u2014 your use is the verdict.'],t.s)
B.b1p=new A.v(1125,B.abs)
B.asP=s(['THE READER NO LONGER STOPS FOR NOTHING (phone). Your report: it went quiet for long stretches when you had not said a word. It was mis-hearing the microphone switching on: it took its idea of a "quiet room" from the first moment of the recording, before any sound had arrived, so afterwards every ordinary noise looked like you talking. It now waits until it can actually hear the room before deciding what quiet sounds like.',"AND THE READING NO LONGER TYPES ITSELF INTO YOUR ITEM (phone). Your second report, and the more serious one. The app checked for its own voice at the START of a dictation and then stopped checking - so once the reading carried on, its words went into your item like anything else you say. It now keeps subtracting its own reading for as long as the reading is playing. It only ever removes words it can prove IT was speaking at that moment; anything it is not sure about is kept, because losing your sentence is worse than leaving a stray line you can see and delete."],t.s)
B.aT0=new A.v(1098,B.asP)
B.alx=s(["TASKS SHOW THEIR TITLES. Your report: the green-headed items in the Inbox looked untitled. The titles were there all along - a task's title is its own first words, stored with the item - but the title row was built for AI-titled dictations and meetings and deliberately skipped every task. Your pick: show it. A task now wears its label as a bold title line like everything else; on a short task that means the same words appear as title and body, the trade you chose over a green bar with no title at all."],t.s)
B.b_t=new A.v(1097,B.alx)
B.ass=s(["MOVE ONLY EXISTS WHEN IT MEANS SOMETHING. Your call tonight: with an item in several folders, the Move button silently meant 'and unlink it from everything else' - one tap could drop links you meant to keep. So the button is simply gone while an item has more than one home: those items offer Link and Unlink only, and dropping a link is always the deliberate Unlink tap, named in the sheet as it happens. An item with a single home keeps Move exactly as it was - take it from here to there, nothing else touched. The sheet's little legend now matches whichever buttons are really on offer.","THE PHONE CAN NOW TELL YOU APART FROM ITSELF. Listening while dictating never worked on the phone because its speaker comes back into the microphone exactly as loudly as you do. It now listens for something else entirely: the reader stops between words, and when you talk you fill those silences. That gap is 20+ decibels wide even when the volume difference is nothing, so the reading now quietens and stops for you on the phone the way it does on Windows - and it costs nothing, needs no internet, and reacts in a fraction of a second.","On Windows nothing changes yet - the new method runs quietly alongside the old one, recording whether it would have caught the same moments, so any switch there is made on your own usage rather than a guess."],t.s)
B.aWL=new A.v(1096,B.ass)
B.aqn=s(["THE FIRST UNLINK NO LONGER JUMPS THE LIST. You caught it precisely: 'the first time I unlink in the link to a folder moves the grid, 2nd and subsequent ones do not.' Every unlink was quietly closing the item's open editor as a safety step - the first time visibly collapses the box and everything below it jumps; after that there is nothing left to close, which is why only the first one moved. That safety step exists for a real reason (an open item leaving the list used to hand its cursor to the neighbour, which then opened itself), so it was not removed - it now fires ONLY when the unlink actually takes the item off the list you are looking at: unlinking its last folder (Trash), or unlinking the very folder whose list is behind the sheet. Unlinking any OTHER folder leaves your screen exactly as it is.","AND THE SAME GUARD NOW COVERS MOVE, which had no protection at all - moving an item out of the folder you are viewing while its editor was open could hand the cursor to the neighbour. Same rule: the cursor is released only when the row really leaves the screen."],t.s)
B.b25=new A.v(1095,B.aqn)
B.asY=s(["ONE LINK SHEET, EVERY DOOR. You asked to unify the folder sheets: putting an item in a folder now opens the SAME Link sheet from every place you can start - the folder label under an item, the ... menu on the item's full page, the right-click menu, and the + on the folder chips. Same back / forward arrows through the folder's items, same Done button, same staying-open while you file, every folder named. Before, only the footer label opened the good sheet; the other three doors opened the old one-shot picker, which closed on you after every choice. The old picker keeps exactly one job, the one the Link sheet does not do: moving a FOLDER into another folder.","AND THE FOLDER LABEL CAN NO LONGER CROWD THE ROW. Follow-up to yesterday's one-line fix: on a narrow screen - the Fold closed - a long folder list under an item could still ask for more width than the screen has and shove the row's controls. It now takes only the space beside them and trims, whatever the screen size.","YOUR PHONE NOW CHECKS ITSELF BEFORE IT TRUSTS THE MICROPHONE (Android, still the experiment - off until you switch it on in Settings > Listening). Start dictating while the reading list is playing and the phone now captures through the route it uses for phone calls - the one built to cancel the phone's own speaker back out of the microphone. For the first four seconds it holds your words instead of writing them down, and reads what came back to work out whether that cancelling actually worked on YOUR phone. If it did, everything you said lands untouched and the reading gets out of your way when you talk, the way it does on Windows. If it did not, the app takes its own reading back out, keeps what YOU said, stops the playback and tells you why in the status line: a phone that cannot do this now says so, instead of quietly filling your item with the reading."],t.s)
B.aS1=new A.v(1094,B.asY)
B.as7=s(["THE LIST NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU LINK. You said it: 'it is working, but when I link the grid moves.' The folder label under an item had been allowed to wrap onto a second line so it could name every folder - which made the item grow taller the moment a link landed, pushing everything below it down. Now that label stays on one line and simply trims if it runs long, so nothing moves while you work through a folder linking items. The full list of folders is still spelled out in the Link sheet itself."],t.s)
B.aRp=new A.v(1093,B.as7)
B.acQ=s(["LINK A RUN OF ITEMS WITHOUT LEAVING THE SHEET. Open Link to a folder from an item inside a folder and you now get back / forward arrows on the item line, with 'item 3 of 12' underneath: file this one, hit next, file that one, and so on through the folder - the sheet never closes and never loses your place. Pairs with the Done button from the last build.","AND THE SELECTED ITEM IS UNMISTAKABLE AGAIN. You said the folder list looked like it was selecting several items at once - it was not: since the last build every saved item wears a light shade on its header (the type colour you asked to keep), and beside a thin halo that read as 'selected'. The selected item now wears a solid, thicker blue outline, so one glance tells you which one it is.","AND IT NAMES EVERY FOLDER AN ITEM IS IN. That line used to say 'linked to 2 folders' as soon as there was more than one; now it names them - 'in Kitchen - Sales - Tasks' - the same way the item footer does."],t.s)
B.b3e=new A.v(1092,B.acQ)
B.ahY=s(["THE ITEM HEADER STOPS MOVING WHEN YOU SELECT. Selecting an item slid its Listen, headphones and fold controls 82 pixels toward the middle - measured, not guessed: the Dictate button and the header's stretchy gap were both claiming the same free space, and the half the button did not use turned into dead space on the right. The button now takes exactly its own width, so the controls sit in the same column whether an item is selected or not.","THE LINK SHEET STAYS OPEN. Link, Unlink, Move and Create no longer close the sheet - it updates in place and tells you what it did on a line inside it, so you can put an item in three folders without reopening it three times. A Done button closes it, and tapping outside still works. The one exception: unlinking an item from its LAST folder sends it to Trash, so that still closes, and it still asks first.","AND THE LINK SHEET IS EASIER TO READ. Folder names are full size now, counts and the path line are one size up, your item's words show in normal ink instead of grey, and the buttons are bigger.","NEW FOLDERS ARRIVE AT THE TOP of the folder list instead of somewhere alphabetical. Sub-folders already did this; now the top-level ones do too.","THE LEFT PANE NO LONGER DISAPPEARS WHEN YOU CREATE A FOLDER on the Fold. The keyboard opening was making the app think the screen had become phone-sized and swap the pane away; the size is now judged without the keyboard. The app also records every pane close and layout change in its log, so if this ever happens again the log names the cause.","AN ITEM IN SEVERAL FOLDERS NAMES THEM ALL in its footer, instead of showing two and a +2."],t.s)
B.aRN=new A.v(1091,B.ahY)
B.afW=s(["THE PHONE GETS THE LISTENING-WHILE-DICTATING SWITCH - AS AN EXPERIMENT, at your own request. On Android the same Settings > Listening toggle now exists, with the honest warning attached: on the phone's speaker the app cannot hear you over the reading (your voice and the speaker arrive at the microphone equally loud), so it will not quiet down for you, and the reading's words will be typed into the item you dictate into. You said you'd test it anyway and delete the test items - so here it is, labeled. The real phone version, which measures its own hardware and refuses where it can't work, is being built behind this."],t.s)
B.b1C=new A.v(1090,B.afW)
B.aj7=s(["KEEP LISTENING WHILE YOU DICTATE (Windows, off until you switch it on in Settings > Listening). Until now, starting to talk stopped your reading list dead, and starting the reading list ended your dictation. Now they share: the moment you start talking the playback drops quiet, and if you keep going it stops properly. Eight seconds after your last word it picks back up, backing up a couple of seconds so you do not come back mid-sentence.","It quiets FIRST and stops second, on purpose - dropping the volume the instant you might be talking is what keeps your opening words, instead of them being drowned by the reader. If it mishears a loud passage it just gives the volume back and carries on; only real talking stops it.","Windows only for now. We measured it on the phone and the speaker comes back into the microphone as loudly as your own voice does - within half a decibel - so the same trick cannot tell you apart there yet. Headphones on the phone were never the problem; the speaker is.","And the player's buttons no longer end your dictation. Found on the very first trial: tapping stop on the little player counted as clicking outside the box, so it posted your note mid-thought. While this mode is on, the player's stop / play / next are part of the dictation - press them freely and keep talking. With the mode off, clicking out behaves exactly as it always has.","THE SHADING ON EXISTING ITEMS - FOR REAL THIS TIME. You asked for it this morning and again tonight: every saved item's top bar now wears a light wash, so an item reads as a card with a header instead of a white box. The morning build raised the shade on the wrong bar (the collapsed row); the open bar had been made invisible on purpose by the Design-v2 pass on 08-17, and stayed that way. The test pictures that guard the chrome were re-taken and looked at: before, every open bar was pure white; now each one is shaded. The dictation box keeps its clean look."],t.s)
B.b0f=new A.v(1089,B.aj7)
B.ao4=s(["TWO OF YOUR TWEAKS. In the Reading list, the card for the item that is playing now has its own back / pause / forward - and they only move inside THAT folder: next is the next item in this folder, previous the one before, wrapping inside the folder and never jumping to another one. The big player at the bottom still runs the whole list.","AND UNLINK. In the Link sheet, every folder an item is already in shows an Unlink button next to Linked - one tap drops that link and the line tells you what it is still in. If it was the item's only folder the sheet asks first, because removing the last link moves the item to Trash (restorable for 30 days), same rule as before."],t.s)
B.aTb=new A.v(1088,B.ao4)
B.ap2=s(["THE DATABASE FIX, THIRD CUT - MEASURED WITH THE REAL PROFILER, AND IT WAS NOT THE DATABASE OR THE MEMORY. Cut 3 ran the Dart VM's own profiler against a copy of your library while the app booted, scrolled and made items. The garbage collector: zero collections, heap never past 110 MB. The single biggest cost on the thread that paints: 39-47% of it was Windows being asked for the TIME ZONE - about 38,800 times per boot.","WHY: in this Dart version, every local date-time the app builds gets validated by computing its local calendar parts, and on Windows that asks the operating system for the zone every single time, uncached. Your library builds five timestamps per item, 7,100 items, every reseed. Nothing you did - the language does it on construction, before anything reads the value.","THE FIX: the app now keeps every item timestamp as a UTC instant (no zone query to build), and converts to your local time only where a clock is actually shown - one conversion per visible row instead of per item in the library. Ordering, 'x minutes ago', pins, trash dates and sync are unchanged; every stamp you read is the same local time as before.","THE NUMBERS, before -> after on the same scenarios: boot+scroll 2,836 -> 504 CPU samples on the UI thread (-82%); making items 4,163 -> 895 (-78%). The profile harness that found it ships in the repo (scripts/profile-pass.ps1) so the next cut starts from a measurement, not a dump."],t.s)
B.aZL=new A.v(1087,B.ap2)
B.aqa=s(["THE DATABASE FIX, SECOND CUT. After every sync the app re-checks your list against the database - 'what changed?' and 'which items should be visible?'. Both of those checks still ran on the screen's own database connection, and your log timed them at six to ten seconds whenever a sync was writing. They now run on the background worker, where waiting costs you nothing; the screen keeps painting. The safety check that forces a full reload if anything were ever missed is unchanged.","YOUR THIRTEEN TWEAKS FROM THIS MORNING, ALL IN THIS BUILD. ONE COUNT UNDER THE MAIN BOX: the second quantity label is gone; the count sits on the left with the status to its right, as you asked.","THE TOP BARS ARE VISIBLY SHADED NOW. Existing items' bars were tinted so faintly they read as white on your screen; notes, tasks and meetings are about two and a half times stronger, and Claude items stay the strongest, as the v810 rule says.","'DICTATE HERE' IS NOW 'DICTATE' - the whole word, never clipped - and THE PIN MOVED OFF THE BAR into the full-size view's ... menu as 'Pin to top' / 'Unpin' (it pins in the view you came from, exactly as the bar control did).","THE LEFT PANE: the Inbox is no longer anchored above the list - it scrolls with your folders; the Inbox icon is a tray; every count is the same size and weight, and the Inbox and Reading list rows match.","THE LINK SHEET: two lines of the item's words under its name; a NEW FOLDER row (made where you are browsing, then linked at once); and the order is yours - Pane order (the default, the same order as the left pane), A-Z, or Recent (the folders you linked into most recently). Your choice is remembered on every device; 'newest linked first' is no longer forced on you.","THE FOOTER NAMES BOTH FOLDERS when an item is linked to two ('Tasks \xb7 Health'), and '+N' only past two - you asked to see multi-folder items at a glance.","THE WEIRD TEXT IS FIXED. The small a with a hat and the backwards c were double-encoded dashes and quotes - over a thousand of them, in Settings' voice commands and eleven other places, since a July build. Every one is the dash or quote it was meant to be.","THE FULL-SIZE ITEM: the small 'dictate' pill under the title and the '+ Folder' chip are gone; the ... menu keeps Add to folder / Move to folder, and the page's own Dictate control is the one that stays.","THE CANVAS FOLDER IS GONE and cannot come back on its own: the parked Canvas feature no longer creates it. Its two items keep their other folders, and its 'Group' sub-folder now sits at the root.","THE DUPLICATE 'I do think right now' was two real rows - your dictation, and a task your PHONE created from it a minute later (it arrived here by sync). The task is in the Trash on your word; the phone's own log will name which door made it, next time it is plugged in."],t.s)
B.aYh=new A.v(1086,B.aqa)
B.ajV=s(["THE DATABASE FIX, FIRST CUT - the waits behind 'slow', 'pauses' and 'not working'. Your log named one statement behind every long wait this week: the Projects list counting every one of your 7,800 items, re-run on the screen's own database connection every time any item changed, and kept alive by every box's footer. It no longer runs there: the footers and pickers read a cheap projects-only list, and the counting query runs only while the Projects screen is open.","NO MORE FULL RELOADS AFTER AN EDIT. After a title change, a move, a stop or every sync, the app used to throw away its live library feed and re-read all 4,800 rows. It now asks 'what changed?' - one small delta - in all nine of those places. The folder tree reads its placements from the same background feed instead of running its own 8,500-row query.","HONEST MEASUREMENT: this is the first cut of a structural fix, judged by your stall log over the next days - the freezes should get rarer and shorter; if they do not, the next cut is already named. Also fixed: the test harness wrote into the same diagnostics file as your real app on this PC and wiped four hours of it; it has its own folder now, and the log rotates instead of erasing."],t.s)
B.b1B=new A.v(1085,B.ajV)
B.aqH=s(["THE LEFT PANE SHOWS WHAT IS DICTATING - AND STOPS IT. The Dictate now button becomes the live indicator: 'Dictating - Inbox \xb7 0:42' when the main box is taking your words, 'Dictating - <item> \xb7 0:42' when a dictate-here button is, ticking as you speak. Tap it to stop. While the words are still being finished it says so, and once idle it is the Dictate now button again.","THE INBOX IS NOT BLUE ANYMORE. A selected row in the left pane is bold on a quiet grey, not accent-blue - the Inbox is selected whenever you are home, so it looked permanently lit. Blue still marks a drop target while you drag.","READING LIST, REARRANGED THE WAY YOU ASKED. The folder icon sits on the name's line; the folder's total time follows the name in parentheses - 'Meetings (12:30)'; the state line below keeps the counts only. The item card shows the title with its time to the right and up to three lines of the words beneath. Tapping the item opens it full size where you can edit the text and delete it; playback carries on.","THE ELAPSED TIME WORKS FOR THE COMPUTER VOICE NOW. Only recordings reported their position; items read aloud by the voice sat at 0:00. The reader keeps its own clock per item (pauses subtracted), so the card and the bottom player move for both.","WHEN YOU PLAY ONE FOLDER, THERE IS A WAY BACK. The bottom player names the folder it is confined to and offers 'Play all' to return to the whole reading list.","STOPPING TO DICTATE REMEMBERS YOUR PLACE. When a recording starts, the reader stops (it must - the mic needs the audio path). Until now that stop did not save where you were, so after a restart or on another device you resumed at your last deliberate pause instead. Found by the other session reading the code; fixed here.","THE READER NOW WRITES TO THE LOG - when each item starts, which engine read it and how long it took to begin, and when it stopped. Your 'it pauses for long periods' report could not be timed before; next time it can."],t.s)
B.b3v=new A.v(1084,B.aqH)
B.asg=s(["THREE TWEAKS TO THE LEFT PANE AND THE READING LIST, FROM YOUR NOTES. The Play all button under Reading list lost its blue fill - it is an outlined pill now. Dictate now is a real button of the same shape, indented under Inbox, and that group has its own CAPTURE header like Listen and Folders already had.","TAPPING A FOLDER IN THE READING LIST NOW OPENS THAT FOLDER - just its contents, every item in it, including the ones not marked to be read, each with its own headphones. The 'Not in a folder' card still opens the play list, since those items have no folder to open.","THE READING LIST COUNTS INSIDE THE FOLDER NOW. The folder that is playing says 'reading 2 of 5' - and the 5 is how many of THAT folder's items are in your list, not the whole list. The item card under it shows only its own progress (3:07 of 24:31); the old 'item 1 of 4' that counted the entire list is gone, and Resume names the folder and the place in it.","THE LINK SHEET BEHIND A BOX'S FOLDER LINK SAYS WHAT IT DOES. It is 'Link to a folder' now: every row has Link (adds that folder and keeps the others) and Move (makes it the only folder). There is no 'home' anywhere - all links are equal, and a folder the item is already in is simply marked Linked. The Inbox is a row like any other folder, with both buttons, at the top of the list.","SEARCH IN THAT SHEET. Type a few letters and every matching folder at any level appears with its path, the ones you most recently linked something into first. The browse list is in that same order - the folders you file into most recently rise to the top.","TAPPING THE PLAYING ITEM IN THE LEFT PANE OPENS THE READING LIST now, not the full-size Now Playing view - the list is where you see the folder and the item together; Now Playing is one tap further from there.","BIG FOLDERS SCROLL LIKE LISTS NOW. A folder showing 300 or more items (your Backlog has 3,637) draws each item as one compact line - the grip, the type bar, the title and the time - instead of a full box. Compact lines are all the same height, so the scrollbar is exact and a fling costs nothing; the full box is one tap away (the item opens full-size), and a 'Show full boxes' button above the count flips the whole folder back - per folder, remembered on every device. Drag-to-reorder and hold-to-file work exactly as before.","AND THE LIST HOLDS STILL WHILE YOU SCROLL. Until now a sync tick or a refresh arriving mid-scroll could rebuild a big folder under your thumb. While you are scrolling, the folder now keeps showing the list as it was when you started, and adopts whatever changed about half a second after you stop. Nothing about your data waits - only the drawing does.","AN EMPTY CAPTURE SAYS SO. An item with no words and no recording - a box opened and closed in a few seconds - now reads 'Nothing captured \xb7 3 s' instead of a blank line. You have 31 of these. An item that has audio but no words yet still shows as transcribing.","YOUR OFFICE COMPUTER'S EMPTY LIBRARY, FIXED AT THE ROOT. On 1082 it showed no data while signed in and 'synced', and its log said why every ten seconds: it kept trying to open the OLD copy of your library inside OneDrive - a copy it could not read - because an earlier fix (1079) taught the app that 'could not read it right now' means 'someone else is using it, open it where it lives'. That rule was right for your main machine's live library and wrong for a dead OneDrive copy, so the office machine could never start. Now only a file that is genuinely HELD by another program makes the app wait; any other file it cannot read is left exactly where it is, the app starts with a fresh local library, and the server fills it back up. Nothing is deleted or moved.","THE '2 COPIES OF A FILE' MESSAGE IS ONEDRIVE, AND IT STOPS. Every computer wrote its diagnostics into the same file in your OneDrive Documents folder, so OneDrive kept forking it on each conflict - 1,136 copies so far - and prompting you. Each machine now writes its own file (named after the machine), so there is nothing to conflict over. Your phones are unaffected.","NO MORE TYPE LABELS, ANYWHERE. The type button in the dictation box, the type badge on an open box and the type switch in the item sheet are gone, and a box with no destination shows no 'Dictation' or 'Task' word in its footer. Nothing underneath changed: items keep their kind for routing, the colored bar still carries it, Claude items still show their status, and the + menu still creates what it always did.","EDIT FROM THE FULL PAGE - BOTH OF THEM. The item page has a pencil at the top right that puts the cursor in the text (the text was always editable there, but nothing said so). Now Playing has the same pencil: it opens the item you are hearing in that editable page while playback keeps going.","YOUR OLD TITLES GET DONE, QUIETLY. 3,921 items still carry the first sixty characters of their words as their title because the title call never finished the first time. Each time the app sits idle it now titles up to 400 of them - a pause between each, in batches so the list does not churn, stopping the instant you start dictating - and never touches a title you typed or any of your words. A few launches and the backlog is titled.","MOVING A FOLDER TO THE TOP LEVEL NOW PUTS IT FIRST, so you can see it land. Before, it slid into the alphabetical middle of your top level and was easy to take for a failed move - your 'Dwan this year' move this afternoon did work; it was sitting fourth from the top. A drop that genuinely fails now says so instead of staying silent."],t.s)
B.aZV=new A.v(1083,B.asg)
B.ahd=s(["YOUR READING LIST, REBUILT THE WAY YOU DREW IT. The Listening screen is now the Reading list: one card per folder that has something in your list, in your play order, and under the folder that is playing, the one item that is playing - nothing else. As playback moves from item to item and folder to folder, that card moves with it and the screen glides to keep it about a third of the way down. When nothing is playing it still shows you where you are: the item you paused on, the one it would resume at, or the first one up.","THE RULE FOR WHAT COUNTS IS NOW ABOUT ITEMS, NOT FOLDERS. A folder appears in your reading list because it holds an item you added - you no longer have to toggle the folder itself. Flag one item by hand inside any folder and that folder shows up with the item under it, instead of the item falling into a loose pile at the bottom.","RUN TIMES EVERYWHERE. The summary line says how many folders, how many items and how long - tap it for the breakdown. Every folder card says how many of its items are in the list and how long they run. Play all says how long the whole list is, and if you stopped part-way it offers to Resume from where you were (hold it to start over). While playing, the bottom card shows progress with time elapsed and time left - tap or drag the bar to move within the item - and previous, play/pause and next. Hold play/pause to stop.","TAP A FOLDER CARD to open the Play list: every folder in your reading list with all of its items, opened at the folder you tapped. The little play circle on a card plays just that folder; the headphones take the whole folder out of your list (with a six-second Undo) or add the rest of it in. The left pane's row is named Reading list and has its own small Play all button while nothing is playing.","NOW PLAYING FOLLOWS THE VOICE SENTENCE BY SENTENCE - AND YOU CAN TAP ANY WORD TO JUMP THERE. Sentences you have heard fade back, the one being read is highlighted with the spoken word marked inside it, and a tap on any word moves playback to it (for an item read by the computer voice, the voice starts again at that sentence). The screen only scrolls when the current sentence has left the view, and it leaves you alone for six seconds after you touch it. The controls sit above the text now; hold play/pause to stop."],t.s)
B.aSz=new A.v(1082,B.ahd)
B.app=s(["SORTING AN ITEM NOW CLEARS IT OUT OF YOUR INBOX. Drag an item from your Home list into a folder and it LEAVES the Inbox - it lands in the folder and nothing else. Until now it stayed in both places, so sorting never actually emptied the Inbox and you had to remove each item a second time.","This is a change to what you decided on 08-18, at your word. If you ever want an item in a folder AND still sitting in your Inbox, hold Ctrl while you drag - that still links it into the folder and keeps the Inbox copy, the same Ctrl rule Windows uses.","The Move button already behaved this way, so it is unchanged. Two things are careful here: an item that lives in several folders only loses its Inbox spot - the folders you chose yourself are untouched - and moving can never send anything to the Trash, because the item is re-homed in one step instead of being unfiled first.","THE SAME RULE NOW APPLIES FROM SEARCH AND FROM YOUR LISTENING LIST. Filing an item from either place clears it out of the Inbox too. One thing is NOT done yet and you should know it: an item you have flagged for listening still stays in the listening list after you file it, because filing does not clear the listening flag. That half is waiting on your decision."],t.s)
B.aRy=new A.v(1081,B.app)
B.ajf=s(["A BUG THAT WOULD HAVE MADE YOUR APP SLOW FOREVER, DEFUSED BEFORE IT FIRED. Your app keeps a running check that the list on your screen still matches what is in the database - a safety net so an item can never quietly go missing. That check compared two counts that were computed by two different rules, and the rules disagreed about one thing: a recording whose text is nothing but a blank line.","If one of those had ever landed - and it only takes a segment where the only thing you said was 'new paragraph' - the two counts could never agree again. The safety net would have fired on every single sync, and every time it fired the app would re-read your whole library, all 4,800 items, instead of just the handful that changed. Your app would have got permanently slower with no visible cause, and nothing in the app would have told you why.","You had ZERO of these, so nothing was broken - this is a fix for the day it would have happened. The two rules are now one rule, in one place, with tests that pin the exact case. Nothing about your items, your words, or your screen changes."],t.s)
B.b44=new A.v(1080,B.ajf)
B.agA=s(["TONIGHT'S 11-MINUTE FREEZE, ANSWERED AT BOTH ENDS. Every freeze this evening happened while the app was checking for updates against a swamped OneDrive - and our own freeze-detector was making the swamp worse by writing its 'still frozen' note to a OneDrive-synced file every 5 seconds, so OneDrive re-uploaded it all freeze long (your tray literally said 'updating stall log'). Both ends are fixed.","THE UPDATE CHECK CAN'T HOLD THE APP ANYMORE: it now gets 5 seconds, then answers 'no update this time' and tries again on the next cycle - a busy OneDrive costs you one skipped check instead of a frozen window. The app also asks Windows to keep its update folder always-on-this-device, so future checks read from disk instead of waiting on the cloud.","THE FREEZE DETECTOR WRITES LOCALLY NOW and sends only a short summary per episode to the shared log - it can never again feed the problem it is measuring. Your dictate button stays as fast as the Fold showed tonight; this build is about the Windows window never going 'Not Responding' while it checks for updates."],t.s)
B.b20=new A.v(1079,B.agA)
B.aii=s(["YOUR DICTATE BUTTON, FAST AGAIN. The pre-opened speech connection (built so a press wouldn't wait on the network) turned out to have handed over its connection ZERO times since it shipped - your own log proved it. The cause was bookkeeping: after one 10-minute quiet spell it marked itself stale, threw everything away, and then refused every later press forever while never rebuilding - so every press paid the full 1-12 second cold connect you have been feeling.","Fixed three ways: an empty pool now rebuilds itself with the right settings instead of refusing forever; a long dictation session no longer makes its own connection count as stale; and a second press can't kill a rebuild already in progress. The first press after a long idle still pays an honest half-second refresh - every press after that should find the connection already open. The proof will be in your own log: 'warm_taken' lines, which have never appeared before this build."],t.s)
B.b0F=new A.v(1078,B.aii)
B.aij=s(["YOUR APP STOPS RE-READING YOUR WHOLE LIBRARY EVERY TIME ANYTHING CHANGES. Until now, each time sync said 'something changed', the app re-read and re-decoded all 4,869 of your items - on the same thread that draws your screen, dozens of times during a sync burst. That is what your longest freezes were. Now a worker on its own lane answers a much smaller question - what changed since a moment ago? - and your screen updates just those items. A quiet moment costs nothing at all.","Deleted items were the trap worth naming: a 'what changed and is still here' answer can never mention something that was removed, so the worker reports removals separately - your list drops them instead of showing things that no longer exist. Five tests pin that, including the one that deletes a row the way sync does.","If any of it ever has trouble, the app silently falls back to the old, proven way of reading and writes a note in its log - a slow list is a bad day, a wrong list is a lost item. The number that judges this build is in your own nightly scorecard: library refresh, which has been measuring 64 SECONDS, has to come in under a quarter second. You will get that number either way."],t.s)
B.b_n=new A.v(1077,B.aij)
B.acS=s(["THE STRUCTURAL WORK BEGINS - and its first piece is already in your hands. The rework that moves your library's heavy lifting off the drawing thread starts with a foundation piece: your database now keeps an index of WHAT CHANGED. Measured on your own 818 MB library before anything was built on it: asking 'what changed since a moment ago?' cost 91 milliseconds and made the app read past thousands of rows to find a handful; with the index the same question answers in under a millisecond, and the database's own plan confirms it looks the answer up instead of hunting for it.","Why this first, alone: it is small, it is reversible, and it makes even today's un-reworked code cheaper - your app asks that question constantly. The bigger pieces (the worker answering with only what changed, rows arriving ready to draw) build on this one and come next, each measured before it is wired.","YOUR FOLDER RULES ARE COMPLETE (step 5, the last one). Removing an item from its LAST folder now sends it to the Trash - exactly as you decided - instead of quietly dropping it back on Home. The confirmation says which one happened ('moved to Trash, restorable for 30 days' vs 'removed - recording kept'), so you always know where the item went.","DELETING A FOLDER FOLLOWS THE SAME RULE NOW: items that lived only in that folder go to the Trash - the warning dialog already told you the exact count before you tapped, and now says 'go to Trash' because that is what happens. Items that also live in other folders just lose that one link, exactly as before. Everything in the Trash stays restorable for 30 days; nothing is ever silently gone.","THE TRASH GOT SMARTER FOR BIG DELETES: a deleted folder's items show as ONE row - like '3,643 items from Backlog, 30 days left' - instead of thousands of rows to scroll past. Tap the row to open it and restore anything individually. And RESTORE now puts an item back into the folder you removed it from, not just anywhere.","MOVING TO THE INBOX IS SAFE AND REAL: dragging an item onto the Inbox (or 'Move to root') now actually files it in the Inbox folder first and then removes its other homes - an item can never fall through the cracks in between."],t.s)
B.aXl=new A.v(1076,B.acS)
B.acM=s(["YOUR 27-MINUTE FREEZE TONIGHT, ANSWERED. The meter shipped this afternoon billed tonight's monsters precisely: during sync bursts the app re-asked the database for your full 8,543-row folder-membership list on EVERY incoming change, dozens of times back to back, your screen waiting behind the sync lock for each one. A burst now collapses into ONE re-query of the final state; a lone change still lands within half a second. Five tests pin the collapse rules; the full suite ran green on this exact code.","Scoreboard, honestly: the per-box scan and the timezone walk are dead (verified in tonight's freeze photos). This build relieves the re-query storms. What remains - memory churn and the sync lock itself at your library's size - shares one cure: the structural rework, priced and waiting on your word.","ALSO ABOARD - FOLDER DELETE NOW WARNS WITH REAL NUMBERS (your decision #2). Deleting a folder first tells you exactly what will happen: how many items live only there (they return to Home - nothing is ever silently lost), how many also live in other folders and simply stay there, and how many sub-folders move up. And an item that lives in several folders no longer gets re-homed when one of them is deleted - it just loses that one link, keeping the homes you chose. This is the warning the Backlog delete never gave you."],t.s)
B.aSu=new A.v(1075,B.acM)
B.afQ=s(["YOUR INBOX IS A REAL FOLDER NOW - the notes v1073 owed you. An Inbox folder appears in your folder pane with your first capture, and the main window IS that folder: new dictations, notes, tasks and meetings land in it the moment they are created. Nothing was moved, renamed or lost - every item is exactly where it was, and an item that belongs to no folder at all still shows on Home.","SORTING WORKS THE WAY YOU DECIDED: dragging an item into a folder LINKS it there and keeps it in the Inbox too, until you remove it from the Inbox on purpose - the box's delete button does exactly that ('Removed from Inbox - kept in its folder', with Undo). 'Move to Folder' still relocates in one step. An item can live in the Inbox and any number of folders at once.","CLAUDE ITEMS: one you create rides Home through its whole round-trip - sent, received, done - until you remove it. Older Claude items that only ever lived in the archive now show in the Claude folder instead of Home, per your no-special-Claude-rules decision."],t.s)
B.b1k=new A.v(1074,B.afQ)
B.ahj=s(["THE FREEZE HAS A NAME, PHOTOGRAPHED AND SIGNED. Today's fifty freeze photographs were resolved against the engine's own symbol files, and the frozen thread was standing in the same place in every one: Windows' TIMEZONE machinery. Every time-of-day your screen draws walks a lock-taking, registry-touching path - and your screen draws a LOT of times: every box's stamp, every clock tick, every rebuild wave. Enough waves at your library's size and the drawing thread stands in line behind its own clock.","THE FIX: one shared clock. The app now asks Windows for the time at most three times a second TOTAL, instead of once per box per redraw - your boxes' stamps and clocks read the shared answer. Nothing you see changes; the line behind the clock is gone.","AND THE METER'S BLIND SPOT IS CLOSED: freezes that used to be billed to 'idle' now name themselves - database waits say db, so if anything still freezes after this build, its receipt names the culprit instead of shrugging."],t.s)
B.aTB=new A.v(1073,B.ahj)
B.arY=s(["THIS MORNING'S FREEZE LOOP, CUT WHERE THE EVIDENCE POINTS. Since 11:54 your window hung over and over (the camera photographed fifteen-plus episodes, 5 seconds to 7.5 minutes), and every restart re-entered the storm. What this build fixes, measured: v1070's folder chip re-derived its folder names against your ENTIRE membership list (8,543 rows) inside every visible box on every sync wave - that churn is gone; the lookup now computes once per wave and an untouched box doesn't even redraw.","Said honestly: your stall meter billed the LONGEST hangs to a wait the app's own probes cannot yet name - this build removes the proven churn, and the freeze camera will tell us within a day whether a deeper wait remains. The dumps are saved either way; naming that wait is the next hunt if it survives this build.","Also in this build's tooling: the test harness can no longer sync-pollute your real library (137 stray 'Area C' test rows over the week - say the word and they're deleted server-side)."],t.s)
B.aS0=new A.v(1072,B.arY)
B.aqN=s(["YOUR ONE-TAP ANSWER BUTTONS WORK. When I ask you something with choices, they arrive as buttons you tap - and they never once have. Every question I have ever sent you with options wrote its marker wrong, so you got raw text like 'u{27E6}options:...' stuck on the end of the question and no buttons at all. Twelve of them, across ten of your items, going back to the day the feature shipped. Fixed at the source, and the ones already sitting on your phone now show their buttons too.","The cause, in case it saves you wondering: the script that sends them used a shortcut that only works in a newer PowerShell than this machine runs. The old one quietly dropped it and wrote the shortcut itself as text. It looked correct in the code, which is why it survived so long.","MY ANSWERS NO LONGER FAIL ON A STRAY CHARACTER. Posting an answer containing an em-dash or a curly quote could fail outright with a confusing error we had been blaming on message length. Every write now goes out properly encoded, so an apostrophe in the wrong shape can't lose you an answer.","AND A GUARD BEFORE THE NEW FOLDER RULES: with Claude's folders about to become ordinary folders, the desk now refuses to remove an item's last folder rather than letting it fall into the Trash unseen."],t.s)
B.aWb=new A.v(1071,B.aqN)
B.aqc=s(["YOUR DESIGN IS COMPLETE - all twelve screens of 'Voicley Current App' are now the app. This build lands the last five surfaces:","THE QUICK-PEEK SHEET is each item's control room now, exactly as you drew it: the type badge lives in its header (tap it there to change the type - it left the bar), the item number, 'Copy all', the player, all inside the type-tinted border.","NOW PLAYING gets its chrome: 'Playing x of x' centered up top, and a real progress bar - elapsed on the left, time remaining on the right - under the transcript that follows the voice.","THE LISTENING SCREEN wears your design's face: the soft grey ground, your flagged items in a white card titled 'Flagged to be read aloud', and the breakdown line now opens THE PROGRAM TREE - your design's new screen showing every folder linked into listening, whole-folder vs some-flagged at a glance, with play buttons on every row and the currently-playing item shown nested inside its folder.","FOLDERS everywhere gained the play button beside their headphones - the whole folder, one tap, from the Folders screen or the left pane.","And every box's bar is finished to the drawing: the invisible handle strip, the neutral grip, play-this-item, the folder chip. The full design walkthrough is ready when you are."],t.s)
B.b06=new A.v(1070,B.aqc)
B.ajC=s(["THE GREY WASH - your design's biggest visual signature lands. The working area now sits on the soft grey your drawing uses, and every white card pops against it exactly like the prototype.","EVERY BOX KNOWS ITS HOME. The footer carries your design's folder chip: the item's folder name in blue ('Work +2' when it lives in several, 'Move to a folder' when unfiled). Tap it and your MOVE/LINK sheet opens - the 'All folders' screen from your design, with its own rule printed on it: Move gives it one home, the link button adds a home and keeps the others. Browse into folders, file directly, or move out of every folder - all on the same sync-safe filing machinery the app has always used."],t.s)
B.aSB=new A.v(1069,B.ajC)
B.aoo=s(["YOUR DESIGN'S DRAWER, EXACTLY AS YOU DREW IT. Capture (Inbox + Dictate now), Listen ('Everything flagged' with its count and the player card - title opens Now Playing, red stop, elapsed/total clock), then Folders - every folder row now carries a PLAY button (plays the whole folder) beside its headphones - then Search. The pane's type rows and the pinned Claude row are retired per your design: types live as Inbox filters, Claude is its folder row.","THE BOX RESTYLE HAS BEGUN: the type-tinted banner is gone from open boxes - the handle strip is invisible now, exactly as your design draws it, with a neutral grip. Every box gains a PLAY button beside its headphones that plays just that item. The rest of the box's new anatomy (folder chip, Move/Link sheet, controls moving to the peek sheet) lands in the next builds today."],t.s)
B.aUI=new A.v(1068,B.aoo)
B.ahy=s(["THE TRANSCRIPT FOLLOWS THE VOICE. In Now Playing, every word lights up as it's spoken and the view scrolls to keep it in front of you - and the moment you scroll by hand, the following stops until the next item, because your hand always wins. From your own design session, built exactly to its rules. (Timing is estimated for now; word-perfect timestamps slot in later without changing anything you see.)"],t.s)
B.aUQ=new A.v(1067,B.ahy)
B.aqG=s(["YOUR LISTENING PROGRAM LIVES IN THE LEFT PANE NOW. The Listening row shows how many items you've flagged with the headphones, and while the Reader plays, a mini-player sits right in the pane - what's playing, previous/play/next/stop, and where you are in the queue - so you can control listening from anywhere in the app."],t.s)
B.aWR=new A.v(1066,B.aqG)
B.abY=s(["DICTATE NOW, FROM ANYWHERE. Your new design's dictate button sits in the left pane under Inbox - one tap from any screen takes you Home with the mic starting, running the exact same rules as the main button. The pane also gets its LISTEN section, and Settings gains your design's 'Show item numbers' switch (numbers stay on until you turn them off; full views always keep them - that's the address you read me when something's wrong)."],t.s)
B.aUU=new A.v(1065,B.abY)
B.aiT=s(["BOTH OF YOUR HELD TWEAKS: selecting ANY part of an item now shows its 'Dictate here' button (not just clicking into its text), and an item taking dictation shows the ticking elapsed clock beside its mic pill - same honest clock as the main box, counting this dictation only.","AND YOUR BUTTON-FEEL REPORT NOW MEASURES ITSELF: every press logs how long the app took to visibly react, split into the two halves that matter. Your normal use this week IS the diagnosis - no more guessing at what 'still feels tied to something' means."],t.s)
B.aWM=new A.v(1064,B.aiT)
B.amb=s(["CLICKING 'DICTATE HERE' NO LONGER NUDGES YOUR TEXT. The movement you reported was exactly three pixels of padding that changed when a box armed for dictation. One constant padding now - nothing you click can move what you're reading.","THE RED-AND-BLUE BORDER IS GONE. Two frames were claiming the same box: the blue selection ring kept painting while the red recording frame was live. One owner now - while a box is taking your words, the red frame IS the selection signal; blue returns the moment recording ends. Red only ever means recording, blue only ever means selected."],t.s)
B.aZ4=new A.v(1063,B.amb)
B.aj8=s(["THE LIBRARY MOVES OFF THE DRAWING THREAD - the structural fix you asked for, landed. Your full library read now runs on a dedicated worker: the query, the text decoding, all of it happens off to the side, and the screen receives ready-made rows in fractions of a millisecond each. Measured on your own library before it was wired: 173 milliseconds for all 4,763 items, against the 1-3 seconds the old path took ON the drawing thread. Clicking between items and scrolling during list refreshes is where you'll feel it.","This is the second attempt - the first failed its own tests two nights ago and was reverted before reaching you. This one passed every tier clean, first try, because the failure taught us the real rule: heavy machinery exists in your app, never in the test bed. If the worker ever hits trouble on your machine, it falls back to the proven sliced read automatically and says so in the log."],t.s)
B.aZM=new A.v(1062,B.aj8)
B.asa=s(["THE DICTATE PRESS GETS ITS INSTANT BACK. The warm connection was sometimes a zombie - alive on this side, dead on Deepgram's - and pressing Dictate burned seconds discovering it. The connection now carries a system-level heartbeat that detects a dead line within seconds (immune to app stalls, which were what killed the old keepalives), plus an age limit so a stale connection is refused up front. A genuinely warm press measured 6 milliseconds.","DELETING THE ITEM YOU'RE DICTATING INTO now resets the target properly - no more banner claiming it's dictating into an item that no longer exists. Your words were never at risk; the label was lying."],t.s)
B.aZo=new A.v(1061,B.asa)
B.atH=s(["THE SETTINGS LIBRARY THAT FROZE YOUR TOGGLES IS GONE. Every settings save used to rewrite the whole settings file synchronously on the drawing thread - through a stock library whose own source showed it. Voicley now uses its own settings writer: instant in memory, saved to disk quietly off to the side, crash-safe (an interrupted save leaves the previous complete file, never a torn one), and reading the exact same file as before. Rapid dictate toggling no longer touches the disk at all in the moment.","Overnight engineering note, in the interest of the honesty you've been owed all week: the first attempt at moving the library read to a separate worker measured SLOWER than the current sliced read (8 seconds vs 1-3) and broke a pinned rule in testing - so it was reverted by its own rules before it ever reached you, and the proven sliced read stays. The worker returns only with a design that beats the numbers. That is the system working: bad ideas now die in the test bed instead of on your screen.","A build-failing test now guards the new settings writer's installation, so it can never silently unwire."],t.s)
B.aRV=new A.v(1060,B.atH)
B.aee=s(["FEWER STALLS ON RAPID DICTATE TOGGLING. Every posted note was rewriting your entire settings file - synchronously, on the drawing thread, through the settings library itself (its own source code shows it). That write is now batched a few seconds behind, so toggling dictation quickly no longer fires a burst of them. Your words were never involved - this was bookkeeping.","The freeze camera now takes TWO photographs of any freeze lasting past 30 seconds - comparing them reveals whether it's one stuck loop or a storm passing through phases. Last night's unexplained 2.5-minute spin gets named the next time it dares show up.","This is the last patch-style build: the structural fix - moving all file and data work off the drawing thread entirely - is written up, priced, and starts on the owner's go."],t.s)
B.b0B=new A.v(1059,B.aee)
B.ah7=s(["THE LIST RE-READ BREATHES. The last known freeze type was the app decoding your entire library's text in one gulp on the drawing thread - your 15-to-40-second lockups, photographed twice. That read now works in small slices with the screen given a turn between each one: same data, same order, but a stall physically can't exceed a fraction of a second. The log counts the slices so the first launch proves it with numbers.","Two smaller holes from the same photographs: the diagnostics journal no longer rewrites itself on the drawing thread at launch, and a file handle abandoned by a failed recording start can no longer be closed by the memory janitor mid-paint (that was yesterday's 3-minute freeze).","With this build, every freeze cause we have photographic evidence for is fixed. If anything still freezes, it photographs itself and bills the exact function - and that evidence goes straight into the structural rework (the data worker) we've committed to next."],t.s)
B.b1e=new A.v(1058,B.ah7)
B.am_=s(["THE LAUNCH FREEZE'S LAST HOLDOUTS ARE OUT. Your very first freeze on v1056 - 26 seconds, right at launch - was photographed and it named the survivors: disk checks I had skipped as 'boot-only', still running on the drawing thread after the screen was up, including one loop that checked every recording's file at startup, one by one, across your whole library. Every remaining check now waits off to the side. The only two left in the entire app are inside code that deliberately runs on its own worker.","One known freeze type remains - the list re-read that decodes all four-thousand-plus items' full text at once. That fix is tomorrow's build, it's designed, and your log bills every occurrence in the meantime."],t.s)
B.aZU=new A.v(1057,B.am_)
B.atg=s(["THE IDLE FREEZE'S CAUSE IS REMOVED - the first of your two freeze types, fixed at every site it lived in. Your 42-second freeze this afternoon was photographed checking files on disk on the same thread that draws the screen; the app does that in background sweeps (audio cleanup, upload checks), and one slow disk answer froze everything you saw. Every one of those checks now runs politely off to the side - including one hiding in the item detail view that ran on every open. A test now fails any build where a new one sneaks in.","Still open and next: the freeze type that hits WHILE dictating (the app reprocesses the whole transcript on every partial result - grows with session length), and a rare case where clicking an item then the main box leaves the main box needing a second click under heavy load. Both are named, both have evidence on file, both are the next build's work."],t.s)
B.aVS=new A.v(1056,B.atg)
B.aoH=s(["EVERY PAUSE NOW SIGNS ITS WORK. Yesterday's freeze photograph proved the pauses are the app building text furiously on the thread that draws the screen. This build puts a meter on each of the four operations capable of that - the transcript assembler, the draft mirror, the launch-time draft scan, and the live dictation writer - and when the app next stalls, the log states which one ran and exactly how long it took, even if the app was completely frozen at the time (the meter's receipt survives where a live report cannot). Nothing changes in how anything works or feels; this is the build that makes the pause fix a one-line diagnosis instead of a search."],t.s)
B.aSA=new A.v(1055,B.aoH)
B.adW=s(["YOUR LATEST CLICK OWNS THE CURSOR. The 'I click an item and the focus jumps back up' bug - your most-reported problem - was finally caught in the act: 25 recorded cases from your own machine, in two shapes. When the app was busy, the fallout of your click arrived 15+ seconds late and the main box decided nobody owned the cursor and took it. And when you clicked the main box then an item within a second, your OLDER click outranked your newer one. Both were the same mistake: the rule kept time windows where it should have kept ORDER. Now: whatever you clicked LAST owns the cursor and everything that follows it, at any delay. Every one of those 25 recorded cases is now a permanent test.","YOUR RECORDINGS' AUDIO STOPS MISSING THE CLOUD. Every dictation's sound file was being grabbed by the uploader about a second and a half before the recorder let go of it - 19 recorded failures in two days. Worse, the fallback could upload a file still being written: cut-off audio, marked as done. The uploader now leaves any file alone for 20 seconds after its last write; the next pass 40 seconds later takes it cleanly. Your words were never at risk - this was the sound copies.","And the freeze hunt scored twice today: the freeze camera photographed a real 11-second freeze mid-test (the frozen thread is parked in a Windows wait inside Flutter's engine - the first hard evidence of WHERE), and your 12.7-second freeze this morning reconstructed completely from the journals: one click on an item row, then frozen within 2 seconds. The next freeze on your machine gives the final answer."],t.s)
B.b_4=new A.v(1054,B.adW)
B.aeb=s(["THE FREEZE-AT-LAUNCH SUSPECT IS FIXED. Yesterday's 13-second freeze right after an update pointed at one line: the folder your diagnostics live in sits inside OneDrive, and the app was checking it in a way that could wait on OneDrive - on the thread that draws the screen, at every launch, first thing. On a morning when OneDrive was hours behind and churning, that check logged at 70 seconds instead of instantly. It now waits off to the side like everything else. Same disease as the update check fixed yesterday - third place it was hiding, and a test now fails the build if it appears anywhere a fourth time.","WHEN THE APP IS DRINKING MISSED SYNC, IT SAYS SO. The first launch after an update catches up on everything it missed, and that wait used to look exactly like a dead app. The status row now reads 'Catching up on sync...' while it is actually downloading - a named wait instead of a mystery.","Straight talk: this build shipped on your say-so with one real-app test red - the 'one click gets into the box' scenario, which is the OLD one-click bug's family, not something these changes touched. It failed while your live app was open next to the test app during the run. Chasing that bug is next, and for the first time there may be a way to reproduce it on demand."],t.s)
B.aUe=new A.v(1052,B.aeb)
B.aez=s(["WHEN THE APP FREEZES, IT NOW SAYS WHAT IT WAS DOING. The freeze reporter that shipped in v1048 worked - it recorded 63 freezes yesterday, including one that lasted 15 minutes - but every single one was labeled 'boot', because the part that was supposed to note what the app was doing was never connected to anything. Now it is: the next freeze will say 'recording' or 'updating the list' or 'checking for updates' instead of a word that told us nothing. This build does not fix the freeze - it makes the next one leave a name behind, which is what has been missing all week.","The check for new versions can no longer hold up the app. It was reading files out of the OneDrive folder in a way that could wait forever on the thread that draws the screen - at every launch, every ten minutes, and every time you clicked back in. It now waits politely off to the side. Honest note: your freeze happened once tonight with OneDrive completely off, so OneDrive is NOT the cause of the freeze - this is repaired because it was wrong, not because it is the answer.","A test now fails the build if the freeze reporter's name-tags ever come unplugged again."],t.s)
B.aXv=new A.v(1051,B.aez)
B.ajo=s(["WORDS YOU ALREADY POSTED STOP TURNING UP IN THE NEXT ITEM. Press the check to post a note, keep talking, and the words from the note you just posted were quietly saved a SECOND time inside the next item. That is the duplicate you have been counting since #140567/#140568. The cause was one value doing two jobs: the box the app saves an item from is also the whole session, because that is what OneNote needs to write an unbroken page - so the moment you posted mid-dictation, 'the whole session' included words that were already an item of their own. From now on the item you close keeps only what you said since your last post, and your OneNote page is unchanged. Eight earlier fixes chased this duplicate after it was made; this one stops it being made.","THE PHONE'S 'DICTATE HERE' GOES TO THE MAIN BOX AGAIN - on purpose, and temporarily. Yesterday's fix for it, and yesterday afternoon's fix to that fix, each broke something the real-app test catches, so both came out. It gets re-fixed at a safer place: reading where you aimed at the moment you pressed, instead of re-reading it a third of a second later. Do not spend time testing that on the phone - it is a known return, not a new bug.","AND THE TEST THAT SIGNS OFF ON YOUR BUILDS CAN NO LONGER LIE TO YOU. The real-app check - the one that opens a real window and clicks the way you do - reported PASS whenever exactly ONE of its four scenarios failed. It could do that from 10 August onward, which is most of the week you have been telling me it feels like we are going backwards; eight runs in that window called themselves green over a real failure. It counts correctly now, and this is the first build gated by the fixed version."],t.s)
B.b0l=new A.v(1050,B.ajo)
B.ait=s(["THE APP NOW REPORTS ITS OWN FREEZES. Four times today the app stopped responding and every log it keeps said nothing - because the part that writes the log is the part that froze. A watchdog now runs alongside it, on its own thread, and writes down WHEN the app stopped, HOW LONG it was stuck, and WHAT it was doing at that moment - including a freeze it never recovers from. Next time it locks up, the answer is in a file instead of in another report from you.","Smaller pauses get recorded the same way, so the 'sluggish' moments finally have numbers and a name attached."],t.s)
B.aZ1=new A.v(1048,B.ait)
B.aea=s(["FIXES THE FREEZE v1046 CAUSED. Clicking 'dictate here' on an item locked the app up - my bug, shipped this afternoon, and I am sorry. While an item is being dictated into, the app re-checks every frame that your cursor is still in that box, and it stops re-checking once the box registers as selected. Yesterday's mobile fix taught the app to ignore focus it moved itself - and that registration was one of those. So the check never finished and the app span forever. The box being dictated into may now always register itself; the mobile fix is unchanged for every other box."],t.s)
B.aYT=new A.v(1047,B.aea)
B.alA=s(["YOUR CURSOR STAYS WHERE YOU PUT IT. Put the cursor in the middle of a long note, press Dictate, and the words land THERE. The cause, finally named: opening the editor without a position ran an old rule that parked the cursor at the end to match the scrolled-to-bottom view - and turning dictation ON opens the editor exactly that way. It only ever happened with text long enough to scroll, which is why every short-text test passed while your real notes failed, four builds running. Your position now outranks that rule, and it cannot expire or be consumed.","ON THE PHONE, 'DICTATE HERE' GOES TO THAT ITEM. It was switching on the main dictation box instead. Your phone's log showed why: your press aimed at the item correctly, then the app moved focus itself a tenth of a second later, that move was read as YOU pressing a different box, and the rule that lets a real press cancel a starting dictation killed the one you had just asked for. The app can no longer press buttons on your behalf - only a real finger counts.","If the app ever stops following your taps on the phone, it now says so in the log with timings, instead of leaving you to report it twice."],t.s)
B.b47=new A.v(1046,B.alA)
B.alP=s(["THE DICTATE BUTTON STOPS WAITING FOR THE LAST ONE. Measured on your own machine this morning: a press that took 7.5 seconds, 4.1 of them BEFORE the microphone even engaged - and the connection was already warm the whole time. Those 4 seconds were the new press waiting out the PREVIOUS dictation's microphone teardown, which was itself timing out at exactly 4 seconds. Stop, press two seconds later, wait four: that was the slow button, all week. The press now takes a quarter-second grace and goes; the only case that still needs the wait (the microphone genuinely still held) pays it once, by itself.","THE ITEM COUNT IS BACK NEXT TO THE NOTICE, below the dictation box, with the 'something is happening' message to its right - where you asked for it.","Your diagnostics stopped hiding. The folder path they were written to was built wrong (a backslash silently eaten), so since last night every click you made was recorded into a dead folder next to OneDrive that nothing reads - which is why I kept asking you for information the app had already written down. Fixed; the trail syncs again.","Each of the four microphone-teardown steps is now timed and named in the log, so if one of them hangs again it says WHICH one instead of costing you four seconds anonymously."],t.s)
B.aSm=new A.v(1045,B.alP)
B.atM=s(["NO MORE SILENT WAITING. When the app makes you wait, it now SAYS what it is doing - 'Connecting the mic...', 'Freeing the mic...', 'Checking your library...', 'Updating the list...' - in the fixed slot beside the item count, which never moves the grid. If the dictate button is slow, the reason is on screen instead of in a log.","THE THUMB WHEEL IS THE GRID'S, EVERYWHERE. Item boxes with lots of text used to swallow the wheel and feel stuck; on Windows they now decline it entirely, so the list always scrolls no matter which box the pointer is over.","SCROLLING WHILE DICTATING IS LIGHTER. The breathing red border re-drew the whole dictation box on every audio sample, ~12 times a second, competing with your scroll. It now PAUSES while you scroll and resumes the moment you stop - and it counts its own cost so the slowdown can never hide again.","'DICTATE HERE' NOW WORKS INSIDE THE FULL-SIZED ITEM. Open an item to full size and the same pill is in its title row; your words land at the cursor, exactly like the box view.","A DAMAGED LIBRARY NO LONGER FREEZES THE APP. The check that finds database damage used to run on the screen's own thread - that was the 'not responding' bar you saw. It runs off to the side now, and says 'Checking your library...' while it does.","The two-items-selected family loses its last hiding place: the app's second opinion about which box you are in is now a single value that CANNOT name two boxes at once - the wrong state is impossible to write, not just guarded against."],t.s)
B.aXx=new A.v(1044,B.atM)
B.ap1=s(["THE GRID CANNOT BE MOVED BY MACHINERY - your absolute, enforced at the source. The scroll channel Flutter's own text-focus machinery uses to 'helpfully' reveal things is now dead on the main list: clicks, focus changes, and caret placement physically cannot scroll it - zero moved frames, not a quick snap-back. Your wheel, your finger, and the thumb-jump band are untouched."],t.s)
B.aRe=new A.v(1041,B.ap1)
B.aeB=s(["YOUR WHEEL IS A GESTURE. The click-then-scroll stick, the pause, and the vibration you named three ways tonight were one fight: the anti-shift guard yielded to your finger but never learned about the mouse wheel, so for a moment after every click it reverted each notch you rolled - and when you rolled through it, the tug-of-war was the vibration. The wheel now dismisses the guard instantly, exactly like a finger does."],t.s)
B.b2a=new A.v(1040,B.aeB)
B.aks=s(["THE RESEED REWORK - your go, delivered the same evening. The multi-second full-list rebuild behind a week of slowness (click-then-scroll pauses, the 4-second Dictate press, boxes shifting) is now the LAST resort instead of the default: while you dictate, the list updates additively in milliseconds and can never touch the words on your screen; deletes are learned by ID instead of re-reading 4,800 rows; and the one remaining full pass waits for a moment you cannot feel - no recording, no click in the last second, no scrolling. Every shortcut still falls back to the full read at the first sign of doubt, so your list can never go quietly wrong. First hard evidence: the release test suite's own real-window pass dropped from 15-33 minutes to 9.7 the moment this landed.","Also in this build: every diagnostic your machines write (click trails, boot lines, logs) now uploads from EVERY machine - the computer you actually use had been silently keeping its trails local all week, which is why so many of your reports took a day instead of a minute. From now on your finds arrive with their own replay."],t.s)
B.b02=new A.v(1039,B.aks)
B.ap4=s(["BOXES HOLD STILL. The last two movers are closed: a click on a row the app had no stored cursor position for was falling back to a caret at the END of the text - and revealing THAT caret is what scrolled your grid. Machinery-placed cursors can now only ever sit where revealing them moves nothing. And the anti-shift guard now outlasts the click-delay it was racing, so the deferred list refresh can't shove content under your thumb.","NO MORE SILENT SLOWDOWNS - your rule, standing from today: any background work heavy enough to feel must say so in the status line (the fixed-height one that can never move your grid). First up: 'Updating the list\u2026' whenever a refresh runs long. More background work gets named as we find it.","And your one-click law passed its first machine test in this very release: a real mouse click was driven into both boxes at a mid-text spot, and the release only shipped because focus arrived and the cursor sat exactly there."],t.s)
B.b_r=new A.v(1037,B.ap4)
B.ajO=s(["ONE CLICK, EVERY TIME. Your first click on the Dictate button always lands now - it fires the moment your finger goes down, before the focus-shift that was eating it and making you click twice. And clicking into any box - main or item, fully visible or half off-screen - focuses it AND puts the blinking cursor at your spot in one click.","THE CLICK-THEN-SCROLL PAUSE IS CLOSED: your click was quietly scheduling a full list rebuild that detonated right as your thumb reached the wheel. Clicks now hold rebuilds off for a moment (deferred, never dropped), so click around and scroll freely.","Your one-click law is also now a MACHINE-ENFORCED TEST: from the next build onward, every release drives a real mouse click into both boxes at a mid-text point and refuses to ship unless focus arrives and the cursor sits exactly there."],t.s)
B.aW4=new A.v(1036,B.ajO)
B.aba=s(["YOUR CLICK OWNS THE CURSOR, EVERYWHERE. Single-clicking into any box puts the cursor where you clicked and it STAYS there - the app's own focus machinery used to re-place it at the end a frame later, which was also why 'Dictate here' seemed to jump your cursor. Every internal cursor move now carries YOUR last real position.","THE GRID NEVER MOVES ON A CLICK. The jolt when clicking a partly-visible item was the app auto-scrolling to reveal the box its machinery had just focused. Machinery can no longer focus anything that isn't already fully on screen - only your own scrolling moves the view.","The red control now reads 'Dictating/Recording' - your words.","The folder-suggestion chips are OFF for now, per your call - and it doubles as the speed experiment you proposed: every one of your 4,800 items was watching suggestion state. Tell me if clicking and scrolling feel different in this build."],t.s)
B.aYr=new A.v(1035,B.aba)
B.as4=s(["THE BLUE IS GONE FOR GOOD - your third ask in this family, so the rule went blanket: the main dictation box shows blue ONLY when it is itself the live box. While an item is being dictated into (or just selected), the Dictate control, the type badge, and every other blue on the main box go quiet neutral - the text alone names where words are going. The biggest offender was the main Dictate control itself, which lit blue precisely when an item was dictating.","THE SLUGGISHNESS FIX: the automatic library repair from v1031 wasn't catching YOUR specific damage (the rot lives inside the items table's own pages, which the first detector didn't walk). It walks them now - so this build's first launch actually repairs your machine: expect the 'library rebuilt' message, a minute of re-downloading, and then a machine that feels new. The bedroom computer heals the same way on its next start."],t.s)
B.aRq=new A.v(1034,B.as4)
B.als=s(["DICTATE AT THE CURSOR, YOUR REAL FLOW THIS TIME. v1031's version captured your cursor when you pressed Dictate - but pressing the button is itself a click outside the box, and it wiped the cursor a split-second before the capture read it. Now the box REPORTS your cursor position the moment you click in it, and Dictate uses your freshest real click - the button can't destroy what was already reported. Click mid-word and it slides right; click somewhere new mid-dictation and the landing spot follows.","THE TWO-SELECTED FACE YOU NAMED THIS MORNING IS CLOSED: the old bar-click outline (a second 'selected' look with its own memory) collapsed only when you clicked a ROW - the Dictate button was a door it never heard about, so it stayed painted on an item while the main box went live. Pressing Dictate now clears every selected look an item can wear, and a test drives the real button to keep it that way.","Your scroll-wheel pause: the list's heavy rebuild now WAITS until you stop scrolling (deferred, never dropped) - the wheel stays smooth through 4,800 items.","And the phone bar breathes: the item ID lives in the full page view on mobile (the Dictate button fits), and the date/time moved to the bottom-left on the delete button's line - your pick, no extra height."],t.s)
B.b3t=new A.v(1033,B.als)
B.apO=s(["DICTATION LANDS AT YOUR CURSOR IN THE MAIN BOX - your order, done properly this time: the cursor you place is captured the instant you press Dictate, BEFORE the app's own repainting can move it (that race is why every earlier attempt fell to the end). Words continue in order from that spot, a cursor inside a word slides to the word's end first, and clicking somewhere new mid-dictation moves the landing spot with you. No cursor placed = everything appends at the end exactly as before.","YOUR LIBRARY NOW HEALS ITSELF. Two of your machines have a damaged local database file - 200+ 'malformed' errors in your logs, the bedroom computer's weeks of empty screens, and display glitches (like a stale selection ring) that no app logic could fix because the screen literally couldn't rebuild. From this build, any launch that finds its database damaged sets the broken file aside (kept, dated - nothing deleted), starts fresh, re-downloads your items from your synced copy, and tells you it happened. No hands needed - this machine and the bedroom one both fix themselves on their next launch.","And your pick: the main dictation box shows NO blue at all while an item in the list is selected - the box only ever lights up when it is truly the active box."],t.s)
B.aZE=new A.v(1031,B.apO)
B.aqT=s(["YOUR DUPLICATE PAIR CAN'T HAPPEN AGAIN - #140674/#140675 measured and closed: one utterance was being stored twice (the session's whole recording row AND the item you filed it as, 9 seconds apart) because the cleanup that retires the duplicate copy could be beaten by a timing race and could even have its own decision overwritten. The cleanup now runs from BOTH sides of that race - whoever finishes last still retires the copy - and every time it deliberately KEEPS a row it writes down its four reasons, so any future pair names its own cause.","THE REWRITE'S DELETION PHASE, PART ONE: four of the old mechanisms that could each hold their own opinion about which box you're in are now simply GONE from the code - the machine's one answer replaced them. Your rule that 'after a dictation ends the aim is dead' was sharpened in the process: leaving an item by ANY door (clicking away, navigating, closing) now tells the state machine directly, so nothing can keep an old item lit. The remaining legacy machinery (one more mechanism family) comes out in part two."],t.s)
B.aUg=new A.v(1030,B.aqT)
B.aqB=s(["THE WARM CONNECTION - your ask, verbatim, built: the app now opens its line to the dictation engine AT LAUNCH and keeps it warm between sessions, so pressing Dictate adopts a connection that already exists instead of building one while you wait. Your own stage clocks showed the engine connect WAS the wait (up to 2.7 seconds of it, 73 at the worst); a warm start skips it entirely. And it can never be slower than before: if the warm line ever died silently, the press just takes today's path.","SELECTION MEANS CURSOR, everywhere - your law, implemented: whichever box is selected shows the blinking cursor, period. Hitting resume in the main box brings the cursor back; clicking an existing item while the main box is dictating shuts the main box off, selects the item, AND puts the blinking cursor in it - one click, all three.","Dictating into an existing item now shows NO color anywhere - your pick: the strip's text alone says where words are going. The selection ring still marks the box you're in; nothing else lights up."],t.s)
B.aUi=new A.v(1029,B.aqB)
B.aof=s(["THE DELETE BUTTON JUST DELETES - no blue selection flash first. A control's press is an action now, never a selection.","THE FROZEN RING IS GONE: clicking away from a dictating item used to freeze the screen up to ~3 seconds while the microphone released - the old item's ring stayed painted the whole time ('it looked like both items were selected'). The release runs in the background now; the frame never freezes; the next dictation still waits for the mic to be truly free.","AND THE APP NOW GRADES ITSELF NIGHTLY - your ask, working: your machines' own numbers (dictation start stages, screen freezes, delete speed) are scored against budgets every night at 1 AM. Its very first run flagged both of the things you reported today without being told. A breach turns the morning report red and gets fixed before you feel it. Next target, already measured: the engine connection (~2.5s of every dictation start) - the warm-connection build is queued."],t.s)
B.aSH=new A.v(1028,B.aof)
B.arq=s(["DELETING FEELS INSTANT AGAIN - the scroll-pin added yesterday to stop click-reveals was fighting the reflow that closes a deleted row's gap for half a second. The pin now stands down the instant the list's content actually changes; it only ever suppresses reveal-scrolls.","And your pick, done: the Dictate toggle's outline no longer carries the faint blue fill while dictating into an item - the outline alone marks it, nothing pretends the main box is active."],t.s)
B.aTU=new A.v(1027,B.arq)
B.aer=s(["PRESSING DICTATE NOW RESPONDS INSTANTLY - the box arms the moment you press, on items and the main window both, while the engine connects behind it. Your report was right and the numbers agree: today's starts ranged from a third of a second to 73 seconds, and nothing on screen admitted anything was happening. The look is now immediate; the words begin when the engine lands.","AND THE SLOW STARTS NOW NAME THEIR CAUSE: every start writes its stage times (permission/mic, engine connect) to your diagnostics. Your next slow one tells us exactly where the seconds went - and if it's the engine connection, the next build keeps the connection warm between your quick start-stops.","The empty 'Segment' shells from this morning can't recur: the row's quiet-mark now lands BEFORE the row exists to draw, so a capture with no words yet stays hidden from the very first frame (it still appears the instant real words or recovery need it - nothing is ever hidden that matters)."],t.s)
B.aX1=new A.v(1026,B.aer)
B.akL=s(["YOUR MORNING'S FIVE REPORTS, ONE BUILD. The grid holds still while you dictate (it was rebuilding all 4,700 rows on every landed phrase - ten times in one 55-second stretch of your log). Clicking a partially-visible item no longer scrolls the list to reveal it - selection happens in place, always. And the main dictation box drops its blue frame while you dictate into an item: the destination strip still tells you where words are going, but nothing falsely claims the main box has focus.","DICTATE AT THE CURSOR NOW SURVIVES YOUR REAL FLOW. Third report, finally the real fix: the cursor you place BEFORE pressing the dictate button was dying in the rebuilds that arming causes - so words fell to the end no matter what the insert logic said. The field now remembers your last real cursor position and the session anchors to it: words land where you clicked, mid-word cursors snap to the right of the word, in both item modes.","The 'duplicates' you reported (#140556 area) were two EMPTY capture rows from your 7:52/7:53 attempts - audio saved, transcription never finished, two identical 'Transcribing' shells. Not the duplicate class; your words are recoverable via Retry, and why those transcriptions stalled is under investigation."],t.s)
B.aVl=new A.v(1025,B.akL)
B.aoj=s(["THE MACHINE NOW DRIVES THE STOPS. Phase 3 of the rewrite: when you press away from a dictating box, the state machine itself ends that dictation and routes the tail - the scattered door-by-door judgments are retired on the stop side. Off and press-away are now distinguished by the machine's own state, which closed a subtle hole the quick check caught before this build existed: routing Off through the leave lever would have brought the phantom-row class back.","Shipped on your skip-the-test order: the full gate, rule table and real-app passes did NOT run for this build (named here per the receipts rule). What did run: the compile battery - 34 tests across the six files closest to the change, including the one that caught the phantom regression. Tonight's 1 AM nightly runs the full bar over it."],t.s)
B.aUs=new A.v(1024,B.aoj)
B.adD=s(["THE REWRITE YOU ORDERED IS HALFWAY IN, RUNNING SILENTLY. Selection is now driven by a single state machine - a handful of legal states where two-selected literally cannot be written down - and it watched every click in this build's testing without one disagreement against the old rules. Nothing about how the app behaves changed on purpose in this build; what changed is WHO decides what's selected: one machine instead of six signals.","Every session you run now also writes the machine's decision trail next to the click recorder. That trail is the evidence the second half of the rewrite (the machine taking over dictation control entirely) will be judged against before it ever reaches you."],t.s)
B.aTT=new A.v(1023,B.adD)
B.af9=s(["YOUR EVENING'S THREE REPORTS, ONE MECHANISM, CAUGHT BY THE NEW RECORDER. Your click trail showed the dictation engine taking FIFTEEN SECONDS to start (twice, measured) - and every protective rule in the app was asleep during that window, because they all asked 'is recording on?' and the answer mid-start is no. You pressed the main box inside that window; nothing ended the item's dictation-in-waiting; the session then arrived pointed at the item while you sat in the main box: both lit, and the words fed into both - your 'same text in both boxes.'","NOW: a press-away kills the dictation aim in ANY engine phase - starting, started, or off - and a press during the start window VETOES the start's own follow-through, so what you clicked last always wins.","AND THE SLUGGISHNESS: the background recovery scan (68 stuck old recordings) was re-running and uploading between your clicks, fighting your engine starts for the network. It now yields whenever you've touched the app in the last two minutes - it drains overnight, not while you work.","The recorder that caught all of this shipped yesterday afternoon. This is the first bug it solved from your clicks alone - no reconstruction, no guessing, minutes not days."],t.s)
B.aYZ=new A.v(1022,B.af9)
B.aq1=s(["THE CAPTURE LAYER IS ON - your answer to 'you spend hours testing and I find bugs within seconds.' The app now keeps a local trail of every press, selection change, dictation aim and mic start/stop - IDs and timestamps only, never a single word you said or typed. Next time you find something in fifteen seconds, those fifteen seconds arrive as a replayable record instead of a day of reconstruction. The recorder physically cannot change how the app behaves: it watches, it cannot touch.","The trail lives beside the boot beacon in your synced diagnostics folder, so every machine's sessions are readable from here without you doing anything."],t.s)
B.b3n=new A.v(1021,B.aq1)
B.alm=s(["YOUR CLICK'S WHOLE MEANING IS NOW DECIDED THE INSTANT YOU PRESS - your 15-second day-5 report, all three symptoms. Your trace showed the first click DID end the dictation, but its second half (select the box, place the cursor) raced the screen reflow that stopping triggers, and lost - no cursor, old box still lit. Now everything happens on the press itself, before any reflow exists: the box you press is selected, a dictation aimed elsewhere ends right there, and the cursor follows unless a real field genuinely holds it.","AND THE SEVENTH SIGNAL IS GONE: 'both are selected' had one more lawful face - the multi-select OUTLINE (the bar-click selection from v563) lived in its own store that a click into another box's TEXT never cleared. One box could wear the outline while another wore the ring. A plain press now collapses a stale outline; Ctrl/Shift multi-select for dragging groups is untouched.","Dictation still goes to the END of the text - said plainly: dictate-at-the-cursor was never built. It is not broken; it does not exist yet. It is the next build, with your mid-word rule (start to the right of the word your cursor is in)."],t.s)
B.aYF=new A.v(1020,B.alm)
B.ap7=s(["EVERY MACHINE NOW REPORTS ITS OWN BOOT. 'No data on bedroom computer' has cost days each time because that machine's logs are out of reach until you are standing at it. Every launch now writes one line - build number, item count, signed-in, database location - into the synced Documents folder, so the next time any machine boots empty, the reason arrives on its own: signed out, sync broken, or data present but not drawn. Counts and paths only, never your words.","MY TEST RUNS CAN NO LONGER TOUCH YOUR LIBRARY. Your #140567/#140568 report caught something real: the test copy of your library was still signed in to sync, so test runs were uploading their throwaway items into your real list - and one run appended a test sentence into those two real items. Both are cleaned (your item keeps only your words; the old duplicate twin is in Trash, restorable 30 days), and the test app now runs signed out, permanently.","ANDROID: the Claude status line stays out of the item's header - on the phone it lives in the full-sized view only, per your call. Windows keeps it on the header row beside the date."],t.s)
B.aXL=new A.v(1019,B.ap7)
B.akK=s(["THE SECOND HALF OF THE COLLAPSE, DONE - and your '2 items currently are selected!!!!!' report tonight is the exact case it kills. Your diagnostics log named it: two seconds after 1017's first boot, the app RESUMED the dictation the updater had interrupted, pointed the dictation at that item - and never told the selection. The ring sat on the box you clicked while the resumed item wore the live-dictation look and pulled the cursor into itself three times in 130 milliseconds. Two boxes lit, zero clicks, on the very build that collapsed the ring. Stage 1 made the ring ONE value; stage 2 makes the dictation OBEY it: the aim is now either empty or equal to the selected box, everywhere it can be set, so words heading to an unselected box is a state the app can no longer write down.","THE CURSOR CANNOT BE PULLED INTO A BOX YOU DID NOT SELECT. The hold that keeps the cursor in a live dictation now checks the selection before every pull and dies the moment you click elsewhere - it was fighting your clicks in tonight's trace, three yanks against three of the app's own focus moves.","AND THE LAST TWO OLD-STORE RINGS ARE GONE: the folder/search/outline screens' ring and the Dictate-here pill both read the one selection value now. They were still on the old multi-id store, which is why a second box could still light there after 1017.","THE CLAUDE STATUS LINE MOVED ONTO THE ITEM'S LABEL ROW - right side, next to the date, one line, trimmed. It sat on its own row under the item number and pushed the whole list down every time a message came in; the label row is fixed-height, so status updates now repaint instead of moving your grid.","The self-check learned tonight's shape by name (AIMSEL): every other law read 'lawful' while your screen showed two selected - one ring, one engaged id, aim stores equal. Now the split between aim and selection is itself a named violation, written to diagnostics the moment it exists.","Recorded for the next build, your words: dictation into an existing item should start where the cursor is - and if the cursor is mid-word, start to the right of that word."],t.s)
B.aUo=new A.v(1018,B.akK)
B.akh=s(["ONE BOX SELECTED, BY CONSTRUCTION. Four days of 'two items selected' came down to this: SELECTED was never one fact in the app - six separate signals could each light a box, each individually correct, none obliged to agree. Every fix this weekend fixed one signal, and a new face appeared from another. Selection is now a single value: the box you last clicked, item or main box, one id. Every ring reads that one value. Two lit boxes is no longer a bug that can come back - it is a state the app cannot write down, the way one number cannot be two numbers.","THE TEST GATE EARNED ITS KEEP ON THIS ONE: my first version of this change made every click rebuild all 4,700 rows (which you would have felt as fresh jank). The suite caught it against a copy of your real library before the build was made, and the fix rebuilds exactly two rows per click - the one losing the ring and the one gaining it.","STILL AHEAD, said plainly: the CURSOR and the dictation aim are the remaining half of this collapse - deriving them from the same single value is the next build, and until then a wrong-place cursor is still possible even though a second ring is not."],t.s)
B.aVV=new A.v(1017,B.akh)
B.abF=s(["THE SLOWNESS IN 1015 WAS MINE, AND IT IS OUT. The self-check I added in 1013 was supposed to protect a machine whose library failed to load. Instead it fired during YOUR boot: your library takes about 20 seconds to load, the check looked after 3, decided the empty screen was a fault, and loaded all 4,768 items itself - twice in one session, competing with the real load. That is the dead-slow app you just used, and the churn it caused is the most likely source of the extra selected boxes on top of it.","THE SELF-CHECK NOW ONLY WATCHES. It still notices when a rule breaks and writes it down so I can find it in one line - but it no longer changes anything in your app while you are using it. No reloading, no collapsing rows, no clearing state. It has to earn the right to act, and it has not.","WHAT IS STILL FIXED FROM TODAY: pressing the main box ends an item's dictation, the session backup row no longer appears beside your real item, and your library lives outside OneDrive where two machines can no longer fight over it."],t.s)
B.b2f=new A.v(1016,B.abF)
B.aeK=s(["YOU CAN CLICK INTO THE MAIN BOX WHILE AN ITEM IS DICTATING. Your trace named it exactly: seven presses recorded, ZERO verdicts. The rule that stops dictation when you click away latches your press and decides on the release - and in between, the cursor-hold rebuilds that row every 20 milliseconds and wipes the latch. Your click was seen and then thrown away, every time, and the hold pulled the cursor back into the item.","NOW A PRESS ON THE MAIN BOX ENDS THAT ITEM'S DICTATION IMMEDIATELY - decided on the press itself, with nothing left to race it.","AND THE DUPLICATE ITEMS: #140567 and #140568 were not one item shown twice. #140567 was the session's hidden 146-second BACKUP row leaking into your list beside your real 50-second item. The backup is supposed to retire itself once your words are safely somewhere - but it only recognised one of the ways an item gets posted, and you had posted yours mid-dictation. Fixed at the single place every item is written, with a guard so a recording that holds the ONLY copy of your words is never retired.","THIS BUILD WAS PROVEN ON THE REAL APP BEFORE YOU GOT IT - not in a test harness. A real Voicley window, your real library, real clicks: the app was put into item-dictation, the main box was clicked once, and the test demanded the dictation end. It failed the way your screen failed until the fix went in. That check now runs before every release."],t.s)
B.b1l=new A.v(1015,B.aeK)
B.agn=s(["YOUR LIBRARY WAS LIVING IN ONEDRIVE - that is the bedroom computer, and no app fix could ever have touched it. Windows redirects your Documents folder into OneDrive, and that is where the app's database file sat. OneDrive cannot merge a database, so with two machines using it, it renamed the loser and the app opened a brand new EMPTY file instead. Your items were never lost - that machine was never opening your library. The proof is still on the disk: dictate_db-Book5.sqlite, dictate_db-Book5-2.sqlite, and one from the bedroom machine.","THE DATABASE NOW MOVES OUT, once per machine, on the first launch of this build - into the local app folder where a live database belongs, never synced. It is a COPY: your old file stays exactly where it is until you decide to delete it. The database's companion file travels with it (leaving it behind would silently roll back your most recent dictations), the copy is completed under a temporary name before it counts, and any failure at all just keeps using the old file. Nothing about your data is risked to fix this.","AND THE LAST FACE OF THE THREE-DAY BUG IS GONE. The watchdog you asked for caught it on your machine within the hour: when you clicked out of an item, the app killed only half of its 'where the words go' marker. The visible half cleared instantly; the internal half - the one that decides who owns the cursor - stayed alive for the whole shutdown, and sometimes forever. That is why an item you left kept looking selected and why the main box refused your clicks. The marker now dies whole, in the frame you click, and where your last words go is tracked separately so nothing is lost.","THE WATCHDOG ALSO REPAIRS IT NOW: if that marker ever outlives its dictation again, the app clears it on sight rather than leaving you fighting a ghost."],t.s)
B.aSS=new A.v(1014,B.agn)
B.apP=s(["THE APP NOW WATCHES ITSELF - you asked for option B, this is it. Every few seconds, while you work, it checks the rules your bug reports are made of: one item can only be one box, only one box can wear the selection ring, only one cursor can be blinking, and the two internal ideas of 'where the words are going' must agree. The moment one breaks, it writes a single line naming the item, on YOUR machine, at that instant.","WHY THAT MATTERS: every hunt this weekend started with you noticing something and me reconstructing it hours later from a trace that never recorded the moment. Now the moment records itself. You say 'it happened' - I read one line.","AND ONE OF THEM IT REPAIRS ON THE SPOT: if an item ever gets a second box again, the duplicate is dropped as soon as it appears - before it can light two rings or fight over your cursor. That repair can never cost a word: the box is a duplicate, your recording and its text are one row underneath, untouched.","IT STAYS QUIET OTHERWISE - one line per episode, never a flood, never while you're scrolling, and it can't affect the app if it fails. Fifteen tests hold it: the judgment on every rule, the eyes against a real screen, and a check that fails the build if the watchdog is ever left switched off.","AND THE EMPTY-LIBRARY SYMPTOM NOW HEALS ITSELF. You told me 1012 still wasn't loading data, so instead of guessing again: whenever the dashboard is empty, the app asks the database whether your library is actually there. If it is, it writes a line naming the count and FILLS THE SCREEN. Your items were never lost - they sit in the database the whole time - and now a machine that loses that race recovers in seconds instead of showing you nothing."],t.s)
B.aTx=new A.v(1013,B.apP)
B.ak6=s(["YOUR BEDROOM COMPUTER LOADS AGAIN - and that one was mine. Build 1011 made the library read start earlier to speed up launch, but the old order was what guaranteed the database was actually open first. On a machine where that takes a moment longer, the read came back with nothing and the screen showed an empty library. Reverted, with a test that fails if anyone ever reorders it again. Your data was never in danger - it was on disk the whole time, just not read yet.","AND THE 'TWO ITEMS SELECTED' BUG IS FIXED AT ITS REAL CAUSE - three days, and it was never a focus bug. Your dictation's BOX exists a moment before its database row does. If a library refresh spots that new row in the gap, you get a SECOND box for the same item - and since a box lights up by asking 'is the selected item mine?', both answer yes. One click, two rings.","IT EXPLAINS THE REST OF TODAY TOO: two boxes answering to one item fight over the cursor, which is exactly why the main dictation box took several clicks to enter and why the list kept scrolling to that Claude item. One cause, three symptoms.","NOW: one item can only ever have one box - enforced where the duplicate was born and again on every path that publishes the list. Four tests hold it, and the automated walker now fails outright if any item is ever drawn twice, so it cannot come back quietly."],t.s)
B.aRC=new A.v(1012,B.ak6)
B.ar2=s(["THE '3 ITEMS SELECTED' IS FIXED AT THE ROOT. Your trace showed it: a dictation restarted automatically (the resume - no button press) was INVISIBLE to the click-outside-stops rule, so the old item's ring fought every click you made, sometimes for 12 straight seconds. The rule is now enforced at the state level too: the moment your click lands in any other item, the dictation stops and your click wins - whatever the touch geometry missed.","FIRST LOAD IS FASTER. Measured on your machine: 2.27 seconds to ready, 0.89 of it the library read - which only STARTED after the first frame painted. The read now begins the instant the screen initializes and runs in parallel with everything it used to wait behind. Launch once after this update and the nightly stopwatch will tell you the new number.","TAP-TO-RESUME ANSWERS INSTANTLY: the amber mic-stopped banner flips to 'Starting the mic\u2026' with a spinner the same frame you tap, instead of sitting unchanged for the seconds the engine takes to spin up. A failed start puts the tappable banner back instead of wedging.","AND THE OLD 16-PIXEL OVERFLOW IS OFFICIALLY DEAD - the layout defect the automated bed caught on its very first run is gone (killed by the button restructure two builds ago), now proven by a permanent test that engages every row and demands zero layout errors. Your overnight report is item #140551; your document's new lines graded 7 uncovered - that is the next build queue, dictate-at-the-cursor first."],t.s)
B.b3j=new A.v(1011,B.ar2)
B.ady=s(["THE FIFTH SIGHTING FOUND THE REAL ONE. 'Main box dictating and the item below it selected' - your trace caught it red-handed: an aim still pointing at an item from an EARLIER dictation. The cause: stopping is deliberately instant (so you can start the next dictation without waiting), and when you use that speed - click out of an item, immediately dictate in the main box, your normal rhythm - the old session's background cleanup saw a newer session running and SKIPPED ITSELF. Its aim stayed alive: the old item kept the target ring, and the main box's focus claims were refused.","WORSE, WHEN THAT LATE CLEANUP DID RUN, it wiped the NEW session's published state wholesale - which is why the bug wore different faces all day.","THE FIX: every aim now carries its OWNER. A session's death always kills its own aim - whether or not a newer session has taken over the screen - and never touches an aim a newer session took for itself. And the late cleanup can no longer flatten a newer session's state. Your exact rapid click-out-then-dictate sequence is a permanent test now, alongside its mirror: proof that a newer session's aim SURVIVES an old session's late cleanup.","AND THE PICTURE-TESTS GOT HONEST: the reference images of what 'selected' looks like were being photographed mid-animation, so they'd randomly disagree with themselves between runs. They now capture only fully-settled frames, verified stable across consecutive runs - so when they go red, it's real."],t.s)
B.aVG=new A.v(1010,B.ady)
B.atz=s(["THE PHANTOM ITEMS ARE DEAD. All three of tonight's reports were ONE disease: every dictation session quietly saves a whole-session backup row the moment the mic opens (so a crash can never lose your words) - and that backup was showing up as a normal item. It was the 'random phantom items', it was the 'first item selected while the main box dictates' (that first item WAS the live session's backup), and old twins of it have been sitting in your library for weeks.","NOW: the backup hides while it works, and the moment your words land somewhere you can see - the box, the item you dictated into, a posted note - the backup removes itself (Trash keeps it 30 days). If a session ever crashes so the backup is the ONLY copy of your words, it stays and shows - that case has its own test, because never losing words outranks tidiness.","AND THE WORD-THEFT IS FIXED: pressing 'Dictate here' on an item while the main box was keeping text used to ADOPT the box's words and dump them into that item when you stopped - your item #140523 got 96 characters of main-box text that way, and your box was emptied. There is now a fence: an item dictation can only ever deliver words spoken in THAT session. The box's kept text stays in the box, untouched, always.","PROVEN BEFORE THIS BUILD: four new permanent tests - one reproduced the #140523 theft exactly and went red on the old code; one proves the box keeps its words; one proves the backup absorbs itself; one proves a sole-copy backup is never touched. Rule CROSS-4 in your table now, checked on every build forever."],t.s)
B.b24=new A.v(1009,B.atz)
B.atq=s(["THE TWO-BOXES-SELECTED GHOST IS DEAD. Your report - the main dictation box AND an existing item both showing selected - was a leftover 'resume' aim: an earlier dictation that ended on its own quietly re-pointed the NEXT session at its old item, in a place only half the app could see. Pressing Dictate now kills any leftover aim outright, and the two halves of the app that track the aim can no longer disagree - the disagreement itself is what your tests now watch for.","ONE CLICK INTO THE MAIN BOX, EVERY TIME - and the other direction of your click-out rule is now tested the way you actually hit it: main box dictating, you click an existing item - the main dictation stops, its text stays, the item you clicked takes the selection with the cursor blinking where you clicked. Both directions run against a copy of your real library on every build.","THE 'DICTATE HERE' BUTTON MOVED NEXT TO THE ITEM NUMBER, as you asked - and the date can no longer move when you click into a box. The selection ring also keeps one constant thickness in every state now; the earlier version was thicker when selected, which physically nudged everything inside the frame - a test now fails if the date drifts even two pixels.","YOUR MINUTE RULE IS LAW: when the internet drops, every reconnect path now tries at least once a minute, forever - sync was waiting two minutes between tries, live captions already retried and never give up. Both cadences are pinned by tests that fail the build if anyone ever loosens them. And a scroll-timing defect the automated walker first caught two builds ago stopped being 'rare' and got properly fixed at the source."],t.s)
B.b2O=new A.v(1008,B.atq)
B.alh=s(["CLICKING OUT OF A DICTATING ITEM NOW RELEASES IT - your rule, exactly: dictation shuts off at the click, the old item drops its ring and cursor that same instant, and the selection follows your click - whether you clicked another item or the main box. The words you spoke before clicking out still land in the item you were dictating into.","WHY IT USED TO STICK: ending a dictation takes the engine a moment, and until that finished the app still considered the old item 'aimed' - so the machinery that rightly defends a dictating item's cursor kept re-lighting it against your click. The aim now dies the moment you click; only the save finishes in the background.","THREE MORE REAL DEFECTS FELL IN THE SAME HUNT: the click-anywhere-stops-dictation rule had no diagnostics at all (it has a full trace now); stopping a recording could crash internally wherever sync was not ready; and two background cleanup steps blew up if the app closed at the wrong moment. All fixed, all tested.","AND THE BUG-FINDER LEARNED TO DICTATE: the automated random walker that hammers your real library now starts real dictations mid-walk and speaks phrases into them - the exact situation every bug you found this week lived in. It runs on every build and every night."],t.s)
B.aWw=new A.v(1007,B.alh)
B.apn=s(["THE CURSOR NO LONGER GOES AWAY WHEN YOU CLICK 'DICTATE HERE'. Two bugs were stacked under that one symptom - which is why it survived two days. First: while the speech engine was starting (up to a second), the main box's cursor-recovery machinery stole the caret from the item you aimed at. Second: even without the theft, nothing put the cursor back once the engine came live. Both fixed: the box you name now OWNS the cursor, and re-claims it on every screen update while dictation is aimed there.","AND THIS TIME THE PROOF EXISTS BEFORE YOU TRY IT: a new test runs the exact press with a real-length engine start on the clock - it failed on the old code with your exact symptom, rejected two earlier versions of the fix that would have broken again, and passes now. It runs on every build forever.","SELECTION NOW LOOKS DIFFERENT FROM A NOTE. Your launch mystery - items looking selected when nothing was - was two near-identical blues: the thin trim every Note wears, and the selection ring. Selected items now wear a thick ring with a glow that no type trim shares. A reference image of every type beside a true selection is locked into the tests.","TESTING KEEPS RUNNING WHILE YOU SLEEP: a nightly job now runs your expected-behavior document, the full test suite, and a startup stopwatch that reads YOUR machine's real launch times - and leaves a one-line morning report in your inbox."],t.s)
B.b1K=new A.v(1006,B.apn)
B.apB=s(["THE DICTATE BUTTON NOW HONORS YOUR RULE DIRECTLY: pressing it while a list item is selected RELEASES that item - ring off, cursor into the main box - written as a direct assignment on the press itself, not a chain of focus events that could lose a race. Verified two ways: automated tests pressing the real button, and a live driven session on this machine where the release was watched happen on screen, frame by frame.","PRESSING 'DICTATE HERE' ON AN ITEM NOW FOCUSES THAT ITEM: the cursor appears and blinks in the box you aimed at, instead of the mic arming with no cursor anywhere.","A ZOMBIE TIMER, FOUND AND FIXED: if the microphone permission was ever denied, a 5-minute check-in timer kept running forever for a recording that never existed. The button-level test bed caught it because it could not even shut down cleanly with the timer leaked.","AND THE RING MYSTERY FROM YOUR LAUNCH REPORTS HAS AN ANSWER: the blue trim every Note-type item wears is nearly the same blue as the 'selected' ring - so several Notes on screen LOOK like several selected boxes even when the app's selection is provably empty. That is a styling decision now waiting on your call: distinct selection look, or quieter Note trim."],t.s)
B.aWG=new A.v(1005,B.apB)
B.apN=s(["ONE CLICK GETS YOU INTO A TEXT BOX. Your report, one sentence, now the law. The cause: a box at rest is a painted picture of its text - the real text field only appears when you click, and the click's position was being thrown away during the swap, parking the cursor at the end. One click now does the whole job: the field is live and the cursor is exactly where you clicked.","PROVEN BEFORE YOU GOT IT: a test that clicks once - only once - and demands the cursor land at the clicked spot went RED on the old code with your exact symptom (cursor at position 59 of 59) and green on the fix. It is rule LAW-3 in your spec now, checked on every build forever, alongside the other twelve."],t.s)
B.b1b=new A.v(1004,B.apN)
B.aeZ=s(["YOUR FOCUS RULES ARE NOW MACHINE-ENFORCED. The spec you wrote - one box has focus, it is the one you LAST clicked, the cursor lands where you clicked, dictation goes where the cursor is - became 12 named rules with 69 automated tests behind them. Before every future release they all run against a copy of your real library (4,809 items) with dictation live, sync bursting, and the reconnect banner jarring the list. Any build that breaks one of your sentences now fails before it reaches you.","A CRASH ON THE WAY OUT, FIXED. Closing the app could hit an internal error in the reader's shutdown (it asked for a service that was already gone). Invisible most days, but it was real - the new test bed could not even close the app without tripping it. Guarded.","TWO SMALLER DEFECTS THE TESTS CAUGHT are queued for their own fixes: a 16-pixel layout overflow when an item row lights up, and a rare timing slip when a fling scroll lands mid-frame. Both are named, reproducible, and tracked - neither affects your words."],t.s)
B.b3_=new A.v(1003,B.aeZ)
B.ac4=s(["THE FREEZE WHEN YOU CLICK INTO AN ITEM. Your own machine measured it: one operation was taking 616 milliseconds, on the screen thread, and it ran every single time anything in your library changed. Clicking into an item causes a save, a save causes that operation, and with 4,725 items it locked the screen. It now does a fraction of the work for the same result.","WHAT IT WAS DOING: rebuilding two complete indexes of your entire library from scratch on every change - about nine thousand needless operations each time - to end up with almost exactly what it already had. It now updates what changed and leaves the rest alone.","AND IT WAS PROVEN SAFE BEFORE SHIPPING, because this is the code that decides which items exist: an automated test runs a five thousand item library through both the old and new versions and checks they agree item for item, including when items are deleted and restored. Nothing may appear, vanish, or change."],t.s)
B.aWt=new A.v(1002,B.ac4)
B.aqR=s(["ONE BOX HAS THE FOCUS, AND IT IS THE ONE WITH THE CURSOR. Clicking into the main dictation window now releases the item you were just in. Until now the app kept two separate ideas of which box was active, and nothing made them agree - which is why the cursor could blink in the main window while an item below it still looked selected.","AND THE WORDS FOLLOW THE BOX YOU NAMED. When you tap 'Dictate here' on an item, the main dictation box can no longer quietly take the words back a fraction of a second later. That race was the real cause of sentences landing in the wrong item.","TESTED BEFORE IT SHIPPED, THIS TIME. Your whole spec is now a set of automated tests that run without the app: one focused box at a time, focus moving when you tap another box, the focused box always showing its cursor, and dictation inserting where you put the cursor rather than at the end. Build 999 would have been caught by these before it ever reached you."],t.s)
B.aXk=new A.v(1001,B.aqR)
B.alS=s(["PUTTING BACK WHAT I BROKE. Build 999 hid your cursor and made new dictations stop appearing in the list. Both were changes I made today and could not run before shipping them, and both are out of this build. The cursor behaves the way it did before, and dictated words always land somewhere you can see them.","WHAT SURVIVED, because your own log proved it: when you tap 'Dictate here' on an item, the main dictation box can no longer quietly take the words back a fraction of a second later. That was the real cause of words landing in the wrong box.","STILL OWED, and not attempted here: one item focused at a time, with the cursor blinking in it, and clicking the main box releasing the item you were in. That is a focus rule, and it needs to be built against the focus system properly rather than patched at the paint layer, which is what went wrong today."],t.s)
B.b2S=new A.v(1000,B.alS)
B.adX=s(["YOUR WORDS GO WHERE YOU SAID. Day two of this one, and your own log finally named it: when you tapped 'Dictate here' on an item, the item took the words for about eighty milliseconds - and then the main dictation box quietly took them back. Sometimes the item won that race and it worked; sometimes it lost and your sentences landed in the main box or the item above. That race is gone. A destination you state by tapping outranks anything the app's own focus does, always.","AND THE CURSOR NOW TELLS YOU. There is exactly one flashing cursor in the app, and it sits in the box your next sentence is going into. Dictating into an item - that item blinks and the main box goes dark. Dictating into the main box - only the main box blinks. It is calculated from the same rule that routes the words, so what you see and what happens cannot disagree.","CLEAR NOW CLEARS EVERYTHING. When you hit Clear while a phrase was still arriving, the half-heard words stayed on screen and could not be cleared at all - Clear only wiped the finished text, never the live grey draft. It wipes both now, and Undo gives back everything you could see rather than half of it.","THE ITEM COUNT MOVED to Settings, under Library. It was interesting but it was not what the main screen is for."],t.s)
B.aYG=new A.v(999,B.adX)
B.afh=s(['YOUR RULE: "WHEN I LEAVE THE MAIN DICTATION BOX THE DICTATION STOPS." It does now. Yesterday that rule covered items only - click away from an item you were dictating into and it stopped. The dictation box itself was the exception, and there was no good reason for it. Whatever your words are landing in, clicking outside it ends the dictation. Scrolling still does not, and a menu you have open still does not.','YOUR REPORT: "CLICK THE MAIN DICTATE BUTTON... THE ITEM BELOW IS SELECTED STILL AND I THINK RECEIVES TEXT." It was, and you were right. Typing in an item leaves the cursor sitting there, and the app treated a cursor as a decision about where dictation should go - so pressing the button on the dictation box started a recording that fed the item you had been editing, while the button itself lit up red over the empty box. The button aims at the box it lives on now. Dictating into an item is one gesture: that item\'s own mic.','AND THE SAME BUG WAS IN THE INDICATOR. The red "Listening" was reading one thing and the words were following another, which is how you can be recorded into something you are not looking at. Those are now the same fact in the code, not two facts that agree when nothing goes wrong, so the button can no longer say one thing while your words do another.','YOUR REPORT: "THERE IS STILL TEXT IN THERE AND I CLOSE THE ALERT - THE TEXT SHOULD STAY IN THE BOX." The X was innocent. When the microphone timed out it was SAVING your open box as a finished item on the way out, and the yellow banner only appeared afterwards - so the words were already gone before you touched anything. Stopping the microphone no longer ends your note. The words stay in the box, the banner sits above them, and tapping it picks up where you left off.','THE ONE RULE UNDERNEATH BOTH: stopping the microphone is not closing the note. Your note ends when YOU end it - the check mark, Enter, "new dictation", the New button. A timer running out, or you clicking somewhere else, now only closes the microphone. That also retires the "resume" machinery from last week, which existed to reunite you with a note the app had closed behind you. Nothing to reunite if nothing was separated.',"WHAT YOU WILL NOTICE, and it is the honest cost: the dictation box is where nearly every dictation happens, so this rule fires far more often than the item version did. A stray tap on the list mid-thought will stop the microphone. Nothing is lost when it does - your words stay in the box and one tap on Dictate carries on - but you will see it stop more than you used to. If it turns out to be too eager, say so and it gets a longer leash.","THE OTHER THING TO WATCH: dictating INTO an existing item is now only the mic on that item. Putting the cursor in an item used to redirect a running dictation there, and that is the behaviour that fed your words to the wrong box - it cannot coexist with the rule you asked for, so it is gone. 2,337 tests passing, 28 of them new, including one that proves your words survive the moment the microphone comes back to a box it left full."],t.s)
B.aRg=new A.v(998,B.afh)
B.aib=s(['A FREE DICTATION ENGINE THAT RUNS ON YOUR DEVICE. There is a new choice in the speech engine list: "Local (offline, free)". Tap it once and it downloads its engine - 189 MB, one time - and from then on it dictates with no internet at all and costs nothing per minute, ever. Pick it and dictate normally; everything else works the same way it always has.',"IT IS ROUGHER, AND IT SAYS SO. The local engine has no punctuation and mishears more often than Deepgram does - on your own recordings it was about ten points behind. The picker tells you that before you choose it, because an engine that quietly did worse would be the app misleading you. Deepgram is still there and still the default; switching back is one tap.","WHY THIS EXISTS. Every word you dictate today needs the network, which is why the screen goes quiet in the car. This is the first half of fixing that permanently - and the measurements behind it were made on your own recordings, not a benchmark: your voice, your background noise, your way of talking.","ON iPhone the same job is done by Apple's own on-device engine, which is built into iOS and costs nothing to ship - so the Local entry is not offered there; that build takes over automatically when the network drops."],t.s)
B.aZ9=new A.v(997,B.aib)
B.arO=s(['YOUR REQUEST: "THERE NEEDS TO BE AN ACTUAL RESTORE BUTTON IN TRASH, THE SWIPE IS NOT TOO OBVIOUS." There is one now. Every item in the Trash carries a green Restore button, right on the row, saying what it does. The swipe still restores and the ... menu still restores - nothing was taken away. It wears the same green as the swipe on purpose, so however you reach it, it reads as the same one action.','YOUR REPORT: "THE STATUS BAR IN BETWEEN THE DICTATION BOX AND THE ITEMS JARS THE LIST WHEN IT APPEARS AND DISAPPEARS." It cannot any more. The sync message stays exactly where you had it, but the row it lives in is now always the same height - whether sync is silent, working, or broken. Only what is painted inside it changes, so your item list can no longer be shoved down when a message arrives or snapped back up when it finishes.',"AND THAT ROW NOW EARNS ITS KEEP. To the left of the status sits your item count, the way you asked - so the space is telling you something even when sync has nothing to say, instead of being blank ground waiting to jump.","WHY IT WAS JARRING, SINCE IT LOOKED LIKE A PLACEMENT PROBLEM: the old status bar took up NO height when it was quiet and about thirty pixels when it spoke. Everything below it moved to make room. Putting it somewhere else in that column would only have moved the jolt - the fix had to be a slot that never changes size, which is what this is.","The one thing I left out: the old bar held a message on screen for a minimum time so a flickering connection could not strobe it. With the height now fixed, a brief message is just text appearing rather than the whole list lurching. If it reads as twitchy while you use it, tell me and the hold goes back in."],t.s)
B.aWy=new A.v(996,B.arO)
B.amq=s(['YOUR REQUEST: "IF I CHANGE THE TYPE OF AN ITEM BEING TRANSCRIBED WHILE IT IS TRANSCRIBING LET\'S NOT END THE TRANSCRIPTION. KEEP IT RECORDING." It does now. Change the type mid-sentence - in the dictation box or on the item itself - and the microphone keeps running, your words keep landing in the SAME item, and nothing is cut. Only the label changes.',"THIS IS ALSO THE DUPLICATE-ITEM BUG, from the end nobody had fixed. Your two duplicates on 128110 and 128111 came from exactly this: about 53 seconds into one dictation you changed the type, and that closed the item you were talking into - saving those first 49.8 seconds as an item of their own - while the recording carried on to 148 seconds and became a SECOND item that contained the first. One dictation, two items, one inside the other. The type control was quietly doing what the + menu does, because both went through the same door.",'THEY ARE TWO DIFFERENT DOORS NOW. "New task" from the + menu still means a new item, and still closes what is open - that is what it is for. The type label says "Change item type", so it changes the type of the item in front of you and nothing else. No cut, no seam, no second row.',"AND CHANGING AN ITEM'S TYPE NO LONGER SWITCHES THE MICROPHONE OFF. Yesterday's \"click anywhere else and it stops\" had one honest gap: the menu that opens when you tap the type badge covers the screen, so picking from it counted as clicking somewhere else and stopped the dictation you were in the middle of. While a menu is open there IS nowhere else to click, so it no longer counts. Everything else about that rule is untouched - clicking another item, the dictation box or empty space still stops it, and scrolling still does not.",'CHANGING TO CLAUDE MID-SENTENCE WAITS FOR YOU TO FINISH. Sorting an item to Claude sends it at the tap, on purpose - the tap IS the send. Doing that halfway through a sentence would put half a thought on my desk and there is no taking it back. So the item becomes a Claude item straight away, the send waits until you stop talking, and it goes out whole. The menu says "Claude - sends when you stop" before you tap, and the box says "sends when this dictation ends" while it waits, so it is never a surprise. A Claude item still cannot be changed to anything else.',"NOTHING SPOKEN IS LOST ACROSS THE CHANGE, including the last thing you say before you tap - the send waits for your words to stop arriving before it reads the item, rather than taking a snapshot mid-save. 2,315 tests passing, 14 of them new, including one that opens the real type menu on a real item and proves the pick cannot be read as walking away from it."],t.s)
B.b26=new A.v(995,B.amq)
B.agd=s(['YOUR REPORT: "DELETES ARE TAKING A LONG TIME AGAIN." They are, and it was not the thing that caused it last time. The old cause was a delete forcing the app to rebuild your whole library, and your log shows that guard doing its job perfectly - it skips the rebuild, and the one rebuild that did run finished in 12 milliseconds. All true, and none of it was what you were feeling.',"WHAT THE DELETE ACTUALLY COSTS IS THE ITEMS THAT STAY. The main list keeps roughly 20 to 40 items built and measured ahead of what you can see, so scrolling is smooth. Those items are identified by their POSITION in the list. Delete one item and every item below it moves up a position - and to the list, an item that changed position is a different item, so it throws away every one of them and builds and measures them all again from scratch. Deleting one thing rebuilt forty. Each rebuild costs 20 to 35 milliseconds, which is where your one-second and one-and-a-half-second freezes came from.","THE DELETED ITEM NOW KEEPS ITS PLACE FOR HALF A SECOND. It disappears from the screen the instant you tap, exactly as before - but the empty space it leaves behind holds its position open just long enough that nothing below it has to move. Nothing gets rebuilt, so the tap is instant and so is the next one. The list closes the gap once, half a second after you stop deleting.","WHICH MEANS A RUN OF DELETES NOW COSTS WHAT ONE DELETE COSTS. Deleting ten items in a row used to pay that rebuild ten times over - that is the several seconds of unresponsive window you were sitting through while triaging. Ten taps now pay it once, after the last one.","SELECTING A GROUP AND DELETING IT WAS DOING THE SAME THING PER ITEM. Selecting twenty items and pressing delete, or dragging a group onto the Trash, told the list twenty separate times that something had gone - twenty rebuilds. It is told once now, for the whole selection. Every item is still individually checked and individually protected before anything is written; only the redraw was combined.","NOTHING ABOUT WHAT A DELETE MEANS HAS CHANGED. It is still a move to Trash, still undoable for six seconds, still restorable for thirty days, and your audio is still kept. If a delete fails to save, the item comes straight back into the place it was holding.","AND IT NOW MEASURES ITSELF, so this does not have to be guessed at a third time. Every delete records how long the tap took and how many items had to be rebuilt because of it. 2,301 tests passing, 9 of them new - including one that reproduces the old behaviour exactly (deleting 1 item out of 120 rebuilt 118 of them) and one that proves the obvious-looking fix does not work, so nobody spends a day on it later."],t.s)
B.aSb=new A.v(994,B.agd)
B.ail=s(['YOU ASKED FOR THIS AND YOU RUN IT: "delete the 2700 audios." Settings now has a STORAGE section that deletes the recordings kept only on this device - the ones that never got copied to the cloud. Nothing happens until you scroll to it, read the count, and confirm. It never runs on its own, never on a timer, and never as a side effect of anything else you do.',"IT TELLS YOU EXACTLY WHAT IT WILL DO BEFORE IT DOES IT: how many recordings, how many gigabytes, and that it cannot be undone. Those numbers are measured off the actual files on your phone, not estimated - the number in the warning is the same number it deletes. Cancel is the default button, so a stray tap does nothing.","YOUR WORDS ARE NOT TOUCHED. Only the audio files go. Every item stays, with its full transcript, its title, its folders and its place in your library. What you deleted is the recording, not the dictation.","THE FOUR RECORDINGS THAT HAVE NO TRANSCRIPT YET ARE REFUSED, AND ALWAYS WILL BE. Of the 2,704 recordings sitting only on your devices, 2,700 have their words written down and exactly 4 do not - and for those 4 the audio is the only copy of something you said. Deleting them would destroy words, so the app will not do it. This is a rule about the recording itself, not a list of four items I wrote down: if one of those four gets its words tomorrow it becomes deletable on its own, and any recording that ever loses its words is protected again automatically.",'IT ALSO REFUSES YOUR OTHER DEVICES\' RECORDINGS. Your phone, your PC and your iPhone all see the same list, so the phone could see a file that only exists on the PC and "delete" a recording that is still sitting safely there. It only ever touches a file it can actually see on the device you are holding, and if a file is already gone it leaves that item completely alone rather than guessing.','AND AN ITEM WHOSE RECORDING YOU DELETED NO LONGER CLAIMS YOU TYPED IT. Once the audio is gone the item looks exactly like a typed note, and it would have told you "this item was typed, not dictated" about 2,700 things you definitely said out loud. It now says the recording was deleted to free up space, that you chose it, and that the transcript is kept - and it is not shown in red, because nothing went wrong. 2,286 tests passing, 26 of them new, and the ones that matter most prove a recording with no transcript is never selected.'],t.s)
B.aW1=new A.v(993,B.ail)
B.aor=s(['YOUR REPORT: AN ITEM YOU DICTATED "TOOK A WHILE TO POST." It was not slow at saving. Everything you saved was waiting in line behind hundreds of writes the app was making for no reason at all. Your words were never at risk - they were queued, in order, and they all arrived - but they were arriving behind a queue the app kept refilling faster than it could empty it. Both causes are fixed, and nothing you say or type is treated any differently.',"CAUSE ONE - THE APP RE-UPLOADED THE SAME 267 RECORDS EVERY SINGLE TIME IT STARTED. When your library first moved onto sync back in July, the app kept a backup copy of everything you had, and copies it back in on launch so a half-finished move always finishes. That copy was supposed to skip anything already there, and locally it did. What it did NOT skip was telling the server about them - so every launch quietly queued all 267 records for upload again, carrying values identical, field for field, to what the server already held. Three launches had put 801 pointless uploads in front of your real work. It now copies only what is genuinely missing, which on your machine is nothing, so launching the app queues nothing.","CAUSE TWO - TWO OF YOUR CLAUDE ITEMS HAD GROWN TO A MEGABYTE EACH, of the same few answers repeated over and over. One held four different answers written 4,473 times between them; the other held two, written 4,463 times. Every 45 seconds another copy was added, and adding it re-uploaded the whole megabyte. That is what was actually flooding the queue - and it is also why those two items were heavy to draw, because the app was laying out four thousand answer blocks to show you four.","WHY IT COULD ONLY EVER GET WORSE, in plain terms: the app already refused to ADD an answer it recognised as a repeat. But when it wrote the list back out it copied every repeat that was already in there straight through. So a single duplicate that slipped in - two writers can touch that list, the app and the desk - was permanent, and the next one stacked on top of it. Refusing to add is not the same as cleaning up, and only one of those two ends.","THE LIST NOW TIDIES ITSELF EVERY TIME IT IS WRITTEN, and your two runaway items collapse from about a megabyte each to a few hundred characters the first time the app touches them. Only EXACT, character-for-character repeats are dropped, and the surviving copy keeps its original position - so every answer that says something different is still there, still in order. Two answers that differ by a single word are two answers.",'AND AN UNCHANGED SAVE NOW WRITES NOTHING AT ALL. The check that decided "has anything changed here?" was comparing against a tidied-up copy of the record rather than the record itself, so a record that differed from its tidy form by one character was rewritten every 45 seconds forever with nothing changing but the timestamp. 2,260 tests passing, 16 of them new, including one that reproduces the exact runaway shape from your database and proves it heals once and then goes quiet, and one that reproduces the launch trap itself so nothing else can copy records back in the same way.'],t.s)
B.aTM=new A.v(992,B.aor)
B.alE=s(['YOUR DESIGN, BUILT EXACTLY AS YOU WROTE IT: "if I click anywhere else it stops." Click inside an item, click Dictate here, and it dictates into that item. Click the button again and it stops. Click ANYWHERE else - another item, the dictation box, the folder pane, empty space, a different screen - and it stops. There is no list of special cases and no exceptions to remember.','THIS IS YOUR OWN ANSWER TO YOUR OWN QUESTION, and it is better than mine was. You asked yesterday: "if you forget where the item is that you are recording to you won\'t be able to shut it off." Build 990 made the lost item findable - the top button names it and stops it from a fixed place. You went one better and removed the possibility: dictation into an item cannot outlive your attention on that item. You can no longer walk away from a live microphone by accident.',"SCROLLING IS NOT CLICKING, AND THAT IS THE PART I WAS MOST CAREFUL WITH. Nudging the wheel or flicking the list mid-sentence must never cut you off - that would lose the end of a thought and feel like the app being unreliable. So the app waits for you to LET GO before deciding: a click is a press and a release in the same place. A scroll travels far past that and is not a click; a mouse wheel does not press anything at all. Every uncertain case leaves the microphone running rather than stopping it, because losing words is the worse mistake.","THE LAST WORDS YOU SPEAK BEFORE YOU CLICK AWAY NOW GO INTO THAT ITEM. This was a real hole and it is the one thing in this build you could actually have been bitten by. Your final phrase is still travelling back from the transcription service for about a second after you stop - and the click that stops you also takes the item off the screen a moment later, so those words had nowhere to land and were saved as a SEPARATE NEW ITEM instead. Nothing was ever lost, but the end of your sentence became an item of its own, which is indistinguishable from a loss when you go looking for it. The tail now merges into the item you were dictating into, using the same merge the app has used since July, whose fallback is to save the words as their own note rather than drop them.",'THE STOP BUTTON IS NOT "ANYWHERE ELSE". Build 990\'s "Recording into: <item>" button sits outside the item, so it needed saying explicitly: clicking it runs its own stop, rather than being stopped halfway through its own click and restarting on the release. That is the exact bug that took six attempts on the Dictate here button, and it is written down and tested this time rather than discovered later.',"IT DOES NOT START A NEW RECORDING WHEN YOU CLICK THE DICTATION BOX. It stops, and it stays stopped. Starting a recording you did not ask for is the worse error. And going back to an item and clicking Dictate here carries straight on with it, exactly as you would expect.",'Build 990\'s "Recording into" button stays, because you can still scroll the item out of sight - that case is now rare rather than routine, and something on screen must always say the microphone is open. 18 new tests, and the ones that matter most are the ones proving a scroll of 20, 60, 200 and 900 pixels never stops you.'],t.s)
B.aWX=new A.v(991,B.alE)
B.ase=s(['YOUR REPORT, TWICE: "STILL LIGHTING UP THE MAIN DICTATE BUTTON WHEN DICTATING INSIDE OF AN EXISTING ITEM." It does not any more. When your words are going into an item, the big button at the top of the dictation box no longer turns red - it turns into a blue outlined button that reads "Recording into: <the item\'s name>".','YOUR PRINCIPLE WAS THE RIGHT ONE: "wherever the focus is is where the dictation button should show as red." Red now means one thing and one place - THIS is where your words are landing. So while you are dictating into an item, the red is on that item\'s own Dictating pill and nowhere else, and the top button says where the words went instead of pretending they came to it.','AND YOUR SECOND QUESTION IS WHY IT NAMES THE ITEM: "if you forget where the item is that you are recording to you won\'t be able to shut it off." That is the real risk, and greying the button out would have made it worse. So the top button always tells you the destination by name, in the same fixed spot, and TAPPING IT STOPS THE RECORDING - whatever it is recording into, whether that item is on screen, scrolled far away, or filed in a folder you cannot remember. One control, one place, ends any recording. You never have to find anything to stop being recorded.',"THERE IS A SMALL ARROW BESIDE THE NAME if you want to go and look at the item rather than stop. It is deliberately the smaller target: stopping is the urgent thing and it gets the whole button.",'WHY IT LOOKED IDENTICAL BEFORE, plainly: the button was asking one question - "is the microphone on?" - to decide both what it DOES and what it SHOWS. Those are different questions. What it shows now comes from where the words are going; what it does when you tap it still comes from whether the engine is running, which is what guarantees it can always stop. Long item names are shortened so the button never changes size or moves the box under it.',"Full suite of 2,224 tests passing, 33 of them new on this one control - including that exactly one thing on screen ever reads as the destination, that something ALWAYS shows the microphone is open even when the item is nowhere in sight, and that the top button still stops a recording aimed at an item you cannot see."],t.s)
B.aTC=new A.v(990,B.ase)
B.ajI=s(['YOUR REPORT: "CREATING A NEW DICTATION IN THE DICTATION BOX SHOULD RESET THE ELAPSED TIMER." It does now. Say "new item", tap the check mark, use the + menu - the number beside the type badge goes back to 0:00 and starts counting the dictation in front of you.',"WHAT IT HAD BEEN COUNTING WAS THE SITTING, NOT THE DICTATION. When the timer came back two builds ago I anchored it to the moment the microphone opened, because that was the honest answer to the older complaint - a timer that runs while dictation is off is a lie. But you dictate for an hour without ever touching Off: a spoken boundary closes one item and opens the next with the microphone rolling straight through. So the number kept climbing across all of them and told you how long you had been at your desk. Right question, wrong thing being measured.","IT STILL SHOWS NOTHING WHEN THE MICROPHONE IS OFF. That is the rule you reported in July and it has not been touched - the number is drawn only while the engine is genuinely capturing, so it cannot sit on an idle note climbing again. The reset moves where the count starts from; it does not change when the count is allowed to appear.","ONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION: RESUMING. If the five-minute quiet timer switched you off, or you tapped Off and came back within ten minutes, your next words go back into the SAME item - so that is one dictation with a gap in the middle, not two, and the clock carries on from the figure it stopped at rather than restarting. The minutes the microphone spent shut are NOT added in: stop at 2:10, come back six minutes later, and it reads 2:10. A break you took is not time you dictated.",'A paragraph break is not a new dictation, so "new paragraph" and "new line" leave the clock running - they break the text, not the item. 27 new tests on this one control, including every spoken phrase that has to reset it and a check that the count still climbs past the hour instead of wrapping.'],t.s)
B.b_x=new A.v(989,B.ajI)
B.aoI=s(['YOUR REPORT, THE SIXTH TIME: "WHEN I CLICK INSIDE OF THE DICTATE HERE BUTTON IT DISAPPEARS AND DOESN\'T DICTATE." That word - disappears - is what finally found it, and I am sorry it took six. The button was deleting itself while your mouse button was still down.','HERE IS EXACTLY WHAT WAS HAPPENING. The Dictate here button only exists while the cursor is inside that item - that is what makes it appear when you click into the box. On Windows, pressing anything that is not a text field takes the cursor OUT of the text field. So the press that was meant to switch the microphone on removed the only reason the button was on screen: one frame later it was gone, with your finger still on the mouse. That is the whole bug, and it is why it looked different every time you reported it - "nothing happens", "doesn\'t activate for a while", "disappears". Whether it worked at all came down to whether the screen happened to redraw between your press and your release. On a phone it would have worked every time; on the desktop you work at all day it almost never did.',"AND THE CLICK WAS NOT BEING DROPPED - IT WAS BEING RUN AND THEN KILLED. This is the part I had never found by reading the code. The release still reached the button's handler, but by then the button no longer existed, so the very first thing that handler did failed with an error that goes nowhere you can see. The instruction to start dictating was never reached. Your log proves it: at 1:22 PM the cursor leaves the item and then there is simply nothing - no record of the command at all - while on the build before it, the same two moments are followed immediately by dictation starting.","THE FIX IS THAT THE BUTTON NOW SURVIVES ITS OWN PRESS. The moment you press it, it takes hold of its own place on the bar and stays there regardless of where the cursor went. You will watch it go from Dictate here, to Starting, to a red Dictating, in the same spot, without it moving or vanishing. It lets go only when you click into a different item - so you can switch the same item on and off as many times as you like, which is what you asked for two builds ago. The handler was also made safe to run on its own, so a click can never again be quietly swallowed by that error.","WHY FIVE FIXES MISSED IT, plainly: all five reasoned about the code and none of them ever clicked the button. The tests that were meant to cover it could not have caught this for two reasons, both now fixed. They pressed and released the mouse in the same instant, with no redraw in between - and that gap is the entire bug. And they all ran as a phone, where taking the cursor out of a text field does not happen at all. The new tests click the way a hand actually clicks, on Windows, against the real button in a real item.","THE THINGS YOU ASKED ME NOT TO BREAK ARE PINNED. The button still does not change size or move when it turns on, the main dictation box still has nothing to do with any of this, and arming a second item still moves your dictation rather than starting a second one. Full suite of 2,165 tests passing."],t.s)
B.b_E=new A.v(988,B.aoI)
B.ag5=s(['YOUR REPORT: "WANT TO BE ABLE TO DICTATE DIRECTLY INTO AN ITEM. THAT IS THE CORE." Dictating into an item now behaves like its own thing, with the main dictation box left completely out of it. Click inside an item, click Dictate here, and everything that happens, happens in THAT item. Nothing else on the screen moves.',"THE WORDS WERE ALREADY LANDING IN THE RIGHT ITEM. I want to be plain about that, because it matters for trusting this: the capability was working. Your speech was going into the item you armed, appearing in its text as you talked, saving about a second after each phrase, and surviving a restart. What was broken was everything AROUND it - the screen behaved as though the main box was still involved, so it never felt like dictating into an item, and it was fair to conclude it did not work.",'THREE SEPARATE THINGS JUMPED ON ONE CLICK, and they fought each other. The main dictation box vanished the moment you armed an item, which yanked the whole list up the screen by the height of a box - so the item you had just aimed at moved out from under your cursor. Then a destination line appeared above it and pushed everything back DOWN. That is your "makes the main dictate box move": it did not slide anywhere, it disappeared from the top and your live words showed up further down, which reads exactly like the box moving there. Both are gone. The main box now stays where it is, at the size it was, whatever you aim the microphone at.',"AND THE MAIN BOX WAS SHOWING YOU YOUR OWN WORDS IN THE WRONG PLACE. This is the one I would have missed by only looking at the jumping. The box asked one question to decide whether the words were its own - is the cursor in an item? - and clicking Dictate here DROPS that cursor. So a heartbeat after your click the box decided the words were its again and started painting them: your sentence appeared in the main box, growing it line by line and shoving the list down as you spoke, then vanished from there as each phrase finalised into the item where it actually belonged. Words appearing in one box and then disappearing from it is indistinguishable from losing them. The box now asks the right question - are these words mine? - and an armed item counts for just as much as the cursor does.","NOTHING WAS AT RISK IN ANY OF THIS, and I checked that before changing it rather than after. Every phrase is written to the recording buffer before any of this display logic is consulted, and if the item you armed is not on screen to receive your words they still fall back into the main box, which still shows them. The change decides what gets PAINTED, never where anything goes. The main box also stays on screen now in cases where it used to hide - which is strictly safer, since hiding it was the one thing that could leave live words with nowhere to appear.",'THE DICTATE HERE BUTTON ITSELF IS UNTOUCHED. You said it works fine now, so I did not go near its click handling, its size or its wording. One thing did get added next to it: the app now times how long it takes from your click to the microphone actually being live, and writes the number down. That question - "why does it not activate for a while" - has never had a real measurement behind it, only arithmetic on time limits, and I would rather answer it with your number than my guess.',"13 new tests, and they are written as the feature rather than as the bug: that the main box cannot be moved, resized or hidden by anything to do with where dictation is aimed - it is no longer even possible to express that question where the decision is made - that it will not paint words belonging to an item, that it still catches words when the item cannot, that arming a second item moves the target instead of forking a second dictation, and that a note growing under live dictation still cannot flip its own editor out from under the fresh words."],t.s)
B.b1h=new A.v(987,B.ag5)
B.acX=s(['YOUR REPORT: "IF IT IS WORKING WHY NOT GIVE A PROGRESS UPDATE RATHER THAN STATING IT IS RECONNECTING TO SYNC." You were right, and your machine was fine the whole time. It had been off sync overnight and came back to a backlog of about a thousand changes, and it was clearing them at roughly a hundred every few seconds - while telling you it was reconnecting. It was working perfectly and reporting a fault.','IT NOW SHOWS YOU THE NUMBER, GOING DOWN. "Syncing - 573 of 961 changes left" - the same catch-up, counted, against the size it started from, so you can watch it shrink instead of guessing. That was your instinct and it is the right one: a number that falls is reassuring, and the word "reconnecting" is alarming.','WHAT IT WAS ACTUALLY READING WAS THE WRONG THING. It looked at one flag - "is the connection up right now" - and nothing else. Under a burst that size the server closes and reopens sockets constantly, so that flag blinks off for a moment many times a minute. One blink was enough to replace the progress with an error message. The honest signal was sitting right next to it: changes only leave the queue when the server has CONFIRMED receiving them, so a falling queue is proof the connection is working, whatever the flag says. A single blink can no longer change what you are told; the fault now has to last, and the queue has to actually stop, before anything alarming appears.','AND THE THREE THINGS THAT CAN GO WRONG ARE NOW TOLD APART, because they need different things from you. Genuinely offline and nothing moving: it says reconnecting, and it will fix itself. Connected but the queue has not moved for a minute and a half: that is a REAL fault and it now says so in its own words - "Sync is stuck" - which the app has never reported before, though it has happened to you (2,408 changes that could not leave, for hours, in silence). Paused because you are dictating: it says that too, rather than looking stuck. And nothing at all when everything is up to date.','THE "UP TO DATE AT" TIME COULD ALSO LIE, and did. In Settings, whenever the upload flag happened to be off for a moment, the line read "Synced - up to date at 11:47 PM" - last night\'s time, while a thousand changes were landing. Two false things in one sentence. That line can now only appear when the queue is genuinely empty. The same fix reached the Settings line that claimed "All recordings uploaded to the server" from an empty count it had failed to read.',"THIS WAS THE FOURTH TIME IN ONE DAY the app stated something untrue about its own state - after the audio message that blamed a feature that did not exist yet, the mic-lost alarm that was measuring loudness, and the red recording frame over a dead microphone. Every one of them was a status worked out from a signal that merely LOOKS like the thing it is reporting. The whole rule now lives in one place with 33 tests on it, including your exact log replayed reading by reading, which must never produce the alarming message."],t.s)
B.aTh=new A.v(986,B.acX)
B.ahD=s(['YOUR REPORT: "THE DICTATE HERE INSIDE OF THE ITEMS STILL DOESN\'T WORK." It works now, and I owe you a straight answer about why the last build did not fix it. Build 982 fixed how that button LOOKS and what it does once it is running. It never fixed the click itself. You were clicking it and the click was being thrown away before anything of mine ever heard it.','HERE IS WHAT WAS EATING YOUR CLICKS, and it is the third time this exact thing has bitten you. The coloured bar at the top of an item is also the handle you drag an item into a folder with. Windows decides a mouse is DRAGGING rather than clicking after it moves just ONE pixel while the button is down - and a hand on a small target always moves a pixel or two. So the bar said "this is a drag, it is mine", and every button sitting on that bar was silently cut off mid-click. Nothing happened, no error, nothing to find. A finger gets eighteen pixels of room, which is why your phone was never affected and only the desktop was.',"YOU HAVE REPORTED THIS THREE TIMES ABOUT THREE DIFFERENT BUTTONS - the type dropdown, the item number, and now Dictate here. Each time only the one button you named was fixed and the rest were left, which is exactly why it kept coming back. So this time, at your instruction, EVERY button on that bar was fixed together: Dictate here, the fold arrow, the headphones, the pin, the remove-from-folder circle, the type dropdown and the item number. There are no known dead buttons left on that bar.","DRAGGING STILL WORKS EXACTLY AS BEFORE. That was the thing most at risk here, because these buttons live on the bar precisely BECAUSE the bar is the drag handle. A real drag still lifts the item and still drops it into a folder; a click is now just a click. And clicking a button on the bar still does not select the item, the way you asked back in build 632 - that used to happen for free as a side effect, and it would have quietly broken in this change, so it is now written down and tested rather than inherited.","33 new tests, and the important ones are the ones that FAIL on the old code: they click each button with the mouse drifting 0, 2, 5 and 10 pixels, they check a real 60-pixel drag still lifts the row instead of pressing the button, and they check a finger still works. The two-pixel case is the one that reproduces exactly what you have been seeing."],t.s)
B.aWs=new A.v(985,B.ahD)
B.alH=s(['YOUR REPORT: "I THINK I JUST LOST A DICTATION." Nothing was lost - but what happened to you was close enough that you could not have known the difference, and it was a real bug. This is the second half of that morning, kept back for its own fix.','HERE IS WHAT YOU SAW. You had words typed in the dictation box. You clicked Dictate - either the main button or the "Dictate here" button on an item - and the box went empty. Your words were saved, correctly, as their own item. But they were saved as a plain NOTE, whatever the box had been saying. So a half-typed To Sort, Task or Meeting vanished from where you were looking and reappeared further down the list as something you never made. Text gone from one place and unrecognisable in another is indistinguishable from a loss at the moment it happens.',"IT NOW KEEPS THE TYPE, and it asks the same question every other save asks. That one line was the LAST place in the app still deciding an item type by hand instead of reading the badge you were looking at - the ninth time in a week that exact shape has cost you something. To Sort stays To Sort, Task stays Task, Meeting stays Meeting.",'AND IT CAN NO LONGER COME BACK AT YOU LATER. This was also the only save in the whole app that never told the draft store its words had become an item - so those words stayed on disk marked "still being typed". After an app update or a restart they could be put back into the dictation box, and posted a second time from there. That is the duplicate-item shape you have reported before, reaching through a restart. It now records the post the moment it makes it, the same as everywhere else.',"ONE DELIBERATE LIMIT, and it is the privacy rule rather than an oversight: this particular save will never turn your words into a Claude item. A Claude item SENDS, and this save happens because you tapped something ELSE - it is a rescue, not a decision to send. So it keeps your words as a note, visible and one tap from being sorted to Claude if that is where they belong. Sending on a guess is not a thing this app will do.","22 new tests hold it: that each type survives this path, that no combination of settings can make it send, that the draft store learns about the post, and that the same words cannot be filed twice."],t.s)
B.aV7=new A.v(984,B.alH)
B.af5=s(['YOUR REPORT: "MIC LOST" NO LONGER APPEARS JUST BECAUSE YOU STOPPED TALKING. Your words: "while dictating in dictation box the red button at top sometimes says mic lost but then speaking makes it go away." It went away when you spoke because speaking was literally the thing it was waiting for.',"WHAT IT WAS ACTUALLY MEASURING WAS LOUDNESS, NOT WHETHER THE MICROPHONE WAS STILL THERE. A live microphone in a silent room still sends the app a continuous stream of audio - that audio just happens to be near-silent. A dead microphone sends nothing at all. Those are two completely different facts, and the watchdog was only looking at the first: four seconds without a sound loud enough to count, and it went red. So every pause to think looked exactly like the microphone being ripped away, and only your next word could clear it.",'IT NOW WATCHES THE MICROPHONE, NOT THE ROOM. As far as "is the microphone still there" is concerned, silence is now treated exactly like speech. Four seconds with no audio arriving AT ALL is still a loss, still red, still caught within a few seconds - a real loss has to stay loud.',"THE ONE CASE WHERE SILENCE REALLY DOES MEAN TROUBLE IS STILL CAUGHT. When a phone call takes the microphone, Android keeps handing the app audio and quietly makes every bit of it digital zero - frames arriving, nothing in them. That still turns the light red. It just gets a thirty-second window instead of four seconds, which no pause for thought can reach.","AND WHEN THE MICROPHONE COMES BACK, IT SAYS SO IMMEDIATELY. Your phone's log holds the other half of this: the microphone was successfully re-acquired, and the red stayed up for another 36 seconds until you happened to speak. A recovered microphone now clears the moment audio flows again, not the moment you talk.","THE ALARM ALSO LEAVES A TRACE NOW. It wrote nothing to the log at all before, so after the fact there was no way to tell a genuine seizure from a pause - the one thing that would have answered this in a minute. Three separate places were making the same mistake (the live engine and both Whisper engines); they now share one rule in one place. Nine new tests hold both directions, because a fix that only silenced the false alarm could quietly have disabled the real one. A false alarm is not harmless: it teaches you to ignore the indicator, and then the day the microphone really does die, you scroll straight past it."],t.s)
B.aSe=new A.v(983,B.af5)
B.amy=s(['YOUR REPORT: THE "DICTATE HERE" BUTTON IS A REAL ON/OFF SWITCH NOW. Click inside an item and the button appears, exactly as before. Click it and that item goes into dictate mode - the button turns RED and reads "Dictating", in the same place it was already sitting. Click it again and dictation stops. Your words go into THAT item, not into a new one.','AND IT NO LONGER JUMPS OUT FROM UNDER YOUR CURSOR - this was the real bug behind "it does not do anything and then acts weird". The instant you clicked it, the button threw its own label away and shrank to a bare microphone icon, so the thing you had just clicked slid sideways; at the same moment a second "dictating here" label appeared beside it and shoved the row along again. The button now holds room for the longest word it can ever say, in every state, so turning it on changes the colour and the wording and moves nothing whatsoever. A control that moves under your finger is how you mis-tap, so it was worth fixing properly rather than nudging.','RED ONLY EVER MEANS IT IS ACTUALLY RECORDING. The button is asked to switch on a beat before the microphone truly opens, and if the microphone fails to open it never goes red at all - it reads "Starting", then goes back to offering. If another app takes the microphone mid-sentence it reads "Mic lost", and in commands mode it reads "Paused". That is the same rule that came out of the night a red frame sat over a microphone Windows had already closed.',"TURNING IT OFF MEANS OFF. Before this, switching it off left the microphone running and quietly re-aimed it at the main dictation box - so you would stop dictating into the item and carry on being recorded somewhere you were not looking. Off now stops dictation. And because switching it off is you saying you are finished with that item, the next time you turn dictation on you get a fresh item rather than carrying on into the one you just closed.","14 new tests hold the two promises that matter here: that the button is exactly the same size in all five of its states, and that red is never shown for a microphone that is not actually capturing."],t.s)
B.aTI=new A.v(982,B.amy)
B.afE=s(["YOUR REPORT: STOPPING AND STARTING AGAIN NO LONGER SPLITS YOUR THOUGHT IN TWO. Turn dictation off and back on, and your next words carry on in the SAME item instead of opening a fresh one. The box even tells you so before you speak - it names the item your words are about to continue.","HERE IS WHAT WAS ACTUALLY STOPPING YOU, because it was not the Off button. Your phone's own log shows it eight times: the five-minute quiet timer fired, the microphone let go, and the note you had open was filed as finished. You had not finished it - you had paused to think, or walked away for a minute. Then tapping On gave you a clean box, because as far as the app was concerned that thought was over. It was a timer deciding you were done.",'THE DIFFERENCE IT NOW DRAWS: if YOU say you are finished, you are. The New button, saying "new item" or "new dictation" or "new note", picking a type from the plus menu - all still start a brand new item, exactly as before. But a stop you did not ask for - the quiet timer, Off then On, the microphone reconnecting - picks up where you left off. Deliberate means new; incidental means continue.',"AFTER TEN MINUTES IT STARTS FRESH AGAIN. Coming back inside ten minutes is plainly the same train of thought; coming back after lunch is not, and gluing this morning's note onto tonight's would be its own bug.","NOTHING IS DUPLICATED AND NOTHING IS RE-TYPED. Your earlier words are NOT put back into the box - they are already a saved item, and text sitting in two places at once is exactly how duplicate items get made. Only the new words are added, onto the end of the item you were in. If that item cannot be found for any reason, your words post as their own note rather than going anywhere near lost.","21 new tests hold both halves of this: that Off then On continues the same item, that New and every spoken boundary still start a new one, that a pause to think does not close anything, and what the quiet timer does at four minutes, at five, and when you set it to zero for never."],t.s)
B.aUw=new A.v(981,B.afE)
B.afu=s(["YOUR REPORT: THE ELAPSED TIME IS BACK WHILE YOU RECORD. Start dictating and the running length sits beside the type badge again - 0:00, 0:01, 0:02 - so you can see at a glance how long you have been talking. It counts past the hour rather than wrapping, so a 90-minute session reads 90:xx and not 30:xx.",'AND HERE IS WHY IT WENT MISSING, because you are owed the whole story: YOU asked me to remove it, on July 28th - "there is a timer next to the item type label and selector, it is running now on the Fold for a note and the dictate function is not on." You were right. It was counting from when the note opened, not from when the microphone opened, so it climbed all day on an idle box and reported recording time for something recording nothing. I deleted it. That fixed the false number by giving up the true one, and ten days later you noticed the hole.',"THE NUMBER WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM - THE CLOCK WAS SET TO THE WRONG THING. It now starts the instant the microphone actually opens and disappears the instant it closes, so it cannot tick on an idle note again. Both of your reports are satisfied at once instead of one at the expense of the other.","THE ITEM WINDOW TICKS TOO. Opening a recording that is still running from the Listening area showed a length frozen at the moment you opened it, while the recording kept going. It counts live now, and it is the same clock the dictation box and the task and meeting boxes use, so the four can never disagree.","It was deleted silently and nothing noticed for ten days, which was the real fault. There are 21 new tests on that number now - that it shows while recording, that it never shows while the mic is off, that it keeps ticking, and that it reads correctly past the hour."],t.s)
B.b_m=new A.v(980,B.afu)
B.aj3=s(['YOUR ASK: THE ITEM NUMBER IN THE FULL-SIZED VIEW COPIES NOW, exactly like the one on the box. Hover it on Windows and the little copy button fades in beside it; one click and it is on your clipboard, with the same "#133771 copied" message you already know. On the phone, tap it - same as always.',"It is not a lookalike, it is the SAME control. The number was being drawn in three separate places - the box bar, the full-sized item, and the item window from the Listening area - and only the box bar could be copied. Rather than paste the copy button into the other two and leave three copies of it to drift apart, all three now run one piece of code, so the button, the message and the clipboard can never disagree again. The Listening-area item window got the copy for free on the way past.","AND IT QUIETLY FIXED THE BOX ONE TOO. The number on the item bar had the same one-pixel problem the type dropdown had in build 975: that bar can be dragged into a folder, so a mouse click that wobbled a pixel was read as a drag and the copy never fired. It reads the click directly now, on every view, while a real drag still lifts the row and still swipes between items."],t.s)
B.b2k=new A.v(979,B.aj3)
B.aln=s(['YOUR REPORT: an item told you it had no audio "because the feature wasn\'t available when it was recorded" - and you had recorded it the night before. You were right and the app was wrong twice: wrong about the date, and wrong that the audio was gone. Your 52-minute dictation was sitting safely on your phone the whole time. That sentence was a guess about the calendar, and it was false for every item it was ever shown on - audio upload has worked since July 12th, with everything older backfilled the day after, so nothing in your library predates it.',"IT NOW TELLS YOU THE TRUTH, AND WHERE YOUR AUDIO IS. If the recording has not reached the cloud yet it says so plainly: the audio is still on the phone (or the Windows PC) that recorded it, nothing is lost, the full transcript is right there - open that item on that device to listen, and leave that device open and online for a few minutes and it will send itself up, after which it plays everywhere. If there IS a cloud copy and it just could not be fetched, it says that instead and gives you a Try again button. A typed note simply says it was typed.","AND IT IS NOT RED ANY MORE when nothing is wrong. Audio still waiting on the device that recorded it was being shown in the same alarm red as a real failure, which read like your recording had been lost. Red is now kept for the case that actually failed."],t.s)
B.aTm=new A.v(978,B.aln)
B.aiM=s(['FIXED, YOUR REPORT: saying "new dictation" made the next item a Note instead of following the previous one. The type memory was working - this one command was overruling it. Saying "new dictation" was quietly filed as "make this a plain NOTE", the same as picking Note from the menu, and a pick beats a memory everywhere in the app. So the one thing you say between thoughts silently reset your type, and worse, it BANKED that Note as the new memory - which is why everything after it came back a Note too.','Now "new dictation" means only what it sounds like: close this one, open the next, keep the type. Say "new NOTE" when you actually want a plain note - naming the type is still how you change it, and it still works.','ALSO FIXED, same cause one tap away: turning dictation OFF on a To Sort, Task or Meeting box saved it as a Note. That last save was the only one still deciding the type by hand instead of asking the same rule every other surface asks. Off, "end dictation" and the check mark now all agree with the badge you were looking at.','NEW COMMAND - "NEW ITEM". The type-neutral one, and the honest name for what you actually want between thoughts: it closes the item you are in and opens the next AS THE SAME TYPE. Set the box once, then just keep talking. "New dictation" still works and means the same thing - your muscle memory is not something to break to win an argument about words.','TO SORT FINALLY HAS A VOICE COMMAND: say "SORT LATER" (or "sort it later" / "sort this later") and the next item lands grey, to be typed later. Deliberately NOT "new to sort" - that phrase falls apart in a car, coming back as "to sport", "to short", "resort", even "Tucson". "Sort later" says the intent and sounds like nothing else in the list. ("new to sort" still works as an alias.)','EVERY COMMAND NOW ACCEPTS THE WAYS THE ENGINE MANGLES IT. "New meaning" starts a meeting. "New tax" and "new ask" start a task. "New node" and "new nope" start a note. "And task" ends a task. BEING STRAIGHT WITH YOU: these are reasoned from how speech engines usually fail, NOT from your car. The hour that makes this list real is you saying each phrase while driving and me reading back what actually got captured - then I replace my guesses with the truth.','THE SEND COMMAND IS DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT, and this is the important part. Every other command only LABELS something - guess wrong and it is one tap to fix. "Send to Claude" SENDS your words off the phone and cannot be taken back. So it accepts plenty of full mishearings ("sent to Claude", "send to clawed", "send to clod"), because a MISS is safe - nothing happens, your words stay in the box, you say it again. But it will never fire on a fragment. "Send it", "send that", or "send" on its own will never send, however convenient that would be. Every phrase that sends has to have Claude\'s name in it.','ONE PHRASE FROM THE PLAN DID NOT SHIP: "next item". You already own that one - it is how you skip forward while the reader is reading to you. Taking it would have silently broken that. "New item" is unambiguous and does the same job.',"Nothing will ever guess Claude on its own - that would send it - so a Claude item still needs you to say so. And an unrecognised phrase always leaves its words in your transcript: a missed command costs you a repeat, never a sentence."],t.s)
B.aTz=new A.v(976,B.aiM)
B.afL=s(["THE TYPE DROPDOWN ACTUALLY OPENS NOW. You were right that it did nothing, and it was nothing you were doing wrong: on Windows a mouse only has to drift ONE pixel while the button is down before Windows calls it a drag instead of a click. The item bar can be dragged into a folder, so that one-pixel wobble was being handed to the drag and the badge never heard the click at all. A finger gets eighteen pixels of room, which is why it only ever failed on the desktop. The badge now reads the click directly instead of competing for it, so a normal hand-click opens the menu every time - while a real drag still lifts the row.",'And tapping the badge no longer ALSO opens the item full screen. It was the one control on that bar that never told the box "this tap was mine".','The "Claude - sends now" line in that menu was being cut off at the right edge. It fits now - that line is the warning, so it was the worst possible thing to clip.','THE X ON THE YELLOW "MIC STOPPED" BANNER WORKS. So does tapping the banner itself to start dictating again. The whole banner was being drawn in a layer that is invisible to the mouse - it had to be, because that layer also holds the grey "Type or press Dictate to start" text, which must let your taps through to the box underneath. The banner is now outside that layer and the grey text is still inside it, so both do the right thing.'],t.s)
B.b38=new A.v(975,B.afL)
B.afN=s(['GO TO THE ITEM THAT IS PLAYING - idea 1, your pick. Scroll away from the item being read and a small blue "now playing" pill appears at the bottom of the folder with its title on it; tap it and the list glides straight back. It stays completely hidden while the item is already on screen, so it costs you nothing until you actually need it. It works even if you have scrolled miles away and the item is long gone from memory - it remembers where the item sat and flies there first.','AND THE LEFT PANE NOW SHOWS WHICH FOLDER IS TALKING - idea 3. The folder currently playing gets a small pulsing blue dot next to its headphones. Tap that folder row and you land inside it, where the pill finishes the trip to the item. The headphones icon itself is untouched - it still means "in the reader" and still toggles the whole subtree, because making one picture mean two things would have been worse than no picture.',"The dot only marks a FOLDER you started. Playing the Listening list or the Inbox marks nothing, since neither is a folder, and it clears the moment the audio stops."],t.s)
B.b0S=new A.v(974,B.afN)
B.agW=s(["PAUSE A FOLDER WHILE IT IS PLAYING - your ask, exactly as you framed it. The play button at the top of a folder now turns into a PAUSE button while it is reading, and tapping it again picks up right where you stopped. It used to be a Stop button, which was the only transport in the app that threw away your place mid-item; every other one - the Now Playing screen, the listening list, your lock screen and the glasses - already worked this way.","Pausing also banks your spot, so coming back to that folder later resumes mid-item instead of restarting it."],t.s)
B.aVu=new A.v(973,B.agW)
B.amY=s(["CHANGE ANY ITEM'S TYPE, RIGHT ON THE ITEM. The type badge on every box is now a dropdown - tap Note, pick Task, done. A mis-typed item gets fixed in place instead of re-dictated. To Sort is a target too, so you can park anything for a later decision.","CLAUDE IS A ONE-WAY DOOR, your call and the right one: you can turn anything INTO a Claude item, never out of one. Those words are already at my desk and cannot be un-sent, and every Claude thing on the box - the status line, my answers, the orange border - only draws on a Claude box, so relabelling one would hide a live conversation while it kept running. A Claude box keeps the plain badge so you can see the rule, and it is enforced underneath too, where a second device cannot slip past it. Turning something INTO Claude still sends at the tap, and that menu row still says so.","FIXED, YOUR REPORT: setting an item to To Sort inside a folder, then dictating the next one, gave you a Note. Being inside a folder was treated as if you had asked for a note, and that assumption outranked what you actually picked. Your type now holds in a folder exactly like it does in the Inbox. One exception on purpose: a folder will never quietly make something a Claude item, because that would send it.","ALSO: the + menu inside a folder was missing To Sort - the Inbox menu had it, its folder twin never got it. Both doors offer the same five types now."],t.s)
B.b3Y=new A.v(972,B.amY)
B.ae2=s(["A CLAUDE ITEM WITH A HOME NO LONGER SITS IN YOUR INBOX. Your words: if it is already linked to another folder, take it off the Inbox - and if you create one INSIDE a folder, never put it there at all. Both are now the rule. Every Claude item quietly files itself into the Claude folder at birth, and that automatic filing was what kept it pinned to the Inbox forever; from now on that only applies to items that have nowhere else to be. An item you filed - by making it in a folder, or dragging it there - leaves the Inbox exactly like a note does.","Nothing hides that you did not file: a Claude item created in the Inbox stays put through its whole round trip, replies and all, exactly as before. I also cleared the 356 already-filed Claude items off your Inbox tonight (they are untouched in their folders), which is why the Claude count dropped from 369 to 13."],t.s)
B.aVq=new A.v(971,B.ae2)
B.asx=s(["THE DIG DELIVERS A VERDICT AND THE FIRST CUT. Last night's meters caught the payer red-handed: when 666 boxes came to life during one scrolling burst, their FIRST layout cost 2.9 of the 6 frozen seconds - one giant note alone took a third of a second - while boxes already alive re-laid-out for practically nothing. Scrolling pain IS the price of waking boxes up, exactly as suspected.",'First cut, aimed by those numbers: every waking box was measuring its ENTIRE transcript - however long - just to decide whether to show the "more..." line, even though the box never displays past its 10-line cap. A box past the cap now skips the measurement entirely (the answer was already known). Zero visual change, every platform. The meters stay on: your next scroll session tells us how much this cut bought and whether the second suspect - the editor each box carries - still needs the knife.'],t.s)
B.aWj=new A.v(970,B.asx)
B.afI=s(['NEW: A "TO SORT" ITEM TYPE - your idea, built as you framed it: everything stays exactly as it was, and there is now a fifth type next to Note / Task / Meeting / Claude. Pick To Sort in the dictate box (the box remembers it, like any type) and just talk - every item lands grey, routes nowhere, and waits for you.','Sorting is one tap: the grey "To Sort" badge on each item is a dropdown - pick Note, Task, Meeting, or Claude and the item becomes that type right there. Sorting to Claude is the moment it sends (that menu row says "sends now") - nothing ships on its own, and an item sorted on one device stays sorted everywhere.',"To Sort items get auto-titles like notes do (a titled pile sorts faster), have their own view chip, left-pane row, and search chip. On a phone still on an older version they simply show as Notes until it updates - nothing hides."],t.s)
B.aZD=new A.v(969,B.afI)
B.ade=s(["THE DIG, STEP 2 - last night's first measurement flipped the case: the boxes' drawing CODE is innocent (63 boxes cost 18ms combined against one 342ms frame). The real suspect is the phase no code-timer sees: creating each box's live machinery and laying out its text. This build adds the meter for exactly that phase - first-layout cost (what scrolling pays) versus re-layout cost (what steady use pays) - still invisible, still every platform, and your normal use keeps feeding the case file.","When these numbers name the payer - machinery creation or text layout - the first real cut follows, aimed by evidence."],t.s)
B.b3U=new A.v(968,B.ade)
B.ara=s(["THE DIG BEGINS - your call, the right call. This build changes nothing you can see or feel: it adds precision cost-meters inside every item box, so the app itself records exactly which part of a box is expensive to draw - the editor wiring, the receipt strip, or something nobody suspects. Your normal use tomorrow IS the measurement: just work as usual, and the diagnostics log fills with the numbers that pick the first cut.","The plan from here: read your numbers, then make the heaviest part of the box lazy - built when you touch it instead of when it scrolls past - in thin slices, each proven by a fresh measurement, zero visual change, every platform. When a box costs 3ms instead of 25, the scrollbar thumb should simply work live, and the workarounds retire."],t.s)
B.b0L=new A.v(967,B.ara)
B.aqp=s(['THE SCROLLBAR, DONE RIGHT - Option 1, your pick. Grab the thumb and the list holds perfectly still; a blue ghost thumb and an "item 2,340 of 5,158" badge track exactly where you will land; let go and it jumps there once, instantly. Nothing builds and nothing changes height while your hand is down, so the stutter and the teleport are not fixed - they are impossible. This is the trade classic Windows made for decades, on the same physics.',"Clicking the empty track still pages one screen toward the click. Wheel and touch scrolling are unchanged, and the phones never see any of this - their bar stays display-only exactly as before."],t.s)
B.aVt=new A.v(966,B.aqp)
B.ae5=s(["SCRUB MODE ROLLED BACK - you said v964 was worse, and you were right. The light rows were a uniform height while your real rows are not, so grabbing the thumb collapsed the list's total length about 3x - the content teleported under your hand at the start AND end of every drag. My miss: the demo could not surface this because its list was uniform in both modes. v965 puts the thumb back to the v963 behavior (occasional stutter, no teleporting) while I build the fix that cannot have this flaw.","The honest path from here: Option 1 from the demo (list holds still while you drag, a badge shows where you will land, one clean jump at release) is structurally immune to both the stutter AND the teleport - nothing builds and nothing changes height while your hand is on the bar. Say the word and it ships tomorrow."],t.s)
B.aY_=new A.v(965,B.ae5)
B.arP=s(["SCRUB MODE - the Option 2 you picked on the demo, built exactly as it felt there. While your hand holds the scrollbar thumb, every row paints as a light one-liner (title only, uniform height) - cheap enough that live tracking cannot stutter, and the uniform heights make the thumb follow your hand exactly instead of wobbling as row sizes get discovered. Let go and the full rows fill back in, in one clean rebuild.","Desktop only; wheel, touch, and the phones are untouched. This stacks on the two earlier cuts (no runway during thumb drags, bare-viewport cache), so a thumb step now builds a handful of one-line rows instead of dozens of full items."],t.s)
B.aTY=new A.v(964,B.arP)
B.ajZ=s(["SCROLLBAR THUMB, ROUND TWO - v962 measured 5x better on your machine but you still felt it, and the numbers agreed: ~95ms of row-building per thumb step remained. The last multiplier was the resting cache - even unarmed, every step pre-built 3,000 pixels of runway (~25 rows) that the next step threw away. While your hand is on the bar the list now builds ONLY what is on screen; the cache comes back the moment you let go.","Wheel, touch, and phone scrolling are untouched. If this one still is not smooth under your hand, the next step is a light scrub mode (simplified rows while dragging the bar) - but that changes what you SEE, so it ships only after you approve the look."],t.s)
B.aS8=new A.v(963,B.ajZ)
B.arK=s(["DRAGGING THE SCROLLBAR THUMB - THE REAL FIX, measured on your machine after the driver update cleared the fog. With the GPU and database exonerated (both now clean), the remaining stutter was arithmetic: each thumb movement teleports thousands of pixels, so every step rebuilds a fresh screenful - and the scroll runway, armed for normal scrolling, was multiplying each rebuild about 7x by pre-building rows the very next teleport threw away.","A drag that starts on the scrollbar now skips the runway entirely: each thumb step builds only the rows you can see, cutting the per-step cost to a fraction. Wheel and touch scrolling keep their runway - it genuinely helps them. Desktop only."],t.s)
B.aUj=new A.v(962,B.arK)
B.atu=s(["THE SCROLL BAR NO LONGER HIDES BEHIND THE WINDOW EDGE - your report after v960. It now floats a few pixels inside the border and clear of the rounded corners, on Windows only.","ON THE REMAINING JUMPINESS when dragging the whole list by the thumb: measured on your machine, the stalls are NOT the scroll bar - they are database reads waiting 5 to 10 seconds behind sync writes landing at the same moment (normally a quarter second), on top of a graphics driver from 2021. Update the Intel driver (Windows Update > Optional updates > Drivers), then judge again - if it is still jumpy on a current driver, the next fix is throttling sync application while your hand is on the bar, and I have the playbook for that."],t.s)
B.aYw=new A.v(961,B.atu)
B.ajB=s(["THE SCROLL BAR NOW WORKS LIKE A REAL WINDOWS SCROLL BAR. Your report: thumb works good, grabbing the scroll bar does not. The truth: TWO scroll bars were stacked on the list - the styled one (made display-only back in v704 because on Android its touch zone was stealing your thumb flicks and killing flings) and an automatic one Flutter adds on desktop, whose thumb you were dragging but whose track ignores clicks. Now there is ONE bar: on Windows it is a real interactive scroll bar - drag the thumb, or click anywhere on the track and the list pages toward your click - drawn slightly wider so it is easier to grab.","ANDROID IS UNTOUCHED, deliberately: on phones the bar stays display-only exactly as v704 made it - every touch near the edge still belongs to the list, so the stolen-flick bug cannot return. The desktop-only switch is the same platform gate the v959 runway fix uses."],t.s)
B.aYQ=new A.v(960,B.ajB)
B.aeM=s(["WHEEL SCROLLING ON WINDOWS - THE SECOND, DEEPER FIX. After v958 you said still slow, so I drove the wheel on your machine myself and measured: the freeze was the scroll runway ARMING - the list pre-builds a 120,000-pixel runway the moment you touch the wheel, and at your library size that single rebuild took 4 to 8.6 SECONDS. That number was sized for phone flings, which travel thousands of pixels; a mouse wheel moves about 100 pixels per notch and never needs it.","Desktop now uses a runway one-tenth the size - still 13 screens of preload, far more than any wheel session consumes - so the pause when you start wheeling drops by the same factor. Phones keep the full fling runway; nothing changes on touch."],t.s)
B.aW5=new A.v(959,B.aeM)
B.alQ=s(["YOUR DUPLICATE ITEMS ARE GONE - and the cause is fixed. The split posted your 23 items but took seconds on a busy machine, the button still looked untapped, and every extra tap posted all 23 again - 14 taps made 326 copies. I removed the 299 extras from your library directly (kept the first of each; recoverable), and the Split button now vanishes the instant you tap it so a slow post can never invite a second tap.","MOUSE-WHEEL SCROLLING IS FIXED on Windows. Every wheel notch was arming the long scroll runway and a 700ms timer was handing it back BETWEEN notches - so each notch rebuilt about a thousand rows (your log showed 2-second builds per notch). Wheel scrolling now keeps the runway armed until 4 seconds after your last notch - one payment per scrolling session instead of one per click of the wheel. Touch scrolling is untouched.","THE CLAUDE STATUS REFRESH HAS BEEN SILENTLY FAILING EVERY 2 MINUTES FOR TWO DAYS on every device - your diagnostics caught it. The status query packed the id of every Claude item you have ever finished into one request, and when your library crossed roughly 5,000 items the request outgrew what the server accepts. It now reads in batches of 100, so it keeps working no matter how big your library gets.","ALSO FIXED: a crash when leaving a folder screen (it was also breaking the page-transition animation), caught in the same diagnostics you sent."],t.s)
B.aYA=new A.v(958,B.alQ)
B.ac3=s(['SPLIT INTO ITEMS NOW WORKS FOR DICTATED TEXT - your report ("the split item into xx items inside of the dictation box isn\'t working" - dictated). The honest cause: it was not broken, it was obeying a rule you set in v664 - back then, saying "new paragraph" made the app offer to cut your note in half mid-dictation, you reported THAT as the bug, and the offer was narrowed to pasted text only. Your workflow has inverted since: now you dictate several thoughts and want them split.','THE NEW RULE, as you approved it: while the mic is LIVE the offer never appears (that part of v664 stands - no mid-dictation noise); the moment the mic is off, two or more paragraphs offer "Split into N items" no matter how they got there - dictated, typed, pasted, or a restored draft. Say "new paragraph" between thoughts, stop the mic, tap the pill, and each thought becomes its own item.',"The rule is one tested function now, with the whole v550-v664-v957 history written beside it so it cannot silently flip again - and if it ever needs to flip, the story of why is right there."],t.s)
B.aSp=new A.v(957,B.ac3)
B.afU=s(['LIVE DICTATIONS NOW SAVE THEIR REAL LENGTH, not 0:00 - the safe half of the duplicate-items bug you said "Fix it" on. When you record with the live engine, the item is saved the instant you start (so a crash or a killed app can never lose your words) - but that first save had no length yet, and the length was never filled in at the end, so a 155-second recording sat in your files reading 0ms forever. Now the finished recording stamps its real length when you stop.',"This is additive and cannot lose a word - it only writes a number that was previously left blank, pinned by a test so it can never regress. It does NOT by itself stop the duplicate item you saw (that is the risky half, which needs one 30-second recording from your plugged-in phone to pin the exact cause before I touch the live capture path)."],t.s)
B.b2U=new A.v(956,B.afU)
B.abD=s(['TIMES NOW READ IN STANDARD 12-HOUR FORMAT, NOT MILITARY - your ask ("use standard time format, not military time"). A few places still showed 24-hour time: the Settings sync footer (up to date at 15:07), the Meeting and Segment row titles (Meeting \xb7 15:07), and the diagnostics log. They now read the way you say them - 3:07 PM, Meeting \xb7 3:07 PM, 11:05:09 PM.',"All of those now run through one shared time formatter instead of each spot rolling its own, so this can never drift apart again - pinned by a test that checks every hour of the day comes out 1 to 12 with AM/PM and never a 15 or a 23. The compact stamps that were already standard (the files list 5:32pm, the item-box date) are untouched."],t.s)
B.aTF=new A.v(955,B.abD)
B.aoX=s(["REPLY TO A CLAUDE ITEM WITHOUT OPENING IT. Your ask, built the way we agreed: not an always-on box on every card - that would turn the list into a wall of text fields - but reveal-on-tap. A card at rest looks exactly as it does today; tap Reply at the bottom of it and the composer opens right there with the caret ready. Send posts into that item conversation and the box closes again.","ONLY ONE CAN BE OPEN AT A TIME, so opening a reply on one card closes any other - the list never grows several tall editors, and you can never leave one open three screens up without noticing.","IT IS THE SAME COMPOSER THE FULL VIEW USES, not a copy. I extracted the full-view composer into one shared widget first, so Enter-to-send and the click-anywhere-to-type fix are one implementation - a fix to either lands on both surfaces at once, and they can never drift apart.","AND IF A SEND FAILS, YOUR WORDS STAY PUT. The box only clears and closes when the post actually lands; a failed send keeps the text on screen and the editor open. Pinned by a test, because losing typed words is the one thing this app is not allowed to do."],t.s)
B.aTK=new A.v(954,B.aoX)
B.adJ=s(["ASK CLAUDE CAN NOW READ YOUR CHANNEL (General Chat phase 4, the read slice you said go on). Until now the chat was a blank scratchpad that knew nothing about your world. Now it can look things up: it searches your items by words, lists your folders, and summarizes your Queue in the order you arranged it \u2014 and answers from your REAL data, referring to items by their number. Ask it what is in my queue or find my notes about cash flow and it actually checks.","It does this by calling read-only tools behind the scenes and answering from what they return. READ-ONLY, deliberately: this slice can look but never touch \u2014 it cannot create, change, or delete an item. Writing items from the conversation is a separate, later slice. Your chat thread is unchanged and still plain text; only the lookups happen under the hood.","Pinned by new tests covering the tool protocol and the whole tool-run loop (a tool that fails is reported back, never crashes the chat); whole suite green. One honest note: because the chat runs on YOUR Anthropic key, the very first time you ask it something about your items is the real proof \u2014 try what is in my queue and tell me if it reads right."],t.s)
B.b_Z=new A.v(953,B.adJ)
B.ahX=s(["YOU CAN NOW SELECT AND COPY ACROSS A WHOLE CONVERSATION. Your report was that you could only ever copy one message. The cause: every message opened its own selection region, and a region only selects inside itself - so each message was an island and a drag died at the first seam. The item now opens ONE region around the whole conversation and the per-message ones stand down inside it. Drag from your question through my answer through the status line and copy the lot. Links still open on one tap - that part is untouched.","FOLDER FILTER CHIPS - the visible half of a feature whose engine has been sitting finished since v937. In any folder holding Claude items you get a chip row: Feature, Bug, Tweak, Question, Untyped, plus Approved and Mockups. They stack the way you would expect - two types widen the net, a type plus a flag narrows it - and they combine with the state chips already on the queue overview above, so Needs-you AND Bug is one tap each. While a filter is on the row says showing 3 of 9, because a narrowed list that does not say so reads as items having gone missing.","THE CLEAR BUTTON IS PINNED, not scrolled - it stays on screen at the right of the chip row no matter how narrow the window, so the one control that undoes a filter can never be scrolled out of reach. That flaw was caught by its own test before it reached a screen.","ALSO: the delete button on item boxes has been anchored bottom-right since v551, but the code comments still claimed top-right - three hundred versions of stale prose that made it look unbuilt. Corrected. If your delete button is NOT bottom-right, that is a real bug and worth telling me."],t.s)
B.b0T=new A.v(952,B.ahX)
B.agc=s(['YOU CAN NOW SEE WHEN A NEW VERSION GOES OUT - your bug ("no release actually cuts and reaches my devices... surface a visible release status so I can watch it happen"). The truth I measured: releases WERE publishing every time - the newest build was always live on both the Windows and Android update channels - but nothing ever told you, so you never knew to pull it and sat on an old version while a dozen features shipped invisibly.','THE FIX (the headless half you approved): every time a release actually publishes, your "Release Does Not Trigger From My Go-Ahead" item now updates its own status line to name the version that just went live and exactly how to get it - Phone: Settings then Check for updates; Windows: fully close and reopen. So that one item becomes a live release ticker you can watch. No new items, no clutter - it just keeps naming the latest build.',"STILL TO COME (your call): the fuller in-app version - a banner with a one-tap Update now button - is a screen I need to see before it ships, so it lands in an attended session; and Android auto-pull so a plugged-in phone updates itself. Say in-app banner or leave it as the item ticker. This build itself is the first one that announces, so watch that item update to v951."],t.s)
B.aZR=new A.v(951,B.agc)
B.abV=s(['GROUNDWORK FOR THE EVIDENCE STREAM - your feature ("pretty much all of the data that you create while processing an item gets sent", "build the evidence stream", "Put it into a different tab"). Today the Activity tab captures the items own data, but NOT my actual work on it - what I checked, what I ruled out, my reasoning, the change I made. This build lays the core to capture that as an evidence note and, next, show it in its own tab. It rides the same synced store your design passes use, so no database migration.',"One thing waits on your call: an evidence note is a bounded summary (what I checked / found / ruled out / changed) so it never bloats sync. If you want the FULL raw detail (every diff and query), that is a bigger storage decision - say full detail and I spec it separately. Pinned by new tests, including a safety test that an evidence note can never be hidden before the tab exists to show it; whole suite green. The visible tab is the attended slice."],t.s)
B.b16=new A.v(950,B.abV)
B.aet=s(['GROUNDWORK FOR CREATE-A-MOCKUP IN THE MOCKUPS TAB - your feature ("add the tab by default and when clicking the tab show a button to be able to create mockups", "build a"). You picked option A: the Create button asks ME to make the mockup - you tap it, say what you want mocked, and I render and attach it. This build lays the logic behind that button: the create-mockup request and the "creating..." in-progress state, so the tab can show you a mockup is on its way (and clear itself the moment it lands) - exactly like the Design tab does after you tap Run.',"Pinned by new tests; whole suite green. What is left is the on-screen part - making the Mockups tab show by default (with a calm empty state) and drawing the Create button itself - which I verify on your screen, so it lands in an attended session. The always-on flip is a one-liner ready to go; the logic it needs is done now."],t.s)
B.aX9=new A.v(949,B.aet)
B.agL=s(['THE ASK-CLAUDE CHAT CAN NOW TURN A MESSAGE INTO A REAL ITEM (General Chat phase 3, groundwork) - your feature ("a general chat screen like the regular Claude Windows app has", "build it"). The chat is your fast, throwaway scratchpad; your items are the durable record you track. This build lays the wire that crosses between them: taking a chat message - or a whole question-and-answer exchange - and saving it out as a real Note, Task, Meeting, or Claude item, born exactly the way the \uff0b menu creates one.',"It is capture-first: every character of what you save survives the crossing (a single message goes in word-for-word; a saved exchange is labelled You:/Claude: so it reads as a conversation), and a saved Claude message also lands in your Claude folder automatically. Pinned by new tests that prove the item lands with the right type and the full text; whole suite green.","WHAT IS LEFT (next, attended): the visible Save button on each chat message that calls this - a small on-screen piece I need to verify on your actual screen, so it lands in an attended session. The hard part - making sure a saved item is correct and loses nothing - is done and tested now."],t.s)
B.aRm=new A.v(948,B.agL)
B.akj=s(['THE QUEUE COUNT NOW COUNTS ONLY REAL BUILD WORK - your feature ("the x of x que is showing 17 items, thought you were going to only include items that are valid and ready to be processed"). You were right: your QUESTIONS were being counted in the queue position number - the 17 you saw was exactly your question count. A question is answered, not built, so it should never take a spot in the build line.','THE FIX: the x of x number on your items (the "#3 of N") now skips questions entirely, exactly as it already skips unapproved and finished items. So the number finally means what you read it as - how many real items are actually in line to be built. It is the same rule your queue status pill already used (v914), now applied to the position number too, so the pill and the number can never disagree about whether a question is work. Pinned by new tests; whole suite green.'],t.s)
B.aSM=new A.v(947,B.akj)
B.adw=s(['THE ASK-CLAUDE CHAT NOW REMEMBERS YOUR CONVERSATION (General Chat phase 2) - your feature ("a general chat screen like the regular Claude Windows app has", "build it"). Until now the "Ask Claude" chat lived only in memory, so closing the app - or a desktop auto-update - wiped the whole thread. That was a capture-first violation: words could be lost. No more. Your chat is now saved the instant you send a message and reloads when you reopen the screen, so the conversation is right where you left it.',"AND IT SYNCS ACROSS YOUR DEVICES - start a chat on your phone, pick it up on Windows. Your message is written down BEFORE it is even sent to Claude, so a crash or a lost connection mid-reply can never swallow what you typed or dictated. The New button still clears the thread when you want a fresh one.","HOW IT SHIPPED SAFELY, NOW: it rides the existing synced-settings store (the same one that syncs your folded boxes and item arrangement), so there was NO database migration to run and nothing waiting on you - it is just live. Pinned by new tests that prove a thread round-trips without losing a character and that a corrupted save reads as an empty chat instead of crashing; whole suite green. Next slices (still to come): a Save button on each message to keep the good bits as items, and pointing the chat at YOUR channel so it can see your items and queue."],t.s)
B.aYm=new A.v(946,B.adw)
B.aty=s(['THE DESIGN TAB NOW SHOWS WHEN YOU LAST RAN A PASS - your feature ("in the Claude design area should we put a button that says when the activate button was pushed"). Under the Run button there is now a "Last design pass \xb7 <date and time>" line, so you can always see when the last design pass was kicked off.',"It updates the instant you tap Run, and it reads the time straight off your Run request itself - so it can never drift from the pass it is timing. If you have never run a pass on an item, the line simply is not shown. Pinned by new tests; whole suite green."],t.s)
B.aTu=new A.v(945,B.aty)
B.aqr=s(['THE DESIGN TAB NOW TELLS YOU IT IS WORKING - your bug ("how long should it take for Claude design to show a design? Been 20 minutes... is it working?"). You were right to wonder: the tab gave you nothing to go on. When you tapped Run, the little spinner vanished the instant the request was sent, and the tab dropped straight back to "No design yet" - so a design pass that was still on its way looked exactly like one you never started.','THE FIX: once you tap Run, the Design tab now shows a clear "Design pass requested - waiting for Claude" notice with a spinner, and it stays until the pass actually lands (then it clears itself). If it has been much longer than a minute, the notice tells you so and the Run button stays live so you can tap it again. No more staring at a blank tab wondering if anything happened.','WHY IT MATTERS: this closes the "is it working?" gap - the tab is now honest about the difference between "nothing run yet" and "pass on its way". Pinned by new tests; whole suite green. (Note: if a pass truly never arrives after a retry, that points at the request not reaching me - tell me the item number and I will trace that delivery path as its own fix.)'],t.s)
B.aRf=new A.v(944,B.aqr)
B.asV=s(['NOTHING BUILDS ON A STRAY TAP ANYMORE - your bug ("an item started building even though I did NOT click the Implement button"). You were right, and I found why. There were TWO ways to authorize a build and one was far too easy to trip: the Implement TOGGLE on a box lost its confirmation back in v861 (the idea then was that a toggle undoes itself), so a single accidental tap sent an item to the builder with no confirm. And the other control - the Implement button next to a message - even showed up next to a design-pass request and defaulted to Build it, so a stray tap or Enter could confirm a build you did not mean.',"THE FIX (all three holes closed): 1) turning the Implement toggle ON now asks you to confirm first, naming the action - turning it OFF stays instant, because stopping is always safe; 2) the Build this one dialog now defaults to Not yet, so a stray Enter cancels instead of building; 3) a Run a design pass turn no longer shows an Implement button at all - designing is not building. So the only thing that ever starts a build is you deliberately confirming it - which is the whole point of the Implement gate.","WHY IT MATTERS: this is the trust piece - you can now rely on nothing building unless you say so, on purpose. The design-turn rule is pinned by new tests; whole suite green. The two confirm dialogs are a look I could not watch on your screen from the unattended lane, so on the update: flip an Implement toggle ON and you should see a short confirm first - tell me if the wording reads right. (This also un-holds your Button Activation Timestamp item, which I was holding until this gate was trustworthy again.)"],t.s)
B.b3N=new A.v(943,B.asV)
B.aj0=s(['GROUNDWORK FOR THE GENERAL CHAT SCREEN (phase 1) - your feature ("a general chat screen like the regular Claude Windows app has but for my channel", approved to build). First, a useful finding: a general chat already exists in the app - it is "Ask Claude" in the nav (a real dictate-or-type, multi-turn chat). So you can use it today. What it is missing is what you actually asked for, and this build starts closing that gap.','WHAT THIS BUILD ADDS: the save-to-item bridge - the one-tap crossing from a throwaway chat message into a durable item (Note, Task, Meeting, or Claude message), built and fully tested. The rule it guarantees is capture-first: when you save a message, or a whole question-and-answer exchange, every word is kept - a single message saves verbatim, a run is labelled "You:/Claude:" and joined, nothing is ever dropped or shortened. So chat stays the fast scratchpad and anything worth keeping graduates into your item system the moment it matters.',"HONEST STATUS: this is the tested foundation (new tests pin that nothing is lost and that a saved item is born exactly like one from the \uff0b menu), whole suite and analyze green - nothing changes on your screen yet. Held for the next slices, on purpose: making the chat REMEMBER your thread across restarts (it is in-memory today, which can lose a conversation - I am fixing that next); the visible Save button on each message (a look change I want to see on your screen first); and pointing the chat at YOUR channel - the Voicley pipeline that knows your items and queue - instead of a generic Claude. The full plan and the two choices I need from you (sync-your-chat-across-devices? your-channel-or-your-own-key?) are written up in docs/general-chat.md and on the item."],t.s)
B.b41=new A.v(942,B.aj0)
B.at4=s(['THE DESIGN CHECKLIST IS LIVE (phase 2) - your feature ("do it unattended"). You ran the database migration, so section 1 of the Design tab is now a real tickable feature-set checklist: each capability is a row you can tap to check or uncheck, and the moment you do, the time estimate and the difficulty at the bottom re-total over just the rows you chose - dial the scope, watch the estimate move. Your ticks save and sync across your devices (they ride the new design_json field the migration added).',"WHERE YOU WILL SEE IT: open a FEATURE Claude item full-size and tap Design. The checklist appears once that item has a design with feature rows; until a design pass fills those rows in, the tab keeps showing the prose passes exactly as before, so nothing you already had changed or disappeared.","HONEST STATUS: you asked me to build this blind and fix the look after, so I did - the checklist wiring, the live re-total, and the tick-syncs-to-your-devices path are built and pinned by new tests (a tick round-trips through the database and re-scopes the estimate; a hardest-chosen difficulty rolls up), whole suite and analyze green. Two things I held back on purpose: I could not see the checklist on your screen from the unattended lane, so please open it and tell me if the rows, checkmarks, and totals look right; and the one-tap Promote-to-Feature button is still to come - it needs a clear definition of what promote should do before I build it, so tell me what you want it to do."],t.s)
B.b3E=new A.v(941,B.at4)
B.aid=s(['GROUNDWORK FOR THE DESIGN CHECKLIST (phase 2) - your feature ("run the migration"). Phase 1 gave you the Design tab and Run-a-design-pass; phase 2 turns section 1 into a tickable feature-set checklist where checking the rows you want re-totals the time and difficulty live, plus a one-tap Promote-to-Feature. This build ships the engine for that - the checklist math (check a row, the estimate and difficulty roll up over just the chosen rows) built and fully tested - and the database migration file it needs (021_design_json).',"WHY IT IS TWO STEPS. Your ticks are your own state that has to save and sync across your devices, so unlike the prose design passes they need a real synced field, not the answer log. Adding that field is server-first (a device that writes a column the server does not have yet would stall its whole upload queue), so the one migration has to be applied to the database before the checklist UI can ship - that is the step we run together, exactly as I flagged in phase 1. The checklist widget and the Promote button then land in an attended pass so I can see them on your screen before they go out.","HONEST STATUS: nothing changes on your screen in this build - it is the tested foundation (10 new tests pin the parse, the re-total, and that an unchecked row never counts) plus the migration, so the visible checklist is a clean wiring job next. Same pattern as the folder-filter engine (v937): logic first and proven, the UI right behind it."],t.s)
B.b2I=new A.v(940,B.aid)
B.adj=s(['THE FILTER CHIPS NOW WORK IN YOUR CLAUDE FOLDERS - your feature ("build it and only insert into Claude item folders for now"). The tappable state chips you already had on the Queue - need you, building, built - now appear on all your Claude-side folders too. Tap one to narrow the folder to just those items, tap it again to clear. Scoped to Claude folders exactly as you asked, so your ordinary folders are untouched.',"This is the safe first cut - the same proven chips, now everywhere your Claude items live. The bigger version you designed - combinable chips that STACK (waiting-on-you plus Bug) and add item-type chips - is built underneath (the engine shipped in v937) and is the next step; it needs its own chip row, which is a layout I want to see on your screen before it ships. Open a Claude folder and tell me the chips look right."],t.s)
B.b3y=new A.v(939,B.adj)
B.ahE=s(['THE CLAUDE DESIGN TAB IS HERE (phase 1) - your feature ("build the Claude Design tab and insert what is needed in order to do designs inside of that tab"). Open any Claude item full-size and there is now a Design tab next to Chat and Activity. It is a cockpit for designing: tap Run a design pass and Claude fills in feature sets, benefits, specs, and a suggested mockup direction for that item, right there in the tab. Each pass is kept and shown, and the text is selectable and copyable like everywhere else.',"It is separate from Chat (so your conversation and your design work do not tangle), and the design content lives on the item and syncs, with no setup needed. This is phase 1: the tab, running a pass, and reading it back. Phase 2 - the tickable feature-set checklist that totals time and difficulty, and a one-tap Promote-to-Feature - needs a small database change and comes next.","HONEST NOTE: this adds a new tab I could not watch on your screen from the unattended lane, so on the next update please open a Claude item, tap Design, and tell me if the tab and the Run button look right - I will adjust anything that is off."],t.s)
B.b_e=new A.v(938,B.ahE)
B.aoS=s(['GROUNDWORK FOR COMBINABLE FOLDER FILTERS - your feature ("filter folder items ... waiting on input from me ... Build it!"). This build lays the engine that powers the filter chips: you will be able to tap chips to narrow a folder by state (waiting on you, waiting on the builder, built), by type (feature, bug, tweak, question), and by flags (approved, has a queue number, has mockups) - and STACK them, so tapping Waiting-on-you plus Bug shows only the bugs waiting on you. Chips in the same group widen (bug OR feature); chips across groups narrow (waiting-on-you AND bug).',"This release ships the filtering logic itself, fully tested, plus the written spec (docs/folder-filters.md). The chips you tap come next: they add a row to every folder view, which is a visual change I want to see on your screen before it ships, so it is the one attended step. The highest-value chip - waiting-on-you versus waiting-on-the-builder, your daily triage - lands with them."],t.s)
B.b2V=new A.v(937,B.aoS)
B.ar_=s(['A FOLDER NOW REMEMBERS WHERE YOU SCROLLED TO - your bug ("when in a folder and have the list scrolled down and then click another folder and then go back to that folder, the position of the list resets to the top"). Each folder now keeps its own scroll position, so when you leave it and come back it drops you right where you left off instead of jumping to the top. Every folder remembers its own spot independently.',"It is remembered for the session (it resets on a fresh app start, which is fine - a stale scroll position is not worth keeping overnight). Done at the safe edge of the scrolling code, not inside the scroll engine, so none of the scroll smoothness work is touched; a test pins the per-folder memory."],t.s)
B.b2e=new A.v(936,B.ar_)
B.aj6=s(['CTRL+SHIFT+ARROW SELECTS TEXT IN A REPLY BOX AGAIN INSTEAD OF JUMPING ITEMS - your bug ("inside of a reply box and trying to use the shortcut CTL Shift arrow to select ... it is moving to the next window"). In the full-window item view, the left and right arrow keys page between items - but they were doing that even when you held Ctrl or Shift, so a Ctrl+Shift+Arrow to select a word jumped you to the next item instead. Now the arrows only page when pressed on their own; the moment you hold Ctrl, Shift, Alt, or the Windows key, the arrow goes to the text box for selecting and word-jumping, the way it should.',"A test pins that a modified arrow never pages and a bare arrow still does, so this cannot quietly come back."],t.s)
B.aVn=new A.v(935,B.aj6)
B.arT=s(['YOU CAN NOW SELECT AND COPY THE STATUS MESSAGES TOO - your feature ("be able to select and copy all text in the items including the status messages"). Your messages and my answers in a full-size item were already selectable, but the STATUS line at the top and the status history and timeline in the Activity tab were plain text you could not grab. Those now select and copy the same way - press and hold (or right-click on Windows) and Copy - so every piece of text in the item, status included, can be lifted out.',"The text looks exactly the same; it just gained the copy ability, using the same selection the answers already use, and links inside a status line are now tappable as a bonus. Each block copies on its own (the same way the messages and answers already do). A test pins that the status text is wrapped for selection so it cannot quietly go back to being un-copyable."],t.s)
B.b3Z=new A.v(934,B.arT)
B.aeO=s(["THE FULL CLAUDE ITEM NOW READS QUESTION, THEN ANSWER, IN ORDER - your feature (\"why isn't the interleave feature working with this implemented? Shouldn't the flow of messages be question and then answer?\"). Before, the main view of a full-size Claude item showed all of your messages first and then dumped every one of my answers in a block below them, so a conversation read as all-questions-then-all-answers. Now each answer sits directly under the question it belongs to - ask, answer, ask, answer - the way a chat should read.","WHY IT LOOKED SEPARATED. Your answers are stored in the item's answer log, and on many items the individual conversation entries had no answer attached to them - so the view had nowhere to put each answer except in one pile at the bottom. It now pairs each unanswered question with the next answer from the log in order, so the answer lands against its question. Any leftover answers (more answers than questions) still show after, just the extras rather than the whole conversation. Nothing is added or lost - only the order changed - and a pure test pins the weave so it cannot regress.","This is the piece that was missing behind the earlier interleave request: the Chat/Activity timeline already read in order, but the main body did not. It is a layout change I could not watch on your screen from the unattended lane, so on the next update open a Claude item with a few back-and-forth turns and check that each of my answers now sits right under your matching question."],t.s)
B.aVf=new A.v(933,B.aeO)
B.aci=s(['THE FOLDERS LIST NO LONGER POPS UP WHEN YOU HIT BACK - your bug ("the folders pane is showing up when I hit the back button", "this is still happening"). When you opened a folder from the folder list on the LEFT and then hit back (the top-left arrow or Android back), the app dropped you on the full-page folders list instead of Home. That was because a folder opened from the pane quietly left that whole folders list sitting underneath it, so back peeled the folder off and landed on the list. Now a folder opened from the pane sits directly on top of Home, so back takes you straight back to Home.',"DRILLING IN FROM THE FULL FOLDERS PAGE STILL WORKS THE OLD WAY. If instead you open the full folders page and tap a folder there, back still returns you to that list - that path was correct and is unchanged. Only the left-pane shortcut was leaving the list behind; the folder still shades in the pane exactly as before whichever way you opened it.","HONEST STATUS: the earlier v776 fix addressed a different flavor of this (a drawer being left open), which is why it kept happening; this time a navigation test proves it - a pane-opened folder now has only Home beneath it, and the list drill-in keeps the list beneath it - so it cannot quietly regress. Whole suite and analyze green. It is a navigation change I could not watch on your screen from the unattended lane, so on the next update: open a folder from the left pane, hit back, and you should land on Home, not the folders list."],t.s)
B.aTV=new A.v(932,B.aci)
B.akQ=s(['NEW TEXT IN A CLAUDE ITEM IS NOW HIGHLIGHTED - your feature ("when I click New answer on the box items, would it make sense to highlight so I know what text is new?"). Open a Claude item and any answer that arrived since you last opened it now stands out: a quiet clay wash behind it, a bolder clay edge down its side, and a small NEW tag on its "Claude - date" line. So when you tap in on a New-answer item you can see at a glance exactly which reply is the fresh one, instead of re-reading the whole thread to find it.','IT CLEARS ITSELF THE NEXT TIME YOU LOOK. The highlight is anchored to how much of the item you had already read when you opened it (the same "how far have I read" mark the New-answer dot uses), so it lights up only the text that landed since then. Opening the item counts as reading it, so next time you come back that answer is no longer marked new - and a brand-new item you are opening for the first time is not lit up all over, since the whole thing is fresh anyway.',"HONEST STATUS: the rule for what counts as new is pinned by new tests (an answer past your read point is new, one you already read is not, first open highlights nothing, a trimmed log never misbehaves), whole suite green. It is a look change I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane - on the next release, open an item that shows New answer and the fresh reply should carry the clay highlight and NEW tag. Bringing you straight to that section on open is a natural follow-up; say the word and I add the auto-scroll."],t.s)
B.aV2=new A.v(931,B.akQ)
B.adO=s(['THE SYNC COUNTER IS HONEST NOW - your bug ("keep getting messages that processing is stalled"). When your device could not reach the server, the little status still said "Uploading 35 items", which reads as stuck work when the truth is those items are simply waiting for the connection to come back. It now says "35 items waiting to reconnect" when you are offline, and only says "Uploading" when it is genuinely connected and sending. Nothing was ever lost - the wording was the problem.','WHERE TO WATCH SYNC, ON PHONE TOO: Settings has a live sync section (under your account) that shows connected / syncing / "up to date at HH:MM", how many recordings are still to upload, and a Re-sync now button to force it - and it works on mobile, not just the laptop popup. So you finally have a place to open on demand and see that sync is healthy, instead of waiting to catch a toast.','HONEST STATUS: the counter wording is now driven by whether the app is actually connected (pinned by new tests: offline reads "waiting to reconnect", online reads "Uploading", and a live recording still wins because uploads pause while you dictate). Whole suite green (1668). One deeper piece remains for a focused pass: when you ARE connected but a delivered item lingers in the local queue, clearing it at the source so the number can never sit high over already-saved work - I will spec that separately.'],t.s)
B.aZ6=new A.v(930,B.adO)
B.as0=s(['EVERY CLAUDE RESPONSE IN A FULL-SIZE ITEM NOW SHOWS A DATE AND TIME - your tweak ("the Claude responses in fullsized items do not have dates/times on them"). Replies already carried a date since v914, but one kind did not: an older answer that had to be rebuilt from the item log because it lost its own entry showed a bare "Claude" with no time. Those now show the item\'s own answer time, so no response in the full-size view is left undated.','IF YOU SEE NONE OF THEM WITH DATES, YOU ARE A VERSION BEHIND. The date-and-time on replies shipped in v914 - if every reply looks undated on your phone, your build is older than that and this next release brings all of it. This build closes the one remaining gap on top of v914, so after updating every Claude response should read like "Claude - Aug 3 - 9:57pm".',"HONEST STATUS: the rebuilt-answer time uses the same fallback ladder the normal replies use (when it was answered, then received, then created), so it is the best time the item knows rather than a guess, and the whole suite is green (1665). It is a look change I could not see from the unattended lane - open a full-size item on the next release and each Claude response should carry its date and time."],t.s)
B.b3C=new A.v(929,B.as0)
B.atB=s(['ITEM NUMBERS IN MY REPLIES ARE NOW TAPPABLE - your feature ("when you reference IDs by their ID number make the number clickable so I can view them easier"). In the full-size item, when I mention another item by its number - like #103588 - that number is now a blue link. Tap it and the item it refers to opens full-size, so you can jump straight to what I am talking about instead of hunting for it.',"IT ONLY LINKS WHAT IT CAN ACTUALLY OPEN. A number becomes a link only when an item with that number really exists on your device - a reference to something that is not here stays plain text, never a dead link that does nothing when tapped. It works in both my answers and your own messages, and short numbers like an option #3 are deliberately left alone so ordinary text never looks tappable.","HONEST STATUS: the rule for what counts as a tappable reference is pinned by a new test (known numbers link, unknown ones and short ones stay text, and it coexists with the existing web links), whole suite green (1665). Tapping opens the same full-size view your box tap opens. It is an interaction I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane - on the next release, open an item where I reference another by number and that number should open it."],t.s)
B.b3D=new A.v(928,B.atB)
B.agy=s(['FOLDER SEARCH NOW OPENS FROM A MAGNIFIER, LIKE THE INBOX - your ask ("implement on folders and leave the format the same as it is now when you select to search"). In a Claude folder the search no longer sits open at the top all the time; it rests as a tidy Search Claude items magnifier, and tapping it opens the exact same search bar you had before - field plus the This folder / All Claude scope chips with their live counts. Nothing about the opened bar changed; it just no longer takes up space until you want it.',"IT STAYS OPEN WHILE A SEARCH IS LIVE. If you have typed a query, the bar cannot collapse out from under you - the results always keep it visible, the same never-hide-a-live-search rule the inbox uses. Tapping away from an empty field closes it back to the magnifier. This reuses the exact open/close rule already shipped for the inbox, so the two searches now behave the same way.","HONEST STATUS: this makes the folder search consistent with the inbox top-bar search you asked for, whole suite green (1657). It is an interaction/look change I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane - open a Claude folder on the next release, tap the magnifier, and the same search bar with its counts should open ready to type."],t.s)
B.b33=new A.v(927,B.agy)
B.aqC=s(['ITEMS WAITING ON YOU NOW WEAR A LOUD ORANGE EDGE - point 2 of your nine-point Claude-box review ("loud Needs-you", "UI is approved"). A collapsed Claude item whose status is Needs you (or Waiting) now gets an orange outline around the whole box, on top of the orange "Needs you" badge, so the items that actually need your attention jump out of a full queue instead of hiding in the stack.',"IT IS BUILT TO YOUR MOCKUP AND CANNOT DISAGREE WITH THE STATUS. The orange edge is derived from the exact same state the badge reads (Needs you / Waiting), using the one orange color already locked to the mockup you approved - there is no new saved data, so the edge and the badge always agree, and it only appears on Claude items that are genuinely waiting on you. An item in any other state looks exactly as before.","HONEST STATUS: this is the loud-Needs-you card from your review, built as a layout-neutral outline (it paints the edge without moving anything), analyze and the whole suite are green. It is a look change I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane, so your eyes on the next release are the check: a Needs-you item should now have an orange outline. The last remaining review point - moving approval to one toggle on the item header - is the one that changes HOW you approve, so I am confirming that with you before I touch it."],t.s)
B.b3g=new A.v(926,B.aqC)
B.arG=s(['PROCESSING LOG NOW HAS A HOME IN THE APP - your ask ("how do I get to this list from within voicley" - "Add UI element"). Settings now has a Processing log row, right under Diagnostics. Tap it and it opens the log of what I processed from the queue - each item with when I started, when I finished, and how long it took - so you never have to hunt for the item link again.',"IT IS THE SAME LIVE LOG. The row opens the same page the desk keeps refreshed, so it is always current - the app owns the doorway, the desk owns the contents, and they cannot drift apart. It sits with the other diagnostics tools (Diagnostics, Network log) because that is where you go to see how the system is running.","HONEST STATUS: this adds one tappable row in Settings, built as an exact copy of the Diagnostics and Network-log rows already there and opened the same way the item links open, so the risk is low - but it is still a look change I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane. Open Settings on the next release and there should be a Processing log row under Diagnostics; tell me if you would rather it live somewhere else."],t.s)
B.b_X=new A.v(925,B.arG)
B.atf=s(['SHARPER TRACE FOR THE COLLAPSED-ITEMS-RESET - your bug ("this folder had collapsed items and I came back and they weren\'t collapsed anymore"). v919 started logging how many collapsed items were restored on load, but a "restored 0" line could not say WHY it came back empty. This build adds one more quiet line that records exactly what the app saw when it loaded your arrangement: whether sync was reachable, whether the saved row was there, and whether this device still had its own copy.','WHAT THIS MEANS FOR YOU: nothing changes on screen - this is behind-the-scenes only, no behavior change at all. The next time your collapsed items pop back open, the two lines together pin the cause exactly: both empty means this device lost its own copy while sync had not caught up yet, an empty saved row means a stale "open all" travelled down from another device, and a present-but-not-restored row would point at a read bug instead. Whichever it is tells me precisely where the real fix goes.','HONEST STATUS: this is diagnostics only, added to the one shared place all your synced arrangements load through, so it also covers any future "my arrangement reset" report. The whole test suite and analyze are green. Still not a fix - it is the evidence that makes the fix certain the next time it happens; note whether it followed an app update or a second device if you can.'],t.s)
B.b2i=new A.v(924,B.atf)
B.ajM=s(['CLAUDE ITEMS NOW WEAR A COLOR STATE BADGE WHEN COLLAPSED - point 1 of your nine-point Claude-box review ("proper priority data presentation" - "do all of these"). A collapsed Claude item now shows one word in one fixed color for where it stands: Answered (blue), Needs you (orange), Building (purple), Held (grey), Built (green), Released (teal). So a whole stack of items triages at a glance without opening any of them.',"IT IS DERIVED, NOT STORED. The badge reads the same status the queue already uses and maps it to the exact palette you approved on the mockup - there is no new saved data, so the badge can never disagree with the status. An item whose status does not match a known state simply shows no badge rather than a wrong one, and the badge only appears on Claude items, sitting between the title and the collapse arrow on the otherwise minimal row.","HONEST STATUS AND WHAT IS NEXT: this wires up the color logic that shipped and was tested in v917, reusing the existing chip style, whole suite green. Two pieces of the review remain and are the more visual ones - the loud orange Needs-you card plus a Needs-you filter bucket, and moving approval to one toggle on the item header - both held for an attended session where I can screenshot them against your mockup. This is a look change I could not see from the unattended lane, so your eyes on the next release are the check: collapse a Claude item and it should show its state badge."],t.s)
B.b0D=new A.v(923,B.ajM)
B.ac6=s(['FULL-SIZE ITEMS NOW HAVE A MOCKUPS TAB - your feature ("can you insert mockup images into a tab of a full sized item" - "do both for now" - "build both"). When an item has mockups attached, a new Mockups tab appears next to Chat and Activity. It lists each mockup as a card you tap to open the live, interactive mockup page in your browser - so the designs for an item live right on the item.',"IT READS THE MOCKUPS OFF THE ITEM ITSELF. The tab picks up the mockup links the desk already posts into an item (the same clickable links you have seen on the color-scheme item), so there is no new place for them to get out of sync - the link on the item IS the attachment. The Mockups tab only shows up on items that actually have mockups, so ordinary items stay clean with just Chat and Activity.","HONEST STATUS AND WHAT IS NEXT: this ships the tab and wires it to your real mockups, pinned by new tests and with the whole suite green. Two refinements are deliberately left for an attended session where I can see the screen: showing the mockup as a flat inline image (needs a screenshot-capture step first) and a separate in-app Live tab that embeds the page inside Voicley (needs a browser-embedding component I will not add without seeing it work). For now the card opens the live page in your browser, which is the same interactive mockup - open a full-size item with mockups on the next release and tap Mockups."],t.s)
B.b_A=new A.v(922,B.ac6)
B.arv=s(['THE FULL-SIZE ITEM NOW SHOWS "#3 OF 13 IN LINE" - your tweak ("in the fullsized view when displaying the Que # add that it is X of X so we can see how many items are processing"). The queue strip at the top of a full-size item used to read "QUEUE - #3 in line"; it now reads "QUEUE - #3 of 13 in line", so you can see both where the item sits AND how many are still in line, in one glance.','IT MATCHES THE LADDER AND CANNOT DISAGREE. The "of 13" total comes from the exact same one place the stage ladder already used for its "#3 of 13" chip, so the strip and the ladder can never show two different totals. The count means pending work only - answered questions, brainstorm ideas, and already-built items are not counted in the total, same rule as everywhere else.','HONEST STATUS: this is a small text change on the full-size queue strip, analyze and the whole test suite are green. I could not see it on your screen from the unattended lane - open a queued item full-size on the next release and the strip should read "#X of Y in line".'],t.s)
B.b3V=new A.v(921,B.arv)
B.apG=s(['HIGHER-CONTRAST DICTATION BOX - the color scheme you picked ("Look at the color scheme for the dictation box" - "Let\'s go with #3", Option 3 Higher contrast). When your dictation lands on a OneNote page, the words on the white box are now pure black instead of a soft near-black, and the faint chrome around them (timestamps, the destination line) is a darker grey - so your words are easier to read in bright light. The look is identical whether the box is live on the main screen, sitting in the list, or opened full-size.','SCOPED ON PURPOSE - this darkens only the white "paper" dictation box (the OneNote look). The everyday dictation box already draws its words in pure black, so nothing changed there, and I deliberately did NOT touch the shared app-wide text colors: darkening those would have restyled every item box in the app at once, which is a bigger visual change than you asked for and needs an attended before/after check.',"HONEST STATUS: this matches the exact colors from the mockup you approved (pure-black ink, darker faint chrome), collapsed four hand-typed copies of the box palette into one shared place so they can never drift apart, and the whole test suite plus analyze are green. It is a color change I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane - dictate onto a OneNote page on the next release and the words should read noticeably crisper."],t.s)
B.aZx=new A.v(920,B.apG)
B.ahG=s(['CHASING THE COLLAPSED-ITEMS-RESET - your bug ("this folder had collapsed items and I came back and they weren\'t collapsed anymore"). I could not reproduce this from the unattended lane, and the collapse state is held in one place that normal navigation between folders cannot reset - so a reset means the app was rebuilt behind you (a restart or the desktop auto-update) and reloaded its saved state. To catch it red-handed I added quiet internal logging that records, every time, how many collapsed items were restored on load and every fold/unfold you make.',"WHAT THIS MEANS FOR YOU: nothing changes on screen - this is behind-the-scenes only. The next time your collapsed items pop back open, that log will show whether the app reloaded and found nothing saved (a restart race) or whether something quietly cleared them, which tells me exactly where the real fix goes. If you can, note whether it happened right after the app updated or whether another device was involved - that pins it faster."],t.s)
B.aZ5=new A.v(919,B.ahG)
B.asU=s(['ITEMS NO LONGER SINK TO THE BOTTOM OF A LIST WHEN MOVED - your bug ("#99569 Why is this item at the bottom of this list?"). When the desk moved an item between folders (for example pulling #99569 back out of Processed and into the Queue to build it), it stamped the current clock time as the item sort position. That number is far larger than every real position, so the moved item dropped to the very bottom of a hand-sorted folder and hid there, even though nothing was wrong with the item itself.','THE FIX: a moved item is now filed at the TOP of the destination folder, the same prepend-at-top rule the app itself already uses everywhere. This was fixed once before for the Processed folder (v875); this closes the same hole on every other place the desk files an item - moving between folders, the In Progress "building" chip, and the dated Releases sub-folders - so an item can never quietly sink to the bottom like this again.',"HONEST STATUS: this is a desk-side ordering fix (it takes effect the moment the desk files an item, not something you tap), traced to the exact line that wrote a raw timestamp as the position. Your current Queue was already healthy - every item is correctly ordered - so this is the permanent guard against it happening the next time an item is moved."],t.s)
B.aRj=new A.v(918,B.asU)
B.alo=s(['GROUNDWORK FOR THE COLOR STATE BADGE - part of your nine-point Claude-box review ("proper priority data presentation" - "do all of these"). The logic that turns each items status into one word and one fixed color (Answered blue, Needs you orange, Building purple, Held grey, Built green, Released teal) is now built and locked to the exact palette from the mockup you approved.',"THIS PIECE IS NOT YET ON SCREEN. The color map is done and tested, but wiring the badge onto the collapsed box is a visual change I will not ship blind from the unattended lane - it lands in the next attended UI pass, where I can screenshot it against your mockup. So you will not see the badge yet; this is the foundation under it, done safely.","HONEST STATUS: the mapping reads the same status the queue pill already uses (one source of truth, no new stored data) and is pinned by a new test covering every state, the newest-status-wins rule, and each approved color. Whole suite green."],t.s)
B.b0H=new A.v(917,B.alo)
B.agm=s(['OFF NOW ALWAYS TURNS DICTATION OFF, EVEN IN "MIC LOST" - your bug ("wasnt able to turn off the dictation when it was in mic lost mode"). When another app or a phone call stole the microphone mid-recording, tapping Off could hang while trying to release a mic that was already gone, so the box stayed stuck on "Mic lost" and would not stop. Now the mic release on Off is time-bounded: if it cannot let go within a few seconds it stops waiting and turns dictation off anyway.',"NOTHING IS LOST WHEN THIS HAPPENS. Your audio is already saved as it is spoken (capture-first), so freeing the screen after a stuck mic-release drops no words - the final segment still finishes transcribing in the background just like a normal stop.",'HONEST STATUS: pinned by the reasoning that the old code left the state reset unreached when the native mic-release deadlocked; the whole test suite and analyze are green. I could not reproduce a stolen mic on your exact phone from the unattended lane, so the acceptance test is the next time you hit "Mic lost" - one tap of Off should now end it.'],t.s)
B.aYb=new A.v(916,B.agm)
B.abx=s(['ANSWERED QUESTIONS NO LONGER COUNT IN THE QUEUE READY NUMBER - your bug ("once you answered a question, why would it still be included in the x of x que listing?"). A question is something I answer, not something I build, so it never belongs in the build-readiness count. It was slipping back in because approving it stamps an "Approved - queued for the builder" receipt over its "Answered" status, and the count read that receipt as still-to-do. Now a question is excluded from the ready and in-review counts no matter what its status says.',"A QUESTION STILL SHOWS AS NEED-YOU WHEN IT REALLY IS WAITING ON YOU. The only place a question can appear in the overview now is the need-you bucket, and only when it is genuinely waiting for your reply - an answered one drops straight out. Nothing about the item itself changes; this is purely what the count includes.","HONEST STATUS: pinned by new tests (an answered question, and even a re-approved one, is excluded; a waiting one still shows need-you; the same status on a real build still counts). Whole suite green. This pairs with the v912 reviewed-and-valid count - together the ready number now means real, buildable work."],t.s)
B.b0O=new A.v(915,B.abx)
B.arV=s(['CLAUDE REPLIES NOW SHOW THE DATE AND THE TIME - your bug ("Claude replies in full sized versions dont have dates on them" - "they also need times"). In the full-size item every one of my replies now carries a stamp like "Claude - Aug 3 - 9:57pm", and it never comes up blank: it uses when the answer landed, falling back to when I picked it up or when you sent it. Your own messages get the date too, so a week-old exchange can no longer read like this mornings.',"IT IS THE SAME STAMP EVERYWHERE. The chat headers, the new Activity timeline, and the pipeline strip at the top of the item now all read their date-and-time from one shared place, so they can never show the same moment two different ways. Times are in your local zone, as before.","HONEST STATUS: pinned by the test suite (1640 green). One edge: a reply that has to be reconstructed from the answer log because it lost its own entry cannot carry an exact time, so those stay label-only; every normally-answered reply now shows date and time. This is a formatting change I could not see on your screen from the unattended lane - open a Claude item full-size and my replies should show the date and time."],t.s)
B.aTg=new A.v(914,B.arV)
B.aqZ=s(['FULL-SIZE ITEMS NOW HAVE TABS - CHAT AND ACTIVITY - your ask ("is there additional information we could save in a tab as to what I see normally coming through on an item" - do all 5 - "either just build it"). Open any Claude item full-size and there is now a Chat / Activity switcher: Chat is the conversation exactly as before, and Activity is a new page showing the five data streams an item already carries.',"THE ACTIVITY TAB SHOWS ALL FIVE STREAMS. Status history (every status I ever set, in order, not just the latest line), the timeline (your messages and my replies with times), the type and approval trail (every time it was retyped or approved or withdrawn, with times), the processing record (sent, picked up, answered, and the round-trip time), and the live draft when I am mid-write. Every stream is read from data the item already had - nothing new is captured - and any empty stream is hidden so a quiet item stays calm.","HONEST STATUS: this is a real new UI - a tab bar plus five rendered sections - and the whole test suite (1640 tests) is green including a new one pinning the status-history stream. But I could not see it on your screen from the unattended lane, so your eyes on the next release are the acceptance test: open a Claude item full-size, tap Activity, and the five sections should be there. If the layout is off or you want the streams reordered or collapsible, tell me and I will tune it."],t.s)
B.b3h=new A.v(913,B.aqZ)
B.acW=s(['THE QUEUE "READY" COUNT NOW MEANS REVIEWED-AND-VALID - your ask ("only include items that you have reviewed and identified as valid items that you can complete in the x of x que display"). Until now the ready number counted every freshly approved item, including questions and things I had not actually looked at yet, so it could claim more work was ready than I could really build. Now an item only counts as "ready" once I have read it and confirmed it is a real build I can finish.','FRESHLY APPROVED ITEMS SHOW AS "IN REVIEW". The queue overview at the top of the Queue folder now reads like "2 ready - 3 in review - 1 need you - 4 built": in review means approved and waiting for me to confirm it is a valid build, ready means I have confirmed it. Both are tappable filter chips like the others, so you can tap "in review" to see exactly what is still waiting on my pass. Nothing on the items themselves moves - it is purely how the count is read.',"HONEST STATUS: pinned by tests (a plain approved item is in review, only a confirmed item is ready, questions never reach ready), whole suite green. This is a count-and-label change on the Queue header I could not screenshot from the unattended lane - your eyes on the next release are the acceptance test: open the Queue and the overview should split into ready vs in review."],t.s)
B.b3K=new A.v(912,B.acW)
B.ajX=s(['INSTANT ACKNOWLEDGEMENT WHEN YOU SEND - your ask ("there is not any immediate response inside the full sized item... build the instant ack"). The moment you send a message from a full-size Claude item, a clay strip now appears reading "GOT IT \xb7 CLAUDE IS ON IT", before the desk has even picked up your message. So you get feedback right away instead of a silent gap.','IT FLOWS STRAIGHT INTO THE LIVE ANSWER. The instant strip turns into the "CLAUDE IS WRITING" strip the moment I start composing, and both clear the instant the finished answer lands - so one send reads as a smooth progression: got it, writing, answer. It arms for both typed replies and one-tap chip answers.','HONEST STATUS: this reuses the exact styling of the existing writing strip, and the code analyzes and tests clean, but I could not watch it on your screen from the unattended lane. Your next send on the next release is the acceptance test: fire a message into a full-size item and the "GOT IT" strip should appear immediately.'],t.s)
B.aZz=new A.v(911,B.ajX)
B.abf=s(['LINKS INSIDE CLAUDE ITEMS ARE TAPPABLE AGAIN ON WINDOWS - your bug, reported about five times ("can not click the links, nothing happens"). The full-size item view rendered my answers as selectable text, and on desktop that selectable text ate the single click to place its cursor - so tapping a web address (like a mockup link) did literally nothing. On your phone it worked, which is why it kept looking fixed. Now one tap opens the address in your browser, on Windows and phone alike.',"SELECTING AND COPYING STILL WORKS EXACTLY AS BEFORE. The text is now drawn the normal way (so every link click registers) wrapped in a selection layer, so you can still highlight and copy any part of an answer, with the same right-click Copy on the desktop and long-press bar on the phone. Nothing about how the text looks changed - only that the links respond to a tap.","HONEST STATUS: this follows the pattern Flutter itself documents for tappable links inside selectable text, and the code analyzes clean, but I could not click it on your Windows machine from the unattended lane. Your tap on the next release is the acceptance test: open a Claude item full-size and tap a web address - it should open your browser."],t.s)
B.b11=new A.v(910,B.abf)
B.am8=s(["THE ITEM ID NOW SHOWS IN THE EXPANDED VIEW - your ask. Open any item full-size (tap a closed item to its full window) and its number now sits quietly under the title, like #94479. It is the same number you read out to me and the same one search now matches on, so the address of an item is visible right where you are reading it.","IT MATCHES WHAT THE LISTENING-AREA DETAIL ALREADY DID. The quick-peek sheet in the listening area has shown the number under the title since v796; the full-window view was the one surface still missing it. Both now read from the same item number, styled the same quiet tabular grey, and it only appears once the item has synced up and earned a number (a brand-new item that has not reached the server yet simply shows no number, same as everywhere else).","HONEST STATUS: a small placement addition I could not screenshot on your device from the unattended lane. Your eyes on the next release are the acceptance test: open an item full-size and its #number should sit just under the title."],t.s)
B.b1O=new A.v(909,B.am8)
B.adA=s(["SEARCH NOW FINDS AN ITEM BY ITS ID NUMBER - your ask. Type an item number like 94007 (with or without the #) into any search box and the item with that number comes up, even when that number appears nowhere in its words. It rides every search at once - the main search, the Claude-only search, and the inbox search box - because they all share one matcher.","HONEST STATUS: pinned by new tests (found by number, with or without the #, and it flows through the inbox filter too); the whole search suite is green. Nothing else about search changed - your word searches rank exactly as before, the ID is just added to what each item can match on."],t.s)
B.b35=new A.v(908,B.adA)
B.api=s(["ANSWERS NOW APPEAR AS THEY ARE BEING WRITTEN - streaming, your ask, same day. Ask a question on any item and within seconds the full view shows CLAUDE IS WRITING with the answer growing live, paragraph by paragraph, exactly like watching a chat reply form. The finished answer seals into the conversation and the draft vanishes.","BUILT SO A DRAFT CAN NEVER LIE: the draft is its own field, never mixed into the real conversation, and a safety rule hides any draft whose words already landed in a posted answer - so even a lost network write cannot leave a stale draft shadowing the sealed answer. Pinned by tests.","THE SERVER STEP ALREADY RAN: you executed the enabling database migration yourself on Aug 2 (alongside the v894 pin-location one), and the first streaming answer was verified live on item 94013 the same evening. Nothing to do - this note is history, not homework."],t.s)
B.aSj=new A.v(907,B.api)
B.amH=s(["TAPPING ITEMS IN A FOLDER NOW SELECTS JUST ONE - your Android bug. Randomly tapping items in a folder was piling up a multi-selection instead of moving the selection to the item you just tapped. Now a plain tap selects only that item and drops the rest - the selection follows your last tap, on Android exactly like it already did on Windows.",'THE ONE TRADE-OFF: on Android you can no longer build a multi-selection by tapping several items in a list (to drag them into a folder in one go). If you want that back, the clean way is a "select" mode you enter with a long-press, then taps add - say the word and I will add it. Ctrl+click and Shift+click on Windows are unchanged, and the canvas keeps its own tap-to-multi-select.',"HONEST STATUS: pinned by a new test - repeated plain taps always follow the last one and never accumulate, even starting from a multi-selection. This is a touch behavior on your phone I could not tap-test from the unattended lane; your eyes on the next build are the acceptance test - tap around a folder and only the last-tapped item should stay selected."],t.s)
B.b19=new A.v(906,B.amH)
B.aqP=s(["THE FOLDER LIST STOPS JUMPING TO THE TOP WHEN YOU TAP A FOLDER - your bug. Scroll to the bottom of the folder pane, tap a folder down there, and the whole list used to snap back to the top so the folder you just tapped was scrolled out of view. Now the pane keeps its place: tap a folder near the bottom and the list stays where you left it.","WHY IT HAPPENED. The pane forgot how far you had scrolled every time it was rebuilt (the mobile drawer reopening after a tap, or the list redrawing when the selection changed) and started again from the top. It now remembers the scroll position and puts it back on the next redraw. Nothing about the drag-to-scroll behavior changed - only the forgetting.","HONEST STATUS: this is a candidate fix I could not tap-test on your device from the unattended lane. Your eyes on the next release are the acceptance test: scroll the folder pane to the bottom, tap a folder there, and the list should hold its place instead of snapping to the top."],t.s)
B.aRr=new A.v(905,B.aqP)
B.akm=s(["THE SEARCH ICON MOVED UP INTO THE TOP BAR - your ask. The magnifier is now in the very top bar, at the left of the buttons up there, instead of resting inside the list. Tap it and the search box opens right below the bar with the caret ready, exactly the box you know. It is the same search - only where you reach for it changed.",'THE TOP-BAR ICON TELLS YOU A SEARCH IS LIVE. While a query is narrowing the list the icon fills in and turns your accent color, so even with the box scrolled away the top bar shows a filter is on - the same "a search must never be invisible" rule the box already followed. Clear the box and the icon goes quiet again.',"HONEST STATUS: the box still opens just BELOW the top bar (not inside the bar itself), reusing the exact field and clearing rules you already have; the whole test suite is green including the search law. This is a placement change I could not tap-test on your device - open the Inbox, tap the top-bar magnifier, and the search box should drop in ready to type. If you would rather the box expand INSIDE the bar (iOS style), say so and I will switch it."],t.s)
B.b40=new A.v(904,B.akm)
B.aga=s(["TAP THE QUEUE OVERVIEW TO FILTER THE FOLDER - your ask. The overview beside the item count ('3 ready - 2 need you - 4 built') is now three tappable chips. Tap 'need you' and the Queue narrows to just the items waiting on you; tap 'built' to see only what is riding the next release. The chip you tapped lights up, and tapping it again (or another chip) clears back to the full list.","IT ONLY HIDES, IT NEVER CHANGES ANYTHING. The filter is a pure view - no item moves, no number changes, nothing is marked. The chips are always drawn from the full folder, so they - and the way back to everything - can never disappear under their own filter, and if the last item of a bucket leaves, that filter clears itself.","HONEST STATUS: the chip counts and the filter buckets read from one shared source (pinned by tests, all green), so a chip can never show a count the list disagrees with. The feel is yours to confirm on the next release: open the Queue, tap a chip, and only that bucket should remain."],t.s)
B.aVv=new A.v(903,B.aga)
B.ak8=s(["THE QUEUE OVERVIEW NOW SITS BESIDE THE ITEM COUNT, NOT BELOW IT - your ask. Open the Queue folder and the ready / need-you / built overview now rides on the RIGHT of the same line as the item count (e.g. '6 ITEMS' on the left, '3 ready - 2 need you - 4 built' on the right). One line instead of two, so the top of the queue reads in a single glance.","NOTHING ELSE CHANGED ABOUT IT. The overview is still counted straight from the items themselves - your approval toggle plus each item newest status line - and it still only shows in the Queue folder. Empty overview, or any other folder, and the item count stands alone exactly as before.","HONEST STATUS: a visual placement change I could not screenshot from the unattended lane. Your eyes on the next release are the acceptance test: open the Queue and the overview should sit to the right of the item count on one line."],t.s)
B.aRx=new A.v(902,B.ak8)
B.ai7=s(["A FOLDER STAYS SHADED WHILE YOU ARE INSIDE ITS ITEMS - your ask. Opening an item to its full-window view used to drop the highlight off the folder in the left pane, so you lost your place. Now the folder you came from stays lit the whole time you page through its items, and the highlight returns to normal the moment you back out.","IT ONLY LIGHTS THE FOLDER YOU ACTUALLY CAME FROM. Open an item from Files, from search, or from a type view - places that are not a folder - and no folder lights up, exactly as before. The pane never shades the wrong folder.","HONEST STATUS: the shading rule is pinned by new tests (own screen lit, origin folder lit while browsing, a different folder never lit, non-folder sources light nothing). This is a visual change I could not screenshot on your device from the unattended lane - your eyes on the next release are the acceptance test: enter a folder, open an item full-size, and that folder should stay highlighted on the left."],t.s)
B.b_q=new A.v(901,B.ai7)
B.alz=s(["THE QUEUE NOW WEARS A STATUS LINE AT THE TOP - your ask. Open the Queue folder and one quiet line under the dictate box reads something like: 3 ready - 2 need you - 4 built. Ready is approved work no one has started; need you is items waiting on an answer from you; built is finished work riding the next release. Zero counts drop out, and when everything is resting the line disappears entirely.","COUNTED FROM THE ITEMS THEMSELVES - your approval toggle plus each item newest status line, the same words you can read on any item. No stored counters that could drift from what the folder actually shows.","HONEST STATUS: the classification and wording are pinned by tests against the same status vocabulary the desk writes. If the line ever disagrees with what you count by hand in that folder, name the miscounted item - its status line is the input, so the lie will be visible right on it."],t.s)
B.aXO=new A.v(900,B.alz)
B.ajn=s(["FOLDERS CAN NOW SORT BY ITEM TYPE - your ask for the claude folders, available in every folder. Open a folder, tap its actions, choose Sort by Type: the items group as Bugs, then Features, then Tweaks, then Questions, with untyped items last, newest-first inside each group. Sort Newest First switches back.","IT REARRANGES NOTHING REAL. The grouping is pure presentation: no item position moves, Queue numbers stay exactly what they were, and switching the mode back restores the previous view untouched. Dragging an item while grouped adopts the manual order, exactly as dragging from the newest-first view always has.","HONEST STATUS: the grouping rule is pinned by tests (stable order inside groups, unknown types always last). The feel test is yours: your queue folder under Sort by Type should read as a bug list, then features, then questions holding their answers."],t.s)
B.aRH=new A.v(899,B.ajn)
B.acH=s(["CTRL+A SELECTS EVERYTHING IN THE VIEW YOU ARE LOOKING AT - your ask, built the Windows way. Inside a folder it selects all of that folder items; on the main list it selects everything visible there. From a full selection you can drag the lot to a folder, Delete them, or Shift+click to trim the range - all the existing selection moves just work on it.","YOUR TYPING IS NEVER TOUCHED: with the caret in the dictate box or any text field, Ctrl+A stays exactly what it has always been - select the text. The shortcut only acts when no text field owns the keyboard, the same rule the Delete key has always followed. And a stray Ctrl+A on an empty view does nothing at all - it can never silently eat a selection you made somewhere else.","HONEST STATUS: rules pinned by new tests at the selection layer; the suite is green. The feel test is yours: select one item in a folder, press Ctrl+A, and every bar in that folder should light. If a view ever selects more or less than what you can see, name the folder - the visible-order it read from is one probe away."],t.s)
B.b1o=new A.v(898,B.acH)
B.ahz=s(["THE INBOX SEARCH BOX NOW RESTS AS A SMALL MAGNIFIER ICON - your ask. Tap the icon and the same search box you know opens with the caret ready; tap away from an empty box and it folds back to the icon. Less standing furniture above your items.","ONE RULE UNDERNEATH THAT IS A LAW, NOT A STYLE CHOICE: while a search is actually narrowing the list, the box CANNOT collapse - it stays visible until you clear it. A list quietly filtered by an invisible search would read as items having gone missing, which is a bug class this app has been burned by twice before. Pinned by a test.","HONEST STATUS: the icon lives in the Inbox, where the always-visible box was. The earlier note on your item recommended icons inside folder views too - folders have no search field yet at all; that is a separate build and your item stays open for it if you want it."],t.s)
B.aWS=new A.v(897,B.ahz)
B.aml=s(['CLAUDE ITEMS NOW SAY WHEN YOUR QUESTION IS SITTING WITH ME. You asked for exactly this: post a follow-up on a Claude item and, until I answer it, the status strip on the box and in the full view reads "your question is with Claude" - alongside my current status line when one is set, alone when none is. The moment my answer lands, the notice clears itself.',"NO NEW BOOKKEEPING BEHIND IT. The app already tracks every message through sent, received and done for the little footer stamps - this reads that same signal, so the waiting notice can never disagree with the footer or the thread. It also shows on a brand-new item whose conversation has not started yet, which is precisely the moment you most want to know I have not seen it.","HONEST STATUS: rule pinned by new tests on both the signal and the strip wording; the strip renders through the same shared line on both surfaces, so they cannot drift apart. If an item ever says waiting after I have clearly answered, name it - the round-trip stamps on that exact item will say which side lied."],t.s)
B.b_k=new A.v(896,B.aml)
B.aoL=s(['THE LEFTOVER-IN-THE-BOX BUG, ONE MORE ANGLE. v893 added a backstop that drops the restored box text if it already became an item. But it asked "is this whole box contained in ONE item?" - and your default dictation stores each PARAGRAPH you speak as its own row. So a box holding two paragraphs matched no single row, even though both paragraphs were already saved, and it came back anyway. This build checks the box PARAGRAPH BY PARAGRAPH: if every paragraph is already an item, the box is cleared; if even one paragraph was never saved, the whole box is kept (your words are never eaten). Pinned by new tests.',"STILL HONEST: this closes the specific multi-paragraph gap I could prove - it is not a claim the bug is dead. If a leftover survives this build, the box now logs exactly what it restored and how many items it checked, so the next report points straight at the cause. (This also brings Android current: v894 - per-location pins - had not been built for your phone yet, so it rides along in this build.)"],t.s)
B.aRZ=new A.v(895,B.aoL)
B.ate=s(["PINS NOW BELONG TO THE PLACE YOU MADE THEM. Both your pin complaints were the same disease: a pin was a property of the item everywhere, but only the main window ever lifted pinned items - so pinning inside a folder looked like it did nothing there, while haunting the Inbox instead. From this build, the pin remembers WHERE you tapped it: pin an item in the queue folder and it rides to the top of the queue folder; pin it on the main list and it rides the top of the main list; the same item can be pinned in one place and not another. The pin icon lights up only in the place the pin lives.","YOUR EXISTING PINS DID NOT MOVE. Anything pinned before this build keeps behaving exactly as you last saw it - lifted on the main list - until you unpin it or pin it somewhere new. No item lost a pin in the change.","ONE THING TO KNOW UNTIL A SMALL SERVER STEP RUNS: the pin itself still syncs between devices today, but WHICH VIEW it belongs to needs one migration run against the database before it travels - until then a pin made on one device reads as a main-list pin on your other devices. The migration file is ready (019_pinned_in.sql); it is safe to run at any time and I will bring it to you.","HONEST STATUS: the where-does-a-pin-count rule is pinned by new tests, and the safe direction is built in - anywhere the new wiring were missed, the pin simply behaves exactly as it did before this build, never somewhere new. I did NOT drive the running app to watch it (a debug copy would have opened your live database alongside the real app - not worth the risk to your data). Your first pin tap in a folder is the acceptance test: if it does not rise to the top of that folder, say so and the location it recorded is one query away."],t.s)
B.aXV=new A.v(894,B.ate)
B.abK=s(["A SECOND, INDEPENDENT GUARD ON THE LEFTOVER-IN-THE-BOX BUG. You asked me to look again at the box holding an old item after an Android release. v892 fixed the specific path I could prove; this build adds a backstop that does not depend on that path - or any path - being remembered. Every previous fix hardened the same idea: the moment I save an item, I write down its exact words so the box refuses to hand them back. The bug kept returning because one more place would forget to write them down, or write down a shorter version than the box ended up holding.","THE NEW GUARD CHECKS THE ITEMS THEMSELVES. When the app reopens and is about to restore whatever was in the box, it now also looks at the items you actually created in the last two days. If the words about to come back are already contained in one of those items, it drops them - whatever path saved that item, remembered or not. It only runs when something was actually left in the box, so it costs nothing the rest of the time, and it can never eat a genuinely-open note: only words that already became an item.",'HONEST STATUS: this is a belt-and-suspenders on top of v892, not a claim the bug is dead. It closes the whole family of "one more path forgot" rather than a single path. If you still see a leftover after this build, tell me which item and whether it was typed or dictated - the box now logs exactly what it restored, so the next report points me straight at the cause.'],t.s)
B.aYM=new A.v(893,B.abK)
B.aoM=s(["ANSWERED QUESTIONS NOW STAY IN THE QUEUE. You asked for this: a question you sent me was quietly moving itself into Processed the moment I answered it, so the answer scrolled out of the place you were looking. A question is answered, not built - so it now stays put in the queue, with my answer on it, until YOU clear it. This only touches items typed Question; features, bugs and tweaks still graduate to Processed when they ship.","THE LEFTOVER-IN-THE-DICTATION-BOX BUG, ON ANDROID AFTER A RELEASE. You reported that after an update-and-restart the box comes back holding the last item you made before that release. I found a real path for it. The app keeps a private backup of whatever is open in the box so a crash can never lose typed words, and it refuses to hand that backup back if the words already became an item. But it only ever remembered the LAST paragraph you spoke, while the backup held ALL of them - so the instant your dictation had a new paragraph in it, the backup no longer matched what was remembered and slipped straight through the guard. It now records the whole thing, every paragraph, exactly as the box holds it.","HONEST STATUS ON THAT ONE: this closes a specific, provable gap and it is covered by new tests - but this bug has come back before, so I am not calling it dead. If you see it again after this build, tell me which item and whether it was typed or dictated, and it points me straight at the next path."],t.s)
B.aZN=new A.v(892,B.aoM)
B.asE=s(["WINDOWS: DRAG DOWN, THEN SIDEWAYS. Your oldest queue item. Hold an item to lift it as if reordering, carry it sideways over the folder pane - the folder under your mouse lights up - and let go: the item files into that folder instead of reordering. Let go over the list and it reorders exactly as it always has.","HOW IT AVOIDS BREAKING THE REORDER YOU USE DAILY, which is why this waited so long: the reorder drag keeps ownership of your mouse for the whole gesture - nothing is handed off mid-drag. The only thing that changed is what LETTING GO means when you are over a folder. If anything is uncertain, the drop reorders, never files: that is the tested failure direction.","HONEST STATUS: this is built and safe by construction, but a gesture can only be proven by feel. Try it - if the threshold or the feel is wrong, say so and it gets tuned with you at the screen. Windows only, on purpose: on the phone this would fight the scroll."],t.s)
B.b_U=new A.v(890,B.asE)
B.ai0=s(["THE APP NOW TELLS YOU WHEN IT IS SYNCING, right above your item list - the place you were already looking when the folder counts were wrong. Getting the latest from your other devices while it pulls; Sending 3 changes while it pushes, with the real count; Reconnecting to sync when it is not connected, which is the state that explains stale numbers in the first place.","IT KNEW ALL ALONG AND NEVER SAID SO. Connected, uploading, downloading and the pending count have been tracked since the library was built - shown only in a Settings row nobody watches while waiting. Your thirty seconds of wrong counts were thirty seconds of the app knowing it was behind and staying quiet.","IT STAYS QUIET WHEN NOTHING IS HAPPENING, which was the harder half. Sync flickers busy for a fraction of a second constantly, so the strip only appears once work has been running for a beat - but it vanishes the instant work ends, because is-it-done-yet should never lag. Signed out shows nothing at all: that is sync being off, not sync being slow."],t.s)
B.b0J=new A.v(889,B.ai0)
B.abd=s(["FOUND AND FIXED THE DELETE FLASH you reported from the night out folder - the item blinking back for an instant before it goes.","THE CAUSE: when you delete something, the app hides it straight away so the screen feels instant, then writes the deletion. It was UNHIDING the row the moment that write finished - but the list you are looking at is fed by a stream that arrives a beat later. In that gap the row was no longer hidden and not yet gone, so any redraw - and these lists redraw constantly while syncing - painted it one more time before it vanished. Exactly the blink you saw.","THE FIX: the row now stays hidden until the DATA actually removes it, rather than until the write says so. It cannot be unhidden while it is still in the list, and it cannot leave a stale hide behind either.","IT WAS IN BOTH DELETE PATHS - inside a folder and on the main list - so both are fixed. And while I was in there I found the main list was missing a guard every other delete path in the app already had: the one that stops a background refresh resurrecting an item from a snapshot taken before you deleted it. Added."],t.s)
B.aSk=new A.v(888,B.abd)
B.aj_=s(["QUEUE NUMBERS NOW COUNT ONLY WORK YOU HAVE TURNED ON. You asked why an item with the implement toggle OFF still held a queue number - and you were right that it reads wrong. QUEUE #3 sounds like third in line to be built, and an item you have not switched on is not in line at all. Those items keep their exact place where you dragged them and keep every control; they just stop holding a number, and the of-N total now counts only real work.","THE PROCESSED BUTTON IS GONE FROM UNTOUCHED ITEMS. Processed means my side of the work is finished, so putting that button on a brand new item was inviting you to record a finish that never happened. It now appears once something has actually happened to the item - I have written an answer or a status on it, or it has been through Building. Mark Done is deliberately unchanged: that one is yours to press whenever you like, including on something I never touched.","BOTH ARE DERIVED FROM WHAT THE APP ALREADY KNOWS - your toggle, the answer log, the folder - rather than a new flag. A flag would be a second place for a fact that already has a home, which is the single most repeated cause of bugs in this system."],t.s)
B.aVR=new A.v(887,B.aj_)
B.aku=s(["EVERY CLAUDE ITEM CAN NOW WEAR A STATUS LINE - one current sentence from me, like Reproducing on my machine or Waiting on your answer or Built in v886, awaiting release. It shows on the box under the conversation header and at the top of the full sized view, so where-is-this-now is answered at a glance without reading the thread.","THE HISTORY IS KEPT. Every status I have ever set on an item stays in its log with the rest of the conversation - the strip just shows the newest one. And it rides the same synced machinery as my answers, so it reaches every device with no database change and no waiting.","STATUSES AND ANSWERS NEVER MIX: a status cannot show up as a chat answer, and an answer cannot become the strip. Six tests pin the boundary.","FIRST LIVE USE: the item that asked for this feature - System For Item Status Updates - already carries its own status."],t.s)
B.aSL=new A.v(886,B.aku)
B.atN=s(["RED NOW MEANS RECORDING, AND NOTHING ELSE. Tonight you spoke for twenty minutes into a mic the auto-off had already released, because the dictation box wore a red border while idle - and red means live in every recording convention there is. Windows own microphone ledger proved the mic closed at 23:09:35 to the second; the red you saw after that was paint. The box border is now neutral whenever the mic is off, and red appears only while audio is genuinely flowing. The task and meeting boxes got the same rule.","WHEN THE MIC AUTO-STOPS YOU NOW GET A BANNER YOU CANNOT MISS - amber, bold, and it stays until you act on it. Tap it and dictation resumes on the spot. The old announcement was grey placeholder text, which was technically an announcement and practically invisible from across the room.","AND THE TIMEOUT IS YOURS NOW: Settings has a Mic auto-off field - minutes, decimals fine, 0 = never turn off on its own. It syncs to all your devices, and changing it applies to the session already running.","WHAT DID NOT CHANGE: the capture engine, the stop itself, and your words up to the moment it fired - all of that worked correctly tonight. The 1,141 characters you dictated before the quiet gap were saved. What failed was the telling, and that is what this build fixes."],t.s)
B.b1H=new A.v(885,B.atN)
B.apt=s(["ITEMS CAN NOW BE LINKED TO EACH OTHER - the first step of your project management idea. The full view of a Claude item has a RELATED section: tap Link an item, search for the one that belongs with it, and they are connected. Tap a related item to jump to it, and the little minus unlinks without touching either item.","LINKS GO BOTH WAYS AUTOMATICALLY. Link A to B and B shows A - there is no wrong direction to do it in, and linking twice or linking the other way round never makes a duplicate.","LINKS, NOT MERGES, ON PURPOSE. Your own words when we discussed re-reported bugs: link them into a family, do not merge them. A merge destroys which idea came from which moment; a link keeps both and just records that they belong together. Unlinking is always safe - the items themselves are never touched.","AND THEY SYNC. A link made on the phone is on the desktop when you sit down - the connection travels with your data like everything else."],t.s)
B.aUq=new A.v(884,B.apt)
B.agS=s(["THE ASK BUTTON IS GONE, and you were right to ask whether it still earned its place. It was built before items had types. Once the type drop down arrived, Ask became the only control in the app that changed TWO things in one tap - it set the type to Question AND approved the item. Side effects on type are what have gone wrong for you most often, and I had left it sitting there beside the thing that replaced it.","THE GO BUTTON NOW SAYS WHAT IT WILL DO. Set an item to Question and the button reads ASK. Set it to Bug and it reads FIX. Anything else, or nothing set, and it reads IMPLEMENT. So you get two controls instead of three - one for what the item IS, one for go - and go never claims to do something other than what it does.","THE COST, HONESTLY: a question you have not typed yet is now two taps instead of one. I think that is the right trade, because the old single tap quietly changed your items type as a side effect of approving it."],t.s)
B.aSK=new A.v(883,B.agS)
B.asB=s(["THE TYPE PILL IS NOW A PROPER DROP DOWN, opening right at the pill instead of sliding a sheet up from the bottom of the screen. It carries a chevron so you can see it opens something before you tap it, and the current type is ticked.","IT IS THE SAME CONTROL AS THE ONE IN THE DICTATION BOX - same anchoring, same coloured dots, same menu - rather than a second thing that merely looks similar. You asked for the dictation box behaviour back in July with the words: can that drop open there locally so you are not going to a different area. Same reasoning applies here."],t.s)
B.aX4=new A.v(882,B.asB)
B.aik=s(["A SEARCH BOX AT THE TOP OF THE INBOX. Type and the list narrows to what matches, with the matched words highlighted the same way the folder search highlights them.","IT USES THE SAME SEARCH as everywhere else, so an item that matches in the search screen matches here. It does not touch the Notes/Tasks/Meetings chips either - searching inside Tasks searches Tasks.","BUILT SO IT CANNOT HIDE YOUR ITEMS. Twice before, a filter in this list has hidden things by treating did not recognise this as do not show it. So the search can only ever produce a list of items to SHOW, and a blank box produces no opinion at all rather than an empty list. There is a test whose only job is to keep those two apart.","AND A BOX YOU ARE DICTATING INTO IS NEVER HIDDEN by a search, whatever you have typed in the box."],t.s)
B.b0i=new A.v(881,B.aik)
B.ac7=s(["YOU CAN DRAG AN ITEM BACK TO THE INBOX NOW. Drop it on the Inbox row in the left pane and it comes out of whatever folders it was in.","THE REASON IT NEVER WORKED IS SMALL AND A BIT EMBARRASSING: Inbox was the ONLY row in that pane that was not a drop target. Trash takes a drop, Canvas takes a drop, every folder takes a drop - Inbox never did. So items could go anywhere except back.","NOTHING IS DELETED and nothing you wrote is touched. Being in the Inbox is not a place, it just means the item is not filed anywhere - so this takes the filing away rather than filing it somewhere new. The item keeps its type, its approval and its whole history. There is an Undo that puts it back in exactly the folders it came from."],t.s)
B.b12=new A.v(880,B.ac7)
B.ajK=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS CAN NOW TRANSFORM. Every item carries a TYPE chip - Feature, Bug, Tweak or Question. Tap it and pick a different one, and the item becomes that. Ask a question at 10am, decide at 2pm it is really a bug, tap Bug: it is a bug, and the item still shows that it started life as a question.","THREE THINGS RETYPING DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: it does not move the item in the queue, it does not change whether you have approved it, and it does not touch your title. Changing what a thing IS should not quietly change where it is or whether I am allowed to work on it.","I ALSO UNDID PART OF YESTERDAYS QUESTION MODE TO DO THIS PROPERLY. I had folded is this a question into the approval itself. That was fine for a two way choice and wrong for four types - it puts one fact in two places, which is behind nearly every bug that keeps coming back here. The type now lives in exactly one place. Nothing you already marked is lost: the old question marker still reads as a Question.","AND THE POINT OF ALL THIS: the type tells me what working an item means before I start. A Question gets answered. A Bug gets reproduced first, then fixed. A Feature gets designed and confirmed with you before any code. A Tweak just gets done. Right now I guess that from your wording, and I get it wrong sometimes."],t.s)
B.aTr=new A.v(879,B.ajK)
B.aej=s(["QUESTION MODE. Beside Implement there is now an ASK button. Press it and the item goes into the queue as a QUESTION - I answer it rather than build it - and the pill then reads SENT AS A QUESTION with the time, so the item says what it is.","NOT A CHECKBOX, and that was deliberate. A question tickbox sitting next to Implement would be two controls able to contradict each other, and one fact in two places is behind nearly every bug that keeps coming back in this pipeline. There is one question here - what are you asking me FOR - so there is one control that answers it.","I ALSO CHANGED MY MIND ABOUT WHERE TO STORE IT, and you should know why. I recommended tagging the item TITLE with the word QUESTION, reusing a type system we designed in July. On building it, that turned out to be the worse of the two: it edits YOUR text to record a routing decision, and titles are yours. Pressing Ask instead writes a timestamped line into the conversation - same durability, same visibility to me, and it does not touch a word you wrote.","TAKING IT BACK WORKS THE SAME WAY as taking back an Implement, and switching an item between build and question travels between your devices."],t.s)
B.aZq=new A.v(878,B.aej)
B.abu=s(["A MORE BUTTON AT THE BOTTOM OF ANY BOX WITH MORE TEXT THAN FITS. It opens the full sized view, and it only appears when there is actually more to see.","WORTH KNOWING, BECAUSE IT PARTLY REVERSES SOMETHING YOU ASKED FOR: back in July you told me that opening a large item into the full view was jarring, because the whole card grabbed every tap and threw you out of the list. I retired that, and long items got an editor that scrolls inside the box instead. That stays exactly as it is. What is new is a deliberate button you press - which is the opposite of a card that grabs every tap.","AND ONE CASE HAD NO ESCAPE AT ALL: items you cannot edit in place were cut off at the fold with three dots and no way to reach the rest from the box. Those now get the button too."],t.s)
B.b1Q=new A.v(877,B.abu)
B.aeC=s(["THIS IS THE ACTUAL FIX FOR YOUR ANSWERS NOT SHOWING IN THE EXPANDED VIEW. My previous two attempts fixed a real problem, but not YOUR problem - and I only found that out by checking item #73966 against the server instead of assuming.","WHAT WAS REALLY HAPPENING: a while back you told me the expanded view was printing your words twice - once in the box at the top and once again below it. So I made it skip the first message when it just repeats the item text. But it skipped the WHOLE first entry, and my answer lives inside that entry. On an item with only one message - which is most of them - that meant the entire conversation vanished, while the box kept showing the answer from its own copy.","SO: it now hides the repeated words and keeps the answer. Two separate questions that had been sharing one decision. There is now a test whose only job is to assert that hiding a repeat can never hide an answer."],t.s)
B.b_8=new A.v(876,B.aeC)
B.alY=s(["AND A GAP IN YESTERDAYS FIX FOR THE SAME THING: if the app could not load the conversation at all - offline, signed out, a thread that no longer resolves - the full view said Nothing sent yet even when the item was full of answers. An empty list and a failed load are not the same thing, and it was showing both the same way. The answers now show regardless.","THE PURPLE COMMANDS ONLY LABEL NO LONGER COMES BACK ON ITS OWN. After 90 quiet seconds the app used to park itself into Commands only, which looked like it had changed modes behind your back.","AND IT TURNED OUT TO BE DOING NOTHING. That park was added to stop room talk flowing into a live box - but a later fix made the next real speech wake it up AND land, and room talk IS speech. So it woke up and let the talk in exactly as before. What was left was a mode switch you could see and no behaviour behind it. It is gone. The five minute auto-off is what actually bounds a session left open in a noisy room, and unlike the park it tells you it happened.","DELIBERATE PARKS ARE UNTOUCHED: the three-way control, saying end dictation, and tapping into an item all still park, because you asked for those.","SEARCH CHIPS NOW SHOW THEIR COUNTS. This folder and All Claude each carry the number of matches, so you can see what widening the search would find before you tap it. Both numbers come from the same search that produces the list, so they cannot disagree with it."],t.s)
B.b1R=new A.v(875,B.alY)
B.ai1=s(["THE BUILDING NOW CHIP HAS A CLOCK. It shows how long the item has been in building - seconds while it is short, then minutes, then hours, then days if something is genuinely stuck. It ticks on its own rather than rebuilding the whole item every second, because that has been the performance bug of the week here before.","TAPPING THE LISTENING BAR NOW TAKES YOU TO WHERE THE ITEM LIVES. This reverses something you asked for in July, on purpose and on your word: back then you wanted the bar to scroll the listening list to what was playing, and it did. You now want to reach the item where it actually lives so you can edit or delete it, which the listening list cannot do - it is a program, not the items home. So the bar leaves for the folder the item is in, or Home if it is unfiled.","THE FULL-SIZED VIEW NO LONGER SHOWS LESS THAN THE BOX. You said item #17183 had lots of text in the box and hardly any inside the full view. It did, and here is why: your conversation is stored in two places and the two screens were reading different copies.","THE BOX reads the answer log kept on the item, which holds every answer I have ever written on it. THE FULL VIEW was reading the reply attached to each individual message. On that item the log held four long answers while three of its four messages had an empty reply - so one screen looked full and the other looked bare.","THE FIX IS ON THE READING SIDE, deliberately. I could have tried harder to write both copies every time, but keeping two copies in step is exactly what already failed. The full view now shows every answer in the log, including any not tied to a specific message, and matches them by text so nothing appears twice."],t.s)
B.aUG=new A.v(874,B.ai1)
B.afd=s(["YOUR IMPLEMENT MARKS NOW SHOW ON EVERY MACHINE. You said the items you had marked to implement were no longer marked. I checked the server before touching anything: every approval was still there, posted between 13:23 and 13:51, and nothing had withdrawn them. Nothing was lost. The buttons were lying.","THE CAUSE: this machine only ever checked the server about TWENTY of your open Claude items, and it picked those twenty in no particular order. You have far more than twenty. So if you pressed Implement on your phone, whether Windows ever found out was luck. That check now runs for every item - it costs nothing, because the app had already downloaded the information and was throwing most of it away.","THE LISTENING AREA REMEMBERS WHICH FOLDERS YOU OPENED. Leaving the screen used to shut them all. It now remembers, and it SYNCS, so a hierarchy you opened in the car is still open at the desk.","THE INBOX QUANTITIES ARE READABLE. You said they were too light and asked for the same darkness as the folder quantities - so they are literally the same two values now, not my guess at a match. The little arrows beside folders got the same treatment."],t.s)
B.aXY=new A.v(873,B.afd)
B.arj=s(["THE MOUSE WHEEL NOW SCROLLS FROM ANYWHERE, not just when the cursor is sitting on a box. You have been aiming at a box to scroll, and you should not have to.","WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG: the wheel goes to whatever is under the cursor, and the list only counts as being under the cursor where a box actually is. The gutter beside the boxes and the empty space under the last one belonged to nothing at all, so the wheel landed nowhere.","I BUILT THIS AS A FORWARDER SITTING ON TOP, not as a change to the scrolling itself - I have not touched that, per your rule. It can only act where nothing else wanted the wheel, so when the cursor IS over the list, the list still handles it exactly as before. And it stands down completely while a touch scroll is running, so it can never fight a fling in progress."],t.s)
B.aYO=new A.v(872,B.arj)
B.afg=s(["SEARCH RESULTS NOW HIGHLIGHT THE WORD YOU SEARCHED FOR. Every match carries a wash behind it, so your eye lands on WHY an item came back instead of re-reading the whole box. Search two words and the phrase highlights as one, not as two blocks with a white seam down the middle.","AND IF THE MATCH WAS BELOW THE FOLD, THE BOX NOW SHOWS IT. Your second question - whether search reads the whole item or only what the box displays - it always read the whole item. But a box only shows its first lines, so an item that matched further down looked like a false hit, because the word you searched for was nowhere on screen. A result now opens at the match instead of at the top when the match is further down. Near the top, which is most of them, nothing changes.","AN ITEM ALREADY IN PROCESSED IS NEVER PULLED BACK INTO THE QUEUE. Two items I had finished and moved to Processed both reappeared in your Queue at the same second today - one sweep, on your second Windows machine as it came up. The sweep was asking whether it REMEMBERED queuing the item, and a fresh machine remembers nothing. It now looks at where the item actually is, which every machine can see."],t.s)
B.aUR=new A.v(871,B.afg)
B.ag2=s(["DELETING WHAT YOU ARE LISTENING TO NOW MOVES ON TO THE NEXT ITEM. Two separate things were wrong. First, deleting an item that lives in more than one folder does not delete the item at all - it just takes it out of the folder you are in - and the reader was only ever told about deleted ITEMS and deleted FOLDERS, never about something leaving a folder. So it kept reading an item that was no longer there.","SECOND, ONE DELETE TOUCHES SEVERAL TABLES, and those updates arrive in a burst. The reader was already busy handling the first one, so the rest were thrown away - and the one thrown away was sometimes the one that mattered. It now finishes and takes another look.","THE RULE ITSELF IS NOW A TESTED PIECE OF CODE rather than a comment: delete what is playing and it goes to the NEXT item, delete something later in the list and what you are hearing does not skip or restart, delete the last one and it wraps to the top.","PICKING NOTE NOW STICKS. You changed the item type to Note, typed, sent - and the very next box came back as a Claude item. Your pick was being used for that one item and then thrown away, so the box fell back to remembering an OLD item that happened to be a Claude one.","THIS IS THE SIXTH TIME THIS FAMILY OF BUG HAS COME BACK, and the reason is the same every time: your choice had nowhere permanent to live, so something the app merely guessed could outrank it. Your pick is now written down where the guesses cannot overwrite it.","IT WAS NOT ONLY COSMETIC. That stale memory also decides whether your next SPOKEN item is addressed to Claude - so after picking Note, the next thing you dictated could have gone out as a Claude message."],t.s)
B.aWx=new A.v(870,B.ag2)
B.aqL=s(["THE AUDIO CATCH-UP NOW GETS OUT OF THE WAY PROPERLY. Your bedroom machine went sluggish for scrolling right after it updated - and that is exactly what I would expect, because that update gave it the audio converter for the first time, so it started working through its own backlog.","THE FIX I SHIPPED FOR THIS EARLIER WAS FRAGILE. It lowered the converter priority from the app side, moments AFTER the converter had already started - so there was a window where it ran at full speed, and if that call failed for any reason it never lowered at all, silently, because it was deliberately written not to break the conversion.","THE CONVERTER NOW LOWERS ITS OWN PRIORITY AS ITS FIRST INSTRUCTION. A program asking for its own priority cannot race itself and cannot be skipped. It sits just below normal rather than at the very bottom, because the very bottom can starve completely on a busy machine and a catch-up that never finishes is its own problem.","IF IT IS STILL SLUGGISH after this reaches that machine, tell me - because then it is not the converter and I would be guessing to say otherwise."],t.s)
B.b2R=new A.v(869,B.aqL)
B.ajT=s(["PLAY BUTTONS ON EACH FOLDER IN THE LISTENING LIST. Your ask. Every folder row now carries a play button, sitting between the item count and the open-folder arrow, so the row reads count, play, open. Tapping it plays that folder and everything under it without disturbing the rest of your programme.","IT FOLLOWS THE SAME RULES AS PLAY ALL: starting playback ends your dictation for you, and the view STAYS on the listening list so it can follow the playing item down the list rather than throwing you into the full screen player.","A FOLDER WITH NOTHING PLAYABLE SHOWS NO BUTTON, rather than offering one that could only tell you there is nothing to play."],t.s)
B.aZr=new A.v(868,B.ajT)
B.aph=s(["NEW CLAUDE ITEMS NOW SHOW THE GRID. You asked where it was. A brand new item returned early with a bare Sending line and no grid at all - so the one moment the journey is most worth seeing, the very start of it, was the one moment it was hidden.","THE GRID ITSELF ALWAYS HANDLED THIS. There is even a test pinning that an unsent item draws the whole path. It was the box that was suppressing it, not the grid that was missing. The Sending line now sits above the grid rather than in place of it."],t.s)
B.aRd=new A.v(867,B.aph)
B.aeD=s(["TEXT FROM AN OLD DICTATION SHOWING UP IN THE BOX AFTER AN UPDATE. Your report, and this is the third time it has been chased. The guard against it was right - the app records what has already become an item and refuses to restore anything matching. Your log even showed it checking thirteen recorded posts before restoring anyway, which means that particular text was never recorded in the first place.","WHY IT WAS NOT RECORDED: the record was written fire-and-forget on the two DICTATION paths. An app update kills the process, the write never lands, and the next launch restores words that already became an item. The typed path has waited for that write since v715 and was fine, which is exactly why this kept looking fixed - you dictate almost everything, so the broken paths were the only ones you use.","BOTH NOW WAIT for the write to land before moving on. That was the entire point of the earlier fix, which moved the clear off a timer so a dying process could not lose it - and then handed it to a fire-and-forget call, putting the same race back one layer out."],t.s)
B.b_v=new A.v(866,B.aeD)
B.aeg=s(["ITEMS THAT SHOWED NOTHING IN THE FULLSIZED VIEW. You reported two - 23008 and 9844. Both rows were completely intact: title, your text, my answers, all sitting there locally. The view was blank because the whole thing waits behind a spinner while it loads the conversation from the server, and that load had no error handling. One failure anywhere in it left the spinner up forever, which reads exactly like an item with no information in it.","IT NOW SHOWS WHAT IT ALREADY HAS. Your words and my answers are stored on the device and synced; the conversation history is a server read. A server read that fails can no longer blank content the app is already holding - it logs the failure and shows the item text instead.","THE SAME PRINCIPLE AS EVERYTHING ELSE HERE: never let a lookup failure hide words you already have."],t.s)
B.b37=new A.v(865,B.aeg)
B.aot=s(["THE FOCUS BUG - clicking an item and having the cursor jump back up to the dictation box. The rule deciding this was correct; it was being fed bad information. It reads a clock that says when you last clicked something, to tell your click apart from the framework tearing focus down. With no recent click it assumes a teardown and takes the cursor back.","THAT CLOCK WAS BEING TICKED SCREEN BY SCREEN - Home got it, then folders got it after this same bug was fixed there once already. Search and Trash never got it, so clicking an item on those screens read as no-user-input and the cursor jumped. Any screen added later would have inherited the bug for free.","IT NOW LIVES IN ONE PLACE: the shell every screen is built inside. One fact stored in two places is what makes this bug keep coming back, so there is now only one. The old per-screen versions are left in as harmless backup.","IF IT STILL HAPPENS, the log records every input the rule saw and what it decided, on every single loss - so the next report can be answered with the actual numbers instead of another guess.","ALSO: I am now marking items as In Progress the moment I start them, not just when they finish. You noticed that box had been empty while I was working - it had, and that was wrong."],t.s)
B.aZF=new A.v(864,B.aot)
B.abn=s(["THE WINDOWS TITLE BAR NOW SAYS WHEN DICTATION IS LIVE. Your queue item asked for it. It reads a dot and Dictating BEFORE the version, deliberately - the taskbar and Alt-Tab cut titles off from the right, so the part you need while Voicley is sitting behind another window has to come first.","IT COSTS NOTHING WHEN NOTHING CHANGES. The banner only restamps the title when the recording state actually flips, so driving it from the recorder does not cause work on every tick.","ALSO IN THIS BUILD: every item I implement now gets a note posted onto its own conversation - what shipped, in which build, and the cause when it was a bug - before it moves to Processed. You asked for the actions to go into the chat chain. I backfilled the five items finished tonight, so each one already carries its record.","I ANSWERED THE SECOND HALF OF THE BANNER QUESTION on the item itself rather than guessing: what else could alert you that dictation is running, on Windows and on mobile. Nothing built for that yet - it is a list of options for you to pick from."],t.s)
B.aYt=new A.v(863,B.abn)
B.aok=s(["LISTENING RESUMES WHERE YOU STOPPED. Your queue item: can you keep track of where I stopped listening so when I resume it resumes where I left off? It always restarted at item one, so a programme you were forty minutes into began again at the top.","IT REMEMBERS THE ITEM AND ROUGHLY WHERE IN IT, saved whenever you pause or skip - not on a ticker, so it is one small write per action you take rather than one per second of playback. AND IT SYNCS: stop in the car, pick up at the desk.","IT ALWAYS FALLS BACK TO THE TOP. If the item you were on has since been filed or deleted - which happens constantly in a listening list - it starts from the beginning rather than dropping you into the middle of a list you did not ask for. Only the Listening list resumes; opening a folder or the inbox still starts at the top, because that is you asking for THAT list.","THE BACK BUTTON FROM AN ITEM RETURNS TO YOUR MAIN LIST, not the files pane. Your report: in a main list item the back button was bringing you to a folders pane. It was reading the address wrong - for a plain item the marker it looks for sits at the very start, which the check treated as not found, so it fell through to the wrong screen.","FOURTEEN NEW TESTS on the resume rule, including that a re-ordered listening programme follows the ITEM rather than a remembered position number."],t.s)
B.b1S=new A.v(862,B.aok)
B.as5=s(["THREE FROM YOUR QUEUE, worked top-down without waiting to be asked - which is how it should have been all along.","NO MORE CONFIRMATION ON THE IMPLEMENT TOGGLE, either direction. You asked for it and the reasoning holds: the dialog made sense when the button was ONE WAY, because a mis-tap authorised a build you could not take back. Now that it is a toggle it undoes itself - a wrong tap costs one more tap. Guarding a reversible control with a dialog just taxes the ninety-nine correct presses to protect the one wrong one.","THE SEND BUTTON ON A NEW CLAUDE ITEM NOW READS CREATE ITEM. Send described the plumbing; what you are actually doing is making an item.",'AND YOU CAN SAY IT: "create item" or "create the item" now finishes the dictation and creates it, the same as saying "send to claude" always did. The spoken words and the button now match, which was your point.',"ALSO CLOSED: the Processed label question from earlier tonight - that shipped in 859."],t.s)
B.aWC=new A.v(861,B.as5)
B.ap_=s(['FIXING WHAT I BROKE THIS EVENING: the items you had just approved could un-approve themselves on screen. You saw it - "the 10 items I just turned on for implementation are gone or not selected". Your approvals were never lost. They were on the server the whole time and I confirmed all of them, plus every item still in the Queue folder, 69 of them, matching exactly between this machine and the server.',"WHAT WENT WRONG: a background pass re-reads each conversation every twenty seconds to keep the button looking the same on all your devices. It finds the approval by looking at the list of messages that item carries - and when you press Implement, the new message reaches that list by syncing, so for a few seconds there is genuinely nothing there to find. Earlier tonight I taught that pass to work in both directions so it could carry a withdrawal. It then read that brief silence as you having withdrawn, and un-greened items you had just turned on.","THE RULE IT WAS MISSING: not seeing something is not the same as it not existing. The pass now needs a withdrawal it can actually point at before it will take a green button away. Silence leaves your choice alone.","THIS IS THE SECOND TIME TONIGHT I MADE THAT EXACT MISTAKE - the other was treating a missing database value as a real one, which stopped your folders loading. Both are the same error: reading absence as an answer. It now has a test named after it."],t.s)
B.b_f=new A.v(860,B.ap_)
B.ags=s(["THE BUTTONS NO LONGER CLAIM TO BE STATUSES. Your question: why do most of the item boxes say Processed when they were just created and could not have been processed yet? Exactly right. That green pill is an ACTION you press to file the item, and it only appears while an item is sitting in the Queue - but it was labelled with the bare word Processed, so every queued box looked like it was announcing itself finished. It now reads Move to Processed, and the one beside it reads Mark Done instead of Done.",'DICTATING INTO AN ITEM: THE MIC NO LONGER DISAPPEARS UNDER YOUR FINGER. Your report: "the gray box shows up but when I click it nothing happens." The mic on an item shows while you are editing that item OR already dictating into it. Tapping it can take the cursor out of the text, which ended the first condition instantly - and the second one did not begin until the recorder had finished starting up. In the gap the button simply vanished, and if the recorder failed to start at all it never came back.',"IT NOW SHOWS YOUR INTENT IMMEDIATELY, before waiting on the recorder, so the button stays put the moment you press it. If the session genuinely cannot start, it clears itself and says so in the log rather than leaving a dead grey button on screen.","HONEST LIMIT: I found this by reading the code, not by reproducing your exact case - the diagnostic log had already rolled over by the time I looked. The ordering flaw is real and worth fixing either way, but if the mic still does nothing for you, tell me and the log will now record the press and any failure to start."],t.s)
B.b29=new A.v(859,B.ags)
B.af1=s(["THE TOGGLE NOW LOOKS LIKE A TOGGLE. 857 made the Implement button work both ways but left it looking exactly like the one-way button it replaced - so you went looking for a control that was never drawn. There is now an actual switch on the pill: grey when it is off, green when the work is authorised.","THAT WAS MY MISS, not a subtlety. I showed you a switch in four mockups and then shipped something visually identical to what you already had. The behaviour was the risky half and I built that first, which was the right order - but I should have said plainly that nothing would look different, instead of letting the mockups stand as what was coming.","THE SWITCH IS AN INDICATOR, NOT A SECOND BUTTON. The whole pill is still the tap target, as it always was, and the switch deliberately does not handle taps of its own - one control with two gesture handlers is how you end up with the confirmation appearing twice.","NOTHING ELSE MOVED. Same pill, same place in the row beside Processed and Done, same confirmations in both directions."],t.s)
B.aRu=new A.v(858,B.af1)
B.ael=s(['THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON IS A TOGGLE NOW. Press it again and it asks "Stop building this?" - confirm, and I go back to discussing and planning that item until you switch it on again. Your ask: a toggle rather than a one-way button.',"NOTHING IS ERASED WHEN YOU TAKE IT BACK. Withdrawing posts its own line on the conversation, so the thread still shows you approved at one time and withdrew at another, both stamped. That record is what makes the gate worth trusting - the same reason approving was always a message rather than a hidden flag.",'THE PART THAT WOULD HAVE MADE IT LOOK BROKEN: a background pass re-reads each conversation every twenty seconds to keep the button the same on all your devices, and it asked "has this ever been approved?". So withdrawing would have worked - I would have stopped - and then the button would have turned green again within twenty seconds and stayed that way. It now reads your LAST decision, in the order you made them.',"THAT ORDERING MATTERS MORE THAN IT SOUNDS. The list it reads is not in time order, so simply taking the newest entry in the list would sometimes let an older decision win. It now compares timestamps. Twenty-two tests cover the decision rule alone, including that exact trap.","A MIS-TAP STILL CANNOT CHANGE WHAT I AM BUILDING - both directions ask first, and if the message fails to send, the button does not change. It would be worse to tell you the work is stopped when it is not."],t.s)
B.aYV=new A.v(857,B.ael)
B.apQ=s(["THE BOX AND I NO LONGER DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT YOU APPROVED. An item sitting in the Queue folder was drawn as APPROVED even when you had never pressed Implement on it - while I correctly refused to build it. So you could be looking at a green, approved-looking item, waiting on work that was never going to start, with nothing on screen explaining why.","WHERE IT CAME FROM: the progress ladder assumed being in the Queue proved approval, on the grounds that only approved work got filed there. That stopped being true a few days ago when you asked that the folder you are standing in owns whatever you type - so you can drop a raw thought straight into the Queue. The rule I read from was corrected then; the one the BOX draws from was not.","APPROVAL NOW COMES ONLY FROM THE STAMP, never from where the item sits. Being finished still counts, since nothing reaches Processed or Done without having been approved. If you approve on your phone, the desktop catches up within about twenty seconds, the same way it already did.",'AND THE GATE CAN NOW HEAR YOU CHANGE YOUR MIND. It used to ask "has this ever been approved?" - which cannot express a withdrawal. It now reads the LAST decision on the thread, so approve, then withdraw, then approve again all mean exactly what they say. That is the groundwork for the toggle you asked for; the button itself has not changed yet.',"FIFTEEN NEW TESTS on the decision rule alone, because getting it wrong in one direction builds something you withdrew and in the other ignores something you approved. The old test that pinned the wrong behaviour was inverted rather than deleted, so the change is visible in the history."],t.s)
B.aZX=new A.v(856,B.apQ)
B.atK=s(['FIXING WHAT I BROKE IN 853 - your folders would not load their items, and it was my bug, start to finish. Adding the "which device holds this audio" field, I declared it as a value that can never be empty. But your 4,649 existing recordings were already on the server WITHOUT that field, so every one of them came back empty - and the app treats an impossible-but-actual empty value as a hard error.','THE RESULT: every database read of your recordings through the normal path threw an error. Folder contents rendered empty, and the Claude inbox refresh failed once a minute. The main list kept working only because it happens to read a different way, which is exactly why it looked like "folders show but items do not" rather than an obvious crash.',"NOTHING WAS EVER LOST. All 4,649 items, 5,092 folder placements and every transcript were untouched the whole time - I checked the database directly before changing anything. The reads were failing; the data was not.","THE RULE I MISSED: a field added to records that already exist must allow being empty, or every one of those older records becomes unreadable. The audio-location field it was modelled on could get away with it only because it shipped before any records existed. That reasoning is now written next to the field so the next person does not repeat it."],t.s)
B.b3J=new A.v(855,B.atK)
B.aoP=s(['THE AUDIO CATCH-UP NO LONGER SLOWS THE APP DOWN - and this one is my fault twice over. Unblocking the upload queue in 851 and 852 meant it finally started doing the work it had not been able to do for weeks: converting and uploading your backlog of recordings. It works. It also ate a third of the processor, and the list you were looking at went from reading in a fifth of a second to over four seconds. You reported it as "folders are showing up but not the data items".','YOUR ITEMS WERE NEVER MISSING. All 4,647 were there the whole time, the list had built all of them, and there were no errors anywhere - it was just loading too slowly to look like anything. But "folders show and items do not" is indistinguishable from losing your data at a glance, and that is not an acceptable thing to make you wonder about.',"THE CONVERTER NOW RUNS AT THE LOWEST PRIORITY, so it only ever uses processor time nothing else wants. The catch-up still finishes; it just gets out of your way while you are using the app. If setting the priority fails for any reason the conversion still runs normally - a slow catch-up is a nuisance, a failed one leaves your audio stuck on one device.","MEASURED, NOT GUESSED: the converter had used 149 seconds of processor and written 14 files in 15 minutes, while the disk sat at 1.4 percent. So it was competing for processor, not disk, which is exactly what priority fixes."],t.s)
B.aTA=new A.v(854,B.aoP)
B.apW=s(['EVERY RECORDING NOW KNOWS WHICH DEVICE HOLDS ITS AUDIO. Until now a recording only remembered WHERE its file sat, not WHICH machine it sat on - and that note travels to all your devices. So none of them could tell "my file, now missing" from "another machine\'s file, which I am never going to have". Each device now stamps its own recordings, and uploads are claimed rather than guessed.',"YOUR EXISTING 3,649 RECORDINGS ARE UNAFFECTED AND STILL TRIED BY EVERYONE. They predate this and have no owner, so nothing about them narrows - abandoning them would strand their audio for good. Only recordings provably owned by a DIFFERENT device are skipped, which is the one case where trying is guaranteed to fail.",'A BUG I INTRODUCED AND CAUGHT IN THE SAME BUILD: reading the device id sat directly on the path that SAVES a recording, with no time limit. During testing it did not fail - it simply never returned, and hung everything. On your phone that same shape would have stalled a save behind a stuck read. It is now bounded at two seconds and gives up to "unknown", which just means the recording is treated as unowned and still uploads. A timeout costs nothing; a hang costs your words.',"THE ID IS RANDOM AND STAYS ON THE DEVICE. No hardware identifiers, no permissions, nothing that leaves your account - and deliberately not synced, because a shared device id would have every machine claiming to be the same one. A reinstall gets a fresh id on purpose, since that install's files went with it.","THIRTEEN NEW TESTS, and the one that matters checks that ownership can only ever exclude another device's files - never yours, never the unclaimed ones, and never anything at all if the app cannot work out its own identity."],t.s)
B.b3w=new A.v(853,B.apW)
B.aqu=s(['EACH DEVICE NOW ONLY TRIES TO UPLOAD ITS OWN RECORDINGS. Your phone was reading "C:\\Users\\RogerPeters\\OneDrive\\Documents\\recordings\\..." and dutifully reporting it could not find the file - a Windows path, from your other PC, on a phone. It had no way to know that file was never its to upload.',"WHY: a recording remembers where its audio sits on the machine that made it, and that note travels to every device. So all of them walked the same list of 3,649 recordings - 3,250 of which live on the phone, 386 on your two PCs - and each one attempted every single file, eight at a time.","A DEVICE NOW SKIPS PATHS THAT COULD NOT POSSIBLY EXIST ON IT. Windows ignores phone paths, the phone ignores drive letters. Anything it cannot classify is still attempted, deliberately - refusing to upload your audio because a rule was unsure is exactly the kind of quiet loss this app is built to avoid.","THIS IS THE CHEAP HALF OF THE FIX. The proper one gives every recording an owner so uploads are claimed rather than guessed; it changes the synced database and is written up separately rather than rushed in tonight."],t.s)
B.aV5=new A.v(852,B.aqu)
B.aio=s(["YOUR PHONE HAD 3,251 RECORDINGS WHOSE AUDIO COULD NEVER LEAVE IT, and the reason was a single line. The uploader takes the newest 8 recordings still missing a cloud copy, then skips any it has already given up on - but it only skipped them AFTER fetching them. So a handful of files it could never upload came back in every pass, got skipped every time, and sealed the entire backlog behind them. Every 40 seconds, indefinitely, on both your phone and this desktop.","THAT IS WHY THE NUMBERS NEVER MOVED: this machine sat at 664 uploaded, your phone at 594, and nothing looked wrong anywhere - signed in, connected, nothing pending, no errors. The queue was not failing. It was re-reading the same eight rows forever.","THE SKIPPED FILES ARE NOW EXCLUDED BY THE DATABASE QUERY ITSELF, so the queue always advances. Skips only last for the current run, so a file skipped for a passing reason gets a fresh try next launch - nothing is written off for good. Eight new tests pin it.","AND THE SKIP THAT SAID NOTHING NOW SAYS SOMETHING. When the audio file is simply gone from the device, the uploader used to give up in total silence - no log line at all. That was the one invisible path in the whole pipeline, and it is what made your phone impossible to diagnose from here; I had to read its database rather than its log. It now records which recording and which file.","ALSO WORTH KNOWING: some of the phone failures were the cellular problem you have hit before - the network cannot look up the server address at all on that carrier, so uploads die before they start. That is not fixed by this build. On Wi-Fi they go through."],t.s)
B.b17=new A.v(851,B.aio)
B.alv=s(['DESKTOP RECORDINGS NOW CARRY THEIR AUDIO. Recordings dictated on the Windows app reached your other devices as text only - the play button said "no audio to play". Windows now makes the same compact speech copy the phone makes (about 15x smaller than the raw recording) and uploads that, and desktop recordings that were stuck without audio backfill automatically, newest first, while the app sits open.',"THE MEASUREMENT THAT FOUND IT: you asked what else was needed for everything to sync. Your text was fully synced - but 322 recordings on this machine had their audio nowhere else, 4.7 GB of it, and 3,254 more from the phone were in the same state. The transcript was safe on all of them; the recording itself lived on exactly one device.","WHY IT HAPPENED: the uploader skips any file over 25 MB, and Windows had no way to shrink one. The phone converts its recordings to a small speech file before uploading; Windows had no converter, so it tried to send the raw recording, and anything long was skipped forever. Two of yours - 86.6 MB and 45.7 MB - had been retrying and failing over and over in the log.","WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX YET: the phone recordings that have no cloud copy. The phone can already make the small copy, so their cause is something else, and I would rather measure that than assume it is the same problem.","YOUR ORIGINAL RECORDING IS NEVER TOUCHED. The compact copy is made alongside it and only that copy is uploaded - capture-first, same as always."],t.s)
B.aUp=new A.v(850,B.alv)
B.amJ=s(["THIS MACHINE IS SIGNED OUT AGAIN, AND 2,825 OF YOUR CHANGES CANNOT LEAVE IT. Same as 29 July. Everything you have typed and said is safe and stored locally - none of it is lost - but it is not reaching the server or your other machine, and it will not until you sign in here. That is the one thing this build cannot do for you.","THE PART THAT IS MY FAULT: last time this happened I added a heartbeat so the app would SAY it was in this state instead of looking healthy. It has never written a single line. I put it inside the loop that only starts once sync connects - and connecting is exactly what a signed-out app skips. So the one state it was built to report was the one state it could never report from. Same blind spot as last time, one layer further out.","FIXED PROPERLY NOW: the health line is written at startup, before the signed-out exit, and the timer that repeats it starts regardless. Signed in or out, connected or not, the log now says so every couple of minutes - with the number of changes waiting to go out.","HOW I FOUND IT: I was verifying that 848 actually wrote your folded-boxes arrangement. It did - the records were created correctly and are queued - but none had reached the server, and the reason turned out to have nothing to do with 848."],t.s)
B.aWn=new A.v(849,B.amJ)
B.agM=s(["YOUR ARRANGEMENT FOLLOWS YOU NOW TOO - the last five things that lived only on whichever machine you happened to be sitting at: which item boxes you have folded shut, which folders are open in the side pane, the order you dragged your listening program into, the voice phrases you added yourself, and the item type you last picked.","THE ONE YOU WILL NOTICE IS THE FOLDED BOXES - this machine has 170 of them folded shut, and every other machine has been showing all 170 wide open. Folding them was work, and switching machines threw it away every time.","THE VOICE PHRASES ARE THE CLEAREST CASE OF ALL: a phrase you taught the app worked on one machine and did nothing on the other. You would say it, and nothing would happen.","EACH ONE TRAVELS AS A SINGLE RECORD, DELIBERATELY. Your folded boxes could have been stored as 170 separate synced rows, but then one tap on collapse-all would fire 170 sync operations - and the previous night was spent getting that upload queue DOWN, after you asked why so many items were stacked up waiting. So the whole arrangement moves as one small record, written once per change. The trade is that the last machine to change something wins rather than the two merging; for a folded box that costs one tap. Nothing you SAID is ever stored this way.","A CORRECTION TO WHAT I TOLD YOU IN 847. I said the sync fix had been doing nothing because the app already knew about every item on your machine. That reasoning was wrong. The real reason the new table sat empty is duller: this machine has never hand-titled an item and carries none of those pipeline marks, so there was genuinely nothing here to carry up. 846 was not broken and neither was 847 - I diagnosed an empty table by reasoning about it instead of looking at what was actually in it, which is the same mistake I made five times the night before. The carrying-up code is still right, and still needed on the machine that does have those marks.","WHICH MAKES THIS THE FIRST BUILD THAT CAN ACTUALLY PROVE THE SYNCED STORE WORKS, because your 170 folded boxes finally give it something real to write. I will check that rows appear and reach the server rather than tell you it should be fine.","SIXTEEN NEW TESTS pin the parts that would silently cost you the arrangement: that the new store reads what the old build saved (so upgrading loses nothing), that it keeps writing the old format (so rolling back loses nothing), and that a value it cannot read starts blank instead of crashing."],t.s)
B.b2m=new A.v(848,B.agM)
B.amE=s(["CARRIES YOUR EXISTING MARKS UP TO SYNC - and without this, yesterday's sync fix would have done nothing for a single item you already own. I only found it by checking: after installing 846 I watched the new table for four minutes and it stayed empty. The reason was simple and would have been invisible - the app only records a mark when it first learns about an item, and it already knew about every item on your machine, so nothing ever got carried up. Only brand-new items would have synced.","IT NOW CARRIES THEM UP ONCE, in the background, in batches, on each machine. Nothing blocks and nothing is lost if it is interrupted: your local marks still apply throughout, and if a pass does not finish the next launch picks it up again.","WHAT THIS ACTUALLY BUYS YOU: the items you have already dragged out of Queue or Processed stay where you put them on every machine, and the titles you have already typed by hand are protected everywhere - not just on items you touch from now on."],t.s)
B.b2H=new A.v(847,B.amE)
B.amL=s(['YOUR DECISIONS NOW FOLLOW YOU BETWEEN MACHINES - your rule: "everything should be synced between machines, there isn\'t really any work that I do that is machine specific." Several things the app remembered about your choices were stored only on the machine you happened to be using, and each one caused a real problem.',"THE WORST ONE WAS COSTING YOU WORDS: when you rename an item yourself, the app adds it to a do-not-let-the-AI-touch-this list - and that list was local. So on your other machine the list was empty, the guard passed, and the AI re-titled the item, overwriting the name you had typed. Silently. That list is synced now.",'ALSO FIXED - the two marks recording "I already filed this item into the Queue (or Processed) once, so leave his placement alone." Local as well, which is why items you dragged out kept coming back: a machine that had never seen an item had no memory of filing it, put it back, and that move synced everywhere and undid you. I watched exactly that happen to an already-finished item, twice, the second time as a fresh install came up.',"BUILT SO A LOST MARK CANNOT MOVE YOUR ITEMS: the new store reads BOTH the synced list and the old local one and never forgets either, so upgrading cannot lose a mark you already had. It still writes locally too, so the app works signed out and nothing breaks if you roll back. If the synced read is slow or unavailable, your local answer stands rather than the app guessing.","WHAT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SYNC: pane width (that screen), the what-is-new marker (builds differ per machine), the list performance measurement (that hardware), diagnostic logs, and unposted text still sitting in a dictate box - two machines with live drafts could fight over it, so that one is untouched until you say otherwise.","AND THE READING FIXES FROM THE SAME SESSION: the full-size view was showing your paragraphs as single breaks instead of blank lines, because it read the stored text raw while the box view converted it - and worse, editing there SAVED a different paragraph marker than editing in the box, so the two editors disagreed. Both now speak one dialect. The full view also printed a Claude item twice, once boxed and once not, because it shows the item AND the conversation whose first entry is that same message; a duplicate first entry is now hidden, but only when it is provably identical."],t.s)
B.aXw=new A.v(846,B.amL)
B.amA=s(["THE LIST NOW READS ONLY WHAT CHANGED - the last piece of your 1-second delete. With the runway fixed, the remaining time was one thing: every refresh read all 4,500 items from the database (a fifth to a half a second) and then reported finding NOTHING new, over and over. It now reads just the items that actually changed since the last look.","THIS IS THE CHANGE I HAVE BEEN AVOIDING ALL NIGHT, because narrowing what the list reads is precisely how your items got hidden from you twice before. So it refuses itself in three separate ways: it only runs when the item count has not FALLEN (a fall means something was deleted somewhere, and a partial read cannot see a deletion); it forces a full read every couple of minutes regardless, so anything missed can be stale for minutes rather than forever; and after merging it counts the items independently and does the full read anyway if the two numbers disagree by even one. The merge itself can never remove anything - only a full pass may do that.",'THE RULE UNDERNEATH IT: an item missing from a partial read means nothing at all, because a deleted item simply is not in the batch. Treating "absent" as "deleted" is the exact mistake behind both times your items vanished. Ten new tests pin that, including the one that matters most - an item not mentioned in the batch must still be on your screen afterwards.','AND IF SOMETHING EVER DOES LOOK MISSING, the log now says "incremental_mismatch" with both counts at the moment it happened, so it is a fact to read rather than a mystery to reproduce.'],t.s)
B.b1L=new A.v(845,B.amA)
B.adq=s(["CORRECTING MY OWN 843 BEFORE YOU HIT IT. That build was supposed to make deleting instant by keeping the list's big pre-build runway only for scrolling. It would not have worked, and I caught it reading my own code: the runway was armed the moment you TOUCHED the list - and deleting starts with a tap. So the tap grew the runway back to a thousand items and the delete paid the same 1.7 seconds it always did. The fix would have done nothing for the one thing you asked me to fix.","WHAT CHANGED: the runway now arms on the first real MOVEMENT, not on contact. A tap does not move; a scroll does. So tapping delete, or tapping to create, keeps the list on its short runway and the rebuild is small - while a genuine drag still gets the full runway within the first few pixels, before a fling has any speed, which is what the scrolling fix needed. The mouse wheel arms it directly, since there is no finger to watch.","IF DELETING IS STILL SLOW ON THIS BUILD, tell me and I will stop guessing at this area - it will mean the rebuild cost is not the runway after all, and the frame log will say so with a number rather than another theory from me."],t.s)
B.aZb=new A.v(844,B.adq)
B.afS=s(["THIS IS THE ONE - the actual cause of your slow deletes and creates, found and fixed. Your list was told to keep 120,000 PIXELS of items built and laid out at all times. Not the eight you can see - somewhere between six hundred and a thousand of them. Every time the list refreshed, all of them were rebuilt. That is the 1.7 seconds.","HOW IT WAS PROVEN, after three of my theories were wrong: Flutter's own frame data. The worst frame spent 1673ms BUILDING and only 22ms waiting to start, with the graphics card idle at 9ms. So it was real work, in the widget layer, and none of the four things I had instrumented (all of them milliseconds). I had even talked myself into believing that 2-second number was a measurement glitch because it never varied - but a fixed-size runway is a fixed amount of work, which is exactly why it never varied. Right clue, wrong conclusion.","THE FIX KEEPS BOTH THINGS YOU WANT. That long runway exists for a good reason - it is what stopped the scroll braking when passing large items, which you reported back in v690 - but it is only needed WHILE you are scrolling, and refreshes happen when you delete or create, which is when you are not. So the list now takes its full runway the instant you touch it (or turn the wheel on Windows) and hands it back a moment after everything settles. Scrolling is untouched; deleting and creating stop paying for a thousand items they never needed.","THE CARE: the long runway is armed on touch-DOWN, while your finger is still stationary, never once the list is already moving - re-laying out under a moving finger is the exact jank this whole area has caused before. It also arms on the mouse wheel, because on Windows there is no finger to detect, and it never shrinks while a finger is down or the list is still gliding."],t.s)
B.aVM=new A.v(843,B.afS)
B.adP=s(["THE AI TITLE IS OFF THE CREATE PATH - your ask, and it explains why creating always felt a second slower than deleting (your timings: 3 seconds against 2). The title was already fire-and-forget so it never blocked you, but it fired in the same instant you posted: a network call to OpenAI, then a write that bumps the item, which repaints the list exactly while you are watching it appear. Deleting has no equivalent call, which is the whole difference. The title now waits until the create is over and finishes quietly a few seconds later. Same title, same repaint when it lands - it just no longer competes with the moment that matters.","THIS ALSO MATTERS MOST WHEN PASTING - importing several items used to fire one network call per item, all at once. Now they queue up behind the import instead of fighting it.","AND THE 2-SECOND FRAME GETS A STRAIGHT ANSWER. I have instrumented four suspects and every one came back in single-digit milliseconds, while the frame log kept insisting one frame costs two seconds. That number is suspiciously identical every time - 2.0 to 2.4 seconds, never anything else - and real work is never that consistent. So this build logs what Flutter itself knows: how long the frame spent WAITING to start versus actually working. If the two seconds is waiting, I have been chasing a ghost for two builds and will say so.","NOTHING ELSE CHANGES. Your words, your items and your ordering are untouched."],t.s)
B.aZH=new A.v(842,B.adP)
B.afZ=s(["SYNC NOW SAYS WHETHER IT IS ALIVE - and the reason this build exists is uncomfortable. Your two Windows machines were showing different folder counts, and the cause turned out to be that one of them had stopped exchanging anything with the server in BOTH directions: 26 of the server's 40 newest items never arrived, and 2,408 local changes could not leave. That went on for hours with no error anywhere and nothing on screen to tell either of us.",'THE SILENCE WAS THE REAL BUG. Every "cannot sync" path in the code simply stopped quietly - no session, no credentials, nothing to report - because as far as the sync engine is concerned that is not a failure. So the only symptom was two machines slowly disagreeing. A heartbeat now records the truth every cycle: signed in or not, connected or not, when it last synced, how many changes are waiting, and any upload or download error. Whether it is working or not, the answer is in the log.',"WHAT I BELIEVE IS WRONG ON THAT MACHINE: it is signed out. There is no saved session on it, which makes the sync engine authenticate nothing while still reporting itself connected. Signing out and back in there should restore it. Your local items are NOT touched by signing out - I checked the code specifically for that before suggesting it - and none of your words are lost either way; they are stored locally and simply have not travelled yet.","NO OTHER BEHAVIOR CHANGES. This build adds one log line and nothing else."],t.s)
B.aVc=new A.v(841,B.afZ)
B.acI=s(["MEASUREMENT ONLY - nothing about the app changes in this build. After 839 your deletes were still around 3 seconds, and I was honest that my last fix missed: the database is now only about four tenths of a second of that, but ONE frame still costs nearly two seconds, and nothing in the log says WHICH piece of work that is.","SO THIS BUILD NAMES THEM. Four new numbers in the diagnostic log, each around a piece of work that walks your whole library on every refresh: applying the rebuilt list, filtering which items the window shows, counting folder contents, and working out which items are filed. The same kind of probe that caught the last two real culprits - the one that had been quietly recording the answer in your log for weeks before I thought to read it.","WHY I AM NOT GUESSING AGAIN: tonight every time I reasoned about where the time went I was wrong, and every time I measured I was right. Three of my theories died on measurement. Rather than ship a fourth guess at your list, this build makes the next log say the answer with a name and a number attached.","AND THE BIGGER ISSUE ON THIS MACHINE IS NOT SPEED - it is that it has stopped exchanging anything with the server in either direction, which is why your two machines show different folder counts. Signing out and back in on that machine should restore it, and your local items are not touched by doing so - I checked the code before suggesting it."],t.s)
B.aVk=new A.v(840,B.acI)
B.agP=s(["CREATING AND DELETING SHOULD BE QUICKER AGAIN - your timings drove this. After 838 the database was no longer the problem (a list read was down to about a third of a second, with no waiting for a turn at all), so I went looking at the drawing side, and the log was blunt: ONE frame took 1.6 seconds to build, and a six-character note caused TWO complete rebuilds of all 4,535 items, each reporting that it found nothing new.","FIRST FIX - the list stops re-making everything. Every refresh used to build 4,535 brand-new item objects even when nothing about them had changed, so nothing downstream could tell an untouched item from a changed one and the whole list repainted. Untouched items now keep the object they already had, so only what actually changed gets redrawn. Same items, same order, same everything on screen - the wasted work is what goes.","SECOND FIX - the AI title no longer causes a second full rebuild. Posting a note rebuilt the list, then the AI title came back a moment later, changed the item, and triggered the whole rebuild AGAIN. That was half the cost of creating anything. The title now tells the list directly, in the same breath as saving, so the follow-up refresh finds nothing to do. The title still appears - it appears from that update.",'AND ONE GAP CLOSED WHILE I WAS IN THERE, found by the new tests rather than by luck: the check that decides "did anything change?" was ignoring an item\'s audio. An audio clip landing on its own could not repaint the item, so its play control would not appear until something else forced a redraw. That is the same shape as the title bug from v788 - a field the box draws that the change-test could not see. Nine new tests pin all of it, including that a real change (text, title, type, status, reply, audio) always redraws.'],t.s)
B.aWU=new A.v(839,B.agP)
B.am2=s(['THE ANSWER TO "WHY ARE SO MANY ITEMS STACKED UP TO BE UPLOADED" - they were never real changes. Your app was saving the same untouched items over and over. One log held 1,213 saves: the same item, at the same byte length, 27 to 30 times each. Every one of those phantom saves queued an upload, bumped the item, made the app re-read your whole library, and ordered a fresh AI title - 690 title calls, about 24 per item, each a real paid API call.','WHY IT LOOPED: the editor asks "did this text change?" by converting what is on screen into the stored form and comparing. But it compared that against the item\'s RAW stored text - two different alphabets. A paragraph can be stored either as a special paragraph character or as a plain blank line, and the conversion only goes one way. So for any item stored the second way the two sides could never match, the item read as changed forever, and it saved, re-read, and still looked changed. Both sides now go through the identical conversion, so the question is about YOUR typing rather than which marker happens to be on disk.',"THIS WAS THE ENGINE UNDER TODAY'S SLOWNESS - the sluggish creating, loading and deleting, the stalls, and the upload count that would not come down. It was never your data: those writes were byte-identical and I checked your rows against the server, which matched exactly. Nothing was lost or damaged, it was pure wasted work.","THE CARE TAKEN, because this lives on the save path: sixteen tests pin it in BOTH directions. An untouched item must never re-save - stored either way, mixed markers, extra blank lines, trailing breaks. And a REAL edit must still save - a typed word, a deletion, a new paragraph, a soft line break, clearing the box, even a single character. Making this test stricter is only safe if genuine edits still land, so that is asserted, not assumed."],t.s)
B.aZZ=new A.v(838,B.am2)
B.ame=s(["DELETES AGAIN - your three timed tests are what found this. 3 then 11 seconds, 2 then 9, then 2 and 2, then long again. Two things hid in that pattern: a QUEUE, because the second delete reliably waited on the first one finishing, and a floor that stayed around 2 seconds even after the queue drained. Yesterday's build fixed the wrong half of it.","WHAT I GOT WRONG IN 836: I sped up how the app REBUILDS your list after a change (that part worked - it went from 55 rebuilds in two seconds down to one), but the app was still RE-READING your entire library from the database on every single write. Around 4,500 items, each with 28 fields pulled out of stored text, on the one database connection everything else needs. So deleting one item spent that connection re-reading everything, and your next delete had to wait its turn behind it. That is the queue you timed.","THE FIX: the app now re-reads the library ONCE after a burst of changes settles, instead of once per change. Same rows, same order, nothing filtered differently - only how often it asks. Your log made this provable rather than guesswork: a query that does literally nothing waited 35.9 seconds for a turn while the real read took 1.3. The work was never slow; getting a turn was, so the fix is to stop spending turns.","HONEST LIMITS: the deep cause is that syncing and reading share one connection, so a heavy sync catch-up - right after you update a machine, for instance - can still make things crawl. This build reduces how much the app competes with itself, it does not remove that sharing. If deletes still drag once your machines are in sync, the log now has the numbers to prove where it goes, and I would rather have those than another theory."],t.s)
B.aZt=new A.v(837,B.ame)
B.ace=s(["THE LIST AND THE DELETES SHOULD BOTH FEEL FASTER - this is the fix for your question, why the list gets worse as the number of items grows. Your own log convicted it: in one two-second window the app rebuilt your entire library FIFTY-FIVE times, burning over a full second of the drawing thread, with 90 of 122 frames stuttering. Across the session that climbed from 348ms to 1127ms per window. That is the same starvation that made items not appear after you hit send - the app had already fetched your list in a tenth of a second and then took minutes to get a turn to draw it.","THREE CHANGES, and the first one is almost embarrassing: the app already had a throttle to stop that storm, but it sat AFTER the expensive work instead of before it. So all 55 rebuilds happened and about 49 of them were thrown away without ever reaching the screen. The throttle now comes first. Second, when one item changes, only that item is rebuilt instead of all 4,518. Third, deleting no longer makes the app re-read your whole library to be told about the deletion it just performed - it recognises its own work.","WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, deliberately: not one thing about WHICH items the list shows. Every filter is untouched, because narrowing what the list reads is exactly how items got hidden from you twice before, and I am not repeating that to win a benchmark. The delete shortcut is arithmetic, not a guess - it only applies when the numbers match its own deletions exactly, and anything unexpected at all falls back to the full re-read. Nine tests pin that it fails safe, including the case where a new item of yours arrives in the same moment as a delete.","HOW WE WILL KNOW: the same probe that convicted this is still running, so the next log says plainly whether the number came down. If deletes or the list still drag, that log is the evidence and I want it rather than another theory."],t.s)
B.aT6=new A.v(836,B.ace)
B.abq=s(['THE FOLDER YOU ARE IN OWNS THE ITEM - reverting yesterday\'s v834 choice, on your word: "when I am in a folder and use the dictate box to enter text and hit send it should for sure create that item in the folder I was in." v834 stopped an item written inside Queue or Processed from being filed there, because being in the Queue folder is the ONLY record that you approved something - so writing a note there silently marked it approved. I was right about the approval and wrong about the placement, and you watched your items vanish out of the view you were typing in. Placement wins: write in a folder, the item is in that folder, Queue included.','AND THE APPROVAL GATE STILL HOLDS, because it no longer has to guess. Tapping Implement already writes a real timestamped record on the item; it just was not what decided anything. Now it is. Your items stay where you put them, and I pick my next job by asking "did he tap Implement?" instead of "is it sitting in the Queue folder?" - so an item you drop in the Queue to get it out of your head can never again jump the line above work you actually approved.',"A PROBE FOR THE DISAPPEARING-ITEM BUG - the other half of this build, and it changes no behavior. It adds ONE number to the diagnostic log, and that number is the whole point: how long the app waits to get a turn at the database, measured right beside how long the read itself takes.","WHY IT MATTERS: the list rebuild has been logging reads of 16 to 452 SECONDS, and until now those two things were indistinguishable in a single number - waiting versus working. I already proved the database itself is innocent (the identical read finishes in a tenth of a second outside the app) and that getting a turn is instant when the app is quiet. What I still need is that same measurement at the moment it goes WRONG. If waiting is also slow then, the cause is proven outright, because a do-nothing query cannot be slow for any other reason.","SO IF IT STARTS EATING ITEMS AGAIN, please do NOT restart the app - that is exactly when the log is worth the most, and I can pull it straight off the machine. Your words were never at risk in any of this: every item you reported was on the server, correct and undeleted. The bug is in showing them to you, not in keeping them."],t.s)
B.b0p=new A.v(835,B.abq)
B.ajh=s(['WRITING IN THE QUEUE FOLDER NO LONGER PUTS THE ITEM IN THE QUEUE - your question ("If I enter a new item without hitting implement why is that item showing that it is #1 in the queue?") and your priority bug were the same bug, and I caught it live in your own data this morning: your three test items went straight to the TOP of the queue, above real work, with no Implement tap anywhere in their threads.',"WHAT WAS HAPPENING: two rules that are each right on their own were fighting. A folder you are working in files what you write there - that is how folder dictation is supposed to work. And being in the Queue folder IS the approval; there is no separate flag. So typing a thought while standing in the Queue quietly granted it an approval you never gave, and it jumped the Implement gate that exists to stop exactly that.","THE RULE NOW: Queue, Processed, In Progress and Done are STAGES, not inboxes - you cannot approve something by standing next to the approved pile, and you certainly cannot write something that is born finished. An item you create in any of those files one level up into the Claude folder instead: still a Claude item, still sent, still right where you were working, and it waits for your Implement tap like everything else. Every other folder is untouched, including Brainstorm - writing an idea while sitting in Brainstorm still parks it in Brainstorm, because that one is you filing, not the app inventing a status."],t.s)
B.aUY=new A.v(834,B.ajh)
B.atI=s(["TOOLTIPS ON THE WINDOWS CONTROLS - your move-forward order, first pass. Hover any icon-only control for a beat and it names its click: the pin (Pin to top / Unpin), the item number (Copy item ID), the fold chevron, the trash can (Move to Trash), the headphones (Add to / Remove from listening list), the type badge (Change item type), the back-to-top arrow, and the folder pane arrows and plus. The rule applied everywhere: a control that already says its name in text gets NO tooltip - repeating it would be noise.","AND THE NEW-ANSWER MARK SHOWS IT NAVIGATES - your follow-up ask. On Windows the cursor becomes the pointing hand over it and a hover tooltip reads Open the full item, so the single-click behavior you asked for in v821 now announces itself. All of this is desktop-only: the phone has no hover, and nothing about any tap changed."],t.s)
B.b_d=new A.v(833,B.atI)
B.ahW=s(["A POSTED ITEM CAN NO LONGER VANISH FROM THE LIST - your count-goes-up-but-no-item report, caught live in your own log. The list rebuild re-reads your whole library (4,494 items), and under heavy load today those rebuilds ran NINE TO EIGHTY SECONDS - so a rebuild that started before you hit send finished after it, and painted the list from a snapshot that predates your item. The count uses a quick separate query, which is why it knew better. The fix: every just-posted item now rides a protection window - a stale rebuild GRAFTS it back in instead of wiping it, the same shield deletes have had since v546, pointed the other way.","THE FEEL THIS BUYS: send an item, it appears below the box and STAYS, no matter what the rebuild machinery is doing behind you. Deletes were already instant to the eye; now posts are too. The rebuild itself still runs behind as the checker - its cost on a loaded machine is real and is its own project - but it can never again be the reason your words are not on screen."],t.s)
B.b0V=new A.v(832,B.ahW)
B.an5=s(["THE ID NUMBER SHOWS ITS COPY BUTTON ON HOVER - your approved Windows ask. Mouse over any item number and a small copy glyph fades in beside it with the pointing-hand cursor; one click copies the number, same confirmation as before. The glyph holds its space invisibly, so the bar never shifts under your cursor. On the phone nothing changes - tap-to-copy stands."],t.s)
B.b1m=new A.v(831,B.an5)
B.alg=s(['SAYING "NEW NOTE" NOW VISIBLY SWITCHES THE BOX - your report an hour ago: it flashed but the type never changed, and back-to-back thoughts read as one item. You had found the last hole in the type system: the spoken new-note command carried no type signal at all, so the fresh box just kept whatever the folder or memory said. It now carries one - the exact machinery "new task" got in v828 - so "new note", "next note", and even the drive-time mishearing "no notes" all flip the box to a plain note the moment they land, in any folder, over any previous type.','THE DELIBERATE EDGES: "resume dictation" does NOT retype - resuming means continue as before, so it only re-arms the box you had. "New note in cash flow" both retypes AND files to the folder. And a "new task" or "message for Claude" spoken in the same breath still wins over the note switch - the more specific command always takes the box.','YOUR TEST: dictate a thought, say "new note", dictate another - two separate note items, visibly typed as notes while you talk. Then the sequence from your report: "new note", "new claude", "new task" - the badge should follow you through all three now, out loud or by button.'],t.s)
B.aSx=new A.v(830,B.alg)
B.akY=s(["PICKING A TYPE NOW WINS EVERYWHERE - your report from the Claude Inbox: clicked New Note and the item would not switch from a Claude item to a note. You found a real hole: your pick had a home the folder rule could not see, so inside any Claude-side folder the folder always outranked you. The ladder is now explicit, in one place, in this order: YOUR PICK first, then a spoken Claude address, then the folder default, then the remembered type. A pick beats every default on every surface - the badge, the typed save, and the spoken close all read the same answer.","TWO SIBLINGS FIXED IN THE SAME SWEEP, because they were the same disease: picking Task or Meeting on a Claude-addressed box now clears the address (it used to stand, and the box could still save as Claude), and a type picked AFTER you tap Off can no longer retype the item that is still finishing in the background - the closing item keeps the type its box showed at the moment you stopped, and your new pick waits for the next box."],t.s)
B.aRk=new A.v(829,B.akY)
B.afz=s(['SAYING "NEW TASK" NOW WORKS EXACTLY LIKE EVERY OTHER ITEM - your Big Bug order, built. The double cursor and the text-landing-in-the-dictation-box both came from one root: "new task" ran through a second capture machine that predates the composer, so one sentence could drive TWO boxes at once. No more. Spoken "new task" now does precisely what tapping Task does - the box you are watching becomes the task, one box, one caret, and your next words land in it.','THE SPOKEN "CREATE TASK ... END TASK" COMMAND IS UNTOUCHED - that flow (which writes your OneNote checkbox page) is the capture machine\'s whole point, and it keeps it. What changed is only "new task" and "next task", which now retype instead of capturing. One guard to know: "that next task" and friends stay plain words - a leading the/that/my blocks the command, because stealing words is worse than missing a command.','YOUR VERIFY, the exact recipe from your report: say "new note", then "new claude", then "new task". The box should follow you through all three, never vanish, and exactly ONE cursor should blink - in the box your words are landing in. Seven tests pin the new routing; if the double cursor survives this build, the log now records a task_switch line at the exact moment, and the next fix comes from that evidence.'],t.s)
B.aRw=new A.v(828,B.afz)
B.ajt=s(['THE RECORDING NOTIFICATION NOW COUNTS - your approved alert. While dictation runs, the persistent notification reads "Voicley - Recording - 47 min" and keeps counting, so a mic you forgot about announces its own age instead of sitting there looking the same as one you started a moment ago. Tap it to come straight back to the app. It updates once a minute on purpose: a per-second rewrite of a system notification spends battery on a number nobody reads that closely.',"ON THE ICON BADGE you also asked about: the honest answer is that badges are the LAUNCHER's decision, not the app's - Samsung honours them but they can be switched off per-app, so a badge can never be the only signal. The counting notification works regardless, which is why it went first. Pairs with the five-minute auto-off from earlier today: the notification makes a forgotten mic visible, the auto-off makes it stop."],t.s)
B.b0q=new A.v(827,B.ajt)
B.aeR=s(["THE FULL-SIZE CLAUDE ITEM NOW CARRIES EVERYTHING THE BOX DOES - your report on 27060: the box was showing controls the full view did not have. It was right, and backwards: the screen you OPEN to work an item had fewer buttons than the one you only glance at. Implement, Processed, Complete, Edited and Send-now now render in the full-size item as well - from ONE shared definition, so the two surfaces cannot drift apart again. Combined with the grid that landed in v822, the full view now has the ticket strip, the stage grid, every action button, the whole conversation and the reply box."],t.s)
B.aSd=new A.v(826,B.aeR)
B.am6=s(["JUMP BACK TO THE TOP - your approved arrow. Scroll a folder or the inbox down a couple of screens and a small round up-arrow fades in at the bottom right; tap it and the list glides back to the top. It fades away again near the top so it never sits over your items when it has no job. It drives the list through the same public scroll call everything else uses - the drag and glide machinery is untouched.",'THE ONENOTE ICONS ARE GONE - your ask: "Let\'s get rid of the Onenote icons anywhere on the UI." The little book glyph that sat on every OneNote-linked item is retired. It followed the master OneNote off-switch you asked for: with syncing optional, a permanent OneNote button on every item was chrome for a road you mostly do not take. Opening a page still lives in the item menu for the times you do.'],t.s)
B.aRL=new A.v(825,B.am6)
B.acb=s(['THE PROGRESS GRID GETS THE FULL WIDTH - your approved fix for "Still can\'t really see the progress grid on mobile." The grid and the action buttons stopped sharing one row: the grid now spans the box edge to edge, and Implement, Processed, Complete and the sent-for-implementation controls sit on their own line underneath, wrapping to a second line on a narrow box instead of squeezing anything. Same information, room to breathe.'],t.s)
B.aS6=new A.v(824,B.acb)
B.amB=s(['THE ID NUMBER IS BIGGER AND COPIES ITSELF - both of your asks in one: the item number on every box is up a size, and TAPPING it copies it to the clipboard with a little "#12345 copied" confirmation. One deliberate choice to know about: you asked for selectable-for-copying, and this is tap-to-copy instead - the number sits on the bar that is also the drag handle, and a text-selection gesture there would fight the drag you use constantly. Tap-to-copy gets you the same thing in fewer moves; if you specifically want press-and-select anyway, say so and I will find it a safer home first.'],t.s)
B.aZs=new A.v(823,B.amB)
B.alr=s(['THE GRID IS IN THE FULL VIEW - your approved one-liner: "Include the grid in the fullsized items." A Claude item opened full-size now shows the same stage grid its box wears - Sent, Received, Queue position, Building, Processed, Done, each with its stamp - right under the ticket strip. Same single source of truth as the box, so the two can never tell you different stories about one item. Brainstorm-parked ideas draw no grid in either place, as designed.'],t.s)
B.aZP=new A.v(822,B.alr)
B.aji=s(['THE NEW-ANSWER MARK IS A DOOR NOW - your approved one-liner: tap the clay "New answer" dot on any Claude box and the item opens full-size in a single click, straight to the conversation where the answer lives. The tap is guarded so it only opens the item - it can never fold the box underneath by accident. Opening it marks the answer read, exactly as before.'],t.s)
B.b15=new A.v(821,B.aji)
B.aob=s(["FOUR MORE SCREENS GET THE TRASH TREATMENT - your Build it on the sweep. Files, Recordings, Projects and Project detail all carried the exact flaw Trash had: the entire list built at once and laid out again on every frame of a scroll. Files holds your WHOLE library, so it was the one that would have hurt first as the library grows. All four now build only the rows on screen, through one shared lazy list that keeps the identical look - same card, same corners, same hairlines - and keys rows by item so a deletion mid-list can never leave the row below wearing the wrong state."],t.s)
B.aY3=new A.v(820,B.aob)
B.at2=s(['ENTER SENDS YOUR REPLY TO CLAUDE NOW - your report: "In full sized view the reply still doesn\'t send when I hit enter." Found and fixed, and it was my miss: when v808 taught Enter to send in the full-size view, it taught the NOTE BODY editor - the Reply-to-Claude field is a separate multi-line field, and on a multi-line field Enter quietly inserts a newline instead of firing send. The send-on-enter wiring on it was dead code from day one. Bare Enter now sends the reply; Shift+Enter still gives you a new line. Same fix everywhere the reply field appears - the full view and the conversation in the box share one widget, so they cannot drift apart.'],t.s)
B.aRE=new A.v(819,B.at2)
B.apf=s(["SEARCH INSIDE THE CLAUDE AREA - your approved feature, with the both-scopes answer you gave this morning. A search box now sits at the top of every Claude folder with a toggle beside it: THIS FOLDER filters exactly the view you are standing in as you type, ALL CLAUDE searches every Claude item everywhere - Queue, Processed, Done, Brainstorm, and Claude items in your own folders. It opens on This-folder because that matches what your eyes are on; widening is one tap.",'THE PART THAT MAKES IT ACTUALLY FIND PAST BUGS: both scopes search MY ANSWERS on each item, not just your words. The version numbers and fix histories live in the answers - search "v814" and the Trash-scrolling item comes up even though you never said v814 yourself. Tapping a result opens the full item, where the conversation and Reply already live - so "when I do find a past bug I could easily add to it" works today: open it, speak, it re-queues. The whole-library search from v622 is untouched.'],t.s)
B.aXi=new A.v(818,B.apf)
B.aiy=s(['THE APP NOW TELLS YOU WHEN THE CONNECTION IS COSTING YOU - your approved indicator. An amber line appears above the dictate box the moment network trouble starts biting, and it names the COST rather than the internet: "Bad connection - recording, not transcribing yet" when the live engine cannot reach its server (your words are still being captured, and the wording says the safe part first on purpose), or "Bad connection - saved here, not synced yet" when your words cannot leave the device. It appears only while you are recording, and it clears itself once the trouble has been quiet for twenty seconds - no nagging about a blip that already healed.',"Why this earns its place: the night you filed it, your Fold sat with sync 38,000 characters behind and 353 voice commands dropped - and nothing on screen said a word. Built without touching the live engine at all: the engine already logs every stall, drop and DNS failure to the network diagnostics, and the notice simply reads that log. Upload failures stay off it deliberately - those already have their own retry button on the item."],t.s)
B.b_j=new A.v(817,B.aiy)
B.ajv=s(['THE MIC NOW SHUTS ITSELF OFF AFTER FIVE QUIET MINUTES - AND ALWAYS SAYS SO. Your approved timer, with your rule built in: "it starts its timer after the last time" you did anything. The old silence clock only counted from your last spoken words, so typing into the box or working the app with the mic open could read as silence and trip the timer mid-use. Both silence clocks now restart on ANY activity - words landing, typing, tapping. When five genuinely quiet minutes pass, the mic stops fully and the dictate box says "Mic stopped after 5 quiet minutes" right where you look while dictating. Never silent, never mid-thought.',"This is the companion to the one-button control: off, listening, and a stop that announces itself. The 90-second soft pause (which parks capture but keeps the mic warm) still exists underneath and now obeys the same any-activity rule. Meetings and tasks are exempt from BOTH - deliberate long-form recording never gets cut. The five minutes is fixed in this build; making it settable in Settings is the named next step."],t.s)
B.b3o=new A.v(816,B.ajv)
B.ai4=s(['DRAG TO THE EDGE AND THE LIST SCROLLS - your report: "I\'m unable to drag up to the top or to the bottom to move the list to place the item that I wanna move in a spot that is not visible." Found and fixed, and the cause was my own earlier fix. When you reported the OPPOSITE problem months ago (holding still near the bottom made the list scroll on its own), I made the scroll zones ignore a finger until it had wiggled 24 pixels inside them. That refused the exact gesture the zones exist for: drag to the edge and HOLD. You arrived, you held still, and it sat there forever.',"THE RULE NOW TELLS THE TWO APART BY TIMING. A finger that is already at the edge the instant a drag starts is the old accident - it still has to move a little before anything scrolls, so your press-and-hold complaint stays fixed. A finger that ARRIVES at the edge later in the drag travelled there on purpose - it scrolls the moment it arrives and keeps scrolling while you hold. Both of your complaints - the old one and this one - are pinned by tests now, so neither can quietly come back while fixing the other. That is exactly how this bug happened the first time."],t.s)
B.b_p=new A.v(815,B.ai4)
B.ajS=s(['TRASH SCROLLS PROPERLY NOW - your report: "Trash is super slow in scrolling." The Trash screen was building EVERY deleted item at once, whether or not it was on screen, and then laying all of them out again on every single frame of a scroll. With a 30-day retention that is hundreds of full item boxes, each one wrapped in a swipe handler. It now builds only what you can actually see, which is what every other fast list in the app already does.',"While I was in there I checked for the same mistake elsewhere and found it in four more places - the Files list, Recordings, Projects and Project detail all build their whole list eagerly too. Those hold your entire library, so they are the same slowness waiting to happen. I did NOT change them in this build: you reported Trash, that is what this fixes, and touching four more lists is its own version with its own testing. They are written down and queued."],t.s)
B.aZm=new A.v(814,B.ajS)
B.ao8=s(['ONE BUTTON - your approved redesign, and the biggest change in a while. The three-segment control is gone. In its place: one button that reads DICTATE in grey when your words are not being captured, and LISTENING in red - breathing with your voice - when they are. You picked the colour ("implement with your color recommendations"), so red it is, matching the recording border you already see. The green retires with the segment it belonged to, which leaves ONE colour in the app meaning "your voice is being captured" instead of two.','COMMANDS-ONLY IS GONE AS A MODE, and that is the real win. It let the microphone stay live while your words were NOT becoming text, and next to Off it was nearly impossible to tell apart. It is the state behind the eaten words, the burst dropped after a Done, and the parked dictation box - your Fold\'s log carried 353 dropped commands in a single day. You lose nothing: "new note", "create task" and "message for Claude" have always worked mid-sentence, so nothing ever needed to BE in that mode. Its only unique power was the failure.','WHAT YOU GIVE UP, said plainly: the deliberate "stop taking my words but keep the mic warm" pause. It is now off, then on - one tap of the button you kept. And if some other path ever parks a session in the old commands state, the button reads GREY, because grey means "your words are not becoming text" and that is the truth there - one tap resolves it instead of stranding you.','NEW STAYS for now, deliberately. It is the only one-tap "close this, start fresh"; you already have that spoken and as the check on the box, so it is a duplicate you can retire on purpose rather than discover missing.',"NOT IN THIS BUILD: the five-minute auto-off after real silence. It is designed and it is next - I would rather ship the button you can see and test than bundle a timer that turns your microphone off with it, on the same night."],t.s)
B.b3i=new A.v(813,B.ao8)
B.aqx=s(['THE PIN IS IN - your approved feature, built as you described it: a pin sits just to the LEFT OF THE DATE on every item. Click it and the item rides to the top of the list; click it again and it comes back down. It is a property of the ITEM, not of one screen, so a pinned item is at the top in the main window and stays at the top in every folder it lives in - your "system wide feature".',"YOU HANDED ME THE TWO OPEN QUESTIONS, so here are the answers I picked. UNPINNING RETURNS THE ITEM TO ITS NATURAL PLACE rather than dropping it just under the pinned block. Your version would have left ex-pinned items stranded near the top in no order, and after a dozen of those the top of the list becomes a graveyard and the pinned block stops being the thing your eye trusts. A pin is a temporary lift; letting go should put the item down where it belongs.","AND ON ORDERING: pinned items sort among themselves by the exact same order you already drag, so arranging inside the pinned block works with the machinery you know - no second system, and it is also WHY unpinning can put an item back where it was (its place was never overwritten to lift it).","STILL TO COME on this feature, so you are not surprised: dragging an item DOWN OUT of the pinned block to unpin it is not in this build - use the pin itself for now. That one reaches into the drag machinery next to the scrolling you asked me not to disturb, so it gets its own careful pass. Also: pinning syncs between your devices only after I run the small database change (017) on the server - until then the pin works perfectly on the device you press it on."],t.s)
B.b3p=new A.v(812,B.aqx)
B.abX=s(['THE CLAUDE STATUS LADDER FITS A PHONE NOW - your report "Claude items with the new table are not rendering correctly", and then the part that actually solved it: "On Mobile the table doesn\'t have enough room." Twice before (v774, v787) I tried to make the label, the badge and the time share ONE line and both times only bought a little room. The honest answer is that on a phone - and worse in a folder, where the box is narrower still - that line cannot hold all three. So each rung now STOPS competing: below phone width the time drops to its own line underneath and the label gets the full width instead of a leftover sliver. Wide screens are untouched. Twelve tests render the worst case ("SENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION" with a "#12 of 16" badge and a timestamp) at seven widths from 200px up and fail if anything overflows, if the label gets cut, or if the time or badge goes missing.'],t.s)
B.b0N=new A.v(811,B.abX)
B.abI=s(['THE QUEUE NUMBERS COUNT REAL WORK NOW - your catch: "the 2nd item in the que list is show #2 of 22 and the item above it hasn\'t been implemented yet... We should instead completely skip the non implemented items when determine the que positions." You were reading it exactly right. A Brainstorm idea parked in the list, or an item already Processed or Done that still carried an old Queue row, was silently holding a position and inflating the total - so real work read one number lower than it was and the "of N" was never the number of things actually waiting. Positions and the total now count PENDING WORK only. Nothing is stored to make that true: it is read from which folders an item sits in, so there is no second copy to drift.','THE CLAUDE AND NOTE BARS ARE MORE SHADED - your ask: "The Claude top bar needs to be a bit more shaded, the notes bar also but not as much as the claude bar." Both are up from the same faint wash every type shared; Claude reads heaviest, notes a step under it. Task and Meeting are deliberately untouched - you did not ask for them, and a change you did not ask for is one you have to re-review. Say lighter or darker and it is one number each.'],t.s)
B.aRG=new A.v(810,B.abI)
B.acL=s(['ENTER SENDS, SHIFT+ENTER MAKES A NEW LINE - your instruction: "Inside of a full size view make enter send the item and shift enter give it a new line." In the full-sized view, Enter now saves the item and closes it; Shift+Enter drops to a new line exactly as before. Dictation is untouched - spoken "new line" and "new paragraph" still work everywhere; this only changes what the physical Enter key does in that one view.',"And an honest note on the Windows drag-down-then-sideways you approved: it is NOT in this build. Reading the code showed it is not a new gesture but a hand-off in the middle of one, which Windows/Flutter only allows one owner for - the right fix reaches into the drag machinery next to the scrolling you asked me not to disturb, and it can only be proven by feel on your desktop. It stays in your Queue, unbuilt and honest, with its own session ahead of it."],t.s)
B.aUM=new A.v(808,B.acL)
B.asb=s(['YOUR TYPED WORDS SURVIVE TASK CLOSES NOW - the real bug behind your Fold recipe ("dictate create new task, enter text, dictate create new task"). Reading the code with your recipe in hand found something worse than a hidden box: closing a task you had TYPED into overwrote it with what the microphone alone had heard - in the box, on the tasks page, and in the saved item. Every close now keeps your typed words and adds the final spoken ones, the same merge the live box uses. Four tests walk your exact recipe step by step. (The vanished-box half still has the v806 tripwire watching - if it recurs, the log names the holder.)',"THE FULL VIEW'S QUEUE NUMBER NOW MATCHES THE BOX - your report: \"The fullsized window Queue #'s are off.\" The full view computed its number with its own COPY of the counting logic, and that copy was missing the v781 fix (memberships pointing at deleted items still counted there). Same cure as the type bug: the second copy is deleted, both views read one derivation, disagreement is now impossible.","ARROW KEYS PAGE BETWEEN ITEMS IN FULL VIEW - your pick #1, approved: Left/Right (and PgUp/PgDn) move to the previous/next item, the keyboard twin of the swipe. Typing in a text field keeps its arrow keys - the caret wins, paging gets what's left.",'ITEM NUMBERS ARE DARKER ("Make the item #\'s a bit darker they are hard to read") - up from the faintest grey to label strength, still quieter than your words. AND THE NEW-ANSWER DOT CLEARS INSTANTLY when you open an item ("can you make it faster") - it listens for the mark now instead of waiting for the row\'s next repaint.'],t.s)
B.b3X=new A.v(807,B.asb)
B.abp=s(['ONENOTE HAS A MASTER OFF-SWITCH - your ask on the OneNote item: "Not as excited about Using the OneNote functionality that this was originally built around... Is there a way to turn it off?" Settings now has "Sync to OneNote". Flip it off and Voicley writes NOTHING to OneNote - no live streaming, no task-page writes - while everything in the app and the sync between your devices stays exactly as it is. It ships ON (nothing changes until you flip it), and flipping it also clears the "Dictate 2" remnant: that line in the full view was your active project\'s old OneNote page label, and with the switch off the destination reads "This device".','AND A TRIPWIRE FOR THE VANISHING DICTATE BOX - your bug: "create a new task item, then create another task item behind it, the dictation box goes away." I could not reproduce it from the spoken path (that one has tests), so this build adds the honest next step: whenever the dictate box hides, the log now records exactly WHICH open item is holding it. If it happens again on this build, the log names the culprit and the fix comes from evidence instead of guessing. The law stands: the dictate box always exists.'],t.s)
B.b_G=new A.v(806,B.abp)
B.ars=s(['THE WINDOWS TITLE BAR NOW TELLS YOU WHAT YOU ARE RUNNING - your ask, approved tonight: "Let\'s put the version in the windows title bar and another message if there is an update available and the version of the update." It reads "Voicley v805", and the moment a newer build lands on the shelf it becomes "Voicley v805 - v806 available". It re-stamps on launch, every 10 minutes, and every time you click back into the window. Born the hard way: your desktop ran v782 ALL DAY today and nothing on screen said so.','CLAUDE FOLDERS DEFAULT THE TYPE SELECTOR TO CLAUDE - your ask ("If I am in a claude folder you should default the item type of claude"), then your nudge when the selector still said Note: "This isn\'t happening." The truth: the SAVE half has existed since v727 - items created in Claude folders already became Claude items - but the selector kept showing Note, the exact badge-vs-outcome split v755 was about, alive in one more branch. Now the selector, the badge and the save all read the folder: composing in Claude, Queue, Questions, Processed or Brainstorm shows Claude up front. Your manual pick still beats it, always.','THE WORD "BRAINSTORM" NOW FILES FOR YOU - type it at the start of a title, or lead a new item with it, and the item LINKS itself into the Brainstorm folder. LINKS, per your rule from tonight: it also stays exactly where it already was - one item, two doors. Strict on purpose: only the word "brainstorm" leading - "brainstorming ideas" or a sentence about brainstorming does nothing, because a false link that hides an item\'s real home is worse than dragging one by hand.',"AND THE STRAIGHT STORY ON TONIGHT'S OUTAGE, for the record: your data was never gone. Two items I created from my desk this afternoon were missing one internal field, and the app's Claude engine crashed every time it read them - on every machine that synced them. That is why items sat at \"Sending...\" (they actually SENT; only the receipt back to your screen died), and why the desktop looked empty after its update. I repaired the two rows on the server, every device healed itself on its next sync, and my desk tools now refuse to create incomplete items. The full post-mortem is in the regression ledger."],t.s)
B.aTy=new A.v(805,B.ars)
B.alt=s(['THE BRAINSTORM FOLDER IS LIVE - built as discussed on the thread and approved. A new Brainstorm folder sits under Claude on every device: "a topical per item area for new ideas that I am not ready to put into the queue and process." Drop any Claude item in and it steps OUTSIDE the pipeline - never auto-filed into Queue, Questions or Processed, even if you press Implement, even if the conversation reads done. Nothing is stored on the item itself: being in the folder IS the state, so there is no second copy to disagree - the disease behind six of this week\'s bugs.','A parked idea\'s box wears a quiet indigo BRAINSTORM chip instead of the status ladder - an idea has no pipeline to climb. The conversation still works exactly as everywhere else: replies flow, my answers land, the new-answer mark still lights. When an idea is ready, drag it out of the folder - your move IS the graduation, and the ladder comes straight back. Two of your calls defaulted the cautious way for now: creation is by drag or my desk command only (no spoken "brainstorm note" yet), and Brainstorm answers light only the per-item mark, not the Queue badge.','A "PROCESSED" BUTTON ON QUEUED ITEMS - you asked "Should we put a button labeled processed in items that are in the que?" three minutes after having to ask me in words to move one. Approved and built: any Claude item sitting in the Queue now has a green Processed button beside Implement. One tap makes the move the pipeline makes when work finishes - on your say-so, whatever the thread thinks. Your tap counts as YOUR placement, so the sweep can never drag the item back. It shows on all queued items deliberately: calling something finished should not require the conversation\'s permission.',"ALSO IN THIS BUILD: answers from my desk now land on your items within seconds (the fast lane you tested this evening on item #9850) - that was server-side, no update needed, noted here for the record."],t.s)
B.b_P=new A.v(804,B.alt)
B.add=s(['THE CLAUDE BOX HEADER READS THE WAY YOU ASKED: the Claude label, then the New-answer mark, then the item number. You: "Lets put your new answer notification to the right of the claude label and the new item number to the right of that." The mark MOVED up from the footer - one place, not two.','THE EDITED-RESEND BUTTON STOPS LIGHTING ON PUNCTUATION IT ADDED ITSELF. You, item #9846: "The edited - resend button just showed up right after hitting send." Your data showed exactly one character of difference: the app\'s own polish step added a period to the stored item, and the comparison counted that as your edit. Now only WORDS decide - case, punctuation and line breaks are cosmetic. Change a word and the button still appears; the app tidying itself no longer does. Existing false-flagged items, including #9846, heal themselves the next time their box draws.','AND WORTH KNOWING: your Windows app has been running v782 all day, declining its update prompt - none of today\'s fixes are on it, which is also why the "Review Later" folder felt slow there. Say yes to the prompt (or restart the app) and it catches up to this build.'],t.s)
B.b2h=new A.v(803,B.add)
B.ahT=s(["THE LIST REBUILD STORM IS CONTAINED - this is the fix for item #9837, the focus jump, and the scrolling feeling broken again. Your log this afternoon showed the app rebuilding its entire list of 4,365 items over and over, each pass taking about a second, with worst frames of a second and a half. When you tapped an item, the list was being rebuilt underneath your finger between the touch and the tap resolving - so focus resolved against a list that had moved. That is also exactly the mechanism the scroll work fought: content moving mid-gesture.","The storm had a trigger - the item-number change I ran at lunch, syncing through all your rows - but the pile-up was always possible: nothing stopped a second full rebuild starting while the first was still running. Your log showed 43 of them stacked in one stretch.","Now at most ONE rebuild runs at a time, and however many requests arrive while it runs, they collapse into exactly one follow-up. COALESCED, NEVER DROPPED - that distinction is the whole design. Dropping requests would mean your list silently stops refreshing, which is the items-vanishing failure and far worse than churn. The follow-up reads the same database a moment later, so the newest data always lands. Eleven tests pin it, including the one that matters most: a request is never lost.","Being straight about the boundary: this contains the storm, it does not cure the disease. The app still re-reads your whole library for a single changed row - roughly a second per pass at your size, growing as your library grows. That is the real root of the scroll arc, it is a bigger piece of work, and I will walk you through it before starting rather than diving in."],t.s)
B.aUb=new A.v(802,B.ahT)
B.akg=s(['ITEM #5789 - THE SECOND COPY IS DELETED, WHICH IS WHAT "PERMANENTLY" ACTUALLY MEANS. You: "I am entering as a Claude item, and you are creating a note. This is a bug that has repeated itself over and over and over again, and you need to get it fixed permanently."','Every fix in this arc kept two copies of one fact in step. "Is this box addressed to Claude?" was stored in two places, and different code wrote each one: the type dropdown wrote one copy, and the code that decides what to SAVE read the other. So picking Claude while the microphone was running set a flag the save never looked at.',"It has been producing the right item since yesterday - but only because I taught the save to fall back on what the badge is showing. A fallback covering a broken primary signal is exactly how this kept coming back: the real answer stayed wrong, and the next change to the fallback exposed it again.",'So the second copy is gone, not synchronised. There is now one place that holds this, everything reads it, and two copies cannot disagree when there is only one. Same treatment the "already posted" guard got in the last build. 1,188 tests green, including the truth table that holds every past attempt at this bug together by name.'],t.s)
B.aTD=new A.v(801,B.akg)
B.akq=s(['UPDATING THE APP NO LONGER LEAVES OLD TEXT IN THE DICTATION BOX. You: "When I updated the app it again put text from a previous item inside of the dictation box."',"There is a safety net that mirrors the open dictation box to disk, so a crash can never lose typed words - they are the only words in the app with no audio behind them. It is supposed to forget that text the moment the words become an item. It did that properly for TYPED notes and not for DICTATED ones, and dictating is nearly everything you do.",'Two things went wrong together. The "forget it" write was delayed by about three-quarters of a second to avoid hammering storage while you talk - and an app update kills the app, which is exactly the moment a delayed write gets lost. The backup guard that would have caught it was only ever kept in memory on the dictated path, so it died with the app too. Next launch, your last dictation came back looking like something still open.',"Now the safety net itself knows what has already become an item and refuses to hand it back - so even if a clear is missed, your words cannot reappear. Both halves happen in one place, so a future path cannot get one and forget the other, which is exactly how this happened. Ten tests, including the ones that matter in the other direction: a genuinely unfinished note still comes back, and anything you added after posting still comes back. Losing real words would be far worse than showing you a duplicate."],t.s)
B.aWK=new A.v(800,B.akq)
B.ajl=s(['THE TYPE YOU CHOOSE STICKS - INCLUDING WHILE YOU ARE DICTATING. You: "I changed it to note, I speak into it, and it changes it to a Claude item. Now I can\'t even get it to change to stay as a note." That is the one that mattered, and this is the cause.','When a Claude item posted, the app wrote "the next box is a Claude box" directly - overruling whatever you had chosen. The bit that makes this ugly: one line above it sat a careful version of the same write, added in an earlier fix specifically so an observation could never overwrite your pick. Two writes doing the same job, side by side, one guarded and one not. The unguarded one won, and it fired whenever a Claude item finished posting - which is why the box could flip WHILE you were talking into it.','The unguarded writes are gone. Nothing you asked for is lost: rapid-firing Claude items still keeps the box on Claude, because that now rides on your PICK rather than on the app watching itself. Only an explicit choice can set the type now - your pick, a spoken "message for Claude", or a folder. The app can fill it in when you have not chosen; it can never overrule you.',"And the whole thing is pinned as one truth table. Every attempt at this bug - five of them, including one of mine from an hour ago - is now a named test, and they all have to pass together. A sixth attempt cannot quietly undo a fifth, which is what kept happening."],t.s)
B.aW7=new A.v(799,B.ajl)
B.afR=s(['PICKING NOTE WORKS AGAIN - AND THIS ONE WAS MY FAULT, IN THE BUILD YOU JUST INSTALLED. You: "I changed it to note, I speak into it, and it changes it to a Claude item. Now I can\'t even get it to change to stay as a note." Yesterday\'s fix made the box save whatever type it was SHOWING. Correct on its own. But I folded that inferred type into the same switch that handles a spoken "message for Claude" - and that switch is deliberately the one thing that outranks your pick. Since the box infers Claude whenever your last item was one, which for you is nearly always, choosing Note became impossible.',"That is exactly the mistake I had described in writing an hour earlier: one value carrying two meanings. An explicit address and an inferred default are different facts and now live in different places, with the default sitting BELOW your pick where you can override it. Five new tests pin it, including one that sweeps every pick against every inferred type and asserts your pick always wins - and one that checks yesterday's fix still holds, so neither can quietly undo the other.",'NEW APPROVED WORK NOW LANDS AT THE TOP OF THE QUEUE. You: "When I create a new Claude item from the inbox, and hit the implement button. It is putting it in the queue, but at the bottom of the list. Seems like it should go on top of the list." You are right. Pressing Implement is you saying "do this" about the thing in front of you, and burying it under everything approved earlier is the app arguing with that. Your drag order still wins - this only decides where a new arrival starts.'],t.s)
B.aSc=new A.v(798,B.afR)
B.afs=s(['REMOVED, at your word: tapping the "Folders" heading no longer opens the reorder screen. You: "When I am in a Folder item and hit the arrow key on the top left of the app, it is bringing me to a folders view and it shouldn\'t. We should remove that."',"The heading looked like a label, so tapping it was usually an accident - and the cost only turned up later. That screen stayed on the stack, so opening a folder afterwards made the BACK arrow return you to it instead of home. You were landing somewhere you never chose to go.","The \u21c5 button right next to the heading is the deliberate way to reorder folders, and it is unchanged - as is the long-press action. Dragging a folder onto the heading still moves it to the top level; only the tap is gone."],t.s)
B.aWa=new A.v(797,B.afs)
B.akv=s(['EVERY ITEM NOW HAS A NUMBER YOU CAN SAY. You: "add an item # to each item for ID purposes, that way when I have an issue with an item I can give you the id #." It is live. Your whole library is numbered oldest-first: #1 is your very first item from Jul 6, and the newest is #5795. It shows right of the item type label on the box - the spot the timer used to occupy - and in the full-sized view under the title, exactly where you asked.','From now on "item 4,812 is doing the thing" is all I need. Today every report meant working out which item you meant from a time or a title, and you have four items with near-identical titles right now - twice this week I chased the wrong one.','The numbers come from the server, deliberately. You create items on four devices and sometimes offline; if each device picked "the next number" two of them out of contact would both mint 413, and a duplicate is worse than no number at all - you would say 413 and I would fix the wrong item. So an item made offline shows no number until it syncs, then gets one permanently. It never changes afterwards, and a deleted item keeps its number rather than handing it to someone else.'],t.s)
B.aRh=new A.v(796,B.akv)
B.ar0=s(["FOLDERS NOW SHOW THEIR ITEM COUNT TOO. You: \"Let's put the folder item count in between the dictation box and the items below the dictation box like we are doing for the inbox view.\" It is the same piece of the screen the Inbox uses, handed the folder's own count - so the line between the composer and the history reads identically wherever you are.",'REMOVED, at your word: the rule to the right of the item count. You: "In the inbox view let\'s get rid of the line to the right of the item count in between the dictate box and the items below." The count on its own marks that boundary perfectly well; the line running to the right edge was furniture.'],t.s)
B.aUk=new A.v(795,B.ar0)
B.abB=s(['PRESSING IMPLEMENT IN THE FULL VIEW NOW VISIBLY LANDS. You: "After I hit the implement button in fullsized view it still shows the same implement button" and "You are putting an implement button next to your replies, don\'t think we need those." Both are the same thing. Pressing Implement posts your approval as a NEW message on the conversation - nothing about the message you approved changes - so the button sat there looking exactly as it did before, which reads as the tap having done nothing, and invites a second tap and a second approval.',"An entry that already has your approval after it has nothing left to authorise, so its button now goes away. The one case it deliberately keeps: post a NEW question onto a thread you approved earlier, and THAT entry still gets its own button - which was the whole reason for keeping the button in v784.","Nine tests, including your exact sequence: ask, approve, and the ask stops offering."],t.s)
B.aUW=new A.v(794,B.abB)
B.aoF=s(['THE EDITED - RESEND BUTTON ACTUALLY WORKS NOW. You: "When I make changes to the text of a Claude item, in the box, I am not seeing the button to resubmit. Yesterday you were phantomly marking items that hadn\'t been changed and ever since I mentioned that, I have not seen that button again." Both halves of that sentence were right, and they turn out to be the same bug seen from two sides.','For the button to know your words changed, the app has to remember what was SENT. The path every dictated Claude item takes recorded that the item went out - but never recorded what went out. So dictated items had no baseline at all, and "edited since sent" could never become true for them. The reason you saw the button before is worse, not better: it was comparing your item against the APPROVAL line, which never matches, so every approved item wore it. That is the phantom marking. Fixing that removed the wrong comparison without ever adding the right one.',"Now the send records the item's own words, so an edit shows the button and a resend clears it. And every Claude item already in your library - which has no record of what was sent - quietly adopts what it says today as its starting point the first time you look at it, so the whole back catalogue becomes watchable from here. It cannot detect an edit you made yesterday, and it will not pretend to: guessing there is exactly the phantom marking you told me about."],t.s)
B.aY6=new A.v(793,B.aoF)
B.acT=s(['THE ITEM IS NOW WHATEVER THE BOX SAID IT WAS. You: "You are doing it again. I am entering as a Claude item, and you are creating a note. This is a bug that has repeated itself over and over and over again, and you need to get it fixed permanently." You are right that it kept coming back, and I found it in your data: your 12:15:09 dictation saved as a plain note and never reached me, and you re-dictated the identical words at 12:17:29 to get them through. You had to say it twice.','WHY IT KEPT COMING BACK. Every previous fix patched one input, and the badge and the save were asking different questions - so they could always disagree somewhere new. The BADGE decided from three things: did you address it to Claude, are you in a folder, and what type was the last item you made (which is the "remember my last type" behaviour you asked for). The SAVE looked only at whether you had addressed it to Claude, and knew nothing about the remembered type at all. So a box that opened as Claude because your previous item was one showed the Claude badge honestly and then saved a Note, just as honestly.',"THE RULE IS NOW ONE SENTENCE: the item is whatever the box said it was. The save is handed the same answer the badge painted, so they cannot drift apart - it is not two places that have to agree, it is one answer used twice. Your explicit pick still beats everything, exactly as before: choose Note on a box showing Claude and you get a Note.","AND IT COVERS TASK AND MEETING, not just Claude. Fixing only the type you reported would have left the identical bug waiting in the other two - which is precisely the mistake that made this a four-time bug. Fifteen tests, including one that sweeps every combination and asserts the saved type equals what you were shown."],t.s)
B.aSP=new A.v(792,B.acT)
B.aeF=s(['MOVING A FINISHED ITEM OUT OF PROCESSED NOW STICKS. You: "I just dragged an item out of processed and into the new folder Need to be tested and it stayed in the processed folder. Should that be able to move?" It should, and your drag worked perfectly - the item really did land in the new folder. The background pass put it back in Processed a moment later, so it looked like the move failed.',"Same cause as the Done fix in the previous build, and the same cause as v751 before that: the app files finished work into Processed, then keeps re-filing it every time it notices the item is not there. Taking something OUT of a folder is you saying it does not belong there, and re-adding it silently overrules you. The Queue learned that rule back in v751. Processed never did. It has now: an item is filed into Processed ONCE, and after that wherever you put it is where it stays.","Being straight about this one: it is the same mistake as the previous build, one folder over, and I found it an hour after writing myself a rule to check exactly this. The rule was right and I did not run it. It is now written into the code itself at the function that keeps breaking - six times so far - rather than in a document I have to remember to open."],t.s)
B.b01=new A.v(791,B.aeF)
B.an_=s(['PRESSING DONE REALLY DOES TAKE IT OUT OF PROCESSED. You: "I hit done on an item in processed and it put it into done folder but didn\'t move it out of processed. Should it be doing that?" No, it should not - and the Done button was innocent. It moves the item correctly. The background pass that files finished work then put it BACK.','That pass knew about two folders, Queue and Processed, and had never been told your Done folder exists. So the moment you moved something into Done it read as "finished and filed nowhere" and helpfully filed it into Processed again. You moved it out, it moved it back. 17 of your items were sitting in both folders because of it.',"This is my code fighting you, which is the worst kind of bug because it silently undoes a decision you made - and it is the same one as v751, when items you dragged out of the Queue kept coming back. One folder further along, and I did not carry the lesson across. Done is now TERMINAL: once an item is in there the app files it nowhere, ever, whatever else it thinks. It only clears the stale rows the move left behind - so your 17 double-filed items clean themselves up on the next check-in, with nothing for you to do.","The Done button also now clears a SECOND Processed row if an item somehow had two, so you see the right thing the moment you tap instead of waiting for the next pass. Sixteen tests pin it, including a sweep of every possible combination proving nothing can re-file an item you have closed out."],t.s)
B.aYe=new A.v(790,B.an_)
B.apF=s(['ANSWERING A QUESTION NOW CLEARS "NEEDS YOU" ON THE BOX. You: "I have answered the question and pressed implement on that reply and inside of the fullsized item it shows that it is 5 on the que but the box view just says needs you even though I have answered." Both views were reading different things and only one was right. Two separate faults had to line up to produce it, and both are fixed.',"FIRST: back in v767 I fixed the opposite bug - pressing Implement was wiping out my question, so I ruled that a later message of ANY kind does not answer a question. That is correct for an approval, which is plumbing. It is wrong for what you actually did, which was ANSWER, in words, and then approve. Under that rule the item stayed blocked on you forever. The app now tells an approval line apart from your own words: your words clear the question, an approval on its own does not.",'SECOND: the item\'s saved status could not come back down from "needs you" even once the question was answered. Statuses only ever move forward, so nothing can walk finished work backwards - but "needs you" had been slotted into that ladder ABOVE "received", which made it a one-way trap with only one exit: marking the whole thing done. It is not a stage, it is an interruption laid over whatever stage the item had reached, and it now clears back to where the item actually is. Nothing else about forward-only changed.','Your existing stuck items heal themselves - the next time the app checks in, any item wearing a stale "needs you" drops back to its real state. Fourteen tests pin both halves, including your exact thread.'],t.s)
B.b1t=new A.v(789,B.apF)
B.ado=s(['RENAMING AN ITEM NOW SHOWS UP IN THE LIST. You: "I just edited the item from 11:22am in the full sized view and when I went back to the list it didn\'t save and update with my changes." Your rename DID save - the server had it a minute before you reported this, and I checked that before touching any code. What failed was the list. The body editor, when it saves, tells the main list in the same breath. The title editor wrote to the database and told nobody. And the check that decides whether the list needs redrawing compared the item type, its status, my reply and the WORDS - but not the title. So a title-only change was invisible to it and your old title stayed on screen.',"That is the same mistake as bug #94 back on 07-18, which added the words to that same check for exactly this reason and stopped one field short. Both title editors now tell the list, the check can see the title, and nine tests pin it - including your exact case: same words, new title.",'AND A TRACE, so the next one takes seconds instead of twenty minutes. There was NO record anywhere of an edit being saved. You have reported "my edit did not save" more than once and there was nothing to check afterwards, which is why answering this one meant digging through the server by hand. Every title save and every body save now writes a line to the log.','REMOVED, at your word: the Reply button on the collapsed box. You: "Let\'s remove the reply on the box view." You asked first whether it was needed there and I said no - on a collapsed box the conversation is not on screen, so it was offering to answer something you could not read. Reply still lives on the OPEN box, where you can see what you are replying to.'],t.s)
B.aTj=new A.v(788,B.ado)
B.atD=s(['REMOVED, at your word: the ticking timer beside the item type selector. You: "There is a timer next to the item type label and selector. It is running now on the Fold for a note and the dictate function is not on. Please remove it." You were right about more than the clutter - it was counting from the moment the section started and never stopped, mic or no mic, so on an idle note it sat there climbing and reporting recording time for a box that was recording nothing. A number that is wrong whenever you are not talking is worse than no number. The red border already says "live", honestly. The timer stays on Task and Meeting boxes, which only exist while that unit is genuinely capturing.',"ALSO REMOVED, and you did NOT ask for this one, so here it is plainly: taking the timer out uncovered a second, invisible one-second refresh in the same box. It was rebuilding the entire dictate box - the one holding the text field you type into - once every second for as long as the app was open, to redraw a number that already redrew itself. It is gone. Nothing on screen changes and no feature is affected. I am not claiming this was behind the cursor trouble; it simply had no business being there.",'THE CLAUDE STATUS TABLE FITS ON A PHONE. You: "On Mobile the table doesn\'t have enough room." The timestamps were carrying a date on every row - Jul 28 - 11:25 AM, seventeen characters fighting a twenty-three character stage name for the same width. Stamps from TODAY now show just the time, which gives nine characters back to the words. Older stamps keep their date, because there the date is the useful part. And on a genuinely narrow box the longest stage name wraps to a second line instead of being cut to SENT FOR IMPLEME.',"A RULE I AM HOLDING MYSELF TO, after you asked whether I remove things without your permission: from here, any build that removes anything you can SEE says so on its own line here and quotes the sentence of yours that authorized it. If a removal cannot cite one of your sentences, it does not ship - it comes to you as a question first. This entry is the first one written that way."],t.s)
B.b0r=new A.v(787,B.atD)
B.ahe=s(["A CLAUDE ITEM TELLS YOU WHEN THERE IS SOMETHING NEW TO READ. You asked three times today whether I had replied - and on at least one of those I HAD, on the item, and nothing on your screen had changed to say so. Boxes now show a small clay dot and the words New answer when the answers on that item have grown since you last opened it. Opening the item clears it; you are never asked to dismiss a marker you did not ask for.","It is deliberately quiet, and deliberately separate from NEEDS YOU. That one means I am blocked and cannot continue without you. This one means there is information here. Two different urgencies, two different marks - putting them together would make the urgent one stop meaning anything. And it never reorders your queue: items with new answers stay exactly where you dragged them, because that order is your priority list and my replies have no business rearranging it."],t.s)
B.b08=new A.v(786,B.ahe)
B.acK=s(["A MANUAL WAY TO CLEAR FINISHED WORK OUT OF THE QUEUE. Open the Queue folder and, when there is finished work sitting in it, a green button appears at the top right showing how many. One tap moves them to Processed. It only ever moves FINISHED work - moving unfinished items would make Processed lie about what has been built, which is worse than a stuck item - and if there is nothing to move the button does not appear at all rather than sitting there doing nothing. The automatic move is supposed to make this unnecessary, and it has failed you four separate ways in two days. A system with that record should have a manual override, so a stuck item costs you one tap instead of a report and a wait."],t.s)
B.aXH=new A.v(785,B.acK)
B.aiC=s(["THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON ONLY APPEARS WHERE IT MEANS SOMETHING. You asked whether you have to press it for a reply to count. You do not - every message you send reaches me whether you approve it or not - so a button offering to implement your ANSWER was implying your words might otherwise be ignored. It is now offered only on a genuine new ask. It no longer appears on answers to my questions, on the approval messages themselves (those were showing up as your own entries, each with a button of its own), or on finished entries. It stays where it earns its place: a thread carrying several asks, where pressing it names which one to build."],t.s)
B.aUy=new A.v(784,B.aiC)
B.aiv=s(["THE GREEN MIC IN TASK AND MEETING BOXES IS GONE - THE ONE YOU ACTUALLY MEANT. The one removed in v777 was in the dictation box; this one lived inside a live Task box (green because Task is green) with a matching amber one in Meeting boxes. It only reappeared for you because v778 finally made Task picks work, so you were creating real Tasks again. You said the main dictate button should cover it, and it does: Dictate and Commands on the bottom bar are the same pause and resume - Commands keeps the session alive and stops capturing. Nothing is lost."],t.s)
B.b1F=new A.v(783,B.aiv)
B.arX=s(['APPROVED WORK CAN FINALLY LEAVE THE QUEUE. You asked why a finished item was still sitting at the top of the Queue. The app was asking the NEWEST message on a conversation whether the work was done - and on anything you press Implement on, the newest message IS the approval, which is not a "done" message. So every item you approved read as unfinished forever and could never move to Processed. It now reads the whole conversation, the same way the rest of the app already did.','THE QUEUE COUNTS ONLY REAL ITEMS. It was showing things like "#36 of" when there were nowhere near 36 items in it. The count included leftover references to items you had deleted - invisible on screen but still taking up positions, which also pushed newly approved work to the very bottom. The position and the total now count only items that actually exist, and the 19 leftover references in your Queue have been cleared out.',"A NOTE CAN NO LONGER BE SENT ONCE PER DEVICE. A message that failed to send gets rescued automatically - but that rescue ran on every device you own, with nothing saying which device was responsible. One unsent note became three copies, one from each device, milliseconds apart. Before rescuing anything the app now asks whether that exact text already arrived, and links to it instead of sending again."],t.s)
B.aU0=new A.v(782,B.arX)
B.alD=s(["MY ANSWERS REACH ITEMS THAT ARE ALREADY CLOSED. You spotted this: I wrote a long explanation onto a Claude item and you could not see any of it. The reason was a real bug, not a slip. The part of the app that copies my answers onto your items only looked at items that were still open - so once an item was marked finished on your device, nothing I wrote afterwards could ever appear on it. The text existed on the server and had no route to you. It now reads finished items too. Same cause as the queue fix a few versions ago, different part of the code: I taught one half about finished items and left the other half with the identical hole."],t.s)
B.b1r=new A.v(779,B.alD)
B.ah9=s(["PICKING TASK OR MEETING NOW ACTUALLY MAKES A TASK OR MEETING. Choosing either from the type dropdown on an idle box gave you a Note - the badge said Task, the saved item was a Note. The pick was being stored on the recording session, and there IS no session when the mic is off, so on a blank box it was written nowhere and silently lost. Claude never had this problem because its type is kept somewhere that exists whether you are recording or not; Task and Meeting now get the same. Seven tests, including the exact case you hit."],t.s)
B.b2w=new A.v(778,B.ah9)
B.at3=s(["THE GREEN MIC BUTTON IS GONE. You asked for it twice and it is out of the dictation box everywhere. CORRECTION to what this entry first said: I warned that this removed the only way to dictate inside a folder. That was wrong. The Off / Commands only / Dictate bar sits on the top edge of the dictate box, and that box is in folders too - so dictating into a folder works exactly as it did. I had checked whether folders have a BOTTOM bar, found none, and stopped looking."],t.s)
B.b_K=new A.v(777,B.at3)
B.all=s(["THE FOLDERS PANE NO LONGER APPEARS WHEN YOU HIT BACK. It was never opening itself. The pane closed only when you navigated FROM it - so if you reached a folder page any other way, the pane stayed open behind it, hidden by the full-page folder view, and hitting back simply revealed it. It now closes on any change of screen, however it happened: forward, back, or the system back gesture."],t.s)
B.b1a=new A.v(776,B.all)
B.arH=s(['THE DICTATION BOX COLOURS NOW MATCH YOUR ITEMS. You asked me twice to look at this, and there was a real mismatch: the same type was two different colours depending on where you looked. A Task was GREEN in your item list but ORANGE in the dictation box. A Meeting was AMBER in your list but INDIGO in the box. Notes and Claude items already matched. Nobody chose that - the box had its own hand-typed colours that had drifted from the ones your items use, and the new type dropdown I built copied the wrong ones. There is now one answer to "what colour is this type" and the box, the dropdown and your item list all ask it.'],t.s)
B.aUF=new A.v(775,B.arH)
B.ao2=s(["THE STATUS ROWS NO LONGER BREAK ON NARROW BOXES. The longest stage name - SENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION - was competing with its badge and its timestamp for the same space, and on a narrow box one of the three lost. The name and its badge now share one slot and the time keeps its own room, so the row shortens gracefully instead of breaking. If you still see an item formatted wrong after this, tell me which one and what it looks like - this is the most likely cause but I am fixing it from the code, not from a picture of your screen."],t.s)
B.aYP=new A.v(774,B.ao2)
B.aeo=s(["CHOOSING A BLUE NOTE STAYS A BLUE NOTE. You said you had tried to make a new blue item a number of times and kept getting a Claude item instead. Two different things were writing the same setting: your PICK, and the app watching what it had just posted. Posting a Claude message quietly re-remembered Claude - so right after any Claude item, the next box turned back into a Claude box and undid the Note you chose. You have made dozens of Claude items, which is exactly what made it stick. Your pick now wins: the app may fill that memory when it is empty, but it can never overwrite a choice you made. Rapid-firing Claude items still works the same way, because that comes from your pick and picks persist."],t.s)
B.aTP=new A.v(773,B.aeo)
B.al3=s(["FINISHED ITEMS REALLY DO LEAVE THE QUEUE NOW - THE SECOND HALF OF THE FIX. You reported this twice, and you were right both times: v761 was not enough. The list of items the app sweeps deliberately leaves OUT anything already marked finished - and the Queue-to-Processed move lives inside that same sweep. So the moment an item was marked done it dropped out of the list, and the move that was supposed to file it could never run. A race the item always lost: marking it finished is exactly what made it invisible to the pass that files it. The sweep now reads finished items too."],t.s)
B.aV_=new A.v(772,B.al3)
B.ahh=s(["A PAUSE BUTTON IN THE QUEUE FOLDER. Open Queue and there is now a Pause button at the top right. Paused means I stop picking up new work from it; press again to resume. The hard part was not the button - a pause has to reach ME, not just your screen, or you would sit there reading PAUSED while I carried on working. So the state is stored as a folder named Paused inside your Claude folder, which syncs to every device and to the server the same way everything else does. That also means you can SEE the pause in your folder list instead of trusting an invisible switch.","One deliberate exception: while paused, work I have already finished still moves through to Processed. Freezing that would only lose the receipts for work that is already done - a pause is about what I START."],t.s)
B.b_c=new A.v(771,B.ahh)
B.afe=s(["DELETES: THE REDUNDANT WORK IS GONE, AND THE REST IS NOW MEASURED. Your log showed the app rebuilding your ENTIRE library - all 4,287 items - after every delete cycle, which is why deleting got slower as your library grew rather than being slow from the start. Those rebuilds also fire on every sync checkpoint and every save, and most of them find nothing new. The app now checks two numbers first - how many items exist and the newest timestamp - and when they are unchanged it skips the whole rebuild instead of re-reading everything. Nothing is filtered out; the skip only happens when the data is provably identical, so it cannot hide an item.","The rebuilds that DO have to run are now timed, so the next slow delete says whether the cost is reading, converting or comparing - and the next fix goes where the time actually is instead of where I guess it is."],t.s)
B.aSV=new A.v(770,B.afe)
B.apE=s(["THE TYPE LABEL IS A REAL DROPDOWN NOW. Tapping Note / Task / Meeting / Claude inside the dictation box used to slide a sheet up from the bottom of the screen - your eye left the box, picked, and came back. The menu now drops from the label itself, right where you tapped. Underneath it still goes through the same one path as the plus menu and the spoken commands, so switching type here behaves identically - it is a different door onto the same room, not a second way of doing it."],t.s)
B.aYU=new A.v(769,B.apE)
B.ape=s(["THE EDITED-RESEND BUTTON WAS APPEARING WHERE IT SHOULD NOT - MY BUG, FIXED. You said it seemed to be firing on its own, and your inbox proved it: two of your messages had been posted to me twice. The cause was mine from v761. The app remembers what text it last sent you so it can tell when you have edited an item - but I recorded that for EVERY kind of send, and three of the four send something else: pressing Implement sends an approval line, tapping an answer sends your choice, a reply sends a new message. So after you pressed Implement, the app thought the approval line was your item text, decided the item no longer matched, and grew an orange Edited-Resend button on it. One tap sent a duplicate. Only a genuine send of the item text now counts.","AND RESEND ASKS FIRST. A mis-tap on that button posts a real message to me, so it now confirms before sending, the same way Implement has always asked."],t.s)
B.aWq=new A.v(768,B.ape)
B.atG=s(["MY QUESTIONS TO YOU STOPPED SHOWING - FIXED. You asked whether the question system was working. It was not, and your own data showed why: I asked you about the green mic at 6:29pm and the item was correctly marked as needing you, then you pressed Implement on it at 6:41pm and that newer message overwrote the flag. The question was still there; the box just stopped saying so. An item took its state from the NEWEST message on its conversation, so any later message of any kind buried an open question. It now reads the whole conversation: finished work wins, an unanswered question beats everything else, and no later message can hide it. Ten tests, built from your actual thread."],t.s)
B.aWm=new A.v(767,B.atG)
B.amM=s(["THE STATUS LADDER USES YOUR WORDING. You asked what APPROVED meant on an item, and the answer is that I used the wrong word. It meant you pressed Implement - you authorised the build - but APPROVED reads like the work itself was approved, which is what DONE means. You settled this back in v765-era feedback: say SENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION. The button has said that for a while and your mockup labels the row the same way; the new ladder was the only place still saying APPROVED. Now they match."],t.s)
B.aY1=new A.v(766,B.amM)
B.alu=s(["THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON NOW SAYS WHY IT IS GREEN. You asked whether a green Approved button on a fresh item was intended - it is not. Green means an approval exists, and there are only two ways it can: your tap on this device, or the server telling this device you tapped it somewhere else. I checked your live data and found no item borrowing an approval that belonged to a different item, so I could not reproduce it - and guessing a third time is how the cursor bug ate three weeks. The button now records which of the two turned it green, so the next time you see it the log names the cause instead of costing another build."],t.s)
B.b49=new A.v(765,B.alu)
B.amt=s(["THE DATES AND TIMES IN THE STATUS BOX LINE UP. They were right-aligned, which lined up the right edge and left the left one ragged - the day and the hour are one or two digits, so every row started somewhere different. Each stamp is now padded to a fixed width with a digit-wide space, so the times form a clean column on both edges. Nothing about how they read has changed."],t.s)
B.aRW=new A.v(764,B.amt)
B.adm=s(['ANSWER MY QUESTION WITHOUT OPENING THE ITEM. When I am blocked, the box used to say only "NEEDS YOU - open to answer", so the question itself was a screen away. The box now shows the question and its choices right there, and one tap IS your answer on that thread. If it is a free-text question it says so and points you at Reply instead of drawing buttons that do nothing.','THE STAGE LIST SHOWS ONLY WHAT HAS HAPPENED. Your call, and the right one: instead of six rows on every box, you see the stages an item has actually reached, with a "+N more" tap when you want the whole path. A brand-new item still shows one live row, never an empty panel.'],t.s)
B.b30=new A.v(763,B.adm)
B.arS=s(["CLAUDE BOXES NOW SHOW THE WHOLE PATH, BUILT FROM THE MOCKUP. Instead of one status line and a run-on trail, every box shows a ladder: SENT, RECEIVED, APPROVED, QUEUE, PROCESSED, DONE - each on its own row with its own date and time. Stages that have not happened yet are greyed with a dash where the time will go, so you can see where an item is AND what is still ahead of it without opening anything.",'THE QUEUE ROW SHOWS DEPTH, NOT JUST POSITION. It reads "#2 of 13" - your place in the line and how much is ahead of it. Drag the item in the Queue folder and the number follows. The PROCESSED row names the release it shipped in, and the DONE row says "your call", because that move is only ever yours.',"WHEN I AM BLOCKED THE LADDER STOPS AT NEEDS YOU. The rungs below it are not drawn at all - they cannot advance until you answer, and greying them out would suggest waiting is enough."],t.s)
B.aTf=new A.v(762,B.arS)
B.ai5=s(['FINISHED WORK NOW ACTUALLY LEAVES THE QUEUE. You were right that completed items were not moving. It was not slow - the move was unreachable. The rule that stops me putting an item back after you drag it out was checked FIRST and skipped everything below it, and the Queue-to-Processed move lives below that line. Two different questions were sharing one gate: "leave it where he put it" is about filing IN, and it can no longer block a finished item from moving ON. Items sitting in Queue AND Processed at once get the stray Queue row dropped on the next sweep.','EDITED SINCE SENT, AND A RESEND BUTTON. Changing the words of an item you had already sent me did nothing and said nothing - it read one way on your screen and another in my queue, with neither of us able to see the difference. The box now shows an orange "Edited - Resend" button, and only when the words really differ. Tapping it posts the current text onto the same conversation. Re-wrapping or stray spaces do not count as an edit, and anything sent before this update is never flagged.'],t.s)
B.b3b=new A.v(761,B.ai5)
B.acR=s(["THE DICTATE BOX NO LONGER SHOWS A CURSOR IT CANNOT BACK UP. On Windows, that blinking caret in the unfocused box is drawn by the app - it is not a live field - so when focus was lost you got a cursor that looked ready over a box that could not take a key. On desktop it now appears only while dictation is actually running. Clicking the box still types. On the phone nothing changes: there the caret honestly marks where your spoken words land.","EVERY LOST CURSOR NOW SAYS WHY. The rule that decides whether the box re-claims focus has six inputs and was logging two - and only when it said yes. Every time it declined, which is the case that leaves you unable to type, nothing was written. All six inputs and the outcome are now recorded on every single focus loss, so the next occurrence names its own cause instead of costing another round.",'THE MOVE-TO INDICATOR READS PROPERLY AGAIN. Item names go straight into that one-line label while you drag, and a dictated title carries the invisible break marks that "new line" and "new paragraph" leave behind. A single-line label cannot break on those, so it drew them - that was the garble. Names are now flattened for display, and over-long ones shorten at a word.',"CLAUDE ANSWERS CAN BE COPIED, AND LINKS ARE CLICKABLE. In the full-size view both your message and the answer are now selectable text - right-click Copy on Windows, long-press on the phone - and any web address in them opens in your browser."],t.s)
B.b1A=new A.v(760,B.acR)
B.aiN=s(["THE CURSOR STOPS JUMPING BACK TO THE DICTATE BOX \u2014 AND THIS TIME IT IS PINNED. Clicking an item below no longer pulls the cursor up. The reason this kept coming back: the app judged a lost cursor by WHO took it, and that cannot tell \u201che clicked the box\u201d from \u201che clicked an item below\u201d \u2014 both look identical. It now reads WHERE YOUR CLICK LANDED, and all three cases (click the box, click an item, no click at all) are locked by tests, so fixing one can no longer break another."],t.s)
B.aWO=new A.v(759,B.aiN)
B.asm=s(["PICKING TASK OR MEETING RETYPES THE BOX \u2014 IT NO LONGER REPLACES IT. Choosing Task from the menu was opening the voice-capture box built for SAYING \u201cnew task\u201d: a green mic appeared and your dictate box vanished. Buttons retype, voice captures. The badge\u2019s promise now binds the save too, so a box labelled Task produces a task."],t.s)
B.aUl=new A.v(758,B.asm)
B.ago=s(["YOUR WORDS ALWAYS POST. The duplicate guard was swallowing real notes: post \u201cTest.\u201d and a follow-up starting with the same word vanished; paste a longer version of something already posted and it never appeared. The rule is narrowed to the one true leftover shape \u2014 a long fragment of what was just posted \u2014 and pinned by tests using today\u2019s exact cases. When in doubt, the note posts: a near-duplicate costs one swipe, a lost note costs your words."],t.s)
B.b_C=new A.v(757,B.ago)
B.acV=s(["THE BOX SPEAKS ALL FOUR TYPES. Picking Task or Meeting used to change nothing you could see \u2014 the badge only knew Note and Claude, which read as \u201cnot letting me choose.\u201d The badge now says Note, Task, Meeting or Claude, each in its own colour.","A TYPE PICK ALWAYS LANDS. Choosing a type during a live dictation left the previous Claude flag standing, so the box could ignore your choice. A pick is a statement about THIS box \u2014 it takes effect mic on or off."],t.s)
B.aYR=new A.v(756,B.acV)
B.aki=s(["THE BOX NO LONGER LIES ABOUT WHAT IT WILL MAKE. It said \u201cNote\u201d and produced a Claude item, because the label read one flag while the save weighed three \u2014 including the remembered last type. Both now read the same answer, so the badge is a promise the save keeps."],t.s)
B.b_y=new A.v(755,B.aki)
B.amX=s(["REMOVING AN ITEM FROM THE QUEUE WORKS ON THE FIRST TRY. v751 stopped items coming back, but only for ones it had filed itself \u2014 everything already sitting in your Queue was unknown to it, so the first removal of each would still have bounced. Anything seen in the Queue now counts as queued, so out means out immediately."],t.s)
B.aYj=new A.v(754,B.amX)
B.aep=s(["THE TYPE LABEL IN THE DICTATE BOX IS NOW A DROP DOWN. Tap \u201cNote\u201d or \u201cClaude\u201d and pick what you want \u2014 the box retypes on the spot, and the next box remembers it, same as picking from \uff0b."],t.s)
B.aVr=new A.v(753,B.aep)
B.abe=s(["A NEW ITEM APPEARS EVEN WHILE YOUR CURSOR IS IN THE BOX. The list refuses to rebuild under a live caret \u2014 right, because a rebuild would steal it \u2014 but that also hid the item you had just created. New items are now ADDED below while the rebuild waits: nothing moves, nothing reorders, your typing is untouched."],t.s)
B.aYK=new A.v(752,B.abe)
B.aic=s(["ITEMS YOU TAKE OUT OF THE QUEUE STAY OUT. My sweep was re-filing them \u2014 right the first time, wrong every time after, because removing one is you saying it does not belong there. An item is queued once; after that your placement stands.","MOVING AN ITEM NO LONGER OPENS THE ONE ABOVE IT. An open row leaving the list was handing its cursor to its neighbour, which then opened itself. The move paths now drop the cursor first, exactly as delete has since v453.","THE PURPLE COMMANDS-ONLY BAR IS GONE from above the dictate box. The same warning still appears inside the box, where you are actually looking."],t.s)
B.aW8=new A.v(751,B.aic)
B.aju=s(["A QUESTIONS FOLDER \u2014 YOURS. Anything you ask me now files itself into a Questions folder inside Claude, at the back of its own queue, and NOTHING takes it out but you. Detection is simple and predictable: ends with \u201c?\u201d or starts with what / why / how / can / is / are / should. Press Implement on one and it becomes work instead \u2014 it moves to the Queue.","MY BLOCKED ITEMS RIDE THE TASK QUEUE. When I need an answer from you, that item sits in the Queue wearing its NEEDS YOU flag, instead of hiding in a folder of its own \u2014 one list to watch, as you asked."],t.s)
B.aZQ=new A.v(750,B.aju)
B.ahu=s(["QUEUE #1 IS NOW THE NEXT THING TO BE WORKED. Newly approved items were landing at the TOP of the Queue folder \u2014 right for a library, wrong for a queue \u2014 so the item you sent last was labelled #1. New work now joins the BACK of the line, and dragging still overrides it: your order beats arrival order."],t.s)
B.b05=new A.v(749,B.ahu)
B.abi=s(["NO MORE TUG-OF-WAR WITH A DIALOG. Open the new-folder box while the dictate box has your text and the two were fighting over the keyboard. A dialog is something you opened \u2014 while it is up, the dictate box stands down completely and lets you type in the dialog."],t.s)
B.aXs=new A.v(748,B.abi)
B.ajc=s(["FOLDERS UNFOLD LESS EAGERLY. Resting a dragged item on a folder still opens its subfolders \u2014 but it now takes 0.9 seconds instead of 0.5, so crossing a folder on the way somewhere else no longer unfolds it."],t.s)
B.b0e=new A.v(747,B.ajc)
B.an9=s(["THE CLICK-ONCE-TO-TYPE BUG, NAMED AT LAST. Your log shows the box losing the cursor to a focus SCOPE \u2014 the framework re-parenting focus, not you clicking anywhere. The guard that re-claims the cursor skipped those cases whenever they happened within a quarter-second of your click on the box, which is exactly when they happen. A scope is never something you clicked, so it is now always treated as a steal and the cursor comes straight back."],t.s)
B.aWp=new A.v(746,B.an9)
B.amg=s(["THE BOX CLEARS THE INSTANT YOU HIT ENTER. Your words were being held in the box until the save, the Claude send and the folder filing all finished \u2014 seconds, on a busy sync \u2014 even though the item had already appeared below. It clears immediately now, and if a save ever fails the words drop straight back in."],t.s)
B.b_Q=new A.v(745,B.amg)
B.afX=s(["THE LOOP CLOSES: A DONE BUTTON. Queue fills itself when you approve, Processed fills itself when the work lands \u2014 and now a finished item carries a green Done button that moves it out of Processed into your Done folder, in one tap. Done stays yours: the app files into it only when you press that button."],t.s)
B.b1D=new A.v(744,B.afX)
B.af4=s(["DRAG A SUBFOLDER STRAIGHT TO A SPOT AT THE TOP LEVEL. The drop strips between root folders only accepted folders that were ALREADY at the top level, so a subfolder aimed there was rejected and fell into the row underneath instead. They now take any folder: it is promoted out of its parent and seated exactly where you aimed, in one drop."],t.s)
B.aWA=new A.v(743,B.af4)
B.akw=s(["THE DUPLICATE-AS-A-NOTE IS DEAD. Your 10:01am note read \u201cWith the proper With the proper time and focus\u2026\u201d and your 10:02am Claude item held the same words: the box kept a TAIL after the Claude message closed and posted it as a fresh note. The old guard only caught a leftover that matched EXACTLY, so a tail with a doubled opening slipped through. Now a leftover is recognised by content, and a tail that follows a Claude message stays a Claude message instead of being demoted to a note. Genuinely new words still post \u2014 that rule is pinned by tests."],t.s)
B.aXy=new A.v(742,B.akw)
B.afk=s(["CLICKING AN ITEM IN A FOLDER STOPS YANKING YOU BACK. Inside a folder, every click looked to the app like \u201cno user input\u201d \u2014 so the box you had been typing in re-claimed the cursor and pulled you back to the top. Folders now feed the same click clock Home always had, so a click is treated as what it is: yours."],t.s)
B.b_6=new A.v(741,B.afk)
B.aqE=s(["ANYTHING YOU CAN SAY WEARS QUOTES. The \u201cCreate dictation\u201d button now matches the spoken hints \u2014 if the words activate dictation, they are quoted."],t.s)
B.aUu=new A.v(740,B.aqE)
B.afm=s(["THE BOX KEEPS THE LAST TYPE \u2014 ONE RULE, EVERYWHERE. Whatever you last created here, the next box is that: dictated or typed, on Home or inside a folder. It was three separate rules before, which is why it worked after some actions and reverted after others. A type folder (Claude and its subfolders) still decides its own type; an ordinary folder no longer forces \u201cnote\u201d."],t.s)
B.aXW=new A.v(739,B.afm)
B.afo=s(["THE BOX TAKES TYPING AFTER EVERY POST. Create an item and the fresh box is ready for the next one \u2014 no clicking into it first. That was the last place the caret drew without the keyboard being handed over.","THE MICROPHONE BUTTON IS OFF THE INBOX BOX. It was mine and it was redundant there \u2014 the On/Off/New control is the mic. It stays in folders, where it is the only way to dictate."],t.s)
B.b1N=new A.v(738,B.afo)
B.ama=s(["FOLDER NAMES ARE READABLE AGAIN. Names were sharing the row\u2019s free space with an invisible spacer, so each one got about half of what was left \u2014 worse the deeper the folder sat. The name (and its chevron) now take all the room actually available; counts and toggles keep their right edge."],t.s)
B.aUD=new A.v(737,B.ama)
B.arf=s(["EVERY QUEUE FILING NOW CARRIES ITS RECEIPT. When an item moves into Queue, the log records which approval authorised it and when \u2014 so \u201cthat looked queued before I pressed Implement\u201d can be settled with evidence instead of memory."],t.s)
B.b0c=new A.v(736,B.arf)
B.ash=s(["THE CLAUDE BOX TAKES TYPING RIGHT AWAY. Picking \u201cNew Claude message\u201d drew the caret but never actually took the keyboard, so you had to click the box first. It now takes focus for real.","\u201cSENT FOR IMPLEMENTATION\u201d. Your wording \u2014 pressing the button is you authorising the work, not the work being finished. PROCESSED is what says finished.","AN ITEM THAT NEEDS YOU SAYS SO. A clay \u201cNEEDS YOU \u2014 open to answer\u201d line sits at the top of that item\u2019s box, so you can see WHICH one is waiting without opening anything."],t.s)
B.aZd=new A.v(735,B.ash)
B.ach=s(["THE BLANK NOTE IS ACTUALLY GONE. v730 aimed at it and missed: your 8:26am blank was saved ELEVEN SECONDS BEFORE the Claude item it belonged to, so the test could not have fired yet. A session addressed to Claude no longer saves an empty note at all.","THE BOX KEEPS THE TYPE. Post a Claude message from the Inbox and the next box is still a Claude box \u2014 rapid-fire without re-picking every time."],t.s)
B.aUd=new A.v(734,B.ach)
B.aiR=s(["A QUESTIONS FOLDER THAT EMPTIES ITSELF. When Claude is blocked on you, that item files itself into a Questions folder inside Claude \u2014 and the moment you answer, it leaves on its own. Your design, exactly.","THE CLAUDE ROW TELLS YOU. A clay \u201c2 need you\u201d badge sits on the pinned Claude row whenever questions are open, and disappears when the last one is answered."],t.s)
B.b_2=new A.v(733,B.aiR)
B.akk=s(["PICKING \u201cNEW CLAUDE MESSAGE\u201d ON A BLANK BOX WORKS AGAIN. v730 stopped the \uff0b from switching the mic on \u2014 but nothing then retyped the box, so the pick did nothing you could see and the item came out as a note. The blank box now becomes a Claude box the moment you pick it, and speaking into it lands as a Claude message.","PROCESSED NAMES ITS RELEASE. \u201cPROCESSED \xb7 Jul 27 \xb7 7:51am \xb7 v730\u201d \u2014 the version taken from the reply that closed the item, so each finished item carries its own receipt."],t.s)
B.aRD=new A.v(732,B.akk)
B.aeS=s(["THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON SAYS IMPLEMENTED \u2014 EVERYWHERE. Pressing it on the phone left Windows showing a plain Implement button, because that green state was remembered only on the device you pressed. It is now read from the approval on the thread itself, so every device shows \u201cIMPLEMENTED \xb7 Jul 27 \xb7 4:53am\u201d.","TURNING DICTATION OFF NO LONGER TURNS YOUR CLAUDE MESSAGE INTO A NOTE. The box you typed into decides what it is \u2014 switching the mic off is not a change of mind about what you wrote."],t.s)
B.aRR=new A.v(731,B.aeS)
B.at1=s(["THE BLANK NOTE BESIDE EVERY CLAUDE MESSAGE IS GONE. Your log showed it: an empty note saved 15 seconds before each Claude item \u2014 the box that was open when you started talking, whose words went to the Claude box instead. It is no longer saved, because its words were never lost.","THE \uff0b NO LONGER TURNS THE MIC ON. Creating an item opens the box in that type and waits \u2014 dictation starts only when you start it. If you are already dictating, nothing changes.","FINISHED WORK REACHES PROCESSED. An item approved before the pipeline existed never passed through Queue, so it never moved out. Done + approved now files straight into Processed.","THE PROCESSED / QUEUE LABEL CAN\u2019T BE CUT OFF. It has its own line above the status trail instead of competing with five timestamps for the same two lines."],t.s)
B.b1z=new A.v(730,B.at1)
B.arZ=s(["SEARCH IS ACTUALLY BACK ON WINDOWS. v727 said it was fixed; the code never made it into the build. It is in this one \u2014 checked in the file, not taken on trust.","A DICTATION CAN NO LONGER BE DROPPED AS \u201cBLANK\u201d. v725\u2019s no-blank-boxes rule judged silence using the live engine\u2019s recording; on the Whisper engines that recording lives elsewhere, so a box whose words were still being transcribed could have been discarded. When it cannot be proven empty, the item is kept \u2014 always."],t.s)
B.aUA=new A.v(729,B.arZ)
B.akN=s(["THE INBOX BOX REMEMBERS. Make a Claude message from the Inbox and the next box opens as a Claude message too \u2014 until you pick something else. Rapid-firing no longer means re-choosing the type every time. Folders still decide their own type, and a filtered view still wins.","TYPING IN A CLAUDE FOLDER MAKES A CLAUDE MESSAGE. v727 shipped only the spoken half of this by mistake \u2014 dictating there worked, typing there still made a note. Both halves work now."],t.s)
B.aZy=new A.v(728,B.akN)
B.anc=s(["SEARCH IS BACK ON WINDOWS. v721 dropped it from the desktop sidebar while leaving it on the phone \u2014 my mistake, and exactly what the comment above that code warns about. Restored.","THE FOLDER DECIDES THE TYPE. Talking or typing in the Claude folder \u2014 or in Queue, Processed, or any subfolder you make there \u2014 creates a CLAUDE message and sends it, instead of a note.","ONE CLAUDE FOLDER. The old \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder folds into your current Claude folder, contents and subfolders intact, and the empty shell is removed. Nothing is deleted that holds anything."],t.s)
B.b__=new A.v(727,B.anc)
B.abC=s(["THE QUEUE NUMBER IS ON THE BOX. A Claude item now leads its status line with where it sits \u2014 \u201cQUEUE #3\u201d while it waits, green \u201cPROCESSED\u201d once the work is done \u2014 so you can scan your whole queue from the main window without opening anything.","EVERY STATUS CARRIES ITS DATE AND TIME. Queue shows when you approved it, Processed shows when it finished, and the full-size ticket strip spells out the day too (\u201cPROCESSED \xb7 Jul 27 \xb7 5:51am\u201d)."],t.s)
B.b1j=new A.v(726,B.abC)
B.abZ=s(["FOUND YOUR MISSING FOLDERS \u2014 there were THREE folders named \u201cClaude\u201d. A cross-device race made duplicates, and because they shared a name the app picked a different one each time: the pipeline filed Queue and Processed into one while your pinned row opened another. They were never lost, just filed in a twin. Duplicates now fold into the original (oldest wins, everywhere, on every device) and Queue/Processed come home inside it.","NO MORE BLANK BOXES. An item that closes holding no words is never created in the first place \u2014 with one deliberate exception: if its audio actually contains speech (a network drop can leave your words in the recording only) the item stays, because losing words is never allowed."],t.s)
B.aZ3=new A.v(725,B.abZ)
B.agp=s(["THE PIPELINE SPEAKS YOUR LANGUAGE. The folders are named Queue and Processed now, exactly as you specced \u2014 approved work waits in Queue (its order is the work order), finished work lands in Processed for your review, and moving to Done is your call, by hand, on purpose. Your overnight items rename over automatically, and everything lives inside your Claude folder.","THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON REMEMBERS WHEN. After you press it, the button reads \u201c\u2713 Approved Jul 27 \xb7 4:51am\u201d \u2014 the date and time of your tap, right on the item.","I READ YOUR QUEUE ORDER. When several items are approved, I work them top-down in the order you arranged the Queue folder \u2014 no telling me which is next."],t.s)
B.aYf=new A.v(724,B.agp)
B.akn=s(["\u201cUPLOADING 32 ITEMS\u201d NOW TELLS THE TRUTH. While you are dictating, uploads deliberately wait (recording owns the phone) \u2014 but the pill said \u201cUploading\u2026\u201d anyway, which read as stuck. It now says \u201c32 items wait for dictation to end\u201d, and the moment the mic goes off they drain."],t.s)
B.b2F=new A.v(723,B.akn)
B.aeq=s(["THE PIPELINE IS LIVE. Press Implement and the item files itself into a Building folder inside Claude; when the work completes it moves to Recently finished \u2014 the folders ARE the board, on every device. Both folders create themselves the first time they are needed.","THE TICKET STRIP. An approved item\u2019s chat now opens with BUILDING \xb7 #N in queue (drag items in the Building folder to reprioritise \u2014 the number follows), turning green RECENTLY FINISHED when it ships.","ONE-TAP ANSWERS. When Claude asks a question with choices, the choices appear as buttons \u2014 tap one and that IS your reply. No typing."],t.s)
B.aSG=new A.v(722,B.aeq)
B.akV=s(["ONE DICTATE BOX EVERYWHERE. The green mic that folders had is now on the Inbox box too \u2014 every composer is the same box with the same controls: tap the mic to start talking into it, tap the red stop to finish. The bottom 3-way on Home still works; this is a second way in, not a replacement.","MEETINGS AND NOTES ARE GONE FROM THE LIBRARY LIST \u2014 the type rows under Inbox are the one home for types now. Search and Listening remain."],t.s)
B.aTO=new A.v(721,B.akV)
B.apY=s(["THE BLANK SCREEN ON DOUBLE-CLICK IS FIXED. Your log caught the crash: the animation that flies the top bar between screens died mid-flight, killing the push half-way \u2014 blank screen, and the dead route then held the cursor hostage (your unkillable blinking caret). The flight is off; opens are instant and can\u2019t crash.","NO PATH CREATES ITEMS BELOW THE DICTATE BOX ANY MORE. The folder screens\u2019 \uff0b was the last one \u2014 it now points the dictate box at the chosen type, same as everywhere. Your rule, everywhere, no exceptions.","\u2715 CLEARS WORDS, NOT THE TYPE. Clearing a Claude box keeps it a Claude box.","TIMES AND STATUS ARE BIG AND DARK \u2014 in the box trail and in the chat entries. Important info, no longer hidden."],t.s)
B.b_i=new A.v(720,B.apY)
B.aiX=s(["CLAUDE TIMES ARE YOUR TIMES. The chat stamps were server time \u2014 five hours ahead. Everything converts to local now.","THE BOX SHOWS THE WHOLE TRAIL. A Claude item\u2019s box now carries the time for each status \u2014 Sent, Received, Done \u2014 under your original question, with the current state highlighted; the full conversation stays in the max view.","THE REPLY BOX TAKES THE CLICK. Tapping anywhere on the reply bar lands the caret, so typing works the first time on Windows.","THE PINNED CLAUDE FOLDER SITS ABOVE LIBRARY on the desktop sidebar too \u2014 it had slipped under the Library header there, reading as a Library entry."],t.s)
B.b4d=new A.v(719,B.aiX)
B.aes=s(["TOP-LEVEL FOLDERS CAN BE REORDERED AGAIN. The drop target for \u201cplace above this folder\u201d was eight pixels tall \u2014 everywhere else on a row meant \u201cmove inside it\u201d, which is also how folders kept nesting where you never aimed. Now: top third of a row drops above it, bottom third drops below, only the middle nests, and the blue line shows exactly where it will land.","DELETE IS INSTANT. The box used to stay on screen until the database write finished \u2014 seconds, on a busy desktop \u2014 which read as a dead button and invited the re-presses behind the stacked Undo bars. The box now leaves the moment you press; the write follows behind, and a failed write brings it straight back.","THE \uff0b FOLLOWS YOUR RULE AGAIN: creating a new item points the dictate box at that type \u2014 it never inserts a row below. My v709 change broke that; reverted.","THE CLAUDE CHAT ACTUALLY SHOWS UP \u2014 AND WITH EVERYTHING. The conversation was fetching fine (your log proved it) but rendering into a box with no height; fixed. Each entry now carries its full trail: Sent \u2192 Received \u2192 Needs you / \u2713 Complete, each with its time."],t.s)
B.aYs=new A.v(718,B.aes)
B.akU=s(["THE FOLDER DICTATE BOX IS FULL WIDTH. It was carrying its own side padding on top of the screen\u2019s, which made it 32px narrower than the item boxes under it."],t.s)
B.b_F=new A.v(717,B.akU)
B.ajE=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS ARE A REAL CHAT NOW. The full-size view shows the conversation as dated messages \u2014 yours and Claude\u2019s, each with its own time and state (Sent \xb7 Received \xb7 Needs you \xb7 \u2713 Complete), an Implement button on every entry you wrote, and a proper reply box pinned at the bottom instead of the big blank text area.","CLAUDE CAN NOW SAY \u201cNEEDS YOU\u201d. When Claude is blocked on a question, the entry turns orange with the question right there \u2014 so work never silently stalls waiting for an answer you did not know was wanted.","ONE CLAUDE IN THE PANE, NOT THREE. The old \u201cClaude\u201d Library entry opened a retired chat screen with no dictate box \u2014 that is what you kept landing on. It is gone; the type row and your pinned Claude folder remain, and the folder opens with the same dictate box as everywhere else.","THE FOLDER EXPAND ARROW IS BACK ON THE RIGHT of the folder name, where it used to be."],t.s)
B.aZi=new A.v(716,B.ajE)
B.afK=s(["THE DUPLICATE ITEMS ARE EXPLAINED AND FIXED. Your 8:54 and 8:55 pair were two real rows with identical text, and the log showed the same 154 characters posted twice \u2014 both at the very start of a session. The guard against re-posting lived only in memory, so an app restart wiped it, and the saved draft was then restored and posted again. Your desktop has been restarting all evening for updates, which is why you kept seeing this and I never did. The draft is now cleared the instant a note posts, and the guard survives a restart.","THE CIRCLE HANDLES ARE GONE FROM FOLDER ITEMS. The bar on each item is the handle everywhere, as it already was on the main screen; folder rows keep a grip because they have no bar."],t.s)
B.b0Z=new A.v(715,B.afK)
B.aqe=s(["THE \uff0b CAN MAKE A CLAUDE MESSAGE AGAIN. Two of my own bugs from the other night: a new item created from the \uff0b was saved but never joined the list on screen, and a new Claude message was filed into the Claude folder the instant it was born \u2014 which takes it out of the Inbox, so it vanished as you watched. New items now appear where you made them and stay unfiled until they are sent."],t.s)
B.b0W=new A.v(714,B.aqe)
B.aiL=s(["WINDOWS: THE BLINKING CURSOR YOU COULD NOT TYPE INTO. Your desktop log showed the box losing focus to the route\u2019s own scope over and over, with no click behind it \u2014 a teardown stealing the cursor. The box then fell back to its read-only view, which paints a fake caret, so you got a blinking cursor over a field that could not take keys. It now re-claims the cursor when \u2014 and only when \u2014 nothing was clicked. Clicking away still means you left.","THE \uff0b ALWAYS ASKS WHICH TYPE. It creates nothing on its own any more; the menu opens every time, with the type you are viewing at the top of the list.","YOUR EXISTING CLAUDE MESSAGES ARE LINKED INTO THE NEW CLAUDE FOLDER \u2014 a link, not a move, so whatever folder they are already in keeps them too. Runs once, automatically.","THE DICTATE BOX BUTTON NOW SAYS \u201cCREATE DICTATION\u201d."],t.s)
B.aSX=new A.v(713,B.aiL)
B.aoc=s(["A PERMANENT CLAUDE FOLDER IN THE PANE, above the Library rows. Every new Claude item links itself into it, so it works like an inbox for Claude work, and you can build whatever folder structure you want underneath it. It is a real folder \u2014 it also still appears in your folder tree, where dragging and expanding already work. Note: your existing 33 Claude items are still in the old \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder; nothing was moved for you.","FOLDERS CAN GET OUT OF A FOLDER AGAIN. There was no drag anywhere that moved a folder to the top level \u2014 dragging one up to the top dropped it INTO whatever row was there instead, silently. Now: drop a folder on the FOLDERS header to send it to the top level, or use the new \u201cMove to Root\u201d line in its \u22ef menu.","A MOVE TELLS YOU WHERE IT WENT. It used to just say \u201cMoved\u201d. It now names the destination, so a wrong drop is obvious immediately instead of looking like nothing happened."],t.s)
B.b_a=new A.v(712,B.aoc)
B.alW=s(["THE VIEW YOU ARE IN SETS THE TYPE. Press Dictate while the Inbox is filtered to Claude and you get a CLAUDE box \u2014 clay badge and all \u2014 instead of a note you then have to convert. Same for the \uff0b: inside a type view it skips the menu and makes that type on one tap, so you can fire off several in a row. Unfiltered, \uff0b still asks which kind."],t.s)
B.aWl=new A.v(711,B.alW)
B.asl=s(["THE WAY BACK OUT OF A TYPE VIEW. Tapping Notes, Tasks, Meetings or Claude filters the Inbox \u2014 but the header still said \u201cInbox\u201d, both rows lit up in the pane, and tapping Inbox did nothing because you were already on that screen. The header now reads \u201cInbox \u203a Claude\u201d, only the type row lights up, and tapping Inbox clears the filter and takes you back.","THE FULL-SIZE ITEM IS CLEANER. The summary block is gone for now, read-aloud moved to the top right where every other screen keeps it, and the audio player is about 40% shorter \u2014 same controls, far less of the screen on a short note."],t.s)
B.aRY=new A.v(710,B.asl)
B.alK=s(["THE \uff0b WORKS AGAIN \u2014 AND THE INBOX HAS ONE. The folder scrollbar was drawn on top of the \uff0b beside FOLDERS, so the scrollbar was eating your taps; the pane now keeps a gutter and every trailing control there is clickable again. Separately, the Inbox never had a \uff0b at all, so a Claude message could only be made by voice or inside a folder. There is now one at the top of the main screen: note, task, meeting, or Claude message, born with the cursor already in it.","YOU CAN SEE WHERE YOU ARE. The location sits right after the \u2630 \u2014 \u201cInbox\u201d on the main screen, and folder screens keep their name in the bar even after the big title scrolls away.","THE EMPTY BOX INVITES TYPING TOO. With the mic off it now reads \u201cType or press Dictate to start\u201d, because the box takes typing either way."],t.s)
B.aYu=new A.v(709,B.alK)
B.aiZ=s(["THE MAIN SCREEN SAYS \u201cINBOX\u201d. Every folder already showed its name at the top; the screen you live in showed nothing. Now it is labelled, so it is always obvious where you are.","FIRST ONE THROUGH THE NEW GATE. You dictated this request into a Claude item and pressed Implement \u2014 that approval is what authorized the build, and this line is the proof the loop works end to end."],t.s)
B.aV4=new A.v(708,B.aiZ)
B.aj4=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS ARE CONVERSATIONS NOW. A Claude box keeps every answer instead of replacing the last one, so one item holds the whole back-and-forth about a feature or a bug. Tap REPLY to send what is in the box as the next message on that same thread \u2014 dictate the follow-up straight into the box and press Reply.","THE IMPLEMENT BUTTON \u2014 you decide when I build. Every sent Claude item now has an Implement button. Until you press it, I only discuss and plan the item; pressing it (with a confirm, so a mis-tap can not do it) sends a timestamped approval on the thread and turns the button green. Nothing gets built without it.","NAME YOUR CLAUDE ITEMS. Claude items finally have an editable title, so a long conversation is findable by the name you gave it instead of by its first sentence."],t.s)
B.b2v=new A.v(707,B.aj4)
B.asS=s(["RE-FLICKS NO LONGER PAUSE. Touching a flying list used to stop it dead for the whole time your finger was down, then start over. Now a finger landing on a fast glide lets it keep coasting while the touch declares itself: flick again and the speeds ADD (no more losing 10% every time), hold still and it stops as it always did.","THE DRAG HOLD GETS ITS SNAP BACK. Holding an item\u2019s bar to drag it now arms in 0.3 seconds instead of the half-second it was raised to this morning during the flick hunt. The real thief turned out to be elsewhere \u2014 and it is caught now \u2014 so the extra caution was just costing you time on every drag.","THE SCROLL WORK IS WRITTEN DOWN. Every mechanism behind the last two days of scrolling fixes is now recorded in the repo so it can never be re-litigated from scratch."],t.s)
B.aYJ=new A.v(706,B.asS)
B.aqo=s(["FIXED: FLICKS POPPING THE KEYBOARD. Your diagnosis connected it: when the system fails to read a flick as a scroll \u2014 the same failure behind the missing glides \u2014 the item under your finger takes it as a TAP, focuses for typing, and up comes the keyboard. There is now a referee that cannot be argued with: a gesture that traveled far was never a tap, so any focus it produced is revoked on the spot and the keyboard stays down. Real taps are untouched."],t.s)
B.b0g=new A.v(705,B.aqo)
B.adH=s(["FLICKS NOW LAUNCH INSTANTLY AND STACK UP. Correcting yesterday\u2019s conclusion with a better experiment: the stalls hit the middle of the screen too, so the scrollbar was a contributor, not the cause. Two changes built on what the log proves works: the glide now launches ONE FRAME after your finger lifts (it waited far longer before, so your next rapid flick kept catching a list that had not started moving), and repeated flicks ADD \u2014 each flick inherits the speed of the glide it caught, or of the flick before it that never got to launch. Flick-flick-flick builds speed the way it should. A flick in the opposite direction still stops the list dead, as it must."],t.s)
B.aVH=new A.v(704,B.adH)
B.ajz=s(["THE FLICK MYSTERY, SOLVED \u2014 IT WAS THE SCROLLBAR. A controlled test on your own phone settled it: identical fast flicks glide perfectly in the middle of the screen and die at the right edge. The scrollbar was INTERACTIVE \u2014 its touch zone runs down the right edge where your thumb naturally flicks, it was claiming those touches for itself, and tapping its track moves exactly ONE SCREEN \u2014 your very words. Every earlier theory tested clean because test swipes ran dead-center. The scrollbar is now display-only: it shows where you are, and every touch belongs to the list. Flick anywhere \u2014 including the edge."],t.s)
B.aUC=new A.v(703,B.ajz)
B.akp=s(["FIXED: THE APP FREEZING \u2014 my bug, introduced this afternoon. The new touch-quieting checked only ONCE whether it was safe to wake the list back up; if that one moment looked busy, it never checked again, the screen stopped updating forever, and the app read as dead. It now keeps checking until the list is truly at rest, and after three seconds it wakes up no matter what \u2014 a briefly stale row is nothing, a dead screen is everything.","FIXED: FLICKING A MOVING LIST STOPPING IT. Catching a glide does not count as a new scroll internally, so the flick-helper thought the previous fling was still this gesture and refused to help \u2014 that was your repeatable stall. Catch-flicks now get a fresh slate the instant your finger lands. Also: the helper can no longer start a glide while any finger is still on the glass."],t.s)
B.b0x=new A.v(702,B.akp)
B.aft=s(["THE FLICK FIX, AT THE ROOT. Your report drew the line exactly: glides that start run clean to the end \u2014 only the flick itself dies. A fast flick lasts a twentieth of a second, and the quiet-the-app protection was arming only once scrolling REGISTERED \u2014 which for a flick is after it is already over. So your quickest gesture ran against the busiest moment, its touch readings collapsed into one or two points, and the phone concluded the speed was zero. Now the app goes quiet the INSTANT your finger touches the glass, before anything else can win the race \u2014 so the flick is measured honestly and glides natively. The rescue from the last build stays as a backstop."],t.s)
B.aXn=new A.v(701,B.aft)
B.alX=s(["QUICK FLICKS FINALLY GLIDE. Your phone\u2019s log gave the answer outright: the swipes that stalled were your FASTEST ones \u2014 up to 8,600 pixels a second \u2014 while the ones that worked were slower and four times longer. Speed was never the problem; DURATION was. Under about a tenth of a second, a flick delivers so few movement readings that the phone cannot work out how fast it went, decides the answer is zero, and starts no glide. The momentum was real and simply got rounded away. The app now measures your flick itself and hands that momentum back when the system throws it out \u2014 so a fast flick travels, which was the whole point of flicking."],t.s)
B.b1T=new A.v(700,B.alX)
B.at7=s(["SWIPING AGAIN NOW SPEEDS THE LIST UP INSTEAD OF SLOWING IT DOWN. You spotted the real thing: flick a moving list and it got SLOWER. Touching a gliding list stops the glide \u2014 correct \u2014 but the next flick was starting from a standstill, throwing away the speed already there, and a second flick is usually lazier than the first. So hurrying it made it slower, every time. The list now carries that speed into the new flick, the way a well-behaved list should: flick, flick, flick and it accelerates.","THE LINE ABOVE YOUR ITEMS NOW SHOWS THE COUNT instead of the word \u201cEARLIER\u201d. The word only restated what the position already told you; the number answers something real, in the same place."],t.s)
B.aRX=new A.v(699,B.at7)
B.arR=s(["CLAUDE\u2019S ANSWERS ARE READABLE NOW. The reply was small, faded AND italic \u2014 three separate strikes against the one line you come back to the box to read. It is now full body size in normal text colour, upright, and shows up to six lines instead of three so a real answer is not cut off mid-sentence. The round-trip times got the same treatment: bigger, and no longer the palest grey in the app."],t.s)
B.b23=new A.v(698,B.arR)
B.asq=s(["THE EMPTY BOX NOW SAYS THE RIGHT THING FOR THE MOMENT YOU ARE IN. Before Dictate is pressed it reads \u201cPress Dictate to start\u201d \u2014 the button is the only thing between you and a note, so that is the one useful instruction. Once you press it, it changes to \u201cSpeak or type\u201d and the two spoken shortcuts appear beneath it. Those shortcuts only show while something is actually listening; \u201csay new note\u201d was never useful advice with the mic off."],t.s)
B.aUZ=new A.v(697,B.asq)
B.aeJ=s(["MEASURING THE STALLED SWIPES PROPERLY. Yesterday\u2019s attempt to record how fast your finger was moving came back as zero on EVERY swipe \u2014 including ones that glided six thousand pixels \u2014 so it was measuring nothing. This build times the gesture itself and works out the speed of its final stretch, which is the number your phone actually uses to decide whether to glide. One more stalled swipe from you and it says plainly whether the momentum is being thrown away, or whether your finger had already come to rest before lifting \u2014 which stops the glide in every app ever made and would mean the remaining stops are not a defect at all."],t.s)
B.b0z=new A.v(696,B.aeJ)
B.apl=s(["THE EMPTY BOX NOW SAYS \u201cSPEAK OR TYPE\u201d \u2014 your wording, and better than mine: it names what you do rather than which button to press, and it covers both ways in three words.","Also, one more scroll measurement: the app now records how fast your finger was moving when you lifted it. That single number separates \u201cyou stopped before letting go\u201d, which is normal everywhere, from \u201cthe momentum was thrown away\u201d, which is a bug. The next stalled fling answers it."],t.s)
B.b2l=new A.v(695,B.apl)
B.afB=s(["THE LIST NOW HOLDS COMPLETELY STILL WHILE YOU SCROLL. Your phone\u2019s new gesture log finally showed the difference: swipes driven by a script on that same phone glide perfectly (a hundred-plus frames of momentum), while YOUR swipes register barely one or two movements and no momentum at all. The list was too busy to feel your finger \u2014 because while the mic or sync is live, the whole stack was rebuilding several times a second, and those rebuilds were landing during your swipe. That only happens when dictation is running, which is why every scripted test of mine looked fine. Now, for the length of a gesture, the list is handed back exactly as it was \u2014 no work at all \u2014 and refreshes the instant your finger lifts. Your dictation box is above the list and keeps running normally throughout."],t.s)
B.aXA=new A.v(694,B.afB)
B.ap3=s(['THE SWIPE THAT BECAME A GRAB. Every item\u2019s colored bar is a drag handle, and it armed after only 220 milliseconds of your finger resting on it \u2014 but a normal scroll often starts with a rest of about that long. When it did, the bar took the gesture as a LIFT and the list simply did not move: your swipe "stopped" the scroll because it never became a scroll. Bars run the full width of every row, so that is most of what a thumb lands on, and it explains the "sometimes" \u2014 it depended on how long you lingered. Holding to drag now takes half a second, the same as press-and-hold everywhere else on your phone. Dragging items to folders is unchanged apart from that extra beat.'],t.s)
B.b2Y=new A.v(693,B.ap3)
B.an3=s(['FOUND IT: THE APP WAS KILLING YOUR OWN FLING. Your diagnosis was exactly right \u2014 the swipe brings the scroll to a stop, sometimes. Here is why: when a new item arrived while you were mid-glide (a sync, an AI title, a fresh capture), the app nudged the scroll position to keep your place. That nudge cancels the glide outright \u2014 the list does not slow down, it stops dead. "Sometimes" because it only happened when something landed during that particular swipe. Now nothing may move the list while you are moving it: your finger owns the position, period. The place-keeping still works when your hand is off.'],t.s)
B.b_S=new A.v(692,B.an3)
B.adG=s(["THE SWIPE ITSELF WAS THE PROBLEM \u2014 YOUR HUNCH WAS RIGHT. Two things I had added were firing in the gaps BETWEEN your swipes: the app rebuilt the entire list at the end of every single scroll (to release a hold that keeps rows from shifting under your finger) even when nothing had changed, and its one-time measuring pass was re-running every time you left the screen and came back. Both are gone: the release is free when nothing moved, and the measuring happens once per app start, full stop. The guessing this arc has been chasing is already fixed \u2014 the measurements now show none of it \u2014 so what was left really was my own scaffolding landing under your thumb."],t.s)
B.b_I=new A.v(691,B.adG)
B.aq6=s(["THE SCROLLING SEESAW ENDS. Both phones were finally telling the same story: whatever part of the list is not measured yet gets GUESSED, and the guess keeps getting corrected under your finger \u2014 that is the drag at the end on the Fold and the roughness on the S23. One setting could never suit both, because the S23 wraps text harder, so the same items make a much taller list on that screen. Now there is no setting to get wrong: the list opens with a small runway so it appears instantly, then a moment later \u2014 while you are reading the top, never during a scroll \u2014 it quietly measures the whole stack. After that there is nothing left to guess, on either phone."],t.s)
B.aV3=new A.v(690,B.aq6)
B.att=s(["SCROLLING: SPLITTING THE DIFFERENCE, WITH MEASUREMENTS. The last build cured the S23 by taking back what had cured the Fold \u2014 the two phones wanted opposite things, because the real cost is per ROW, and the S23 was spending several times longer building each one. Removing the swipe pane made every row lighter for both, so the middle ground is back on trial: enough runway to cover your whole inbox, at a fraction of the burst that choked the S23. Both phones are logging as you scroll, so the next report settles it with numbers rather than another guess."],t.s)
B.aX8=new A.v(689,B.att)
B.agG=s(["SWIPE-TO-DELETE IS GONE from the main list. Deleting now happens where you mean it \u2014 the trash button on the box, or the \u22ef menu. A destructive action should not be one stray sideways finger away on the screen you scroll all day. Bonus: every row got lighter, since each one was carrying a swipe pane it never showed."],t.s)
B.aX7=new A.v(688,B.agG)
B.am1=s(["TAPPING DICTATE PUTS THE CURSOR IN THE BOX. You can see where your words are going, and if you want to type instead you can start immediately \u2014 no extra tap. The keyboard deliberately does NOT pop up: you pressed Dictate to talk, not to type, so the caret appears and the screen stays whole (tap the box itself and the keyboard opens as always). And if your cursor is already inside an item you are editing, Dictate leaves it there \u2014 your speech keeps going into that item, exactly as before."],t.s)
B.aUt=new A.v(687,B.am1)
B.ao3=s(["FIXED: SCROLLING STOPPING DEAD ON THE S23. Its own measurements caught what your Fold hid: the last scrolling change asked the app to measure your ENTIRE library in a single frame \u2014 one to two SECONDS of work on that phone, against thirteen milliseconds on the Fold. A fling runs on a clock, so a two-second frame burns the whole glide at once and the scroll simply stops. The app now prepares about three screens ahead instead of everything, which was always the safer answer for any phone that is not your fastest one.","FINISHING AN ITEM NO LONGER THROWS AWAY YOUR PLACE. Tapping \u2713 Done while scrolled down used to yank the list back to the top. The new item still goes in at the top \u2014 but the list stays exactly where you were, with the same words under your eye. (That jump existed so a fresh box would be visible; the dictate box has been pinned above the list for a while now, so it was only ever costing you your place.)"],t.s)
B.aSo=new A.v(686,B.ao3)
B.aqf=s(["CLICKING OUT LEAVES YOUR WORDS ALONE. Tapping an item while the dictate box has words no longer posts it. The box keeps your words and stays live \u2014 come back and it is exactly as you left it. Only \u2713 Done, Enter, saying \u201cnew note\u201d, and Send turn what is in the box into an item. An EMPTY box still evaporates when you click away.","AND THEY SURVIVE A CRASH. Because a note can now stay open indefinitely, the app quietly mirrors the open box to storage as you write; if it is ever killed \u2014 force-quit, battery, crash \u2014 the words are waiting in the box next time you open it.","CLEAR GETS AN UNDO. After you tap Clear, an Undo appears next to it for thirty seconds and puts every word back."],t.s)
B.b2L=new A.v(685,B.aqf)
B.apC=s(["SCROLLBAR, TRUED UP. With the thumb finally steady, one old flaw became visible: at the very top of the list the thumb rested a little low, while the bottom was right. The track was quietly reserving space for the phone\u2019s status bar \u2014 even though the list doesn\u2019t sit under it. The track now spans exactly the list it scrolls: top means top."],t.s)
B.b3W=new A.v(684,B.apC)
B.ath=s(["THE LAST STRETCH OF THE LIST. Round 11 ended the guessing for the first twenty thousand pixels of your inbox \u2014 your verdict: \u201cMUCH better\u201d \u2014 but your list turned out to be longer than the measured runway, so the far end still ran on guesses and the slowdown retreated to the tail. The runway now covers the whole list, whatever its length. Measuring only ever costs what your list actually contains, so short lists pay nothing extra."],t.s)
B.aS5=new A.v(683,B.ath)
B.aov=s(['GUESSING IS OVER. Round 10\u2019s counter caught the slowdown in the act: flinging past tall items, the list\u2019s idea of its own length was lurching by up to two thousand pixels at a time \u2014 twenty-six corrections in one two-second window. Every "slows down past big items" you felt was those corrections landing mid-glide. The cure: the list now measures essentially your whole inbox up front, so heights are FACTS, not guesses \u2014 no corrections, steady scrollbar, flings that land where your finger aimed. This is only affordable because earlier rounds made settled rows nearly free to keep around. The counter stays on: if it reads zero on your next scroll, this case is closed.'],t.s)
B.aYk=new A.v(682,B.aov)
B.alZ=s(["CHASING THE SLOWDOWN PAST BIG ITEMS. Your report narrowed it to one mechanism: the first time the list scrolls past a tall item it has never measured, it GUESSES the height, then corrects the guess mid-glide \u2014 tiny position fixes that feel like braking even at a perfect frame rate (and exactly why fully collapsed items feel smooth: uniform rows need no guessing). This build counts those corrections on your device as you scroll, and prepares rows even further ahead so first-time measuring happens beyond the screen instead of under your finger. If the counter confirms the story, the lasting fix is teaching the list every item\u2019s REAL height so it never guesses \u2014 that\u2019s the next piece."],t.s)
B.aRl=new A.v(681,B.alZ)
B.adT=s(["THE FREEZE YOU ASKED FOR \u2014 WORD FOR WORD. The measurements settled it: your latest choppy scroll had ZERO slow frames \u2014 the app was painting at full speed while things still felt off. What you were feeling was the CONTENT moving: titles, sync arrivals, and background saves landing mid-swipe and nudging rows under your finger. So, exactly as you prescribed: the moment a scroll starts, the list takes a snapshot and NOTHING may move a settled row while the window is in motion \u2014 everything that arrived lands in one clean update when your finger settles. The live dictation box stays live throughout; your words never wait."],t.s)
B.aT5=new A.v(680,B.adT)
B.afG=s(["CAUGHT IT \u2014 BY ITS ABSENCE. Round 7 proved no single step is slow: every stopwatch stayed under 25ms while the frame stalled at 204ms. The log\u2019s confession was elsewhere: while you dictate, the app was re-reading your ENTIRE library \u2014 five thousand items \u2014 NINE times in under three seconds, once per saved chunk. No step slow, but tens of thousands of throwaway objects per second, and the cleanup pauses from that churn are exactly the random stalls no stopwatch can catch. Fix: rapid-fire changes now coalesce into at most one library refresh every 0.3 seconds (the first one still lands instantly), and the list reuses one shared index instead of rebuilding lookup tables on every repaint. The churn drops several-fold; the stalls should go with it."],t.s)
B.b3L=new A.v(679,B.afG)
B.aqw=s(["THE HUNT, SHARPENED: round 6\u2019s stopwatches came back \u2014 all four suspects innocent (the worst was 14ms; the stall is 200+). So the net widens and tightens at once: the per-word pipeline that runs on every flicker of live speech now carries its own stopwatch, and ANY measured step that takes over 50ms is logged BY NAME with a timestamp the moment it happens, instead of hiding inside a two-second average. The next stall signs its work."],t.s)
B.aZg=new A.v(678,B.aqw)
B.aow=s(["THE HUNT NARROWS: round 5\u2019s numbers cleared the list itself \u2014 its worst rebuild is now 13ms \u2014 but caught a single frame elsewhere burning 283ms while dictating. Four named suspects now carry their own stopwatches: re-reading the whole library after each save, weaving a finished chunk into your text, the transcript cleanup pass, and the box\u2019s follow-the-words scroll. The next choppy moment writes their names and times to the log \u2014 the culprit gets fixed by name, not by hunch."],t.s)
B.aXX=new A.v(677,B.aow)
B.aiA=s(["SCROLLING, ROUND FIVE \u2014 FOUND THE TRIGGER. All night the fixes made each refresh cheaper, but the real problem was HOW OFTEN refreshes fire: the whole list redraws on every flicker of the live transcript \u2014 several times per second while the mic is on \u2014 a trigger none of the earlier holds could see. Now a settled item is handed back as the exact same object when nothing about it changed, and the app skips it entirely: those rapid ticks redraw only the live box. Also added: the diagnostics now count how often the list rebuilds and what it costs, so the next report names the culprit with numbers instead of a hunch."],t.s)
B.b1x=new A.v(676,B.aiA)
B.akc=s(["A MOVING LIST IS ALWAYS CATCHABLE. The last surface that could steal a scroll was the text of the item you are actively editing \u2014 land a finger there while the list was gliding and the text field grabbed the drag. Now, while the list is in motion, even that field yields: your finger catches and moves the LIST, and the field goes back to scrolling its own text the moment the list settles."],t.s)
B.aTR=new A.v(675,B.akc)
B.agQ=s(["SCROLLING, ROUND FOUR \u2014 THE BIG ONE. The measurements showed round 3 helped the averages but not the spikes, which pointed at the last standing cost: every screen refresh was constructing the FULL visual anatomy of every item in your inbox \u2014 hundreds of items, borders, swipe panes, text processing and all \u2014 then keeping only the handful on screen. Now each row builds itself, lazily, only when it actually scrolls into view \u2014 and things like the cursor ring or selecting an item repaint just that one row instead of the whole list. This is the same idea as your suggestion tonight: while the window moves, settled rows are left alone."],t.s)
B.aZn=new A.v(674,B.agQ)
B.asD=s(["SCROLLING, ROUND THREE \u2014 THE INSTRUMENTS FOUND TWO REAL COSTS. First: every row was re-deriving the ENTIRE sub-item map \u2014 and rows with sub-items built an index of your whole library \u2014 every single time it repainted. That work is now done once and shared by all rows, so a row costs a lookup instead of a rebuild of everything. Second: the list was preparing three screens of rows ahead of your finger, and any change mid-scroll re-prepared ALL of them in one frame \u2014 that burst was the stutter the measurements kept showing. The runway is now sized so the burst fits inside a frame. Same look, same behavior, less work per frame."],t.s)
B.aWi=new A.v(673,B.asD)
B.air=s(["SMOOTHER SCROLLING, GUIDED BY THE INSTRUMENTS: your phone\u2019s own frame measurements pointed at one culprit \u2014 every long item was laying out its ENTIRE text just to show ten lines, and those layout spikes were the stutter. Rows now hand layout only what can possibly show. The full text is untouched \u2014 open any item and it\u2019s all there."],t.s)
B.b4c=new A.v(672,B.air)
B.amc=s(["CHASING THE CHOPPY SCROLLING WITH INSTRUMENTS INSTEAD OF GUESSES: the app now measures its own frame times as you scroll and quietly records where any stutter comes from \u2014 the app\u2019s own layout work, or the phone\u2019s renderer. Costs nothing while things are smooth. One choppy scroll from you and the diagnostics log tells us which fix is the real one."],t.s)
B.b0C=new A.v(671,B.amc)
B.aoV=s(["SMOOTHER, STEADIER SCROLLING. Three changes under the hood: list refreshes now WAIT until your finger stops moving instead of landing mid-swipe and re-measuring every row under it; rows are prepared well ahead of the screen so a fast fling doesn\u2019t hitch on each new item; and the live task/meeting boxes no longer grab your swipe \u2014 a drag over them moves the whole list, with their newest words still following along by themselves."],t.s)
B.aUV=new A.v(670,B.aoV)
B.aq9=s(["THE DICTATION BOX REALLY SCROLLS NOW. The box was showing a fixed window onto your text \u2014 it could be aimed at the newest words, but it could not be SWIPED, and the aiming itself kept misfiring. It is a true scrolling view now, the same kind the task and meeting boxes have always used: the newest line stays anchored at the bottom while you talk, and you can swipe up any time to read back through everything. No measuring, no guessing \u2014 the anchoring is physics."],t.s)
B.b3z=new A.v(669,B.aq9)
B.atL=s(["THE BOX SCROLLS UP WHEN IT FILLS. Once your words pass the eighth line, the box now follows wherever they are landing \u2014 including when your cursor sits in the MIDDLE of the text. Dictated words were being placed correctly but the view never chased the cursor below the eighth line, so the newest words landed out of sight. The view follows the cursor\u2019s line now; the cursor itself is never moved while you edit."],t.s)
B.aVz=new A.v(668,B.atL)
B.acj=s(["THE INBOX SHOWS ITS COUNT \u2014 [n] on the right of the Inbox row in the left pane, in the same style as the type rows under it, always live.","THE DICTATION BOX BREATHES NOW: it rests at two lines, grows with your words up to eight, and after you send it starts fresh at two again. Past eight lines your newest words stay in view and the older ones scroll off the top.","SMOOTHER LIST SCROLLING OVER BIG ITEMS: swiping across an item whose text is longer than its box used to spend the first part of your swipe scrolling the text INSIDE the item before the list would move. At rest, items no longer grab the swipe at all \u2014 the whole window scrolls in one motion. The text inside an item still scrolls when you are actually in it editing, where your cursor needs to travel."],t.s)
B.aVO=new A.v(667,B.acj)
B.afT=s(["FIXED: AI TITLES VANISHING SECONDS AFTER THEY APPEARED. While your cursor was in a box, the app held back list refreshes \u2014 and when the cursor left, it replayed the held one. But that replay was a snapshot from BEFORE your title arrived, so it painted the title right back off. The app now re-reads the latest data instead of replaying anything old, so what lands stays.","FIXED: TAPPING THE DICTATION BOX SOMETIMES DID NOTHING until you had clicked an item first. An earlier \u201coptimization\u201d of mine skipped part of the tap\u2019s work when no item held the cursor \u2014 turns out that part was carrying the hand-off. It runs every time again."],t.s)
B.aTl=new A.v(666,B.afT)
B.apA=s(["FIXED: THE KEYBOARD CLOSED AFTER YOUR FIRST TYPED CHARACTER. The last build drew the empty-box instructions as a layer that existed only while the box was empty \u2014 so your first letter tore that layer down, and the typing area was rebuilt out from under your keyboard. The layer is permanent now and just goes invisible when words exist. Typing is back to normal."],t.s)
B.aRi=new A.v(665,B.apA)
B.aew=s(["NO MORE \u201cSPLIT IN 2 ITEMS\u201d WHEN YOU SAY NEW PARAGRAPH. That offer exists for pasted documents \u2014 several notes arriving at once. Since the blank-line fix, saying \u201cnew paragraph\u201d looked like a paste to it. It now watches HOW the paragraphs arrived, not how the text looks: only a real paste \u2014 a whole document landing in one jump \u2014 brings the offer up.","TAPPING AN EMPTY DICTATION BOX NO LONGER SHIFTS. The instructions and the typing area used to be two different pieces of text that swapped places when you tapped in. They are one piece now, drawn in the same spot whether you are typing or not.","AND THE TWO-LINE JUMP IS GONE: with text exactly the height of the box, the app could not decide whether it overflowed, and the answer flipped as you tapped around \u2014 changing which line you saw. It now requires half a line of REAL overflow before anything moves, and text always wraps in whole lines, so the answer can never sit on the fence again."],t.s)
B.b2o=new A.v(664,B.aew)
B.apX=s(["THE TEXT NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU TAP THE DICTATION BOX. Before you tap, the box shows the END of your words. Tapping handed over to a real text field that started from the top \u2014 same words, different place, which is what you were seeing move. The field now opens exactly where you were already looking, with the cursor at the end."],t.s)
B.aZ_=new A.v(663,B.apX)
B.ajw=s(["TAPPING THE DICTATION BOX IS QUIETER. The last build told your items to release the cursor every single time you tapped the box \u2014 even when none of them had it. That was needless work on the control you touch most. It now only speaks up when an item actually holds the cursor."],t.s)
B.b0k=new A.v(662,B.ajw)
B.at9=s(["SMOOTHER SCROLLING, AND NO MORE TEXT JUMPING WHEN THE KEYBOARD OPENS. The dictation box was re-measuring its text on every single frame \u2014 every frame of a scroll, and every frame of the keyboard sliding in and out. That is why the words shifted when the keyboard appeared and shifted back when it left, and part of why the list felt like it was fighting you. It now measures only when the words themselves change, so nothing can re-settle while the screen is already moving."],t.s)
B.aXu=new A.v(661,B.at9)
B.amR=s(["THE GRID NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU TOUCH SOMETHING. While your cursor is in a box, Voicley holds back any rebuild of your item list so it cant yank the page under you \u2014 but those held rebuilds were piling up and then all landing at once the next time you tapped or deleted something, which is why the grid seemed to shift for no reason. The held rebuild now lands when you STOP typing, a moment where a little movement is expected, instead of ambushing your next tap."],t.s)
B.aTq=new A.v(660,B.amR)
B.atn=s(["THE \u2922 EXPAND BUTTON IS GONE FROM THE DICTATION BOX. Opening a note full-size is something you want for a note you have finished, not for the couple of lines you are still saying \u2014 and it is one tap away the moment the note posts."],t.s)
B.aSq=new A.v(659,B.atn)
B.af8=s(["TAPPING THE DICTATION BOX NOW TAKES THE CURSOR BACK. If you had clicked into one of your items, the cursor got stuck there \u2014 tapping the dictation box did nothing, and the only way out was collapsing the item. The box now asks the item to let go first, the same way saying \u201cnew note\u201d always has."],t.s)
B.aST=new A.v(658,B.af8)
B.ag6=s(["THE APP NOW CHECKS ITS OWN UPDATER AT STARTUP. Tonight\u2019s \u201cupdate didn\u2019t start\u201d was a missing piece of plumbing that nothing noticed until you pressed the button three times. Voicley now tests that piece quietly every launch, and if it is ever missing again it says so right next to the Check for updates button \u2014 with what to do about it \u2014 instead of letting you find out the hard way."],t.s)
B.aSl=new A.v(657,B.ag6)
B.aeY=s(["FIXED: \u201cUPDATE DIDN\u2019T START\u201d. The in-app updater was downloading the new version perfectly and then failing silently at the very last step \u2014 handing the file to Android to install. A piece of the app\u2019s plumbing had gone missing from recent builds, so the install screen never opened. This build is a clean rebuild with that plumbing restored, and updates work from here on."],t.s)
B.aYc=new A.v(656,B.aeY)
B.ahO=s(["THE AUTO-MEETING IS GONE FOR GOOD. A dictation that ran past a few minutes used to turn itself into a Meeting \u2014 and because a meeting owns the live text, your dictation box would silently disappear mid-session. That is what was happening when the box \u201cvanished\u201d. Nothing was ever lost; your words carried on into the meeting. The whole feature is now removed, along with its setting.","If you WANT a meeting, say \u201cnew meeting\u201d or make one from the left \u2014 the app just will not decide it for you any more."],t.s)
B.aZ0=new A.v(655,B.ahO)
B.ahS=s(["THE DICTATION BOX IS DOWN TO TWO LINES \u2014 as small as it can get while still showing the line you are speaking and the one before it, which is what lets you notice a wrong word as it lands. Everything else on screen is your items.","At that height the two command hints no longer fit, so the empty box just says \u201cPress Dictate or type in box\u201d. The commands are still listed in Settings."],t.s)
B.aU3=new A.v(654,B.ahS)
B.aka=s(["YOUR WORDS START AT THE TOP OF THE BOX AGAIN. Since the box became a fixed height, short text was sitting at the BOTTOM of it with empty space above \u2014 so it looked like your dictation started halfway down. It now starts at the top and grows downward like any editor, and only once it fills the box does it stick to the bottom so your newest words stay in view.","THE DICTATION BOX IS SMALLER \u2014 three lines instead of seven, giving most of the screen back to your list of items.","THE INSTRUCTIONS IN THE EMPTY BOX ARE TWICE THE SIZE, and shorter: \u201cPress Dictate or type in box\u201d, \u201cSay \u2018new note\u2019\u201d, \u201cSay \u2018new task\u2019\u201d. It says Dictate now, matching the button."],t.s)
B.b3c=new A.v(653,B.aka)
B.apg=s(["BIGGER BUTTONS: Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate are a quarter taller, and so is the \u2713 Done button \u2014 easier to hit without giving back the space we saved.","A QUIETER DICTATION BOX: the date and time are gone from the top of it (you know when you are talking; every saved item still keeps its own timestamp), and the \u201c\u2192 project name\u201d label is gone from the bottom left. The line above the box still speaks up when your words are heading somewhere you would NOT expect \u2014 which is the only time it was telling you anything.","NO MORE COMMAND CHIPS WHILE YOU TALK: the \u201cnew note\u201d / \u201cnew task\u201d / \u201cend dictation\u201d buttons no longer appear at the top when dictation starts. They showed up exactly when you were mid-sentence and pushed the box you were watching down the screen. The empty box now teaches those commands before you start, which is when you can actually read them."],t.s)
B.aWZ=new A.v(652,B.apg)
B.akW=s(["SAYING \u201cNEW PARAGRAPH\u201d NOW SHOWS THE BLANK LINE IMMEDIATELY. The paragraph was always being saved correctly \u2014 you just couldn\u2019t SEE it until your next word arrived, because an empty last line had nothing in it to draw. Now the gap appears the moment you say it, with the cursor sitting on it.","THE EMPTY DICTATION BOX TELLS YOU WHAT TO DO: press Dictate or type in the box, then talk. It also names the two commands worth knowing on day one \u2014 \u201cnew note\u201d to finish this one and start another, and \u201cnew task\u201d to make a task instead. It all disappears the instant your first word lands."],t.s)
B.b1Z=new A.v(651,B.akW)
B.abg=s(["THE DICTATION BOX IS A FIXED SIZE NOW \u2014 SEVEN LINES, EMPTY OR FULL. It used to grow from one line upward as you spoke, which pushed everything below it down the screen right when you started talking. Now it is already the size it is going to be, so nothing moves when your first words land.","SCROLLING THROUGH A LONG ITEM NO LONGER DEAD-ENDS: if an item is tall enough to scroll inside itself, reaching its top or bottom used to stop your finger cold \u2014 you had to lift off and swipe again to move the list. Now the leftover motion carries straight through into the list, so one swipe keeps going from inside the item out into the rest of your notes. (The live recording box has worked this way since July; saved items just never got it.)"],t.s)
B.aZW=new A.v(650,B.abg)
B.ag3=s(["THE DICTATION BOX CAN\u2019T DISAPPEAR ANY MORE: it used to be one more entry in the same list as your items, so every time that list rebuilt itself \u2014 and while your phone catches up on old recordings it rebuilds constantly \u2014 the box could vanish until something forced a repaint. Pulling down to refresh \u201cfixed\u201d it because refreshing repaints. The box now lives ABOVE the list instead of inside it, where nothing that happens to your items can touch it. It also stays put while the list scrolls under it.","Your words were never at risk in any of this \u2014 the box going missing was a drawing problem, not a saving one."],t.s)
B.b3l=new A.v(649,B.ag3)
B.as6=s(["THE COMMAND BUTTONS ARE HALF THE HEIGHT: Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate now put their icon BESIDE the word instead of above it, so the control is a slim bar across the top of the dictation box rather than a row of tiles. Same words, same full-width tap targets \u2014 half the space.","THE APP NOW TELLS YOU WHEN IT\u2019S USING A WORSE MICROPHONE: if Voicley\u2019s own dictation engine can\u2019t start, it quietly switches to the one built into your browser or phone so you still get words. That switch used to be invisible \u2014 which is why dictating in a browser looked like the app was broken, when really it was your browser\u2019s speech recognition dropping and repeating words. Now it says so, and in a browser it points you at the installed app, which has the full engine."],t.s)
B.aTX=new A.v(648,B.as6)
B.akJ=s(["THE SCREEN HOLDS STILL WHILE YOU TALK: Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate moved to the TOP of the dictation box. They were underneath it, so every new line you spoke pushed the buttons \u2014 and everything else \u2014 further down the screen. Now your words grow away from the controls instead of through them, and the buttons stay exactly where your thumb left them.","AND THE BOX STOPS GROWING: the dictation box now tops out at six lines instead of ten. Past that it keeps your NEWEST words in view and lets the older ones scroll off the top, so your list of items never gets shoved down the screen mid-sentence. Nothing is trimmed \u2014 tap into the box, or open the item, to see all of it.","THE BOX YOU TALK INTO LOOKS LIKE A TOOL: it now sits raised off the page, and a quiet \u201cEARLIER\u201d line separates it from the items below. One is the thing that makes notes; the rest are notes you already made.","THE ITEM MIC MOVED ONTO ITS BAR: when you click into an item, its \u201cDictate here\u201d button now sits on the item\u2019s coloured top bar instead of under the text \u2014 so turning it on no longer makes the item taller and nudges everything below it."],t.s)
B.b00=new A.v(647,B.akJ)
B.agU=s(["PRESSING DICTATE IS SAFE AGAIN \u2014 REALLY THIS TIME: v645 fixed one cause of the grey screen; testing on a real device found a second. The animated red dot I\u2019d put on the Dictate button couldn\u2019t survive the button moving into the dictation box, and it crashed the display when your words arrived. The dot is gone: the Dictate button simply turns RED while the mic is live, heating toward orange as you speak. Same signal, nothing to break.","LIGHTER ITEM BARS: the coloured bar across the top of each item is half as strong, so it still tells you the type at a glance without competing with your words.","QUIETER EDITING: two internal errors that fired when you clicked out of an item being edited are fixed. Your text always saved correctly \u2014 these only made noise in the logs."],t.s)
B.b09=new A.v(646,B.agU)
B.ajp=s(["FIXED: PRESSING DICTATE GREYED OUT THE WHOLE SCREEN. Two builds ago I moved the red \u201cmic on\u201d marker onto the Dictate button, and to do that I had the floating red pill ask which screen you were on \u2014 but that pill sits outside the part of the app that knows the answer, so asking crashed the display the instant recording started. It now gets told by the screen instead of asking. Your recording and your items were never affected \u2014 only the drawing stopped."],t.s)
B.aTp=new A.v(645,B.ajp)
B.arc=s(["ONE BOX, AND IT OWNS THE MIC: \u201cSay or type your text here\u201d is now inside the real dictation box \u2014 the separate card above it is gone. And Off \xb7 Commands only \xb7 Dictate moved INSIDE that box too, just under \u2713 Done. The box that takes your words now holds the controls that steer them, so they can\u2019t drift apart.","A MIC ON THE ITEM YOU\u2019RE EDITING: click into any item\u2019s text and a mic button appears under it. Tap it and your dictation goes into THAT item; tap it again and your words go back to the new-note box \u2014 the mic doesn\u2019t stop, it moves. Dictating into an existing item is now something you press, not something a stray cursor decides for you."],t.s)
B.aX3=new A.v(644,B.arc)
B.ag_=s(["LESS ON THE MAIN SCREEN: Notes, Tasks, Meetings and Claude now live on the LEFT, right under Inbox \u2014 each with its count in brackets and a \uff0b to make one. Tap a row to see just that type. The filter row and the create buttons are gone from the inbox screen entirely, so it\u2019s now the dictation box and your items, nothing else.","THE BOX SAYS WHAT IT\u2019S FOR: \u201cSay or type your text here\u201d in faded text, and talking simply makes a new note \u2014 no label above it announcing that. The \u201c\u2192 New note\u201d line under the controls is gone too; it only appears now when your words are going somewhere you might NOT expect, like into an existing item or when the mic is in commands-only mode."],t.s)
B.b36=new A.v(643,B.ag_)
B.aeH=s(["FIXED: ONLY ONE ITEM SHOWING, WITH A GREY BLOCK BELOW IT. A change I made two builds ago put an internal marker on every row in the list; those markers collide when the list redraws, and the rest of the list fails to draw \u2014 which a release build paints as a plain grey box. That\u2019s why you could only reach one item and nothing would scroll, on Windows and in the browser alike. The markers are gone. Nothing was ever wrong with your items \u2014 they were all there the whole time, just not being drawn."],t.s)
B.aUT=new A.v(642,B.aeH)
B.ak5=s(["THE RED \u201cMIC ON\u201d MARKER MOVED INTO THE DICTATE BUTTON: instead of a floating red pill hovering over the main screen, the Dictate button itself now wears the pulsing red dot while the microphone is live. The floating pill still appears on every OTHER screen \u2014 Settings, a folder, the reader \u2014 because there the button isn\u2019t visible and that pill is the only thing that can tell you the mic is still hot."],t.s)
B.aSR=new A.v(641,B.ak5)
B.ag1=s(["GESTURES NOW FOLLOW YOUR POINTER, NOT YOUR DEVICE LABEL: the app used to guess how you were touching it from what kind of machine it thought it was on \u2014 which is wrong in a browser, where a tablet in desktop mode claims to be a PC. That guess is what made folders unscrollable on your tablet. It now watches what\u2019s actually touching the screen: a finger gets touch behaviour, a mouse gets mouse behaviour, and a browser assumes touch until it sees otherwise. Dragging a folder with a real mouse is instant again, while a finger swipe scrolls."],t.s)
B.b1q=new A.v(640,B.ag1)
B.afb=s(["FOLDERS SCROLL BY FINGER IN THE BROWSER \u2014 THE REAL FIX: v638 wasn\u2019t enough. Every folder row was set up so a straight up-or-down drag picks the folder up to reorder it \u2014 safe with a mouse (the wheel scrolls) but on a tablet that IS your scroll gesture, so the list never got it. In the browser, reordering a folder now needs a short hold first, which leaves plain finger swipes free to scroll.","THE DICTATE BUTTON IS THE MIC METER: the button now breathes with your voice \u2014 brighter and hotter the louder you speak \u2014 and turns red saying \u201cMic lost\u201d if the microphone is taken by another app. The bar meter and \u201cMic active\u201d strip underneath are gone, giving that space back to your items.","ONE PLACE TO CREATE: the \uff0b has left the filter row. Making a new note, task, meeting or Claude message now lives only in the dictation box at the top \u2014 one door instead of two."],t.s)
B.aZ8=new A.v(639,B.afb)
B.arr=s(["SCROLLING WITH YOUR FINGER WORKS IN THE BROWSER: on a large Android tablet, Chrome reports finger touches as a MOUSE \u2014 and the web app only drag-scrolled for touch, so lists like the folder pane simply refused to move under your finger. The web app now drag-scrolls for every kind of pointer. Phone, Windows and iOS builds are unchanged."],t.s)
B.aSf=new A.v(638,B.arr)
B.apT=s(["\u201cNEW PARAGRAPH\u201d NOW MAKES A REAL BLANK LINE: while you\u2019re dictating into an existing item, saying \u201cnew paragraph\u201d gave you a single line break \u2014 you had to close the item to see the blank line appear. The open item now shows the blank line as you speak it, exactly like the closed one. Paragraphs are still saved the same way underneath, so OneNote keeps splitting them correctly."],t.s)
B.b2n=new A.v(637,B.apT)
B.ami=s(["THE MAIN SCREEN NOW READS TOP-DOWN: Dictate/Commands/Off has moved to the TOP, with the destination chip and status line right under it, then the composer, then your filters and items. Everything that explains what the app is doing now sits where your eyes land first instead of at the bottom edge.","THE COMPOSER IS A REAL CARD: \u201cTalk or type your next item\u2026\u201d now sits in its own framed box with the \uff0b Note \xb7 Task \xb7 Meeting \xb7 Claude buttons on its footer \u2014 the visible birthplace of items. It glows with an accent border while your words are heading into a new item."],t.s)
B.aXN=new A.v(636,B.ami)
B.aoq=s(["YOU CAN ALWAYS SEE WHERE YOUR WORDS ARE GOING: a destination chip now sits right above the Dictate/Commands/Off control and always answers the one question that matters \u2014 \u201c\u2192 New note\u201d, \u201c\u2192 \u2018Cash flow ideas\u2019\u201d when you\u2019re adding to an existing item, or \u201ccommands only\u201d. Beside it, one plain sentence says what the app is doing right now.","THE ITEM YOU\u2019RE DICTATING INTO SAYS SO: when your words are going into an existing item, that item\u2019s colored bar reads \u201cdictating here\u201d (and \u201cpaused here\u201d in commands mode). Dictating into an existing item was always possible \u2014 now it\u2019s obvious.","CREATING AND FILTERING NO LONGER LOOK ALIKE: the counts row is labelled VIEW and is purely a filter. Creating lives on its own strip below it \u2014 \u201cTalk or type your next item\u2026\u201d with \uff0b Note \xb7 Task \xb7 Meeting \xb7 Claude pills. Tap the line or a pill to start one; the \uff0b menu still works exactly as before.","THE APP TEACHES ITSELF: an empty inbox now shows the loop in three lines (turn on \u2192 talk \u2192 say \u201cnew note\u201d between thoughts), and while you dictate, small hints show the voice commands. The hints retire themselves after a few sessions, or immediately if you tap the \u2715."],t.s)
B.aXt=new A.v(635,B.aoq)
B.am5=s(["SELECTED COLLAPSED ITEMS AREN\u2019T DARK ANYMORE: selecting a collapsed item used to tint the whole row a murky blue \u2014 the selection outline was showing through the row\u2019s translucent bar. The bar now goes solid while selected, so you get a crisp blue outline and the row keeps its normal brightness."],t.s)
B.aWD=new A.v(634,B.am5)
B.ahr=s(["CREATE BUTTONS NOW OWN YOUR WORDS: on web and Windows, pressing \u201cnew task\u201d or \u201cnew note\u201d while your cursor sat inside an existing item could send your dictation into THAT item \u2014 and then deliver the same words a second time when the unit closed. Pressing a create button now releases the old item\u2019s claim and pulls the cursor out, so the box you just created is always where your words land. Clicking into an item to dictate there still works exactly as before."],t.s)
B.aXf=new A.v(633,B.ahr)
B.al2=s(["ONE HANDLE EVERYWHERE \u2014 THE CIRCLES ARE GONE: Windows and web now work like mobile. Every item\u2019s colored bar shows the grip dots and is the handle: drag it to file or reorder, double-click to open. Boxes get the full width the circle column used to take.","CLICK A BAR TO SELECT: clicking an item\u2019s bar selects it (the whole box outlines in blue). Ctrl+click adds or removes one item; SHIFT+CLICK selects everything between your last click and this one, just like Windows Explorer; Ctrl+Shift+click adds that range. On the phone, taps on bars now toggle items in and out of the selection \u2014 multi-select is back on the main screen. Headphones, the fold arrow, and the other bar buttons keep doing their own jobs.","COLLAPSED ITEMS \u2014 CLICK TO SELECT, CLICK AGAIN TO RENAME: the first click on a collapsed item selects it; click the title of an already-selected item to edit it in place \u2014 the same rhythm as renaming a file in Explorer. Double-click still expands."],t.s)
B.aRS=new A.v(632,B.al2)
B.arm=s(["AUTO-MEETING MOVED TO ADVANCED: the \u201cAuto-meeting after N minutes\u201d setting now lives in Settings \u2192 ADVANCED with its own card, next to segmentation \u2014 it\u2019s a set-once option, so the main settings stay lean. Behavior unchanged."],t.s)
B.aU1=new A.v(631,B.arm)
B.agT=s(["PLAYING A FOLDER STARTS AT THE TOP: playback used an internal stored order instead of the order on your screen, so a folder you\u2019d never hand-reordered could start reading a couple of items down. Folder play now follows the screen exactly \u2014 newest-first by default, your dragged order once you\u2019ve reordered \u2014 subfolders first, then items, every folder by its own setting.","THE LISTENING PANEL\u2019S JUMP LANDS ON THE ITEM: tapping the panel to go to what\u2019s playing could drop you at the TOP of the main screen when the item sat far down the inbox. The list now travels to the item wherever it is and settles right on the glowing box."],t.s)
B.aXP=new A.v(630,B.agT)
B.abM=s(["PLAYING THE INBOX FOLLOWS ALONG: the main screen now tracks the reader the same way folders do \u2014 the item being read glows blue and the list scrolls to keep it in view as playback moves down your inbox. Works for any playback that lands on an inbox item, not just the \u25b6 button.","THE LISTENING AREA\u2019S TOP PANEL ALWAYS TAKES YOU TO WHAT\u2019S PLAYING: tapping the panel already jumped to the item when it was part of your listening list \u2014 now, when the reader is playing something that isn\u2019t in that list (the inbox, or a folder played directly), the tap takes you to where the item actually lives: its folder, or the main screen for an inbox item, landing right on the glowing box."],t.s)
B.aTc=new A.v(629,B.abM)
B.adN=s(["DELETING WHAT\u2019S PLAYING NOW STOPS IT: listening to a folder and deleting that folder used to keep right on playing from a snapshot. The reader now watches the library \u2014 delete the folder you\u2019re listening to and playback stops; delete a single item mid-listen and it skips to the next one. Unrelated changes (titles, listen flags) never interrupt playback.","PLAY THE INBOX: a \u25b6 button at the top-right of the main screen reads your inbox top-down \u2014 the list exactly as you see it, newest first. Same rules as playing a folder: it ends a live dictation first, loops at the end, and plays each item\u2019s recording (or reads it aloud when there\u2019s no audio)."],t.s)
B.aRF=new A.v(628,B.adN)
B.ahs=s(["YOUR OLD UNFILED NOTES ARE IN ONE PLACE NOW: the flood of items that appeared after the visibility fix turned out to be weeks of real notes an old filter had been silently hiding. They\u2019ve all been filed into a new \u201cBacklog\u201d folder at the bottom of your folder list \u2014 nothing deleted, everything searchable, review them whenever you like. Your inbox is back to just current items.","EVERY NOTE HAS A PROPER TYPE AGAIN: nearly all of your items had been saved with a blank internal type (the hidden cause of this whole episode). All 3,800+ have been repaired to proper notes \u2014 so the Notes/Tasks/Meetings chips count them correctly \u2014 and new items are always typed from now on."],t.s)
B.b0m=new A.v(627,B.ahs)
B.aqO=s(["THE EMPTY \u201cTRANSCRIBING\u2026\u201d BOXES ARE GONE: yesterday\u2019s fix stopped real notes from being hidden, but it also surfaced some old empty shells \u2014 zero-length captures with nothing in them \u2014 showing a permanent \u201cTranscribing\u2026\u201d spinner. The inbox now decides what to show by CONTENT: anything with words shows, a recent capture still being transcribed shows, and a zero-length shell (nothing was ever spoken) doesn\u2019t appear. Nothing is deleted \u2014 real notes can never be hidden by this rule, because text always shows.","THE SPINNER STOPS LYING: a capture still waiting on its transcription after 10 minutes no longer shows a spinner as if work is happening \u2014 it says \u201cWaiting to transcribe \u2014 needs a connection\u201d with a clock. Honest, and your audio is safe the whole time; it transcribes when it gets a connection."],t.s)
B.aVg=new A.v(626,B.aqO)
B.aos=s(["FIXED: NEW ITEMS DISAPPEARING FROM THE INBOX. A note would show up when you hit Done and then quietly vanish a moment later, and the item count never went up. The inbox was only willing to display items whose internal type was one of four exact values \u2014 and items were being saved with a blank type, so the next background refresh rebuilt the list without them. Your words were never lost (they were on the server the whole time), but they were invisible. The inbox no longer filters by type at all: anything you capture shows up, and an unfamiliar type is simply drawn as a plain note."],t.s)
B.aTs=new A.v(625,B.aos)
B.af7=s(["VOICLEY NOW SUGGESTS WHERE A NOTE BELONGS: when a new note clearly fits one of your folders, its box shows a small chip in the bottom-left \u2014 \u201cStrategy \u203a Q3 ?\u201d. Tap it and the note files itself there. Tap the \u2715 and the suggestion is gone for good on that item. Ignore it and nothing happens. NOTHING EVER MOVES ON ITS OWN \u2014 the suggestion just sits there until you say so. It only ever suggests folders you already have, stays quiet when it isn\u2019t sure, and costs nothing extra: it rides along with the AI title call that already runs. Turn it off any time in Settings \u2192 \u201cSuggest folders for new items\u201d."],t.s)
B.aWu=new A.v(624,B.af7)
B.ad8=s(["DRAG AN ITEM LEFT TO REACH YOUR FOLDERS: hold an item by its colored bar and push it toward the LEFT EDGE of the screen \u2014 pause for a moment and the folder menu slides open, still carrying the item, ready to drop on any folder. The whole edge is the target, so there\u2019s nothing to aim at and no need to travel up to the \u2630 (which still works too). Carry the item back toward the list and the menu closes again."],t.s)
B.b3Q=new A.v(623,B.ad8)
B.adr=s(["SEARCH IS HERE: there\u2019s a Search row in the menu now. Type a word or two and every item carrying them appears \u2014 notes, tasks, meetings, anywhere in your library, including items you\u2019ve filed away in folders. Results show the matching sentence so you can see why each one turned up, and the type chips (All / Notes / Tasks / Meetings) narrow them down with counts. It searches on the device, so it\u2019s instant and works with no signal. Best match first, then newest \u2014 because with dictation, the recent one is usually the one you meant."],t.s)
B.b3H=new A.v(622,B.adr)
B.as9=s(["LONG ITEMS EDIT IN PLACE \u2014 NO MORE JUMP TO FULL SCREEN: tapping an item too long to fit used to fling you into the full-size view. Now it edits where it sits, like every other item: the box shows its ten lines and the text SCROLLS inside, so you can read and change anything past the cap without leaving the list. Adding text scrolls rather than growing the box. Full-size is still one double-tap of the item\u2019s colored bar away.","PAUSE ON A FOLDER WHILE DRAGGING AND IT OPENS: hold a dragged item \u2014 or another folder \u2014 over a folder in the left pane for half a second and its subfolders unfold, so you can drill in and drop into a child without ever putting the item down. Sweeping past a folder on the way somewhere else leaves it closed."],t.s)
B.aYH=new A.v(621,B.as9)
B.atj=s(["CARRY AN ITEM BACK TO THE LIST AND THE MENU CLOSES ITSELF: the drag now runs the menu in both directions. Pause an item on the \u2630 and the folder menu opens; carry it back over the list and the menu slides shut so you can keep dragging where you were. Reach your folders, change your mind, come back \u2014 one motion, no taps. (Both directions need a brief pause, so the menu never flickers open and shut while you move.)"],t.s)
B.aRv=new A.v(620,B.atj)
B.acY=s(["THE APP NO LONGER FREEZES ON A DRAG: v613\u2019s one-time ordering repair tried to renumber every item in your library in a single go \u2014 with 800+ items that locked the app up for as long as it took, with nothing on screen to say why. The repair now works in small batches: it fixes a slice per drag and finishes over a few drags, so nothing ever stalls. If you were stuck, force-closing and reopening was safe \u2014 nothing was lost."],t.s)
B.aXz=new A.v(619,B.acY)
B.agw=s(["DRAG AN ITEM TO THE \u2630 MENU TO FILE IT IN A FOLDER (phones): hold an item by its colored bar, drag it up onto the \u2630 at the top-left, and pause \u2014 the menu slides open while you\u2019re still holding the item, and you can drop it straight onto any folder to move it there. This is the piece that was missing on phones: your folders live inside that menu, and the menu was shut for the whole drag, so there was nothing to drop onto. Hold for about half a second (a quick brush past won\u2019t open it), and dragging away closes it again with nothing moved."],t.s)
B.aXp=new A.v(618,B.agw)
B.at0=s(["HOLDING AN ITEM NEAR THE BOTTOM NO LONGER RUNS THE LIST AWAY: picking up an item close to the top or bottom edge started scrolling the list on its own, before you\u2019d moved a finger. Those edges are scroll zones for when you\u2019re dragging something toward off-screen items \u2014 but they switched on the moment you lifted, and if your finger was already sitting in one, it just took off. They now wait until you actually drag toward the edge."],t.s)
B.aU2=new A.v(617,B.at0)
B.aqv=s(["THE \u201cIT GOES HERE\u201d LINE IS BACK \u2014 AND NOTHING FLASHES: v615 removed the drop bars along with the gaps that were shoving the list around, which left you dragging blind. A bright line now appears on the edge of the item you\u2019re hovering \u2014 along its top if the item will land above it, along its bottom if below \u2014 drawn inside the item, so still nothing shifts. The flash when dropping on Windows is gone too: hovering used to rebuild the row from scratch, and now it only changes colour."],t.s)
B.aXq=new A.v(616,B.aqv)
B.adB=s(["THE LIST NO LONGER JUMPS WHEN YOU PICK AN ITEM UP: holding an item used to shove the whole screen down a couple of lines, because a drop-gap opened between EVERY pair of items at once the moment the drag armed. Those gaps are gone. Instead, while you drag, the item you hover over lights up \u2014 drop on its top half to go above it, its bottom half to go below. Nothing shifts, the spacing stays tight, and the target is a whole item instead of a thin line."],t.s)
B.b2r=new A.v(615,B.adB)
B.apr=s(["HOLDING AN ITEM NO LONGER JERKS THE LIST AROUND: press-and-hold on the phone was starting TWO drags at once \u2014 the list trying to lift the row for a reorder, and the item\u2019s colored bar trying to lift it to move \u2014 and the two fought over your finger the whole time. Now the bar is the only thing that lifts: hold it, drag, and drop on the line between two items to place it there, or sideways onto a folder to file it. Smooth, one motion."],t.s)
B.aWc=new A.v(614,B.apr)
B.ae3=s(["ORDERING, PROPERLY FIXED THIS TIME: v612 made a drag write only the item you moved, but most items had never been given a place in the ordering at all \u2014 and a few older ones shared the exact same place. Items with no place always sort above the ones that have one, so a dropped item still jumped. Your first drag now quietly gives every item its own place, in the order they\u2019re already sitting on screen (so nothing visibly moves), and separates the duplicates. After that, drops stay put."],t.s)
B.b1g=new A.v(613,B.ae3)
B.ac8=s(["ITEMS NOW STAY WHERE YOU DROP THEM: reordering could land an item somewhere other than the spot you dropped it on \u2014 sometimes right away, sometimes only after the list refreshed. Saving a new order used to renumber every item on screen, which quietly clashed with the items NOT on screen (anything hidden by a Notes/Tasks/Meetings filter, filed into a folder, or brand new). Now a drag writes only the item you moved, slotted between its two neighbours \u2014 so nothing else shifts and the order holds, on every device."],t.s)
B.aUS=new A.v(612,B.ac8)
B.an1=s(["\u201cNEW NOTE\u201d ACTUALLY WORKS NOW WHEN THE ENGINE MIS-HEARS YOU: saying \u201cnew note\u201d while driving often came back from the speech engine as \u201cnew notes\u201d, \u201cno note\u201d, or \u201cno notes\u201d \u2014 and every one of those was typed into your note as words instead of starting a fresh one. All of those now start a new note, as do \u201cnext notes\u201d and \u201cnew dictations\u201d. Talking ABOUT a note (\u201cI put a new note in the drawer\u201d) still doesn\u2019t trigger it."],t.s)
B.aSJ=new A.v(611,B.an1)
B.aqk=s(["WINDOWS: YOU CAN DRAG AN ITEM BY ITS COLORED BAR TOO: v609 gave every item a tinted bar with a grip on it, but on the PC only the little circle actually dragged \u2014 so the bar looked grabbable and did nothing. Now grab either one: drag the bar sideways (or the circle, unchanged) to file an item into a folder. Dragging straight up or down still reorders, and click-dragging inside the text still selects text."],t.s)
B.b3s=new A.v(610,B.aqk)
B.atd=s(["WINDOWS: NEW ITEMS SHOW UP AGAIN (and the cursor stops slipping): on the PC the app was re-connecting to sync every few seconds, and each reconnect rebuilt the screen \u2014 so items you just typed didn\u2019t appear (they WERE saved and synced the whole time) and you had to click twice to get the cursor into a box. Reconnects are now limited to at most one a minute, so the list stays put and the first click lands.","REORDERING BY BAR-DRAG IS EASIER TO HIT ON PHONES: while you hold-drag an item by its colored bar, the drop lines that open between the other items are now twice as tall on the phone \u2014 drop on one to place the item exactly there. (Sideways onto a folder still files it; same one drag, you choose by where you drop.)"],t.s)
B.b1f=new A.v(609,B.atd)
B.ar6=s(["NEW BOX DESIGN ON PHONES \u2014 THE COLORED TOP BAR IS THE HANDLE: every item box now has a lightly tinted top bar in its type\u2019s color (blue note, green task, amber meeting, clay Claude) holding the type, the date/time on the right, the headphones, and the collapse arrow. HOLD the bar and drag to move the item \u2014 into a folder, the Trash, or a new spot in the list. DOUBLE-TAP the bar to open the item full-size (the little \u2922 corner button is gone). A single tap on the bar does nothing, so no more accidental opens. The small circle handle is gone on phones; on Windows and the web it stays exactly as before."],t.s)
B.aXT=new A.v(608,B.ar6)
B.an0=s(["OPENING THE APP NO LONGER STARTS RECORDING ON ITS OWN: the app used to begin capturing the moment you opened it. It now stays quiet on open \u2014 start dictation yourself with the mic when you\u2019re ready. (If you liked open-and-record, Settings still has a toggle to turn it back on.)","MOVING AN ITEM TO A FOLDER ALWAYS TAKES IT OFF THE INBOX NOW: the full-size item\u2019s \u201c\u2026\u201d menu had two look-alike options \u2014 \u201cMove to Folder\u2026\u201d and a leftover \u201cMove to Project\u2026.\u201d Only the first actually removed the item from the inbox; the second quietly left it on the main screen. That confusing legacy option is gone, so \u201cMove to Folder\u2026\u201d is the one move \u2014 and it always clears the item off the inbox into its folder."],t.s)
B.aV9=new A.v(607,B.an0)
B.ahw=s(["AUDIO PLAYS ON iPHONE AGAIN: opening an item\u2019s full view and pressing play on iPhone was failing with \u201ccould not load audio (-1002).\u201d The full-window player was handing iOS a file path the wrong way (iOS is stricter than Android about it); it now loads the file correctly. This also makes your Windows-recorded audio playable on the iPhone."],t.s)
B.b0I=new A.v(606,B.ahw)
B.agz=s(["TAP ONCE TO EDIT AN ITEM: tapping into an item\u2019s text used to need two taps \u2014 the first one got swallowed. The trace showed a background rebuild dropping the cursor a split-second after the first tap; now the cursor re-grabs itself and holds, so one tap lands you in the field ready to type.","DRAG A SUBFOLDER OUT TO A MAIN FOLDER: grab a subfolder in the left pane and drop it on the thin strip at the very bottom of the folder list to promote it back to a top-level folder. (Dropping onto another folder still nests it inside; this is the way back out.)"],t.s)
B.aYn=new A.v(605,B.agz)
B.ag0=s(["NO MORE FLICKER WHEN REORDERING ITEMS IN A FOLDER: dragging an item (collapsed ones especially) to reorder it inside a folder used to flash and jump \u2014 because a background sync update was rebuilding the list under your finger mid-drag. The list now holds still for the whole drag and only settles once you drop. Smooth on iPhone and everywhere."],t.s)
B.aTa=new A.v(604,B.ag0)
B.aby=s(["WINDOWS RECORDINGS NOW PLAY ON YOUR OTHER DEVICES: audio recorded on the Windows app is finally uploaded to your account, so it plays on your iPhone and Android too. (Before, only the Windows machine that made the recording could play it \u2014 everywhere else said \u201cno cloud audio.\u201d The upload had a step that only worked on Android, so Windows recordings were silently skipped.) Existing Windows recordings upload themselves over the next little while of the app being open and signed in; recordings over 25 MB are the one exception for now."],t.s)
B.b3m=new A.v(603,B.aby)
B.ae7=s(["DRAGGING A FOLDER ONTO ITS OWN PARENT IS NOW HONEST: it used to say \u201cmoved into folder\u201d even though nothing changed (it was already there). Now it just says \u201cAlready in that folder\u201d and does nothing \u2014 no false move.","ONE VERSION NUMBER EVERYWHERE: the iPhone (TestFlight) build now shows the SAME build number as Windows, Android and the web \u2014 e.g. \u201c0.1.0 (602)\u201d \u2014 instead of its own separate count. (Takes effect on the next iPhone build.)"],t.s)
B.aSW=new A.v(602,B.ae7)
B.ai6=s(["MOVE SUBFOLDERS BY DRAG: you can now grab ANY folder in the left pane \u2014 including subfolders \u2014 and drop it onto another folder to move it inside, Explorer-style. (Before, only top-level folders could be dragged, so a subfolder was stuck.) On Windows, drag any folder with the mouse; on the phone, hold it. Dropping onto a folder\u2019s body moves it IN; dropping a top-level folder on the thin strip at another\u2019s top still just reorders them. It won\u2019t let you drop a folder into itself or its own subfolder."],t.s)
B.b3T=new A.v(601,B.ai6)
B.ah2=s(["BACK TO THE HOME PAGE (web): using Voicley in your browser, the left pane now has a \u201cVoicley home\u201d link at the bottom that takes you back to the landing page. (On the installed Windows and phone apps there\u2019s no website to return to, so it doesn\u2019t appear there.)"],t.s)
B.aUL=new A.v(600,B.ah2)
B.arw=s(["A LIVE \u201cWHAT\u2019S HAPPENING\u201d LINE: when Voicley is working in the background \u2014 finishing transcriptions, uploading to your account, or downloading an app update \u2014 a small line at the top of the inbox now says exactly what it\u2019s doing and how many items, e.g. \u201cTranscribing 2 recordings\u2026\u201d, \u201cUploading 5 items\u2026\u201d, or \u201cDownloading update \u2014 part 4 of 9\u201d. It appears only when there\u2019s something in flight and disappears the moment everything\u2019s caught up. Nothing blocks \u2014 you can keep working while it shows."],t.s)
B.aVD=new A.v(599,B.arw)
B.aiS=s(["YOUR LAST WORDS STAY IN THE RIGHT NOTE: if you dictate a sentence and immediately tap \u2713 Done or New, the tail that\u2019s still coming back from transcription now lands in the note you SPOKE it in \u2014 not at the top of the fresh one. The box waits a beat (up to ~3 seconds, and only if something\u2019s actually still arriving) for that last bit to land before it closes, so nothing jumps to the next note. On a slow connection this is the difference-maker."],t.s)
B.b2M=new A.v(598,B.aiS)
B.ah1=s(["PHONE UPDATES NOW ACTUALLY DELIVER: v596\u2019s new \u201cCheck for updates\u201d button couldn\u2019t receive the app itself \u2014 the release shelf caps files at 50 MB and Voicley is 68 MB, so the very first publish bounced. Releases now ship in parts that the phone stitches back together and integrity-checks (size + fingerprint) before Android\u2019s installer opens. Same one-tap experience, now with something on the shelf."],t.s)
B.b3f=new A.v(597,B.ah1)
B.ajQ=s(["UPDATE FROM THE PHONE ITSELF: Settings now has \u201cCheck for updates\u201d on Android, right by the version number \u2014 exactly like Windows. Tap it and Voicley checks your account\u2019s release shelf; if a newer build is there, it downloads and Android\u2019s installer opens (one tap \u2014 everything stays in place, no cable needed). First use, Android asks you to allow Voicley to install updates \u2014 a one-time toggle. Plugging into the PC still works too."],t.s)
B.aSy=new A.v(596,B.ajQ)
B.ajk=s(["NEW FOLDERS ARE BORN WHERE YOU ARE: with a folder open, the pane\u2019s + now creates the new folder INSIDE that folder, Explorer-style \u2014 the naming prompt says where it will land, the parent unfolds in the tree so the new row is visible immediately, and a confirmation names where it went. The folder view\u2019s \uff0b menu gains a \u201cNew folder\u201d option too. (Before, the + always created at the TOP level \u2014 a folder made while inside Tasks landed at root, out of sight, looking like it was never created. The stray \u201cTomorrow\u201d from that: one copy is now inside Tasks, the duplicate is in the Trash.)"],t.s)
B.aU9=new A.v(595,B.ajk)
B.aou=s(["FOLDER ARROWS MOVED TO THE LEFT: the expand/collapse chevron now sits before each folder\u2019s icon, Explorer-style \u2014 the arrows trace the tree down the left edge, and folders without subfolders keep the space so the icons stay aligned. The right edge keeps just the count and headphones.","NO MORE BLANK NOTES: an empty note box simply evaporates when it closes \u2014 leave the folder, end dictation, click out \u2014 nothing blank is ever saved. (Notes WITH content always post, exactly as before.)"],t.s)
B.b3M=new A.v(594,B.aou)
B.abR=s(["FOLDER VIEWS SHOW ITEMS ONLY: subfolders no longer take up rows inside a folder \u2014 the left pane\u2019s expandable tree is the one place for navigating the hierarchy. A folder holding only subfolders shows the entry box, ready for its first item."],t.s)
B.b0Q=new A.v(593,B.abR)
B.aeN=s(["THE \u2715 ON THE NOTE BOX ALWAYS CLEARS IT: whether dictation is on or off, tapping \u2715 wipes whatever text is in the box (it used to do nothing when the mic was off). A deliberate discard \u2014 the session audio still keeps everything when recording."],t.s)
B.aYg=new A.v(592,B.aeN)
B.aj9=s(["THE FOLDER INPUT IS NOW EXACTLY THE INBOX BOX: every folder\u2019s entry area is the same live note box the inbox uses \u2014 same look, same anatomy, same rules (Done/\u201cnew note\u201d posts and re-arms, empty box reused, talk or type, click-out posts) \u2014 always there, idle or recording. The only differences: a green MIC inside the box (tap to dictate into the folder, tap the red stop to turn it off), everything you close files INTO that folder, and leaving the folder turns dictation off."],t.s)
B.aTN=new A.v(591,B.aj9)
B.ahA=s(["DICTATE STRAIGHT INTO A FOLDER: every folder\u2019s entry box now has a green MIC \u2014 tap it and the folder becomes your inbox: the same live note box takes over (same rules \u2014 Done/\u201cnew note\u201d posts and re-arms, empty box is reused, talk or type), and every note you close files into THAT folder automatically (the box\u2019s footer shows the folder name). LEAVING THE FOLDER TURNS DICTATION OFF \u2014 the last note still finishes transcribing in the background. No bottom control bar in folders: on/off lives in the box, so the list keeps the space."],t.s)
B.b0y=new A.v(590,B.ahA)
B.aen=s(["\u201cNEW NOTE\u201d ON AN EMPTY NOTE JUST KEEPS IT: if the current note box is still empty and you say \u201cnew note\u201d (or hit the pill), the same empty box is reused \u2014 no more stack of blank notes. The cursor re-arms in it, ready to dictate. (Clicking OUT of a blank note still posts it as a regular item \u2014 that rule is unchanged.)"],t.s)
B.b0R=new A.v(589,B.aen)
B.abz=s(["HOLD A FOLDER ANYWHERE TO MOVE IT (phone): press and hold any folder in the left pane \u2014 it lifts with a buzz. Drag up or down and drop to reorder (the list scrolls at the edges, and the order syncs everywhere). Want the folder MENU instead? Hold and just let go without dragging \u2014 the options sheet opens like before. This is the standard Android pattern: hold = lift, drag = move, release = menu."],t.s)
B.aWo=new A.v(588,B.abz)
B.agl=s(["FIX: MOVING A FOLDER NOW SCROLLS THE LIST TOO \u2014 while you\u2019re dragging a folder in the left pane, hold it near the top or bottom and the folder list scrolls under it, so you can drop it at a spot that was off-screen. (The scroll bands only reacted to ITEM drags before \u2014 folder drags were invisible to them on every platform. Verified working on the phone.)"],t.s)
B.b0u=new A.v(587,B.agl)
B.abS=s(["DRAG AN ITEM PAST THE EDGE AND THE LIST SCROLLS: while you\u2019re dragging an item in the inbox or a folder, hold it near the top or bottom of the list and the list scrolls under it \u2014 so you can drop it at a spot you couldn\u2019t see when you picked it up. Works on Android AND Windows/web (the item lists never had this anywhere \u2014 only the folder pane did)."],t.s)
B.aZu=new A.v(586,B.abS)
B.ajR=s(["DRAG FOLDERS ON ANDROID: hold a folder\u2019s ICON in the left pane for a beat and it lifts \u2014 drag up or down and drop to reorder, right on the phone. Drop on a folder to slot in above it, or on the strip at the very bottom to send it last. Tap still opens the folder and holding the NAME still opens its options, unchanged. And since the order syncs now, the arrangement you make on the phone shows up on Windows and the web too."],t.s)
B.aSU=new A.v(585,B.ajR)
B.anb=s(["YOUR ORDER NOW FOLLOWS YOU EVERYWHERE: the inbox order AND the left-pane folder order are now saved to your account instead of per-device \u2014 arrange them once, on any device, and the Fold, Windows, and the web all show the same arrangement. Reorder anywhere and the others follow. (One-time effect: until your first drag, folders show name-sorted and the inbox newest-first; your first re-drag sets the shared order for every device.) New items still always appear at the top of the inbox."],t.s)
B.aWe=new A.v(584,B.anb)
B.aq_=s(["FIX: NEW ITEMS LANDED IN THE MIDDLE OF THE INBOX \u2014 found it. The inbox\u2019s saved manual order was keyed by temporary numbers that reshuffle every time the app starts, so after a restart the whole list could scramble, and a brand-new item\u2019s number could collide with an old saved slot and get seated mid-list (looking \u201ceaten\u201d \u2014 it was there all along, just buried). The order is now keyed by each item\u2019s permanent id: new items always appear at the TOP, and your arrangement survives restarts. One-time effect: the old scrambled order is cleared, so the inbox shows newest-first once \u2014 re-drag anything you want placed and it now sticks."],t.s)
B.b0E=new A.v(583,B.aq_)
B.asd=s(['FIX: ITEMS NO LONGER "DISAPPEAR" WHILE RECORDING. The list was blocked from refreshing the entire time the mic was on, so anything you saved during a session stayed hidden until you turned recording off \u2014 which looked like items being eaten. Now new and synced items appear on the dashboard live, even mid-recording, without ever disturbing the note you\u2019re dictating. (Nothing was ever lost \u2014 the words were saved; the screen just wasn\u2019t showing them.)'],t.s)
B.b2B=new A.v(582,B.asd)
B.are=s(["EASIER TO CLICK THE CIRCLE: the dot beside each item is unchanged and stays put, but the clickable area around it is now much bigger (the whole strip, not just the 12-pixel dot) \u2014 so selecting an item, in the inbox, folders, and the canvas, takes far less aim."],t.s)
B.aUO=new A.v(581,B.are)
B.aoU=s(["SELECT TEXT WITH THE MOUSE (desktop): on Windows/Mac/Linux the left-swipe \u201cdelete\u201d gesture no longer fights you when you drag across text inside an item \u2014 swipe-to-delete is a touch gesture, so it\u2019s off on a mouse. Delete on desktop with the Delete key, right-click, the trash target, or the box\u2019s delete corner.","CLICK EMPTY SPACE TO SCROLL: clicking the white space around the boxes in the inbox or a folder now drops the cursor out of whatever box you were editing, so the wheel and Page keys scroll the list instead of the text field."],t.s)
B.aTH=new A.v(580,B.aoU)
B.agt=s(["MOVING FOLDERS STAYS CALM (desktop): dragging a folder in the left pane no longer shoves every other row apart \u2014 the list holds still and a thin line shows exactly where the folder will land. Drop on a row to place it just above that row; drop on the strip at the very bottom to send it to the end.","EMPTY FOLDERS GET THE ENTRY BOX: open a brand-new or emptied folder and the same \u201ctype a thought\u201d box is right there at the top \u2014 start capturing immediately instead of staring at an empty screen."],t.s)
B.b_1=new A.v(579,B.agt)
B.aq7=s(["FIX: THE FOLDER PANE NOW AUTO-SCROLLS WHILE YOU DRAG (desktop) \u2014 when you drag an item out of a folder toward a target that\u2019s scrolled out of view, hold near the top or bottom edge of the folder list and it scrolls, just like everywhere else. Drags started from inside a folder weren\u2019t turning that on before, so a long pane couldn\u2019t be reached; now they do (and the folder rows light up as drop targets too)."],t.s)
B.b10=new A.v(578,B.aq7)
B.aia=s(["CIRCLES STAY PUT: the circle handle is now pinned to the TOP of each item, so folding/unfolding never moves it \u2014 repeated double-clicks land in the same spot.","TAB MAKES A SUB-ITEM: select item(s) and press Tab \u2014 they nest under the item directly above. Shift+Tab un-nests. Never fires while you\u2019re typing.","TIGHTER LISTS: spacing between items reduced 65% across the Inbox, folders, and canvas groups.","FIX: rapidly deleting items could fold the survivors that slid under your cursor (phantom double-clicks) \u2014 the fold now stands down for a beat after any delete, same shield the chevron has had."],t.s)
B.b_b=new A.v(577,B.aia)
B.ai9=s(["CHANGE YOUR MIND MID-DRAG: while you\u2019re dragging an item, gaps now open between the rows of the Inbox and folder lists \u2014 drop on a gap and the item lands exactly there. So one drag does everything: sideways onto a folder files it; back into the list reorders it. No modes, no starting over. (Dropping between rows of a DIFFERENT folder files it into that folder at that exact spot.)"],t.s)
B.b3F=new A.v(576,B.ai9)
B.apv=s(["DOUBLE-CLICK THE CIRCLE TO FOLD/UNFOLD: double-clicking an item\u2019s circle handle now collapses an open item and expands a collapsed one \u2014 everywhere the circles live (Inbox, folders, canvas). Single click still selects; right-click still opens the menu."],t.s)
B.b4b=new A.v(575,B.apv)
B.aoK=s(["DELETE KEY DELETES THE SELECTION: select items with their circles (Ctrl+click for several) and press Delete \u2014 they all move to Trash in one stroke, restorable as always. The key never fires while you\u2019re typing in a text field."],t.s)
B.b03=new A.v(574,B.aoK)
B.aoA=s(["DOUBLE-CLICK A COLLAPSED ITEM TO OPEN IT: double-click anywhere on a collapsed row and it expands in place \u2014 no hunting for the \u2304. (Just the in-place expand, not the full-screen view; the chevron and title editing work exactly as before.)"],t.s)
B.aWH=new A.v(573,B.aoA)
B.akr=s(["DRAG FOLDERS TO REORDER, RIGHT IN THE PANE (desktop): grab any top-level folder row in the left pane and pull it up or down \u2014 gaps open between rows while you drag; drop and the order saves instantly. No buttons, no separate screen. (Same saved order the Folders screen uses, so they always match. Phones keep the Folders-screen reorder \u2014 press-and-hold there means folder options.)"],t.s)
B.b34=new A.v(572,B.akr)
B.ao6=s(["FIX: A DICTATION COULD POST TWICE \u2014 once as itself (with audio) and again as a text-only copy about 15 seconds later, if you clicked out of the box shortly after finishing. The finished text is intentionally kept in the box briefly for cloud processing; one exit path didn\u2019t know it was already saved and posted it again. It knows now \u2014 clicking out just blanks the box. If you EDIT the leftover text before clicking out, it still posts (your edit is new content)."],t.s)
B.b2s=new A.v(571,B.ao6)
B.asj=s(["FIX: EMPTY CANVAS ON A SECOND PC \u2014 a fresh device could race sync and create its own empty \u201cCanvas\u201d folder, leaving its canvas blank while your items sat in the original (this hit the office PC today; nothing was lost). Every device now agrees on the ORIGINAL Canvas folder, any accidental duplicates are automatically merged into it and removed, and the app no longer creates the folder just by drawing the sidebar \u2014 only when you actually use the canvas."],t.s)
B.aUc=new A.v(570,B.asj)
B.asZ=s(["CLICK EMPTY CANVAS = NEW NOTE, instantly \u2014 no menu, cursor ready, exactly like OneNote\u2019s click-and-type. The \uff0b button keeps the full menu for tasks, meetings, Claude messages, and groups.","EVERY ITEM IS A CONTAINER NOW (OneNote-confirmed): single items wear the same anatomy as groups \u2014 a top bar that moves them, a bordered body. And dropping an item ON or NEAR another item or group joins them: near a single item \u2192 the two become a group; near a group \u2192 it joins that group. Dropping directly on an item\u2019s words still nests it as a sub-item \u2014 near = peer, on-the-text = child."],t.s)
B.aT1=new A.v(569,B.asZ)
B.ap0=s(["GROUPS NOW WORK LIKE ONENOTE \u2014 NO NAMING: \u201cNew group\u201d creates instantly, no prompt. The region shows just a move bar (no title) until you deliberately rename it via right-click. And the fastest way to group: drop an item\u2019s circle onto another card\u2019s MOVE BAR \u2014 the two instantly become a group right there (drop on the box body still nests it as a sub-item).","REORDER INSIDE GROUPS: while you drag, slim gaps between a group\u2019s rows open up \u2014 drop on a gap and the item lands exactly there. Works for moving items within a group, between groups, or in from the loose canvas. Group order matches the folder\u2019s order everywhere."],t.s)
B.aW2=new A.v(568,B.ap0)
B.aqz=s(["GROUPS ON THE CANVAS \u2014 OneNote-style: pick \u201cNew group\u201d from the canvas menu (tap anywhere or \uff0b), name it, and a titled region appears. Drop items onto the region and they move into the group; drag an item\u2019s circle back out to empty canvas and it leaves the group. The header bar moves the whole group at once; right-click it to rename or delete.","GROUPS SYNC: a group is a real folder under Canvas, so its contents follow you to every device (the region\u2019s position stays per-device for now, like the cards)."],t.s)
B.b2u=new A.v(567,B.aqz)
B.asR=s(["THE CANVAS USES THE WHOLE SPACE: the reading-width cap is gone on the canvas \u2014 it now stretches across everything right of the folder pane.","CIRCLES ON THE LEFT: canvas cards now carry their circle to the LEFT of the box, matching every other item in the app. The slim move bar sits across the top of the box \u2014 drag it to place the card.","TAP ANYWHERE TO CREATE: click any empty spot on the canvas and pick a type \u2014 note, task, meeting, or Claude message \u2014 and it\u2019s born right where you clicked (filed to the Canvas folder, cursor ready). The \uff0b button offers the same menu."],t.s)
B.b_3=new A.v(566,B.asR)
B.afM=s(["THE CANVAS NOW WORKS LIKE A ONENOTE PAGE: drop one card\u2019s circle onto another card and it\u2019s ABSORBED \u2014 its floating card disappears and it renders inside the parent (\u201c\u25b8 N sub-items\u201d, expandable, any depth). Drag a nested item\u2019s circle onto empty canvas and it SPLITS back out as its own card right where you dropped it.","CREATE ON THE CANVAS: the \uff0b button (top right) births a typed note directly on the canvas \u2014 filed to the Canvas folder, cursor ready in its box. Canvas cards are also editable in place now, like folder boxes.","SNAP GRID: cards settle onto an invisible 20px grid when you release them, so layouts stay tidy \u2014 hold Alt while releasing for free placement (OneNote\u2019s exact modifier). Dropping a card\u2019s circle on empty canvas also repositions it there."],t.s)
B.aWF=new A.v(565,B.afM)
B.agq=s(["THE \u2261 PANCAKE HANDLE IS GONE \u2014 EVERYWHERE: the last holdout (folder rows on the Folders screen, and the row handles inside folder views) now uses the same OneNote circle as every item. One glyph, one meaning, the whole app: see a circle, you can click to select and drag to move."],t.s)
B.aRt=new A.v(564,B.agq)
B.asw=s(["SELECTION NOW HIGHLIGHTS THE WHOLE ITEM: a selected item wears a blue outline around its entire box (plus the blue circle), everywhere \u2014 Inbox, folders, lists, canvas cards.","FILING FROM THE INBOX NOW LEAVES THE INBOX \u2014 including Claude items: dragging items from the Inbox into a folder previously left Claude-type boxes still sitting in the Inbox (a deliberate carve-out so in-flight Claude messages stay visible), which read as \u201clinked, not moved\u201d and \u201cthe item is in there twice\u201d. A deliberate drag now always takes the item off the Inbox; it lives in its folder. New Claude replies still appear in the Inbox as before.","ONE COPY PER FOLDER \u2014 confirmed and test-pinned: adding an item to a folder it\u2019s already in is a no-op, and moving one in collapses into the existing copy. An item can exist in many folders, but only once in each."],t.s)
B.aYa=new A.v(563,B.asw)
B.apu=s(["MULTI-SELECT WITH THE CIRCLES: click an item\u2019s circle handle to SELECT it (it turns blue), Ctrl+click to add more \u2014 then drag any selected circle and the WHOLE selection moves together: into a pane folder, a subfolder, onto another item to nest, onto the Canvas, or to Trash. The drag card reads \u201cN items\u201d and one drop files them all. On phones, plain taps on the circles multi-select (no Ctrl needed). A drop clears the selection.","Works everywhere the circles live \u2014 Inbox, folder views, lists, and canvas cards. (Canvas note: the card\u2019s circle now selects/files it; drag the little bar beside it to move the card around the canvas.) Clicking a circle no longer opens the item \u2014 the \u2922 button is the full-window door."],t.s)
B.aWd=new A.v(562,B.apu)
B.ale=s(["RIGHT-CLICK FOLDER OPTIONS ON WINDOWS: right-clicking any folder \u2014 in the left pane, on the Folders screen, or a subfolder inside a folder \u2014 now opens the same options sheet as press-and-hold (rename, new subfolder, play list, delete\u2026). Items already had right-click; folders now match."],t.s)
B.aRK=new A.v(561,B.ale)
B.adC=s(["THE TEST CANVAS IS HERE: a new Canvas button in the left pane opens a free-form surface where your items are movable cards \u2014 drag a card by its circle to place it anywhere, pinch/scroll to zoom, and tap a card for all the normal item behaviors. It\u2019s the first cut of the OneNote-style move-things-around view.","LINKING ITEMS TO THE CANVAS: the canvas is backed by a real \u201cCanvas\u201d folder, so every filing flow works \u2014 drag an item onto the Canvas row in the pane (it LINKS; the item also stays where it was), or use Add to folder \u2192 Canvas. Test-phase note: card positions save per device for now."],t.s)
B.aU8=new A.v(560,B.adC)
B.asC=s(["YOU CAN ALWAYS SEE WHEN THE MIC IS ON: a slim red pill now floats at the bottom of EVERY screen while capture is live \u2014 a pulsing dot, the mode (\u201cdictating\u201d or \u201ccommands only\u201d), and \u201cN waiting to sync\u201d when a live recording is holding the upload queue. Tap it to jump to the live box. Born from tonight: the mic ran unnoticed for half an hour and quietly held 30 items back from syncing \u2014 that can\u2019t sneak by anymore."],t.s)
B.b1P=new A.v(559,B.asC)
B.alV=s(["DELETING NO LONGER SHIFTS THE LIST: while you\u2019re actively deleting, background arrivals (synced items, recovered recordings) now wait \u2014 the list stays frozen under your fingers and everything lands the moment you pause for a couple of seconds. Nothing is lost; it just doesn\u2019t barge in mid-cleanup.","PULL DOWN TO SYNC: pull the Inbox down and release \u2014 it force-syncs right there (ending an idle recording first if one is holding the connection, same as Settings \u2192 Re-sync now). Handy after dictating a batch on one device: pull down on the other and it all arrives."],t.s)
B.b27=new A.v(558,B.alV)
B.aq2=s(["THE HANDLE CIRCLES ARE NOW VISIBLE AND EVERYWHERE: v556 drew them at 8px \u2014 too small to see next to an open box, which read as \u201cno icons at all\u201d. They\u2019re 12px now, and the circle COLUMN is permanent: every item, open or collapsed, phone or desktop, Inbox or folders, carries the same circle in the same spot.","COLLAPSING NO LONGER SHIFTS THE GRID: with the handle column constant across states, folding a box changes only its height (animated) \u2014 nothing jumps sideways and the list stays put."],t.s)
B.aRM=new A.v(557,B.aq2)
B.alN=s(["THE ONENOTE CIRCLE IS THE HANDLE EVERYWHERE \u2014 open items too: every item, open or collapsed, in the Inbox, folders, and listening views, now wears the small circle as its move handle. The six-dot \u2261 is fully retired (the live New-note box and just-created items match too). Same drags: up/down reorders, sideways files into a folder, click opens."],t.s)
B.aTd=new A.v(556,B.alN)
B.aiQ=s(["COLLAPSED ROWS, AS YOU ACTUALLY ASKED (my earlier misread \u2014 sorry): a collapsed item is just the circle handle, the title, and the \u2304. The delete / full-window / remove icons are GONE from collapsed rows \u2014 they live on the open box and in the right-click menu.","CIRCLE HANDLES EVERYWHERE: folder and listening views now swap the \u2261 for the small circle on collapsed items too (before, only the Inbox did)."],t.s)
B.aXh=new A.v(555,B.aiQ)
B.agK=s(["THE ONENOTE ICON IS ON EVERY LINKED ITEM: open boxes now carry a small notebook icon next to the \u2922 \u2014 tap it to jump straight to the item\u2019s OneNote page (only shows when the project is linked). Collapsed rows keep your four-icon set; right-click still offers Open in OneNote there."],t.s)
B.aYS=new A.v(554,B.agK)
B.art=s(["COLLAPSED ROW ICON SET COMPLETE: exactly four controls and nothing else \u2014 \u2304 open, \u2296 remove-from-folder (nested items only), \u2922 full window, and Delete. The \u2296 was the missing one."],t.s)
B.aWV=new A.v(553,B.art)
B.alC=s(["COLLAPSED ROWS CARRY THE SAME BUTTONS AS OPEN BOXES: \u2922 full window and Delete now sit on the collapsed row too (left of the \u2304) \u2014 open or delete an item without unfolding it first. Same icons, same actions, both states."],t.s)
B.b3S=new A.v(552,B.alC)
B.aoT=s(["OPEN-BOX BUTTONS IN THEIR CORNERS (your layout): fold \u2303 at the top right (matching the collapsed row\u2019s \u2304 in the same corner), full-window \u2922 at the bottom left, Delete at the bottom right, and the \u201c\u2192 destination\u201d label is gone. The title row is unchanged.","THE FOLD-INSTEAD-OF-DELETE BUG IS DEAD FOR GOOD: folding now happens ONLY on the \u2303 chevron \u2014 the old click-anywhere-on-the-top-strip fold is retired, so a click that misses a button can never fold a box again. And folding now animates (the box visibly shrinks into its row), so a real fold no longer looks like \u201cdeleted then recreated collapsed\u201d."],t.s)
B.b3r=new A.v(551,B.aoT)
B.akt=s(["PASTE FROM ONENOTE \u2192 ITEMS: copy paragraphs in OneNote (or anywhere), paste into the New-note box, and a pill appears: \u201cSplit into N items\u201d. One tap makes each paragraph its own item \u2014 same top-to-bottom order as the source, each with its AI title, with a one-tap Undo for the whole batch. Ignore the pill and it stays one normal note \u2014 splitting only ever happens on your tap."],t.s)
B.aSE=new A.v(550,B.akt)
B.amw=s(["RAPID-FIRE DELETING NO LONGER FOLDS BOXES: the trash button on an open box was a tiny target sitting inside the click-to-fold strip \u2014 deleting items in quick order, the list shifts after each delete and the next fast click hit the strip, folding a box instead of deleting it. The trash button now has a proper finger-sized target, and for a third of a second after any delete the fold-tap ignores clicks entirely (trash clicks still land instantly). Machine-gun cleanup is safe."],t.s)
B.aVL=new A.v(549,B.amw)
B.am9=s(["COLLAPSED ITEMS GOT THE ONENOTE LOOK (your design): a collapsed item is now a clean borderless row \u2014 a small circle on the left (that circle IS the drag handle on desktop: drag it up/down to reorder, sideways to file into a folder), the title in the middle (still editable in place), and the expand chevron moved to the far right. Open items keep their full box exactly as before."],t.s)
B.aZa=new A.v(548,B.am9)
B.aeL=s(["DELETE BOUNCE-BACK \u2014 EVERY DELETE PATH NOW COVERED: v546\u2019s guard only protected swipe-deletes; deletes from the right-click menu, item boxes in folder/listening/files views, and drag-to-Trash skipped it, so those could still resurrect for a beat mid-cleanup. All delete surfaces now stamp the same short-lived marker the background refresh respects. Deleted means deleted, from anywhere."],t.s)
B.aZ2=new A.v(547,B.aeL)
B.atr=s(["DELETED ITEMS NO LONGER BOUNCE BACK MID-CLEANUP: when you deleted several items in a row, a background refresh could land carrying a snapshot from just BEFORE a delete \u2014 and that item popped back into the list (in its shrunken default look, which read as \u201cit collapsed instead of deleting\u201d). Deletes now leave a short-lived marker the refresh respects, so a deleted box stays gone. If a delete ever genuinely fails, the item resurfaces within two minutes rather than being hidden forever \u2014 nothing can be lost silently."],t.s)
B.aYo=new A.v(546,B.atr)
B.agI=s(["THE INBOX COUNT NOW SYNCS \u2014 RELIABLY THE SAME ON EVERY DEVICE: the one remaining per-device piece of the count was the \u201cdismissed from Inbox\u201d list, which lived in local settings \u2014 every cleanup on one device made the numbers drift (your 59 vs 46). Dismissals are now synced like everything else: dismiss anywhere, hidden everywhere, and each device\u2019s old local list merges in automatically on first run. Every count change also writes a diagnosis line (visible / total / filed / dismissed) so any future mismatch names its cause in one glance. NOTE: needs the one-time backend migration (011) + sync-rules re-deploy before installing."],t.s)
B.b_5=new A.v(545,B.agI)
B.aeA=s(["DUPLICATE BOXES CLEANED UP: notes and Claude items from the bug era could show TWICE \u2014 the un-linked box from a broken close plus its saved copy arriving via sync (v540 kept both on purpose: never lose words). Now, when a box\u2019s own save arrives, it simply replaces the un-linked box \u2014 one item, once. Nothing in your data was ever duplicated; it was two views of the same saved item. (Genuinely repeating the same words still gives each its own item.)"],t.s)
B.aW3=new A.v(544,B.aeA)
B.apI=s(["BAD NETWORK NO LONGER BURNS RECOVERY RETRIES: when your phone can\u2019t reach OpenAI at all (the cellular dead zones), each failed recovery attempt was still spending one of a recording\u2019s 3 auto-retries \u2014 permanently benching it even though nothing was ever billed or judged. Caught live in your 12:51 log. Network-unreachable failures are now free; only real OpenAI rejections count. This is what let the un-transcribed backlog build up over the outage days \u2014 combined with the new Recover button (below), it can\u2019t happen again."],t.s)
B.b1i=new A.v(543,B.apI)
B.ajy=s(["RECOVER UN-TRANSCRIBED AUDIO \u2014 new button in Settings (next to Re-sync): 582 recordings on the server kept their audio but never got words \u2014 mostly the bad-network days, when the automatic retries burned out their 3-attempt safety cap and gave up for good. The button resets those caps on this device and re-runs the recovery scan immediately: silence is skipped (never billed, never fake words), oversized sessions stay manual, and it steps aside instantly if you start dictating. Recovered text fills into the items as it lands. Costs nothing until you tap it."],t.s)
B.b2t=new A.v(542,B.ajy)
B.aix=s(['THE \u2713 DONE EATING BUG \u2014 ROOT CAUSE FIXED: your 12:20 test caught it in the act. Every \u2713 Done (and Enter) close had an internal race: the close answered its own "where did the note save?" question twice, the second answer blew up mid-save, and the explosion skipped the steps that link the box to its saved words, give it its title, and cut its audio. The box was left an unlinked shell \u2014 title-less, headphone-less \u2014 and the next background refresh threw the shell away. That is why notes \u201cwent away\u201d right after Done. The race is gone: closes now finish every step, every time. (Spoken \u201cnew note\u201d closes were never affected \u2014 and none of your words were ever lost: every one of those notes is still in the library and OneNote.)'],t.s)
B.aW6=new A.v(541,B.aix)
B.ajg=s(["NOTES NO LONGER VANISH WHEN YOU HIT DONE (or say \u201cnew note\u201d): while sync was churning against its limit, the note\u2019s save could land a beat late \u2014 and a background list refresh in that gap rebuilt the Inbox WITHOUT the not-yet-saved note, so it blinked out and only a blank box remained. The refresh now carries every in-flight note over until its save lands. (The words were never lost \u2014 the audio and recovery path had them \u2014 but now the box itself stays put.)"],t.s)
B.aXR=new A.v(540,B.ajg)
B.atv=s(["RIGHT-CLICK MENU ON WINDOWS: right-click any item box \u2014 in the Inbox, folders, the listening area, Trash \u2014 and the \u22ef actions open right there: Open in OneNote (when linked), Add to folder\u2026, Move to folder\u2026, Delete (to Trash, with Undo). Same flows as the full view\u2019s three-dots button."],t.s)
B.b_W=new A.v(539,B.atv)
B.afJ=s(["THE STRAY-TYPING BUG IS FIXED (Windows): your log caught it \u2014 my v527 focus-guard was over-fighting: when you clicked into an item, it misread the click as the focus thief it was built to fight and yanked typing back to the New box, while the item still showed its cursor. The guard now only acts at the moment dictation starts and ALWAYS yields to your click. Typing goes where the cursor is.","ITEMS OPENED FROM THE LISTENING AREA ARE NOW FULLY EDITABLE: the opened view already had the editable title (v533) \u2014 now the TEXT edits in place too (saves as you type, closes save too), and a trash button deletes (to Trash, with Undo). Same behaviors as everywhere else."],t.s)
B.aTW=new A.v(538,B.afJ)
B.afy=s(["LISTENING TOP BAR: tapping it now takes you to the playing item IN THE LIST (scrolls there, opens its subfolder) \u2014 it no longer pops the item open in full view on top. Your correction of yesterday\u2019s change."],t.s)
B.aY5=new A.v(537,B.afy)
B.acA=s(["NO MORE EMPTY WINDOW AFTER AN UPDATE: the Windows updater relaunches Voicley while the old copy is still shutting down and holding the database \u2014 the new copy opened data-less until you restarted it by hand. The app now waits out the old process (retrying the database open for a few seconds), so the first launch after an update comes up with all your data."],t.s)
B.aVm=new A.v(536,B.acA)
B.asQ=s(["\u201cOLD FORMAT\u201d ITEMS FIXED \u2014 no more title-less, headphone-less boxes: posting a note while dictation was ON could flip the session ownership mid-save, and the new box never got connected to its saved row \u2014 so it showed without a title, without the headphones toggle, and CLICKING IT OPENED THE BIG VIEW instead of editing in place (your two reports were the same bug). The connection now always completes; every posted box is a full regular item immediately. (No data was ever lost \u2014 the rows were saved correctly all along.)","CTRL WHILE DRAGGING NOW FLIPS THE LABEL EVERYWHERE: hold Ctrl mid-drag and the card switches live \u2014 \u201cMove\u201d becomes \u201cLink\u201d (from a folder) or \u201cAdd\u201d (from the Inbox), and the route line reads \u201cLink into \u2026\u201d / \u201cAdd to \u2026\u201d. Release Ctrl and it flips back."],t.s)
B.aSv=new A.v(535,B.asQ)
B.arL=s(["THE STRAY SCROLLBAR ON THE LEFT IS GONE: the live box\u2019s text area scrolls bottom-pinned (newest words stay in view), and that kind of scroller draws its bar on the LEFT \u2014 on Windows it showed as a odd little bar left of the boxes. It now stays on the right where scrollbars belong, and the duplicate one Windows auto-adds is silenced.","THE \u201cPHANTOM ENTER\u201d WHILE EDITING FULL-SIZE IS FIXED: after every auto-save (about a second after you pause typing), the little \u201cSaved\u201d tick was INSERTING a line above your text for two seconds and then removing it \u2014 the whole transcript hopped down and back up, which read exactly like an enter being added and removed. The tick now sits beside the title and only fades in and out \u2014 your text never moves. (Nothing in \u201cMoving racks and organizing space\u201d was ever altered \u2014 saves store exactly what you typed; the hop was purely visual.)"],t.s)
B.aXE=new A.v(534,B.arL)
B.agF=s(["RENAME ITEMS FROM THE OPENED VIEW: opening a meeting (or note) from the listening area \u2014 or any list \u2014 showed no title at all, so there was nowhere to rename it. The opened view now carries an editable title right at the top: tap, type, done. Hand-set titles stick, same as everywhere else.","TAP THE TOP BAR TO GO TO WHAT\u2019S PLAYING: in the Listening area, tapping the bar that names the current item now takes you TO it \u2014 the list scrolls to its spot and the item opens full-view, exactly as if you\u2019d tapped its box.","PHONE CALLS PAUSE PLAYBACK: when a call comes in while recordings are playing, playback pauses \u2014 and picks back up on its own when the call ends. (Unplugging headphones also pauses, so audio never blasts from the speaker.)"],t.s)
B.b3O=new A.v(533,B.agF)
B.ak7=s(["OPENAI COSTS TRIMMED \u2014 the re-title throttle: while you edited an item, every ~1-second typing pause was quietly firing an AI call to refresh its title (and dictating into an item fired one per phrase). Now the title refreshes ONCE, when you leave the item. Same behavior you see \u2014 the title is up to date by the time you\u2019re looking at the list \u2014 at a fraction of the API calls. (Saving your words is untouched: edits still save as you type.)"],t.s)
B.aZJ=new A.v(532,B.ak7)
B.aiO=s(["SYNC USES FAR LESS DATA (PowerSync limit fix, round 1): the AI titler was re-uploading an item\u2019s ENTIRE text every time it attached a title \u2014 on every closed note, every meeting (the longest texts), and up to 6 old meetings per app launch. Titles now sync as just the title. The cloud-audio backfill also wrote each recording twice where once works. Same features, a fraction of the sync traffic \u2014 this stretches the free tier and keeps the paid tier cheap as the library grows."],t.s)
B.aUn=new A.v(531,B.aiO)
B.alG=s(["THE DRAG CARD NO LONGER HIDES BEHIND THE FOLDER PANE: on Windows, the \u201cMove to \u2026\u201d card that follows your cursor was disappearing in the gap between the item boxes and the left folder pane \u2014 it was rendering in a layer clipped to the main window, so it slid *underneath* the pane. It now rides in the top-most layer over the whole window, so you can see it the entire way to the folder you\u2019re dropping on."],t.s)
B.aZB=new A.v(530,B.alG)
B.alB=s(["THE INBOX NO LONGER JUMPS AROUND WHILE YOU DELETE: your phone\u2019s log showed the cause \u2014 while you were deleting a bunch of items, a burst of items syncing in from another device kept yanking the list back to the top, one jump per arrival. Now a background sync download never scrolls your inbox; only YOUR own new dictations and notes bring the list to the top. Deleting stays put where you are. (Nothing was ever lost \u2014 deletes go to Trash with Undo.)"],t.s)
B.aVX=new A.v(529,B.alB)
B.aey=s(["THE DRAG CARD NOW SAYS \u201cMOVE\u201d OR \u201cLINK\u201d THE WHOLE TIME: while you drag an item, the card under the cursor shows a clear pill \u2014 \u201cMove\u201d, \u201cLink\u201d, or \u201cNest\u201d \u2014 so you always know what a drop will do. It used to show that only when dragging an item that was already inside a folder; dragging from the Inbox just said the vague \u201cAdd to\u2026\u201d. Now every drag states the action, and it flips live to \u201cLink\u201d when you hold Ctrl (where linking applies) and back on release."],t.s)
B.aRc=new A.v(528,B.aey)
B.aem=s(['TYPING INTO THE LIVE BOX ON DESKTOP NOW WORKS: on Windows, going into dictate mode showed a blinking cursor that looked ready \u2014 but that caret was just a marker showing where dictated words land, NOT a live text field, so your typed keys went nowhere until you clicked the box. The composer now mounts the real, typable field the instant dictation starts, and HOLDS its focus against the window glitch that was stealing it a split-second later (the same "keeper" your saved items already use). Dictated words land at that same cursor. (Phone is unchanged \u2014 tap to type.)'],t.s)
B.aT7=new A.v(527,B.aem)
B.aoG=s(["THE CLAUDE FILTER COUNT MATCHES THE LIST AGAIN: you emptied the Claude view and the chip still read \u201c2\u201d, then \u201c13\u201d \u2014 it was counting deleted messages the list itself was already hiding (in-flight Claude messages that stick to the window on purpose were never being subtracted when you deleted them). Deleting a Claude message now drops it from the count too, so an empty view reads 0."],t.s)
B.aVK=new A.v(526,B.aoG)
B.amj=s(["EDITABLE TITLES IN THE LISTS: tap an item\u2019s title right in the Inbox or a folder and type \u2014 it saves on its own, without touching the body. A title you set by hand STICKS: editing the item\u2019s text afterward won\u2019t let the AI re-title over it.","DELETING NO LONGER JUMPS THE LIST TO THE TOP: removing an item used to scroll the whole Inbox back up (a reseed was re-labeling the surviving items as \u201cnew\u201d). The list now stays put where you were."],t.s)
B.aXK=new A.v(525,B.amj)
B.adQ=s(['THE SILENCE-PAUSE NO LONGER EATS YOUR DICTATION: last night\u2019s trace showed it park the mic after a 90-second pause and then silently drop 669 things you said \u2014 that was the "dictation regressed." Now (your pick) when the mic parks on silence, the moment you start talking again it AUTO-RESUMES and your words land \u2014 it only ever skips a truly silent gap, never an ongoing dictation. Tapping Commands-only or "end dictation" still stays parked on purpose.'],t.s)
B.b2C=new A.v(524,B.adQ)
B.aec=s(['"NEW CLAUDE COMMAND" ACTUALLY FIRES NOW: tonight\u2019s trace showed the engine hearing it as "New clog command" and "New Claude quad command" \u2014 neither of which the matcher accepted, so your commands fell through as plain text and everything merged into one blob (the "structure is horrible"). Now it accepts "clog"/"clawed" and, crucially, handles the engine double-transcribing the name ("Claude quad") \u2014 the command fires through the garble.',"A Claude note\u2019s box now has a faint clay-tinted background, so it\u2019s distinct from a regular note at a glance \u2014 not just the border."],t.s)
B.aWk=new A.v(523,B.aec)
B.aod=s(["THE CLAUDE SEND BUTTON MOVED to the bottom-right of the box \u2014 the exact spot the \u2713 Done button sits for regular notes. Same place, same finish-this-entry gesture.","SEND NO LONGER SHUTS OFF DICTATION: hitting Send ships your Claude message AND keeps the mic rolling with a fresh box, just like \u2713 Done does. (It used to end the session \u2014 that was a safety measure against a hot mic, which the silence-pause now handles instead.)"],t.s)
B.aWJ=new A.v(522,B.aod)
B.af_=s(["SILENCE NOW PAUSES CAPTURE (your pick): go quiet for ~90 seconds while dictating and the mic parks itself in Commands-only \u2014 the purple line on the box says so. Room conversation can no longer pile into your note behind your back (today\u2019s \u201cswallowed\u201d items were exactly that: minutes of room talk merging into the box). Say \u201cnew note\u201d or tap Dictate and you\u2019re rolling again; commands keep working the whole time, and the audio is always in the recording."],t.s)
B.aYX=new A.v(521,B.af_)
B.aqY=s(["THE AUTO-MEETING FLIP STANDS DOWN WHILE YOU\u2019RE NOTE-TAKING: it exists for one case \u2014 a first dictation that runs long because it\u2019s secretly a meeting. It had been swallowing an afternoon of short notes into one giant \u201cmeeting\u201d item (that\u2019s where your \u201ceaten\u201d text went \u2014 check Meetings, it\u2019s all there). Once you\u2019ve closed even one note in a session, the flip never fires.","Windows: the drag card now says WHAT\u2019S ABOUT TO HAPPEN \u2014 \u201cMove to Kitchen\u201d, \u201cAdd to\u2026\u201d, \u201cLink into\u2026\u201d (live as you press Ctrl), \u201cNest under an item\u201d, \u201cMove to Trash\u201d \u2014 plus where it\u2019s coming from."],t.s)
B.aWN=new A.v(520,B.aqY)
B.ac1=s(["ENTER RIGHT AFTER SPEAKING KEEPS EVERY WORD: \u2713 Done and Enter now flush the in-flight dictation first \u2014 the grey words you can see get up to ~1.5s to commit before the note posts, so nothing at the tail is lost to fast fingers. The grey draft counts as content too: Enter on a box with only grey words posts them instead of doing nothing.","The Done/Send/\u27a4 buttons are GRAYED OUT until there\u2019s something to post \u2014 no more buttons that look live on an empty box.","Fresh items can be deleted immediately, even mid-dictation: a just-created box no longer refuses the trash while its background save finishes \u2014 the delete wins, and the save honors it."],t.s)
B.b1u=new A.v(519,B.ac1)
B.afY=s(["JUST-CREATED ITEMS LOOK LIKE EVERY OTHER ITEM: a fresh item briefly rendered without its \u2261 handle on Windows, so its box spanned wider than the rest. The handle column is now always there \u2014 a dimmed placeholder holds the exact space until the real grip arrives a moment later. Boxes always look the same."],t.s)
B.aRT=new A.v(518,B.afY)
B.asX=s(["REORDER THE INBOX ON WINDOWS: drag an item\u2019s \u2261 UP or DOWN and it moves in place \u2014 the same axis split as folders (sideways still lifts it to file into a folder, Ctrl links). Your order sticks; new arrivals still land on top. On the phone this already works: hold any box a beat and move it."],t.s)
B.b2q=new A.v(517,B.asX)
B.af3=s(["The on-device diagnostic logs now HEAL THEMSELVES: after an update or storage hiccup they could silently stop recording \u2014 which is why several of today\u2019s bug reports came back untraceable. Every app start now stamps the log, and a failed write re-opens the file and retries instead of going quiet. (This is the eyes for fixing everything else you report.)"],t.s)
B.aYy=new A.v(516,B.af3)
B.alI=s(["ITEMS YOU CREATE ARE NEVER AUTO-DELETED AGAIN: the end-of-session cleanup used to destroy any item that was still empty when the mic turned off \u2014 including ones you\u2019d created on purpose (and ones that LOOKED filled but whose words had routed elsewhere). That rule is retired: a created item survives, empty or not, exactly like the click-out law says. Empty ones are one manual delete away, with Undo."],t.s)
B.b2d=new A.v(515,B.alI)
B.aja=s(["ONE \u2713 DONE, EVERYWHERE AN ENTRY ENDS: every folder\u2019s entry box now carries the same \u2713 Done pill as the Inbox (posts the thought filed into that folder, blanks for the next) \u2014 and an item you\u2019re dictating INTO shows \u2713 Done too: it finishes that entry, the words merge into the item, and dictation keeps rolling with a fresh box. Toward the standing model: every place you can enter a thought has an always-open area and one obvious way to end the entry, typing or speaking."],t.s)
B.b1d=new A.v(514,B.aja)
B.asu=s(["THE \u2713 DONE BUTTON NOW DOES WHAT ITS LABEL SAYS: it posts the note and DICTATION KEEPS GOING \u2014 your next words open a fresh box. It had been silently switching the session into Commands-only, which is the mode that DROPS plain speech: that is exactly how dictated paragraphs were \u201ceaten\u201d on Android today. (The dropped words\u2019 audio was never lost \u2014 full recordings of each session land in Files.)","Whenever Commands-only IS on, the entry box now says so in purple \u2014 \u201cCommands only, speech isn\u2019t captured, tap Dictate or say new note\u201d \u2014 so words can never silently vanish again. The \u2922 and Claude Send keep their close-and-stop behavior."],t.s)
B.b3A=new A.v(513,B.asu)
B.ah8=s(["THE DRAG CARD TELLS YOU THE ROUTE: while dragging an item, the floating card now reads \u201cfrom Inbox\u201d, and the moment you hover a folder it shows the full move \u2014 \u201cInbox \u2192 Kitchen\u201d, \u201cIdeas \u2192 Trash\u201d \u2014 alongside the Move/\u2303 Link chip. No more guessing where a drop will land.","Windows: the app now checks for a new build every 10 MINUTES (was 2 hours) \u2014 this morning a data-loss fix sat published for an hour while the old build kept running. Release builds on the main machine also install locally the moment they publish when the app is closed."],t.s)
B.b07=new A.v(512,B.ah8)
B.ae6=s(["THE DONE BUTTON ALWAYS SAVES NOW: with the mic off, \u2713 Done / Enter / \u2922 on the entry box were doing nothing \u2014 typed text sat unsaved and could be wiped by the next mode switch. Now they post your note as an item and blank the box for the next one, mic or no mic. (With v510\u2019s guard, that\u2019s both halves of the typed-note data loss closed.)","Removing a Claude item from the Inbox no longer deletes it from the Claude messages folder \u2014 the trash on those boxes now just clears them off the Inbox (Undo included); the item stays in its folder.","Windows: drag an item\u2019s \u2261 SIDEWAYS and it lifts instantly to drop on any folder \u2014 no hold, no timer; up/down still reorders. This was why folder-to-folder drags felt broken.","Windows: the left pane now shows folders in YOUR order \u2014 the one you set by dragging on the Folders screen \u2014 instead of alphabetical."],t.s)
B.aXe=new A.v(511,B.ae6)
B.ajU=s(["TYPED WORDS CAN NO LONGER BE LOST TO A MODE SWITCH: text typed into the entry box while the mic was off used to be silently destroyed the moment a session started (turning Commands or Dictate on). Now starting a session treats that typed note like a click-out \u2014 it posts as its own item first, always. This was the last remaining way the app could delete words you gave it."],t.s)
B.b4_=new A.v(510,B.ajU)
B.al4=s(["DELETING FROM ONE FOLDER NO LONGER TOUCHES ITS OTHER HOMES, everywhere: the trash on rows in the Listening List now unlinks just that folder when the item also lives elsewhere (Trash only when it\u2019s the last home) \u2014 and both ways give you a 6-second Undo.","Drop items ONTO SUBFOLDERS inside a folder: the subfolder rows light up as drop targets, same as the left pane \u2014 drop moves the item in, Ctrl+drop links it.","Windows: the bar between the folder pane and the main window is now a real DIVIDER \u2014 grab it and drag to size the pane how you like; it remembers.","Windows: holding the \u2261 to lift an item free now takes a quick quarter-second instead of nearly half \u2014 dragging to a folder feels immediate."],t.s)
B.aRP=new A.v(509,B.al4)
B.ajG=s(["THE ALWAYS-OPEN ENTRY BOX: the Inbox\u2019s top box never disappears \u2014 speak into it or type into it any time, and ENTER (or \u2713/Send) posts the thought and blanks the box for the next one. Shift+Enter makes a new line; an empty Enter does nothing.","Every folder \u2014 including ones you create later \u2014 has its own entry box at the top: type a thought, Enter files it straight into that folder, ready for the next."],t.s)
B.b0a=new A.v(508,B.ajG)
B.asA=s(["The Listening List\u2019s \u201cAll\u201d now means exactly what it says there: everything MARKED TO LISTEN \u2014 your tagged folders and loose tagged items as one playable entry, count matching the \u201citems will play\u201d total. (v506 briefly played the whole library; corrected.)"],t.s)
B.aXc=new A.v(507,B.asA)
B.amW=s(["ALL MOVED TO THE LISTENING AREA, done right: a pinned \u201cAll \u2014 your entire library\u201d entry at the top of the Listening List, always in sync with everything you have (it IS the library \u2014 folders in order, then loose items). Play now plays from it: your whole collection, no tags needed. Yesterday\u2019s All folder is retired from the folders area automatically. Tagged folders below keep their own play buttons."],t.s)
B.aSO=new A.v(506,B.amW)
B.arM=s(["Collapsed rows: the \u25b8 arrow is back on the far left (tap it to open) and title editing is back in place \u2014 with the cleaner look kept (no headphones/trash on the line). Open boxes fold from their \u25be or anywhere on the top strip. Every folder and subfolder screen carries the fold-all/open-all toggle top right."],t.s)
B.b_D=new A.v(505,B.arM)
B.abm=s(["ONE-TAP OPEN AND CLOSE: collapsed items are now a clean title line \u2014 no chevron, no buttons \u2014 and tapping ANYWHERE on the line opens the box; tapping an open box\u2019s top strip (badge/date row) closes it. Folders get the fold-all/open-all toggle at the top right too, scoped to that folder. (Collapsed-title editing traded for the one-tap open \u2014 titles still edit in the open box.)"],t.s)
B.b_Y=new A.v(504,B.abm)
B.adl=s(["THE \u201cALL\u201d FOLDER: every new item and folder links itself into a folder named All, automatically, from any device \u2014 tap its headphones and your entire ongoing library is ONE entry in the listening program. Filing an item elsewhere never pulls it out of All (links, not moves). Going-forward from today; say the word if you want history backfilled too."],t.s)
B.aY4=new A.v(503,B.adl)
B.ahM=s(["OUTLINE ITEMS \u2014 items inside items: drop any item\u2019s \u2630 onto another item and it nests underneath (hold Ctrl to LINK it there instead, so the same item lives in several places \u2014 it wears a \u29c9 badge with its home count). A \u201cN sub-items\u201d line under a parent unfolds them, indented, to any depth, in the Inbox and in folders. The \u2296 removes a sub-item from that spot only; nothing is ever lost.","The full-size view always shows the item\u2019s DATE now \u2014 today\u2019s items were showing time only.","The Claude Send button tells you WHY when it can\u2019t send: signed out (fix in Settings) vs offline (it retries itself every 2 minutes)."],t.s)
B.b2A=new A.v(502,B.ahM)
B.ah_=s(["DELETE AND EXPAND SWAPPED PLACES on every box: the trash now sits top-right, the \u2922 full-window button bottom-right \u2014 and on the live box the X moved left of the \u2922. Same actions, corners exchanged."],t.s)
B.aXr=new A.v(501,B.ah_)
B.ast=s(["RELEASE 500 \ud83c\udf89 \u2014 the \u2713 Done (\u201cnew note\u201d) pill moved to the bottom-right corner of the live box, where your thumb expects it. Same act: the note posts, the mic keeps rolling, your next words open a fresh box."],t.s)
B.b0w=new A.v(500,B.ast)
B.aoZ=s(["MOVE ITEMS BETWEEN FOLDERS RIGHT IN THE LISTENING LIST: drag an item\u2019s \u2630 onto any folder header there and it MOVES into that folder (the header highlights as you hover). Same move rules as everywhere \u2014 moved, never copied, never lost."],t.s)
B.b45=new A.v(499,B.aoZ)
B.ajY=s(["A \u2713 ON THE BOX YOU\u2019RE DICTATING INTO: tap Done \u2014 \u201cnew note\u201d in the label is the spoken twin \u2014 and the note posts as a finished item while dictation keeps running; your next words open a fresh box. Every visible control teaching its voice command is the new pattern."],t.s)
B.b4a=new A.v(498,B.ajY)
B.an7=s(["HOME IS NOW INBOX: the main stack is where every thought arrives before you file it \u2014 so it\u2019s named what it is. Nothing else moved."],t.s)
B.b1U=new A.v(497,B.an7)
B.amx=s(["\u201cPLAY FOLDER\u201d WORKS MID-DICTATION: saying it (or tapping a folder\u2019s play) while the mic was live used to answer \u201cnothing to play\u201d \u2014 the mic guard was lying about a full folder. Now wanting to listen ends the dictation for you, everywhere: the mic stops, your last words finish transcribing in the background, and the folder starts reading."],t.s)
B.b0t=new A.v(496,B.amx)
B.aso=s(["REORDER YOUR LISTENING PROGRAM: hold any folder in the Listening List and drag it up or down \u2014 your order is the PLAY order (Play-all follows it top to bottom instead of the alphabet). A newly tagged folder joins at the end until you place it."],t.s)
B.aSD=new A.v(495,B.aso)
B.aoY=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES CAN\u2019T GET STUCK ANYMORE: two holes closed. Sync\u2019s recording-pause could become permanent if its resume was ever missed (the Windows desktop booted paused and stayed that way \u2014 thousands of rows queued, messages never sent); a watchdog now heals any stale pause within 2 minutes while a real recording still keeps sync off. And the Claude sender now runs on its own clock, independent of sync entirely \u2014 a written message delivers within ~2 minutes of sitting still, from any device, no matter what sync is doing."],t.s)
B.aTn=new A.v(494,B.aoY)
B.adK=s(["CLOSE THE WINDOW YOU\u2019RE DICTATING INTO: the fold chevron now shows on the box that has your cursor while dictating \u2014 tap it and that box saves, folds to its title, and your words flow straight back into the live box at the top. The mic never stops."],t.s)
B.aSZ=new A.v(493,B.adK)
B.aop=s(["CLEANER PHONE ROWS: the \u2261 handle is gone from items on the phone \u2014 hold the item itself to reorder (that shipped in v490/491), so the handle was just noise. Desktop keeps it: there it also drags items into folders. Folder rows keep their \u2261 everywhere."],t.s)
B.b1_=new A.v(492,B.aop)
B.ajJ=s(["HOLD TO REORDER ON THE HOME SCREEN TOO: press and hold any item on Home and move it up or down \u2014 your order sticks on this device. New dictations still land on top so the newest thing stays visible, and the live dictation box stays planted while it runs."],t.s)
B.b2J=new A.v(491,B.ajJ)
B.acJ=s(["HOLD ANYWHERE TO REORDER: press and hold any item in a folder \u2014 collapsed or open \u2014 and it lifts to reorder right there. The \u2261 still moves it to a folder when held, and holding on the words still selects text."],t.s)
B.b2W=new A.v(490,B.acJ)
B.ac2=s(["ONE HANDLE DOES BOTH: in a folder, the single left \u2261 now reorders when you drag it and MOVES TO A FOLDER when you hold it a beat first, then drag to the left pane (Ctrl at drop still links). The second grip inside the box is retired.","TYPE INTO A NEW ITEM IMMEDIATELY ON WINDOWS \u2014 the real fix, from the real trace: the \uff0b menu\u2019s closing animation was silently stealing the cursor a third of a second after the new box grabbed it, right after the old focus-keeper stopped watching. The keeper now outlasts the steal (and yields instantly if you click somewhere on purpose), and a brand-new empty note on desktop opens with its typing field live \u2014 first keystroke lands, no click needed."],t.s)
B.aT_=new A.v(489,B.ac2)
B.acg=s(["THE OPENAI BILL GUARDS: multi-hour session audio no longer transcribes itself (tap Retry on the item when you actually want it \u2014 that\u2019s what turned into $21 today), failed uploads stop auto-retrying after 3 tries, a \u201cbudget reached\u201d verdict from OpenAI pauses background transcription for a day instead of hammering it, and automatic recovery now uses the half-price model. Dictation and live captions never depended on OpenAI \u2014 a tripped budget can\u2019t touch them.","Collapsed rows lose the color square \u2014 the border already tells you the type."],t.s)
B.b0M=new A.v(488,B.acg)
B.adp=s(["UNDO AFTER DELETE: deleting an item now shows \u201cMoved to Trash \xb7 Undo\u201d for 6 seconds \u2014 one tap brings it straight back. The Trash stays your 30-day net either way."],t.s)
B.aTw=new A.v(487,B.adp)
B.aqU=s(["Collapsed titles edit in place \u2014 tap the title on a folded item, type, done. Saves the title only; your words are never touched."],t.s)
B.aXb=new A.v(486,B.aqU)
B.aq4=s(["REORDER ITEMS IN FOLDERS ON WINDOWS TOO: the \u2261 handle now reorders on desktop just like the phone, and the box\u2019s grip still drags items into folders \u2014 both at once.","The \u201caudio isn\u2019t available\u201d message now tells you WHY: no cloud copy exists for that item (recorded on Windows or before audio sync), versus signed out or offline \u2014 different problems, different fixes."],t.s)
B.b0o=new A.v(485,B.aq4)
B.ard=s(["COLLAPSE ALL / OPEN ALL: the toggle sits at the top right of the main screen, across from the menu \u2014 one tap folds every item to its title line, tap again opens them all.","Collapsed items carry their headphones and delete buttons in the usual spots \u2014 listen or trash without opening the box.","The folder headphones icon is back to full size (shrinking it was never the fix \u2014 the hidden button floor was)."],t.s)
B.b_7=new A.v(484,B.ard)
B.asc=s(["FOLDER ROWS FINALLY MATCH HOME\u2019S HEIGHT: the headphones button on every folder row had a built-in minimum height that silently blocked all the tightening \u2014 replaced; folders in the pane now sit as short as the rows above them."],t.s)
B.b18=new A.v(483,B.asc)
B.an6=s(["Collapsed items trade the type label for a small color square \u2014 more room for the title on one line."],t.s)
B.aXa=new A.v(482,B.an6)
B.al6=s(["THE FOLDER SCREENS GOT THE DENSITY TOO: the Folders list and the folder rows inside folders were still at their old tall height while the left pane tightened \u2014 now they match (a third shorter). That was the \u201cfolders haven\u2019t changed at all\u201d miss.","Collapsed items wrap to two lines when the title needs it."],t.s)
B.b_0=new A.v(481,B.al6)
B.ad9=s(["Folder rows sit at their tightest everywhere \u2014 zero gap between rows, uniform top to bottom."],t.s)
B.aS7=new A.v(480,B.ad9)
B.atw=s(["COLLAPSE ANY ITEM TO ITS TITLE: every box now has a \u25b8 arrow at its top left \u2014 tap it and the item folds to one line (badge + title); tap again to reopen. Remembered per device. The live box you\u2019re dictating into never collapses."],t.s)
B.aV1=new A.v(479,B.atw)
B.ah5=s(["FOLDERS SIT CLOSER TOGETHER \u2014 on the phone too now, and desktop tightened one more notch. Noticeably more folders on one screen."],t.s)
B.aVb=new A.v(478,B.ah5)
B.abU=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES SHOW ALL THREE TIMES: the status line now stamps each stage as it happens \u2014 \u201cSent 2:41pm \xb7 Received 2:43pm \xb7 Done 2:50pm\u201d \u2014 on the full-size view, synced across devices. Messages from before today show their latest time only (the history starts now)."],t.s)
B.aTe=new A.v(477,B.abU)
B.aqA=s(["SEND BUTTON ON EVERY UNSENT CLAUDE ITEM: any Claude message that hasn\u2019t gone out yet \u2014 typed in a folder, or stranded by a bad connection \u2014 now wears the Send pill right on its box. Tap it and the message goes immediately instead of waiting for the 2-minute auto-send."],t.s)
B.aXm=new A.v(476,B.aqA)
B.ai_=s(["READ ALOUD WHILE DICTATING: the button isn\u2019t grayed out anymore \u2014 tapping it mid-dictation asks \u201cEnd dictation and read aloud?\u201d, ends the recording cleanly (transcription finishes in the background), and starts reading.","NEW ITEMS TAKE THE CURSOR FOR REAL: a created item (Claude included) kept losing its cursor a frame after grabbing it \u2014 you had to click in manually. The cursor is now held until it sticks; just start typing.","THE DRAG CARD SHOWS MOVE VS LINK: dragging an item out of a folder shows a \u201cMove\u201d chip on the floating card \u2014 press Ctrl mid-drag and it flips to \u201c\u2303 Link\u201d live, so you always know what the drop will do.","MORE FOLDERS ON SCREEN (Windows): the left-pane rows are ~30% tighter on desktop. Phone rows stay finger-sized.","Claude items show WHEN: the full-size status line stamps the current stage with its time \u2014 \u201cDone \xb7 12:19pm\u201d. (Per-stage history for all three comes with a small server migration.)"],t.s)
B.aXj=new A.v(475,B.ai_)
B.akX=s(["DRAGGING BETWEEN FOLDERS NOW MOVES: drag an item out of a folder onto another folder in the pane and it moves there \u2014 out of the old, into the new, exactly like Windows Explorer. Hold CTRL while dropping to LINK instead (the item lives in both). Drags from Home or lists still just file; if the item is in several folders, only the one you dragged from changes."],t.s)
B.aVC=new A.v(474,B.akX)
B.adS=s(["SEE \u2261, CAN DRAG \u2014 everywhere: the reorder handle moved to the LEFT of the folder icon (your call \u2014 the right side was crowded, and a left grab keeps the name visible while you drag), and it now shows on subfolder rows AND item boxes inside folders too. Those could always reorder \u2014 invisibly, by long-press; now the handle is the one drag surface and long-press is purely the options menu. In-folder order syncs across devices.","WINDOWS: hover over any left-pane row \u2014 folders included \u2014 and a tooltip shows the full name when it\u2019s cut off."],t.s)
B.aZ7=new A.v(473,B.adS)
B.afD=s(["The folder options box now shows a folder icon beside its title \u2014 at a glance you know the word at the top is the folder\u2019s name."],t.s)
B.aY8=new A.v(472,B.afD)
B.amZ=s(["FOLDER REORDERING HAS DOORS NOW: tap the FOLDERS header in the left pane (or the new \u21c5 icon beside its +, or \u201cReorder Folders\u2026\u201d in any folder\u2019s \xb7\xb7\xb7 menu) to open the list where you drag folders into order. v469 built the room; v471 builds the doors.","NEW CLAUDE MESSAGE from any folder\u2019s \uff0b menu: type your message instead of speaking it \u2014 it files into that folder plus the Claude messages archive, and sends itself once you stop typing for 2 minutes (a half-typed message never sends early)."],t.s)
B.aYD=new A.v(471,B.amZ)
B.abc=s(["SEND BUTTON ON CLAUDE MESSAGES: the box you're dictating a Claude message into now has an explicit Send pill at the top \u2014 tap it and the message posts right there, no need to click away. Same trip as before: Sending \u2192 received \u2192 done, reply in the box."],t.s)
B.aSC=new A.v(470,B.abc)
B.ati=s(["DRAG FOLDERS INTO ORDER: grab the \u2261 handle on any folder row (Folders screen) and slide it up or down \u2014 finger on the phone, mouse on Windows. The left pane follows the new order instantly, and Move Up/Down stays in the \xb7\xb7\xb7 menu for one-handed use."],t.s)
B.b2G=new A.v(469,B.ati)
B.ajF=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES CAN\u2019T DIE IN LIMBO ANYMORE: a message that never reached Claude (signed out, offline, server hiccup) now re-sends itself automatically once you\u2019re signed in \u2014 same rhythm the status checker already runs. Your words were always saved; now they always arrive too."],t.s)
B.b_w=new A.v(468,B.ajF)
B.abQ=s(["THE MIC CHECKS IN: if a recording runs two hours without a single touch, Voicley asks \u201cStill dictating?\u201d \u2014 Keep recording or Turn Off, your call, never automatic. Born from the two overnight sessions that transcribed the TV till morning.","The Windows app now re-checks for updates every couple of hours while it sits open \u2014 no more running days-old builds because the window never closed."],t.s)
B.aWv=new A.v(467,B.abQ)
B.aly=s(["EXPAND THE NOTE YOU\u2019RE CREATING: the live box now has the \u2922 button too \u2014 one tap closes the note and opens it full-size (capture pauses, exactly like opening any other item)."],t.s)
B.aVF=new A.v(466,B.aly)
B.amF=s(["DELETE THE NOTE YOU\u2019RE CREATING: the live box (Claude ones included) now has an \u2715 at the top \u2014 tap it to wipe the note and keep dictating fresh. The words go on your explicit tap; the session recording still keeps all audio.","FULL-SIZE SCROLL STAYS PUT: scrolling an item no longer snaps back on its own \u2014 background refreshes were resetting the view to the top."],t.s)
B.aVy=new A.v(465,B.amF)
B.apz=s(["The full-size header\u2019s time sits at the top right with proper breathing room from the edge \u2014 visible, compact (\u201c12:21am\u201d), box-style."],t.s)
B.aXg=new A.v(464,B.apz)
B.aqy=s(["The full-size header\u2019s date/time is back on the right \u2014 box-style, compact (\u201c12:21am\u201d)."],t.s)
B.aT4=new A.v(463,B.aqy)
B.aiV=s(["MAXIMIZING A CLAUDE BOX OPENS THE RIGHT ITEM: filed-but-visible Claude messages were missing from the full-screen pager\u2019s list, so maximizing one jumped to a different item entirely. The list now matches what you see, and as a safety net a box that ever goes missing from it opens alone instead of opening the wrong thing."],t.s)
B.aYd=new A.v(462,B.aiV)
B.asH=s(["THE FULL-SIZE VIEW WEARS THE BOX\u2019S BORDER: the title + text card now has the same type-colored outline the main-window boxes wear (blue note, green task, amber meeting, clay Claude) \u2014 the full view reads as a giant version of the box."],t.s)
B.b_g=new A.v(461,B.asH)
B.alM=s(["FULL-SIZE POLISH: the \u201cTranscript\u201d labels are gone (the text just leads), the time moved in from the far right to sit beside the headphones, and it\u2019s compact now \u2014 \u201c12:21am\u201d for today, \u201cJul 11 \xb7 5:32pm\u201d for older items."],t.s)
B.aWB=new A.v(460,B.alM)
B.ain=s(["FULL-SIZE VIEW DECLUTTERED: Read aloud now sits to the right of the folder chips, and the duplicate date/time under the text is gone (the header owns it) \u2014 the meta line keeps just the destination."],t.s)
B.aZS=new A.v(459,B.ain)
B.akA=s(["THE FULL-SIZE HEADER NOW MATCHES THE BOXES: type badge on the left, the listening headphones right beside it (tap to add/remove \u2014 same control as the boxes), and the date/time pinned top right. The separate \u201cAdd to listening list\u201d card below is retired \u2014 one header, one control."],t.s)
B.aUa=new A.v(458,B.akA)
B.agV=s(["THE FULL-SIZE VIEW KNOWS WHAT IT IS: the top bar now shows the item\u2019s TYPE \u2014 the same colored badge the boxes wear (Note / Task / Meeting / Claude) \u2014 plus the date. And there\u2019s just ONE title now: big, bold, wrapping to two lines, and editable right in place \u2014 the little duplicate title field is gone."],t.s)
B.aZv=new A.v(457,B.agV)
B.aoJ=s(["CLAUDE REPLIES YOU CAN ACTUALLY READ: the reply and the Sent \xb7 Received \xb7 Done ladder now show in BOTH big views of a Claude item \u2014 the tap-open sheet AND the full window \u2014 at full reading size (the reply in body text, the ladder a step bigger), pinned right under the message so no scrolling is needed."],t.s)
B.b3G=new A.v(456,B.aoJ)
B.ack=s(["CLAUDE ITEMS IN THE FULL WINDOW: opening a Claude message full-size now shows the whole round-trip \u2014 my reply right under your message and the Sent \xb7 Received \xb7 Done ladder beneath it, updating live as the desk works. The title bar says \u201cClaude\u201d for untitled ones too."],t.s)
B.aU5=new A.v(455,B.ack)
B.aiw=s(["INLINE EDITS STICK NOW: editing an item\u2019s text and leaving the window no longer shows the old text when you come back \u2014 your edits were saving safely underneath, but the main window kept showing its own stale copy. Every save now updates the window at the same instant it writes the database.","Notes finished on the desktop app now show up properly in the main window on every device (they were saved with a blank type that the window\u2019s filter couldn\u2019t see)."],t.s)
B.aUE=new A.v(454,B.aiw)
B.aiJ=s(["DELETING AN ITEM NO LONGER JUMPS THE WINDOW OR THE CURSOR: deleting a box you were typing or dictating into used to hand the cursor to the item below it \u2014 the list scrolled to follow it, and your dictation started landing in the wrong note. Now a delete cleanly lets go first: the cursor drops, the list stays put, and dictation falls back to its own live box."],t.s)
B.aVp=new A.v(453,B.aiJ)
B.akG=s(["YOUR PHONE STOPS FILLING UP: while charging and idle, Voicley quietly swaps the bulky raw audio of already-transcribed, already-uploaded recordings for verified compact copies \u2014 about 90% smaller per file, playing identically. Nothing is ever touched unless the transcript is safe, the upload is confirmed, and the replacement passes a duration check; every swap is listed in Settings \u2192 Item changes."],t.s)
B.aWr=new A.v(452,B.akG)
B.amf=s(["WINDOWS PLAYS AUDIO NOW: the play button in the full item view (and everywhere else on desktop) actually plays your recordings \u2014 Windows was missing its audio engine entirely, so the button did nothing. The reader also plays real recordings on Windows now instead of always reading with the robot voice."],t.s)
B.aYz=new A.v(451,B.amf)
B.atx=s(["THE WHOLE TREE IN THE LEFT PANE: folders with subfolders now expand in place \u2014 tap the \u203a chevron to unfold a folder\u2019s subfolders right in the pane, nested to any depth, indented so you can see the shape of your library. The pane remembers what you left open. Rows keep everything: tap to open, long-press for actions, drag-to-file, counts, headphones."],t.s)
B.aV6=new A.v(450,B.atx)
B.ahI=s(["SPEAK THE DESTINATION: \u201cnew note in cash flow\u201d opens a fresh note that FILES ITSELF into that folder when it closes \u2014 the live box shows \u201c\u2192 cash flow\u201d so you can see where it\u2019s headed. Fuzzy name matching forgives filler and truncation; a genuinely new name creates the folder.","READ ANY FOLDER BY VOICE: in Commands only, \u201cread the cash flow folder\u201d (or \u201cplay ideas\u201d) reads that folder top to bottom. \u201cPause/resume reading\u201d, \u201cskip\u201d, and \u201cprevious item\u201d control playback."],t.s)
B.aZA=new A.v(449,B.ahI)
B.asf=s(["CLAUDE ANSWERS IN THE BOX: when the desk session finishes acting on your message, its one-line answer appears right in your clay box under the Done footer \u2014 \u201cClaude: Filed to brainstorm \u2713\u201d. Every message becomes a tiny completed conversation you can read from the phone."],t.s)
B.aRz=new A.v(448,B.asf)
B.atJ=s(["Sending a Claude message now turns dictation OFF automatically (unless you asked for a new note in the same breath) \u2014 no more open mic collecting the TV after your message ships.","The \u201cplay the listening folder\u201d command accepts your own phrases now \u2014 its row in Settings \u2192 Voice commands has the \uff0b phrase button like the rest."],t.s)
B.b_l=new A.v(447,B.atJ)
B.ahZ=s(["Android now shows the real release number \u2014 \u201cv446\u201d \u2014 in App info and system dropdowns instead of the meaningless \u201c0.1.0\u201d. Settings footer matches."],t.s)
B.b3I=new A.v(446,B.ahZ)
B.ajd=s(["Typing into a Claude box works like every other box now \u2014 its status updates were quietly rebuilding the screen under your finger and stealing the cursor. The screen no longer repaints while your cursor is in any box.","Network log dates are readable now \u2014 brighter and a step bolder."],t.s)
B.aS_=new A.v(445,B.ajd)
B.ahK=s(["\u201cNew Claude command\u201d now works too \u2014 message, note, or command, whichever word comes out.","The Network log now reads human-first: each incident leads with its plain-English story at full size, and the raw technical detail sits under its own \u201cTECHNICAL\u201d divider \u2014 collapsed until you tap it."],t.s)
B.b3x=new A.v(444,B.ahK)
B.aiU=s(["DICTATION RELIABILITY: the engine is now told to listen for \u201cClaude\u201d and \u201cVoicley\u201d by name (far fewer clod/quad mishearings at the source).","Words spoken while the connection is down are RECOVERED: when a dead network outlasts the live buffer, the missing stretch is re-transcribed from the kept audio when you stop, and the words join the session\u2019s recording. Tonight\u2019s 8-minute outage would have healed itself.","The live box stopped flashing: the grey draft now only grows as you speak \u2014 the polished rewrite lands once, when each passage finalizes, instead of repainting every half-second guess.","Fixed: starting a Claude message, saying nothing, and then \u201cnew note\u201d made the NEW note a Claude box \u2014 the leftover addressing now dies with the empty box it belonged to."],t.s)
B.aS2=new A.v(443,B.aiU)
B.agg=s(["\u201cNew Claude message\u201d \u2014 the pause-after-the-phrase case: saying the command and then pausing was splitting the word \u201cmessage\u201d off the end and the command never fired. A complete phrase now always wins. \u201cNewclaud\u201d as one squashed word counts too."],t.s)
B.aRo=new A.v(442,B.agg)
B.ai3=s(["\u201cNew Claude message\u201d now survives being split across transcription chunks \u2014 the exact failure from tonight\u2019s log (the engine heard \u201cNew clod\u201d \u2026 \u201cmessage\u201d as two pieces) is caught and reassembled, so the command fires even when the recognizer breathes mid-phrase."],t.s)
B.aW0=new A.v(441,B.ai3)
B.agJ=s(["Claude messages ACTUALLY appear on the main screen now \u2014 the screen\u2019s loader had a pre-v434 list of item types that silently dropped them (they lived in the folder but never on Home). Your existing messages all show up after this update.","The Android back button, for real this time: Android 13+ routes the button around the fix v438 used; that routing is now configured so back steps back everywhere. Third time\u2019s the proven one."],t.s)
B.aX_=new A.v(440,B.agJ)
B.amu=s(["\u201cNew Claude message\u201d now actually works: it fires mid-dictation like \u201cnew task\u201d (closing your current note and opening a fresh Claude box), and it forgives how the engine hears the name \u2014 \u201cnew clot message\u201d, \u201cnew quad message\u201d and friends all count."],t.s)
B.aWW=new A.v(439,B.amu)
B.asv=s(["Claude messages now STAY on the main screen (and in the Claude chip) after they complete \u2014 they were vanishing into their folder seconds after sending because the desk session got fast. The \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder remains their permanent archive.","The Android back button truly behaves like the app\u2019s \u2039 back now \u2014 v436\u2019s fix registered at the wrong layer and Settings still minimized; this one intercepts the button itself. Dialogs still close first, and on Home the button still minimizes as Android expects."],t.s)
B.aRQ=new A.v(438,B.asv)
B.ah3=s(["YOUR OWN WORDS for voice commands: in Settings \u2192 Voice commands, every command now has a \u201c\uff0b phrase\u201d \u2014 add the wording that comes naturally (\u201cthat\u2019s all\u201d for end note, \u201chey Claude\u201d for message for Claude\u2026). Your phrases ADD to the built-ins, they never replace them, and they\u2019re checked so no two commands can collide."],t.s)
B.aSh=new A.v(437,B.ah3)
B.abE=s(["The Android back button now behaves like the app\u2019s \u2039 back everywhere: in Settings, Files, folders and the rest it steps back (or returns Home) instead of minimizing the app. On the Home screen it still minimizes, as Android convention expects."],t.s)
B.aUr=new A.v(436,B.abE)
B.adM=s(["Create items INSIDE a folder: a new \uff0b at the top of every folder makes a note, task, or meeting that\u2019s born already filed there \u2014 and the cursor lands in its box so you can type (or tap its body and dictate) immediately.","\u201cNew Claude message\u201d now works as a spoken opener too (same as \u201cmessage for Claude\u201d) \u2014 it\u2019s the phrase that came naturally, so now it counts."],t.s)
B.aWP=new A.v(435,B.adM)
B.arD=s(["CLAUDE MESSAGES are now real items: \u201cmessage for Claude \u2026\u201d (or \uff0b \u2192 Message for Claude) makes its own clay-colored box beside your notes, tasks and meetings. Its footer shows the round trip live \u2014 Sending\u2026 \u2192 Sent \u2192 Received \u2192 Done \u2014 so you can see the moment the desk session picks it up.","A \u201cClaude messages\u201d folder appears in the left pane automatically and collects every message; while one is still in flight it also stays on the main window so you can watch its status.","New Claude view chip on the main window counts today\u2019s messages; edits you make inside any item now show up in its main-window box."],t.s)
B.aZY=new A.v(434,B.arD)
B.arl=s(["NEW voice command for the road: start a dictation with \u201cmessage for Claude\u201d and everything you say goes to your Claude session when the note closes (\u201cend note\u201d or Off). The end-of-note \u201csend to Claude\u201d still works too. Both listed in Settings \u2192 Voice commands."],t.s)
B.aUz=new A.v(433,B.arl)
B.atc=s(["Full-screen items now use the same text size as the boxes on the main window (both follow your transcript size setting \u2014 Speakers blocks included).","Fixed: hitting Stop on the Android listening notification left Play dead. Play now restarts the list right where you stopped it."],t.s)
B.aUm=new A.v(432,B.atc)
B.ar3=s(["THE ITEM GUARD: nothing can destroy an item unless you told it to. Every delete now carries its reason; automatic cleanups may only remove items that are verifiably empty at the moment of deletion (the bug that killed real notes mid-save is impossible now); and any code that tries to destroy an item without permission is refused and flagged.","NEW in Settings \u2192 Diagnostics: \u201cItem changes\u201d \u2014 a plain-English history of every delete, restore, and cleanup on this device, with its reason. Anything BLOCKED shows in red with a count on the Settings row, so a misbehaving cleanup gets caught in the log instead of in your library."],t.s)
B.aWz=new A.v(431,B.ar3)
B.aie=s(["RECOVERY: the items that disappeared in the cleanup incident come back on their own \u2014 this build re-matches each restored item to its recording file (which never left your phone) and re-transcribes it. Leave the app open and idle for a couple of minutes, then check your folders.","\u201cEnd dictation\u201d / \u201cstop dictation\u201d now turns dictation fully Off \u2014 the same as tapping Off. For the middle state (pause but keep listening for commands), say \u201ccommands only\u201d.","Faster catch-up: interrupted transcriptions now keep draining while the app sits idle, instead of three per app-open."],t.s)
B.b1X=new A.v(430,B.aie)
B.abL=s(["New voice command: say \u201ccommands only\u201d (at the end of a thought) to pause dictation without closing your note \u2014 the spoken twin of the middle button. \u201cResume dictation\u201d picks it back up. Also listed in Settings \u2192 Voice commands."],t.s)
B.aXM=new A.v(429,B.abL)
B.ats=s(["URGENT fix: yesterday\u2019s automatic cleanup could wrongly remove recordings that were still waiting to upload when the library was opened on a new device (they looked empty from there). The cleanup is removed entirely. Disappeared items are restorable and are being restored."],t.s)
B.b_R=new A.v(428,B.ats)
B.ahN=s(["NEW: a Network log in Settings (under Advanced \u2192 Diagnostics). Every network hiccup \u2014 DNS failures, stalled captions, dropped connections, failed uploads, unreachable sync \u2014 is recorded with a plain-English explanation of what happened, and each issue type shows how many times it has occurred. Bursts group into one entry."],t.s)
B.b2y=new A.v(427,B.ahN)
B.alR=s(["Dictation and command accuracy restored: live speaker detection (added yesterday for the Speakers view) was quietly taxing the recognizer, so it\u2019s off the live stream. Who-said-what returns via a smarter after-the-fact pass soon."],t.s)
B.aVa=new A.v(426,B.alR)
B.agD=s(["Web: your synced items can now actually appear \u2014 the web version was reading a separate empty database from the one sync fills; they\u2019re one and the same now, exactly like on your phones."],t.s)
B.aXG=new A.v(425,B.agD)
B.ald=s(["The sync sign-in got a show-password eye \u2014 tap it to see what you typed before hitting Sign in. (The website\u2019s web-app login got the same.)"],t.s)
B.aZc=new A.v(424,B.ald)
B.akb=s(["The web version (preview) can now sign in to Library Sync \u2014 same account as your phones, in Settings."],t.s)
B.aUf=new A.v(423,B.akb)
B.al1=s(["The \u201c+\u201d create menu now works from Commands only \u2014 even with a task or meeting box open. Creating switches you into capture for the new item, same as the spoken command."],t.s)
B.b_h=new A.v(422,B.al1)
B.ari=s(["The 0:00 items are explained and cleaned up: they were meeting/note placeholders orphaned when a session was killed mid-way. A launch sweep now purges day-old empty ones automatically \u2014 anything with words or audio is never touched.","Fixed a narrow loss window: typing into a blank note and hitting Off within a second could destroy the just-typed words. The blank-note cleanup now waits out the save and never touches a note your cursor is in."],t.s)
B.aTE=new A.v(421,B.ari)
B.aoE=s(["Fixed: a flowing note could wrap past 10 lines and scroll inside its box \u2014 wrapped length now counts toward the 10-line cap, so long notes become \u201cmore\u2026\u201d cards like they should.","This update dialog now shows each release number above its changes."],t.s)
B.b2x=new A.v(420,B.aoE)
B.aeI=s(["Items now cap at 10 lines everywhere (notes, tasks, and meetings alike) \u2014 long ones show their first 10 lines with \u201cmore\u2026\u201d and open full-screen. Steadier scrolling too."],t.s)
B.aXo=new A.v(419,B.aeI)
B.apa=s(["Creating items moved UP: a \u201c+\u201d now sits at the end of the top labels row \u2014 tap it to create a Note, Task, or Meeting. The dedicated buttons at the bottom are gone, so the bottom is just the Off/Commands/Dictate control and the list gets more room."],t.s)
B.b04=new A.v(418,B.apa)
B.ag7=s(["NEW: speakers. When a recording has more than one voice, its full-screen view shows the conversation broken out by speaker \u2014 \u201cSpeaker 1 / Speaker 2\u201d blocks with each person\u2019s words, and a play button on every block that plays exactly that stretch of audio.","Applies to NEW recordings made with the live (Deepgram) engine from this version on \u2014 older items don\u2019t have speaker data."],t.s)
B.aSi=new A.v(417,B.ag7)
B.aqh=s(["Fixed: long items scroll again on Android \u2014 dragging anywhere on the text now moves the page (the text area was swallowing the drag)."],t.s)
B.aRs=new A.v(416,B.aqh)
B.adu=s(["Settings now lists every voice command: tap \u201cVOICE COMMANDS\u201d to see the current phrases, what each does, and their alternate wordings."],t.s)
B.b0d=new A.v(415,B.adu)
B.asr=s(["Saying \u201cstop dictation\u201d now turns off dictation, same as \u201cend dictation\u201d (also: \u201cstop note\u201d, \u201cstop dictating\u201d)."],t.s)
B.b_u=new A.v(414,B.asr)
B.ahf=s(["Fixed: while dictating, tapping an item to move the cursor there now works on the FIRST tap. Putting the cursor in an item no longer snaps the list to the top (which was scrolling the item away and eating your first tap)."],t.s)
B.aZK=new A.v(413,B.ahf)
B.akH=s(["Fixed: deleting an item no longer scrolls the main list. The list now holds its place when you remove a row instead of jumping down."],t.s)
B.aXD=new A.v(412,B.akH)
B.aeE=s(["Fixed: only ONE item ever shows as selected. The focus ring now moves to the item you click instead of piling up on previous ones (was most visible on Windows).","Windows now tells you automatically when an update is ready \u2014 an \u201cUpdate available\u201d box pops up on launch (and when you click back into the window), just like the Android update notice. No more digging into Settings to find it."],t.s)
B.aZG=new A.v(411,B.aeE)
B.afV=s(["Fixed: one tap puts the cursor in an item again (adding the focus ring was briefly stealing the first tap)."],t.s)
B.aTZ=new A.v(410,B.afV)
B.aqb=s(["The focused-item ring now shows on the MAIN list too (it was only wired into folder views)."],t.s)
B.b1E=new A.v(409,B.aqb)
B.aiq=s(["The item holding your cursor now wears the same colored ring the new-note box has \u2014 red and breathing while capturing, calm accent otherwise \u2014 so you can always SEE where dictation goes.","Editing or dictating into a note refreshes its AI title automatically."],t.s)
B.b_V=new A.v(408,B.aiq)
B.apU=s(["Fixed: typing into a new note and then tapping an existing item no longer conjures another empty note at the top \u2014 the typed note posts and that\u2019s it."],t.s)
B.aVU=new A.v(407,B.apU)
B.ahF=s(["The new note box matches regular items exactly: same size (1-line, grows with content), the move handle beside it on desktop, and no microphone icon \u2014 the bottom control is the capture toggle."],t.s)
B.aVx=new A.v(406,B.ahF)
B.apq=s(["The message inside a new note box is gone \u2014 a new note is just an empty box with a cursor.","Tapping \u201cNew note\u201d repeatedly creates multiple blank notes (each tap posts the previous blank and opens a fresh one).","Any note still blank when you turn the system Off is destroyed \u2014 including blanks that posted to the list. Type or dictate anything into one and it stays."],t.s)
B.b1c=new A.v(405,B.apq)
B.arB=s(["Clicking out of a new note now ALWAYS makes it a regular item \u2014 even a blank one posts (as an empty \u201cTap to add text\u201d item). Going back in works like any other item: tap it, get a cursor, dictate.","Fixed the dead tap on the live note box \u2014 tapping it now reliably opens the editor with a cursor."],t.s)
B.b3u=new A.v(404,B.arB)
B.aif=s(["Clicking between items never changes the mode anymore: the cursor only picks WHERE words go \u2014 the Off / Commands only / Dictate control (and the voice commands) are the only things that turn dictation on or off."],t.s)
B.b0K=new A.v(403,B.aif)
B.akO=s(["A just-posted note is editable IMMEDIATELY \u2014 tapping back into it works the moment it appears (its database row now lands before the slow audio processing, not after).","Fixed words being EATEN right after you tapped from a new note into an existing item \u2014 the close was briefly flipping the session into commands-only and dropping what you said next. Dictation mode now stays on across that move."],t.s)
B.b3d=new A.v(402,B.akO)
B.aho=s(["Dictate truly creates nothing now: the note box can no longer summon itself \u2014 not on the button, and not from the live preview while you dictate into an existing item (that was the phantom \u201cnew note\u201d at the top). Only \u201cnew note\u201d creates a note.","Tapping back into the new-note box reliably takes the cursor and routes your words there.","Play in the Listening list now stops an active dictation for you and starts playing \u2014 no more \u201cstop any recording and try again.\u201d"],t.s)
B.b_T=new A.v(401,B.aho)
B.ac_=s(["Fixed: the Android notification could claim \u201ccommands only\u201d while you were dictating \u2014 it now mirrors every mode change (including dictating by placing your cursor in an item), and a session that starts in Commands is labeled correctly from the first moment."],t.s)
B.b0U=new A.v(400,B.ac_)
B.ato=s(["Fixed: tapping Dictate no longer pops an empty note box \u2014 nothing appears until you actually have words (or say \u201cnew note\u201d). Rule 1 for real this time.","The middle segment now reads \u201cCommands only.\u201d"],t.s)
B.aX6=new A.v(399,B.ato)
B.apD=s(["The purple \u201cListening for commands\u201d label is gone \u2014 the highlighted Commands segment on the bottom control already shows the mode."],t.s)
B.b1J=new A.v(398,B.apD)
B.arp=s(["New 3-way control at the bottom: Off \xb7 Commands \xb7 Dictate. Your current mode is always highlighted, and switching is one tap. Dictate turns dictation on (words land wherever your cursor is); Commands listens for voice commands only; Off is instant \u2014 the 3-second countdown is gone.","\u201cnew note\u201d is the only thing that creates a note \u2014 it opens at the top with the cursor in it. A blank note you click away from stays put; a blank note is discarded when you turn the system Off.","New voice command: dictate a message and say \u201csend to Claude\u201d \u2014 it\u2019s delivered to Roger\u2019s Claude inbox with a \u2713 confirmation.","Live captions never give up: on flaky network the app now keeps retrying for the whole session and captions come back by themselves (it used to quit after ~12 seconds and stay text-dead).","The screen stopped moving: no more shifting when the keyboard opens, no frame flicker toggling dictation (the desktop jump), and only the box holding the cursor looks \u201clive.\u201d","The \u201cVoicley updated\u201d notification now opens the app when you tap it."],t.s)
B.b0b=new A.v(397,B.arp)
B.asG=s(["Diagnostics: the main list records exactly what scrolled it, to pin down the \u201clist moves when I delete\u201d report."],t.s)
B.b1Y=new A.v(396,B.asG)
B.aeV=s(["A blank new note is destroyed when you turn the system Off; the four dictation rules are locked into the spec."],t.s)
B.aZp=new A.v(395,B.aeV)
B.alL=s(["The Dictate button never creates a note \u2014 it just turns dictation on; words land wherever your cursor is."],t.s)
B.aVZ=new A.v(394,B.alL)
B.alw=s(["Switching to Commands no longer vanishes a just-opened blank note \u2014 it stays, paused, and Dictate resumes into it."],t.s)
B.aVs=new A.v(393,B.alw)
B.amr=s(["The bottom control is a 3-way toggle now \u2014 Off \xb7 Commands \xb7 Dictate \u2014 replacing the single On/Off button."],t.s)
B.b1W=new A.v(392,B.amr)
B.agE=s(["A new note takes the cursor at session start too \u2014 an item you\u2019d tapped earlier can no longer steal the first words."],t.s)
B.b1M=new A.v(391,B.agE)
B.aiz=s(["Creating a new note puts the cursor in it, ready to dictate; a blank note you click away from stays at the top."],t.s)
B.b2Q=new A.v(390,B.aiz)
B.as2=s(["Off stops instantly \u2014 the 3-second \u201cOff in 3\u20262\u20261\u201d countdown is gone."],t.s)
B.aSN=new A.v(389,B.as2)
B.ap6=s(["Moving the cursor into an item ends the live note cleanly: it posts as a regular item and your words follow the cursor."],t.s)
B.aVj=new A.v(388,B.ap6)
B.afw=s(["Live captions never give up on a bad network \u2014 retries continue for the whole session and captions return by themselves."],t.s)
B.aUJ=new A.v(387,B.afw)
B.asW=s(["Only the box holding the cursor looks \u201clive\u201d \u2014 the new-note box calms down when you dictate into another item."],t.s)
B.b3B=new A.v(386,B.asW)
B.aeT=s(["Diagnostics: every dictated phrase logs where it landed, so a \u201cmy words vanished\u201d report is answerable from the log."],t.s)
B.aYI=new A.v(385,B.aeT)
B.apM=s(["Putting the cursor in an item while recording resumes dictation there (it used to stay paused)."],t.s)
B.aVW=new A.v(384,B.apM)
B.adF=s(["The window frame no longer appears/disappears when toggling dictation \u2014 the desktop screen stops jumping."],t.s)
B.aVA=new A.v(383,B.adF)
B.ahp=s(["A new note opens 2 lines tall, then grows with your words (the 15-line cap is unchanged)."],t.s)
B.aY2=new A.v(382,B.ahp)
B.aiW=s(["The \u201cVoicley updated\u201d notification opens the app when tapped; the screen no longer shifts entering edit mode; the mic meter and status moved below the On/Off button."],t.s)
B.b1G=new A.v(381,B.aiW)
B.ako=s(["New voice command: \u201csend to Claude\u201d \u2014 dictate a message and it\u2019s delivered to the Claude inbox, with a \u2713 confirmation."],t.s)
B.aZk=new A.v(380,B.ako)
B.ams=s(["New \u201cAsk Claude\u201d in the nav: dictate or type a message and chat with Claude, right in Voicley (add your own Anthropic API key once \u2014 stored securely on device). First step of sending messages to Claude from the app."],t.s)
B.aVe=new A.v(379,B.ams)
B.ac0=s(["Fixed the note box flickering on and off while \u201clistening for commands\u201d \u2014 the live preview no longer flashes a box as background speech comes and goes."],t.s)
B.aYY=new A.v(378,B.ac0)
B.aq8=s(["Fixed: turning On really shows no note now \u2014 the session opens directly in \u201clistening for commands\u201d from the first frame, instead of briefly popping a blank note."],t.s)
B.b1n=new A.v(377,B.aq8)
B.amO=s(["Turning On no longer creates a blank note \u2014 it simply turns the system on and listens for commands. Say \u201cnew note\u201d (or tap a note) to start transcribing."],t.s)
B.aTJ=new A.v(376,B.amO)
B.apo=s(["Fixed a crash + vanishing dictation when you started recording while the listening reader was playing: the reader now fully stops (releasing its audio) the instant you turn dictation on, so the mic always has a clean path."],t.s)
B.aTt=new A.v(375,B.apo)
B.apj=s(["The per-item microphone buttons are gone: the system is simply On/Off now, and dictation follows your cursor \u2014 tap any note while recording and your words land there."],t.s)
B.b3k=new A.v(374,B.apj)
B.ai8=s(["Dictation now follows your cursor: while recording, tap into any note and your words land there; tap back to the new-note box and they go there. No per-item mic needed \u2014 the cursor decides."],t.s)
B.b_L=new A.v(373,B.ai8)
B.agX=s(["One cursor at a time: tapping into an existing note now hides the live box\u2019s blinking cursor, so there\u2019s only ever a single cursor \u2014 the first step toward \u201cdictation goes wherever the cursor is.\u201d"],t.s)
B.b0_=new A.v(372,B.agX)
B.afn=s(["Listening now has real system controls: a media notification + lock-screen transport (play/pause/next/previous), and it responds to Bluetooth media buttons \u2014 including a tap on your Ray-Ban Meta glasses to pause/resume. Works for both spoken (TTS) and recorded playback."],t.s)
B.b_s=new A.v(371,B.afn)
B.adn=s(["Fixed: you can type into a fresh new note again \u2014 its tap target was too thin to focus. It\u2019s a comfortable size now (still not tall).","The Listening player is now two rows: the current item\u2019s overview on top, the play controls below. Tap the overview (\u25ce) to jump the list back to whatever\u2019s playing."],t.s)
B.aVQ=new A.v(370,B.adn)
B.amp=s(["Settings is tidier: \u201cDictation destination\u201d and \u201cRecording segmentation\u201d moved into the collapsible Advanced section at the bottom, so the everyday controls lead."],t.s)
B.b1V=new A.v(369,B.amp)
B.apR=s(["Play in the Listening list now stays ON THE LIST and follows the playing item down through folders and subfolders \u2014 it no longer jumps the first item into the full-screen player.","A fresh \u201cnew note\u201d box is the right height again \u2014 a blank note no longer opens very tall."],t.s)
B.aVE=new A.v(368,B.apR)
B.atF=s(["The Listening list now shows ONLY what will play \u2014 an item you\u2019ve un-toggled inside a folder no longer lingers in the list.","Playback now traverses the list in the exact order you see it: a folder\u2019s own items first (top to bottom), then into its subfolders \u2014 so the view follows straight down the list as it plays."],t.s)
B.aUv=new A.v(367,B.atF)
B.apS=s(["New voice command: while listening for commands (after \u201cend note\u201d), say \u201cplay the listening folder\u201d (or \u201cplay my list\u201d / \u201cstart listening\u201d) and Voicley ends the session and starts reading your listening list \u2014 hands-free."],t.s)
B.aYL=new A.v(366,B.apS)
B.atl=s(["The Listening list now FOLLOWS playback through the whole hierarchy: as each item finishes, the view scrolls to the next one, and when playback moves into a subfolder that folder opens automatically \u2014 the playing item is always in view."],t.s)
B.aYW=new A.v(365,B.atl)
B.am7=s(["A new note shows a BLINKING CURSOR from the moment it appears \u2014 always on, right where the next words will land (no keyboard pops up).","The mic label no longer claims \u201cnot available\u201d before you\u2019ve said anything: a quiet room now reads \u201cListening \u2014 waiting for sound\u201d; red is reserved for a mic that actually cut out."],t.s)
B.b14=new A.v(364,B.am7)
B.ala=s(["Your cursor now SURVIVES the mic tap: tapping a note\u2019s mic (or the live box\u2019s) no longer hides the caret \u2014 you can see exactly where the dictation will land."],t.s)
B.b31=new A.v(363,B.ala)
B.an8=s(["Dictating with the cursor inside a word no longer splits it \u2014 the new words land right AFTER that word.","New notes are the right height: a closed note no longer keeps invisible trailing blank lines that made a 1\u20132 line note render very tall (existing tall items flatten too)."],t.s)
B.aZO=new A.v(362,B.an8)
B.ak1=s(["Boxes show up to 15 LINES of text again \u2014 the original spec: a long note lists its first 15 lines with a \u201cmore\u2026\u201d button into the full window (the old rule collapsed anything past ~2 sentences to a 4-line preview)."],t.s)
B.b2_=new A.v(361,B.ak1)
B.ah6=s(["Dictated words STAY where they land: a note growing past the \u201clarge\u201d threshold mid-dictation (or mid-typing) no longer swaps its box out from under your cursor \u2014 the transcribed text no longer appears and then vanishes."],t.s)
B.aUN=new A.v(360,B.ah6)
B.ajP=s(["Dictating into an item now changes NOTHING on screen: the mic meter/chips stay hidden, the list doesn\u2019t shift, and your box, cursor and keyboard hold perfectly still."],t.s)
B.aSr=new A.v(359,B.ajP)
B.alj=s(["Fixed for real: tap an item\u2019s mic and the box holds perfectly still \u2014 your cursor stays exactly where you put it, and dictation lands there, not at the bottom."],t.s)
B.b3q=new A.v(358,B.alj)
B.asi=s(["The mic on an item works the simple way again: for notes under ~15 lines, tapping it changes NOTHING \u2014 same box, same text, and your dictation lands right at your cursor, freely mixed with typing."],t.s)
B.aU7=new A.v(357,B.asi)
B.ajr=s(["Edit mode fixed for real: every note\u2019s text is now fully visible and reachable while editing \u2014 the field follows your cursor and scrolls when needed. Longer notes go to the full window automatically."],t.s)
B.aUB=new A.v(356,B.ajr)
B.as_=s(["Tapping an item\u2019s mic no longer shrinks the box \u2014 the note\u2019s text stays fully visible while you dictate into it (large notes keep their preview height).","This \u201cWhat\u2019s new\u201d popup is easier to read \u2014 bigger text."],t.s)
B.aVB=new A.v(355,B.as_)
B.alb=s(["Long notes no longer fight the list: a big item shows as a compact preview (title + first lines + \u201ctap to open\u201d), and its reading/editing lives in the full window. Short notes still edit right in place."],t.s)
B.aTG=new A.v(354,B.alb)
B.aqi=s(["Dictation now lands WHERE YOUR CURSOR IS when you\u2019re typing \u2014 and the live grey preview flows inline with your words instead of flashing on a line below.","The reader now speaks each item\u2019s TITLE before its text."],t.s)
B.aZj=new A.v(353,B.aqi)
B.ap8=s(["Listening is now ONE simple switch per item: the headphones tag decides if it plays \u2014 uncheck any item (even inside a playing folder) and it\u2019s out of the playlist; a folder\u2019s headphones just flips everything under it. Playback follows your folder order."],t.s)
B.aU_=new A.v(352,B.ap8)
B.adt=s(["The listening list now shows how many items each folder will play (whole subtree) and a total under the play controls \u2014 the numbers always match what actually plays."],t.s)
B.aUx=new A.v(351,B.adt)
B.af2=s(["The listening list\u2019s controls moved to the TOP: Play-all sits there when idle, and while playing it becomes the full transport \u2014 previous \xb7 play/pause \xb7 next \xb7 stop, with the current title."],t.s)
B.aSg=new A.v(350,B.af2)
B.al5=s(["Silent recordings no longer clutter Home as \u201cTranscribing\u2026\u201d \u2014 they stay in Files with their audio, and a manual retry can still transcribe one."],t.s)
B.aWT=new A.v(349,B.al5)
B.aqs=s(["Put your top-level folders in YOUR order: long-press a folder \u2192 Move Up / Move Down \u2014 the order sticks on this device. (Drag-to-reorder polish comes next.)"],t.s)
B.aTS=new A.v(348,B.aqs)
B.alO=s(["The Android notification now shows what\u2019s happening live: \u201cDictating\u201d while your words are captured, \u201cPaused \u2014 commands only\u201d while command-listening."],t.s)
B.aSs=new A.v(347,B.alO)
B.at6=s(["The full-sized item view now leads with the TEXT \u2014 the transcript sits right at the top, with date, folders, player and summary below it."],t.s)
B.aXC=new A.v(346,B.at6)
B.amz=s(["The folder pane no longer flashes when you toggle a note\u2019s listen switch (especially on Windows) \u2014 it holds steady through background syncs."],t.s)
B.aUP=new A.v(345,B.amz)
B.akB=s(["Fixed the main home list jumping around / snapping to the top on every background sync \u2014 it now stays put so you can scroll and focus (especially on Windows)."],t.s)
B.aS3=new A.v(344,B.akB)
B.ary=s(["Home screen scrolls again: a long note\u2019s box no longer grows past the screen and traps the scroll \u2014 it caps at 15 lines, with the full text one tap away (\u2922)."],t.s)
B.aTo=new A.v(343,B.ary)
B.ahl=s(["Voice/shortcut triggers (Hey Google, the launcher shortcuts) no longer switch you to Whisper \u2014 your engine stays put, and the default is always Deepgram (the live one)."],t.s)
B.aTk=new A.v(342,B.ahl)
B.akz=s(["When you dictate INTO a note, its box now glows red and pulses with your voice, and the mic turns red \u2014 just like the live box \u2014 so it\u2019s obvious that note is the one recording."],t.s)
B.aRB=new A.v(341,B.akz)
B.acd=s(["Settings \u2192 \u201cFill in missing titles\u201d now also RE-TRANSCRIBES items that have audio but no text yet \u2014 so text-less notes in your folders/recent get a transcript and a title."],t.s)
B.aWI=new A.v(340,B.acd)
B.ahV=s(["A dictation that hasn\u2019t finished transcribing (e.g. you were offline) now shows on Home as \u201cTranscribing\u2026\u201d instead of disappearing \u2014 its audio is always safe and it fills in when the transcript lands."],t.s)
B.aSF=new A.v(339,B.ahV)
B.aol=s(["Open straight into command-listening: a new \u201cListen for commands\u201d shortcut (long-press the icon) / voicley://command link \u2014 point a \u201cHey Google\u201d Routine at it."],t.s)
B.aRJ=new A.v(338,B.aol)
B.aef=s(["A folder\u2019s headphones are now a master switch: one tap turns listening on (or off) for EVERYTHING under it \u2014 every subfolder and item."],t.s)
B.b2p=new A.v(337,B.aef)
B.adV=s(["Dictating into a note now lets you TYPE while the mic runs \u2014 talk and type at the same time, right in that box, exactly like the live one."],t.s)
B.aSt=new A.v(336,B.adV)
B.afO=s(["Dictate into a note IN PLACE \u2014 tap a note\u2019s mic and your words now stream into THAT box (no separate \u201cnew\u201d box); \u201cend note\u201d saves them to it."],t.s)
B.aXJ=new A.v(335,B.afO)
B.ar1=s(["Item box now keeps the date/time and drops the transcription length (the length shows in the full-window player)."],t.s)
B.b1I=new A.v(334,B.ar1)
B.aoh=s(["The dictation time moved off the item box into the full-sized view \u2014 the box is cleaner (just the length up top)."],t.s)
B.aWf=new A.v(333,B.aoh)
B.aoB=s(["Toggling a note\u2019s listen switch no longer flashes the folder pane on Windows."],t.s)
B.aZC=new A.v(332,B.aoB)
B.af6=s(["The note\u2019s mic now works exactly like the live box: tap to dictate into the note, then tap again to pause/resume (mic \u2194 mic-slash)."],t.s)
B.aUH=new A.v(331,B.af6)
B.abN=s(["The note\u2019s mic \ud83c\udf99 now sits to the right of the text, in the same spot as the live dictation box."],t.s)
B.aTi=new A.v(330,B.abN)
B.aoz=s(["Every note now has a mic \ud83c\udf99 \u2014 tap it and keep dictating into that note; your new words append to it. (Audio of the added part comes next.)"],t.s)
B.aWg=new A.v(329,B.aoz)
B.acU=s(["When a listening playlist reaches the last item, the reader now says it\u2019s restarting from the beginning, then loops."],t.s)
B.b13=new A.v(328,B.acU)
B.abk=s(["Deleting an item from a folder now leaves it in its other folders \u2014 it only drops out of the one you deleted it from."],t.s)
B.b2P=new A.v(327,B.abk)
B.ais=s(["Say \u201cresume dictation\u201d (or \u201cresume dictating\u201d) to pick your note back up \u2014 and the command chip now says so."],t.s)
B.b2K=new A.v(326,B.ais)
B.agZ=s(["While dragging an item to file it, hover the top or bottom of the folder list to scroll \u2014 reach folders that were off-screen."],t.s)
B.b42=new A.v(325,B.agZ)
B.aqD=s(["Tap an item in a folder or on the home screen to edit it right there \u2014 the \u2922 button opens the full window."],t.s)
B.aYE=new A.v(324,B.aqD)
B.aq0=s(["After each update, a quick \u201cWhat\u2019s new\u201d note tells you what changed."],t.s)
B.aXZ=new A.v(323,B.aq0)
B.aca=s(["Edits you make in the full-window now show in your lists right away."],t.s)
B.aW9=new A.v(322,B.aca)
B.afc=s(["Play a folder right in the list \u2014 the current note highlights and stays on top."],t.s)
B.aSn=new A.v(321,B.afc)
B.adf=s(["Folders that hold audio-only notes now play their recordings."],t.s)
B.aYq=new A.v(320,B.adf)
B.amG=s(["\u201cDictation\u201d is now \u201cNote\u201d everywhere \u2014 say \u201cnew note\u201d (the old \u201cnew dictation\u201d still works)."],t.s)
B.b0j=new A.v(319,B.amG)
B.aoi=s(["Type into your task and meeting boxes while they\u2019re recording."],t.s)
B.aWE=new A.v(318,B.aoi)
B.al7=s(["Talk-or-type: type into the live note box, with a mic button to turn capture on and off."],t.s)
B.b0G=new A.v(317,B.al7)
B.afA=s(["The reader no longer traps you \u2014 move around the app while it plays."],t.s)
B.aYp=new A.v(316,B.afA)
B.akP=s(["\u201cTurn On / Turn Off\u201d buttons, a clearer drag handle, and a steadier folder pane."],t.s)
B.b0n=new A.v(315,B.akP)
